window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"dinah-washington-dinah-jams":["A live-in-the-studio jam session in which Washington sings long, loose versions of standards with an all-star band including trumpeters Clifford Brown, Maynard Ferguson and Clark Terry, and Max Roach on drums. Each song runs long, with extended horn solos between the vocal choruses. The programme includes “Lover, Come Back to Me”, “I've Got You Under My Skin”, “You Go to My Head” and a medley. Washington's voice is sharp, bluesy and precise, cutting through the band like a trumpet.","It was recorded in August 1954 in Los Angeles before a small invited audience, a recording format EmArcy used to capture the spontaneity of a club. Washington, the most successful rhythm-and-blues singer of the era, rarely got the chance to make a pure jazz record. Brown and Roach were then touring together as co-leaders of their famous quintet.","Listen to the trumpet chases: three very different players, Brown's warm, flowing lines, Ferguson's screaming high notes and Terry's humorous agility, trade phrases, and Washington holds her own against them. Her diction is striking: every word comes through clearly, even at speed, a skill from gospel singing in Chicago churches.","It is widely regarded as Washington's finest jazz album and a classic of the singer-with-jam-session format. Critics praise it as a rare chance to hear her improvising at length. Some find the long instrumental passages leave too little room for the voice, but most treat the balance as the point.","Begin with “Lover, Come Back to Me” and listen for how Washington attacks the first note without warming up. Then compare Sarah Vaughan's album with Clifford Brown from the same year: two great singers, one trumpeter, very different temperaments. Notice how Washington never scats; her improvisation lives in timing and emphasis."],"dinah-washington-what-a-diffrence-a-day-makes":["A slow love song, in which the singer marvels at how one day with a new lover has transformed her life, set with a steady rhythm section, strings and a soft backing chorus. Washington sings the melody almost straight, with her bright, clipped tone and bluesy bends at phrase ends, and the arrangement keeps a gentle, rocking beat that makes the old ballad sound like modern pop. It is under three minutes long.","The song began as a Mexican bolero, “Cuando vuelva a tu lado”, by the composer María Grever in 1934, with English words by Stanley Adams. Washington's recording for Mercury, produced by Clyde Otis with arrangements by Belford Hendricks, was her biggest pop hit and won a Grammy for best rhythm-and-blues performance. It brought her to a white mainstream audience at the end of the 1950s.","Listen for how the backing rhythm is not swing but a rock-steady triplet beat, the pattern of late-1950s rhythm-and-blues ballads. That choice shows the record's crossover design: jazz singer, Latin song, pop strings, rhythm-and-blues groove. Washington reportedly disliked the strings and chorus, but her phrasing keeps the record from turning sweet.","Jazz critics at the time sometimes dismissed it as a commercial sell-out, but it is now admired as a model of crossover, and it prefigured the pop soul of the 1960s. Washington died in 1963 at thirty-nine, and this record became her best-remembered, sometimes at the cost of her jazz work.","Listen once for Washington's precise enunciation and bluesy slides, then again for the beat underneath. Compare with Dinah Jams to hear the same singer in pure jazz, and with Aretha Franklin, who later recorded a tribute album to Washington, to hear where this sound led."],"carmen-mcrae-sings-lover-man":["Twelve songs associated with Billie Holiday, sung by McRae with a small swinging band that features cornetist Nat Adderley and tenor saxophonist Eddie “Lockjaw” Davis. The programme includes “Lover Man”, “Them There Eyes”, “Yesterdays”, “Miss Brown to You”, “God Bless the Child” and “Strange Fruit”. McRae's voice is lighter and more ironic than Holiday's, with crisp diction and a slight edge, and she sings behind the beat in the Holiday manner.","Recorded for Columbia in 1961, two years after Holiday's death, it was one of the first tribute albums to her. McRae had known Holiday since the late 1930s, and Holiday recorded a song McRae had written, “Dream of Life”, in 1939. McRae also worked as a pianist, which shaped her harmonic sense. The album appeared during a period when jazz singers were struggling against rock and roll for record-company attention.","Listen to how McRae alters Holiday's phrasing rather than copying it: she keeps the delayed timing but makes the vowels shorter and the consonants harder, which turns sorrow into something more worldly. Her “Strange Fruit” is quiet and restrained, and the band drops out almost entirely, a brave choice for a song Holiday had made her own.","It was admired on release and has come to be regarded as one of McRae's finest records and among the best Holiday tributes. Critics note that it avoids imitation and shows McRae's own personality. Some prefer her later, more mature recordings, but this remains the one most recommended to newcomers.","Play Holiday's God Bless the Child and then McRae's, and listen for the difference in weight: Holiday heavy with feeling, McRae cooler and sharper. Start the album with “Lover Man”, then listen for Adderley's cornet answering her phrases and for the way she lets the band breathe between verses."],"carmen-mcrae-carmen-sings-monk":["Thirteen compositions by Thelonious Monk sung with lyrics, backed by a small band with the tenor saxophonist Clifford Jordan. The songs, including “'Round Midnight”, “Ruby, My Dear”, “Straight, No Chaser”, “Well, You Needn't” and “Rhythm-a-Ning”, have awkward leaps and jagged rhythms that few singers dare attempt. McRae, by then in her late sixties with a darker, grainier voice, negotiates them with ease, and her wry phrasing suits Monk's humour.","Recorded in 1988 and released in 1990 on the Novus label, it came near the end of McRae's career and eight years after Monk's death. Most of the lyrics were written by jazz writers such as Jon Hendricks, Abbey Lincoln and others, some specifically for this project. The late 1980s saw a revival of interest in bebop classics among record companies and young musicians.","Monk's melodies were written for piano and horns, not voices, so they often jump across wide intervals or land on unexpected notes. Listen for how McRae hits those odd notes with confidence; she had been a pianist herself and understood the harmony from the inside. Her voice's lower, husky register in old age suits Monk's gruff, oblique style.","It was praised on release as one of the best vocal jazz albums of its decade and received a Grammy nomination. Critics have called it a late-career triumph and one of the few convincing attempts to sing Monk's tunes. Some lyricists' words are clumsy, but McRae's delivery makes them work.","Play Monk's own 'Round Midnight and then McRae's to hear the tune given words. Listen for the tricky leaps in “Ruby, My Dear”. Then go back to her Holiday tribute from thirty years earlier to hear how her voice deepened."],"peggy-lee-black-coffee":["A small-group jazz album of late-night songs, sung by Lee with a quartet built around a muted trumpet and the pianist Jimmy Rowles. The songs include the title track, “I've Got You Under My Skin”, “Easy Living”, “My Heart Belongs to Daddy”, “It Ain't Necessarily So” and “A Woman Alone with the Blues”. Lee's voice is soft and cool, almost whispered, and she keeps it low and conversational so every syllable seems confided.","It was first released by Decca as an eight-song ten-inch LP in 1953, then expanded to twelve songs in 1956. Lee had made her name with Benny Goodman's band during the war and had many pop hits for Capitol, but here she made a pure jazz record at a time when singers were being pushed toward commercial material. She was also a songwriter, co-writing hits with her husband, guitarist Dave Barbour.","Listen for how little Lee sings: she barely raises her voice above a murmur and lets the microphone do the work, a lesson learned from Crosby and extended here. The title song's lyric is about a woman sitting up all night with coffee and cigarettes waiting for a lover who does not come; its slow blues form links this cool nightclub album to much older traditions.","It was admired by jazz critics and has become Lee's most celebrated album, often ranked among the great vocal jazz records. Some regard it as the start of the hushed, intimate style that Julie London and others followed. The 1956 expansion is the usual version heard today, and its extra songs are generally judged the equal of the originals.","Listen in the dark and start with the title track, then compare it with Sinatra's In the Wee Small Hours from the same years. Notice how Lee's quietness forces the listener to lean in, and listen for the muted trumpet answering her."],"peggy-lee-fever":["A song about desire as a physical heat, performed with almost nothing: a walking string bass, a drummer's light touch, finger-snaps and Lee's soft, sly voice. The tension comes from restraint; the arrangement barely changes, but the key rises step by step through the verses, so the heat seems to increase. Lee sings close to the microphone, stretching words and pausing between phrases so the silence becomes part of the music.","The song had been a rhythm-and-blues hit for Little Willie John in 1956, written by Eddie Cooley and Otis Blackwell. Lee's Capitol version in 1958 rewrote parts of the lyric, adding verses about famous lovers from history and literature, and she stripped the arrangement down to bass, drums and snaps. It became her biggest hit in years and is now her signature.","The finger-snaps were part of the record's design, and they turned the song into an act of sly restraint: instead of shouting about passion, Lee withholds. Listen, too, for the key changes between verses, each one a small upward shift that the ear feels as increasing tension without noticing why. Lee's added verses were reportedly uncredited in the songwriting royalties.","It was an instant success and has been covered many times, from Elvis Presley to Madonna. Critics consider it a model of minimalism in pop arrangement, and it is often cited as one of the sexiest records of the 1950s despite its restraint. Some argue that Little Willie John's original deserves more credit, and his version is increasingly recommended alongside Lee's.","Listen first for what is not there: no piano, no horns. Then count the key changes. Compare with Little Willie John's original to hear how Lee turned a rhythm-and-blues shout into cool jazz, and with Black Coffee to hear her hushed style in its first form."],"peggy-lee-is-that-all-there-is":["A spoken-word song in which Lee recites, in a flat, weary voice, memories of disappointment: a house fire seen as a child, a trip to the circus, a first love. After each memory she sings a short, waltzing chorus that shrugs off the disappointment and suggests we might as well keep drinking and dancing. The orchestra, arranged by Randy Newman, plays a cabaret-style accompaniment with a slightly sour, Brechtian flavour.","It was written by Jerry Leiber and Mike Stoller, the rock-and-roll songwriters behind Hound Dog, and was based on Thomas Mann's 1896 short story Disillusionment. Lee recorded it for Capitol in 1969, when she was nearly fifty and the era of crooners seemed over; it became an unexpected hit and won her a Grammy. The late 1960s were a time of disillusionment, and the song's mood fitted.","The arrangement nods to Kurt Weill's Berlin cabaret music, with its bittersweet, slightly out-of-tune oompah, and so the record links rock-and-roll songwriters to European art song. Listen for how Lee's speaking voice becomes more and more resigned through the verses, and how the final verse contemplates death in a matter-of-fact way.","Some listeners found it depressing and odd on release, but it was a hit and critics have come to see it as one of the most original pop records of its era. It has been discussed as an existential statement and as a bridge between cabaret and pop. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is often cited as the most daring record of her late career.","Listen as though to a short story: follow each memory, then notice how the chorus changes meaning as the verses darken. Then play Kurt Weill's Threepenny Opera to hear where the orchestral colour comes from, and Randy Newman's own albums for his dry wit."],"julie-london-julie-is-her-name":["Thirteen standards sung by London with only the jazz guitarist Barney Kessel and a bassist. Her voice is low, smoky and close, almost a murmur, and the sparse accompaniment leaves every breath and syllable exposed. The album opens with “Cry Me a River”, a slow song of bitter farewell, and continues through “I Should Care”, “Say It Isn't So”, “Easy Street” and “Laura”. The feeling is late-night intimacy, as if someone were singing across a small room.","It was London's first album, made for the new Liberty label in Hollywood in 1955. She was an actress with little singing experience, and the album's success made her a star; its cover photograph was part of the appeal. “Cry Me a River” was written by Arthur Hamilton, and it became a million-selling hit. The album benefited from the new high-fidelity recording that made small, quiet ensembles sound present.","Listen to Kessel's guitar: he plays chords and short melodic answers that frame each phrase, a lesson in how little accompaniment a singer needs. London's voice is very limited in range, and the whole album is built around that limitation, keeping every song low and close. Its restraint owes much to Peggy Lee's Black Coffee.","It was a big success and has remained London's best-known record, often praised for its minimalism and mood. Some jazz critics have dismissed her as more a stylist than a singer, but others argue that her restraint is a legitimate art. The album is now a cult favourite and regarded as a classic of the intimate cool-jazz style.","Play it quietly at night and listen for the space between the notes. Start with “Cry Me a River”, then compare with Peggy Lee's Black Coffee and Chet Baker Sings for other versions of the whispered 1950s style, and notice how much the silences between phrases carry."],"chet-baker-it-could-happen-to-you":["Ten standards sung by Baker with a quartet led by the pianist Kenny Drew, with Baker's trumpet appearing in solos between vocal choruses. The songs include “I'm Old Fashioned”, “You're Driving Me Crazy”, “It Could Happen to You”, “My Heart Stood Still”, “Everything Happens to Me” and “How Long Has This Been Going On?”. Baker's voice is soft, high and androgynous, almost without vibrato, and here he also scats, improvising wordlessly as if with his trumpet.","Recorded in New York in 1958 for Riverside, it was Baker's first vocal album after moving east from California, and the backing is more hard-driving than on his West Coast records. Baker was already struggling with heroin addiction, which would bring arrests and exile in Europe over the next years. The album was his attempt to follow up the success of Chet Baker Sings.","Listen for Baker's scat solos: he sings improvised lines in exactly the style of his trumpet playing, so you hear the same musical mind in two instruments. The New York rhythm section plays harder than his California colleagues, and his soft voice floats against it, a contrast that gives the album a tension absent from his earlier vocal records.","It was well received but has always stood in the shadow of Chet Baker Sings. Some critics prefer this album for its livelier backing and the scat singing. Others find Baker's singing an acquired taste, fragile to some ears and weak to others, a debate that has followed his vocal work throughout.","Listen first to the title track, then to “You're Driving Me Crazy” for the scat. Compare with Chet Baker Sings to hear the West Coast versus New York backing. Then play Julie London's Julie Is Her Name for another whisper-close voice from the same years."],"mel-torme-with-the-marty-paich-dek-tette":["Twelve standards sung by Tormé with a ten-piece band of Los Angeles jazz musicians, arranged by Marty Paich. The ensemble includes French horn and tuba alongside trumpets and saxophones, giving a mellow, rounded sound, and the arrangements are tight and swinging. The songs include “Lulu's Back in Town”, “When the Sun Comes Out”, “Fascinating Rhythm”, “The Lady Is a Tramp” and “Lullaby of Birdland”. Tormé's voice is light and velvety, and his phrasing is as precise as a horn player's.","It was recorded in 1956 for Bethlehem, a small independent jazz label. Tormé, known as “The Velvet Fog”, had been a teen idol in the 1940s and co-wrote “The Christmas Song” with Bob Wells in 1945. By the mid-1950s he wanted to be taken seriously as a jazz singer. Paich's ensemble was modelled on the Miles Davis nonet of Birth of the Cool, with its unusual brass colours.","The instrumentation is the secret: the French horn and tuba give the ensemble the soft, dark chords of Birth of the Cool, and Tormé's light voice floats over them almost like another horn. Listen for how the arrangements voice the band in close harmony underneath him, and how Tormé answers the band's riffs with perfect timing. He was also a skilled drummer and arranger.","It was well received by jazz critics and is widely considered Tormé's finest album and one of the great vocal jazz records of the 1950s. Some find it too polished, lacking the emotional weight of Sinatra or Holiday. Admirers argue that its precision is its expressive point, and it has been reissued many times, sometimes as Lulu's Back in Town.","Start with “Lulu's Back in Town” and listen to how the band and voice lock together. Then play Miles Davis's Birth of the Cool to hear where the band sound comes from. Compare with Sinatra's Songs for Swingin' Lovers for another singer-arranger partnership."],"johnny-mathis-chances-are":["A slow, tender ballad by Robert Allen and Al Stillman, sung by Mathis with a lush orchestra arranged by Ray Conniff. The song is a shy confession of love, and Mathis sings it in a high, smooth, sweet tenor with a fast, gentle vibrato. The arrangement is soft and understated, with a steady rhythm, strings and a piano. The record is barely three minutes long and depends entirely on the voice.","Mathis was twenty-one, a young singer from San Francisco who had turned down a place in the Olympic trials for the high jump to sign with Columbia. The label's producer Mitch Miller steered him from jazz toward romantic ballads, and “Chances Are” reached number one on a Billboard chart in 1957. It came at the height of rock and roll, and showed that a crooner could still reach the teenage market.","Listen to Mathis's upper register: he sings high notes with a light, open tone that sounds almost like a falsetto, a sound different from the baritone crooners who dominated the 1950s. His training, which included classical and opera lessons as a teenager, gave him breath control that lets him hold long, soft phrases without strain.","It was a huge hit and became one of Mathis's signature songs. Critics have sometimes found his style too sweet, but others praise his vocal control and his influence on later romantic ballad singers. It is often cited as a key record of the late-1950s romantic ballad, bridging the crooner tradition and teen pop.","Listen for Mathis's softness and his high notes, then compare with Sinatra's In the Wee Small Hours to hear the difference between a young tenor's sweetness and an older baritone's weariness. Try it alongside Nat King Cole's Nature Boy for another smooth crossover voice."],"johnny-mathis-johnnys-greatest-hits":["A collection of Mathis's early hit singles, gathered on one LP: “Chances Are”, “It's Not for Me to Say”, “Wonderful! Wonderful!”, “The Twelfth of Never”, “Wild Is the Wind” and others. The songs are romantic ballads sung in Mathis's high, sweet tenor, with lush studio orchestras. As an album, it plays like a series of variations on one mood: young love, sung softly and sincerely.","Columbia released it in 1958, when Mathis was among the label's biggest sellers. It is often said to be one of the first albums to gather a singer's hit singles, and it stayed on the Billboard album chart for nearly ten years, a record that stood until Pink Floyd's Dark Side of the Moon. It fitted a new market: the long-playing record as a purchase for the home, played over and over.","“Wild Is the Wind”, the theme song Mathis sang for a 1957 film, was written by Dimitri Tiomkin and Ned Washington; Nina Simone later made a very different, darker version, which in turn inspired David Bowie. Listen to Mathis's reading as the starting point of that lineage: the same melody, but sung with innocence rather than anguish.","It was an enormous commercial success, and critics have tended to discuss it more as a phenomenon than as art, noting its role in establishing the greatest-hits format. Some find its sweetness cloying; others admire the consistency of the performances and the arrangers' craft.","Listen to “Wild Is the Wind”, then play Nina Simone's version for a startling contrast. Enjoy the album as a portrait of late-1950s romantic pop, and notice how the arrangements vary while Mathis's voice stays constant. Hear it as a snapshot of the home record player at the end of the fifties."],"andy-williams-moon-river":["A slow waltz by Henry Mancini with lyrics by Johnny Mercer, sung by Williams with soft strings and a gentle choir. The song is a dreamy reverie about a river and a wanderer's hopes, and Williams sings it in a smooth, warm, clear tenor, never raising his voice. The arrangement is spare at the start and swells gently, and the melody's gentle upward reaches give it a sense of openness and longing.","The song was written for the 1961 film Breakfast at Tiffany's, where Audrey Hepburn sang it with a guitar; it won the Academy Award for Best Original Song. Williams sang it at the Oscar ceremony in 1962 and recorded it for the album Moon River and Other Great Movie Themes. It became the theme song of his NBC television show, which ran through the 1960s.","Williams never released it as a hit single, yet it became his signature through television and album sales, a sign of how crooners were moving from the charts into variety shows. Listen to the melody's range: it spans little more than an octave, reportedly because Mancini kept it within Hepburn's modest voice, and Williams, who could sing far higher, keeps it deliberately plain.","It became Williams's defining performance, though many other singers recorded the song. Critics have sometimes dismissed his smoothness as bland, but others praise his control and restraint. The song itself is widely regarded as one of the great film songs, and Williams's version is the most familiar.","Listen for the moment the choir enters, and for Williams's unhurried breath control. Compare with Tony Bennett's I Left My Heart in San Francisco from the same year to hear two crooners at the last peak of their style before the Beatles."],"andy-williams-its-the-most-wonderful-time-of-the-year":["A brisk, bright Christmas song in a fast waltz rhythm, written by Eddie Pola and George Wyle, sung by Williams with a choir and a busy orchestra of bells, brass and strings. The song lists the pleasures of the season, parties, snow, carollers and friends, and the tempo never slows. It is barely two and a half minutes long and runs at a breathless pace, with Williams's smooth voice riding on top.","It was written for Williams's NBC television Christmas special and released in 1963 on The Andy Williams Christmas Album; Wyle was the show's vocal director. The Williams family Christmas shows became an annual American ritual through the 1960s. The same year, Phil Spector released his rock-and-roll Christmas album, a very different take on the holiday.","The song was not a big hit in its first years and gained its present status only through decades of radio play at Christmas; it is now among the most-played holiday songs. Listen to the orchestration: the sleigh bells, the celesta and the vocal chorus are carefully layered, and the waltz rhythm gives it a spinning, dancing feel.","It is now one of the most familiar Christmas songs in the United States, played every year. Critics rarely write about it seriously, but it is admired for its craftsmanship and energy. Some find its relentless cheer overwhelming, and its lyric's mention of ghost stories has puzzled listeners.","Listen once for the orchestration: bells, celesta, choir. Then compare with Nat King Cole's The Christmas Song and Irving Berlin's White Christmas for slower, more reflective seasonal songs, and Phil Spector's Christmas album for the rock version of the same year."],"nina-simone-little-girl-blue":["Simone's debut, a trio album of standards and one original, with her own piano at the centre. The songs include “Mood Indigo”, “Don't Smoke in Bed”, “Little Girl Blue”, “Love Me or Leave Me”, “My Baby Just Cares for Me”, “I Loves You, Porgy” and her “Central Park Blues”. Her voice is deep and grave, and her piano is classical in touch: she plays counterpoint, runs and Bach-like inventions around the songs, so each track is a small piano recital as well as a vocal.","It was recorded in New York in late 1957 for Bethlehem and released in 1958. Simone, born Eunice Waymon in North Carolina, had trained as a classical pianist and hoped to study at the Curtis Institute, which rejected her; she began playing in Atlantic City bars to make money and was asked to sing. She sold the rights to this album for a flat fee, and never earned royalties from it.","In “Little Girl Blue”, Simone plays the Christmas carol “Good King Wenceslas” as a countermelody on the piano under Rodgers and Hart's song, so two tunes run at once. In “Love Me or Leave Me”, her piano solo turns into a Bach-style fugue. Her “I Loves You, Porgy”, from Gershwin's opera, became her only top-twenty pop hit in the United States.","It sold modestly but “I Loves You, Porgy” was a hit, and the album has come to be seen as one of the most remarkable debuts in jazz. “My Baby Just Cares for Me” became a hit again in 1987 after a perfume advertisement. Critics praise the fusion of classical piano and jazz singing that made Simone unlike anyone else.","Listen to the title track and try to hear both tunes at once. Then play “Love Me or Leave Me” and listen for the fugue in the piano solo. Compare with Bach's Well-Tempered Clavier for the source of her counterpoint."],"nina-simone-at-town-hall":["A concert album, partly recorded at New York's Town Hall in September 1959, that shows the range of Simone's early repertoire: the Appalachian folk song “Black Is the Color of My True Love's Hair”, Gershwin's “Summertime”, the film song “Wild Is the Wind”, “The Other Woman” and “Cotton Eyed Joe”. She accompanies herself on piano, with bass and drums, and moves between folk, jazz and art song in one set. Her voice is dark and slow.","It was Simone's first album for Colpix, the label she joined after Bethlehem, and her first major New York concert, a debut at a respected hall usually used for classical recitals. That venue mattered to her: she thought of herself as a classical musician forced into popular music. Some tracks were recorded in the studio and presented as part of the concert.","Listen for her piano introductions, which often quote classical figures and patterns; she constructs each song as a small concert piece. Her “Wild Is the Wind” is slower and darker than Johnny Mathis's original, turning a film ballad into an art song; David Bowie later cited her version as the model for his own.","It was well received and helped establish her as a concert artist rather than a nightclub singer. Critics have praised its range and its folk-song performances in particular. Some discographers note the confusion between live and studio tracks, but most listeners hear it as a coherent portrait of the young Simone.","Start with “Black Is the Color of My True Love's Hair” for her folk side, then play “Wild Is the Wind” and compare it with Johnny Mathis's version. Listen for how she slows the tempo far beyond what other singers would dare."],"nina-simone-in-concert":["A live album recorded at Carnegie Hall in 1964, in which Simone mixes love songs with protest songs. The set includes “I Loves You, Porgy”, “Plain Gold Ring”, Kurt Weill's “Pirate Jenny”, “Old Jim Crow”, “Don't Smoke in Bed”, “Go Limp” and her own “Mississippi Goddam”. Her voice has grown harder and more commanding, and her piano drives the band. The mood swings from tender to furious to bitterly funny.","It was her first album for Philips, recorded in spring 1964 at the height of the civil rights movement. “Mississippi Goddam” was written in fury after the murder of the activist Medgar Evers and the bombing of a Birmingham church that killed four Black girls in 1963. It was banned by radio stations in several Southern states. This record marked Simone's turn from nightclub stylist to political artist.","“Mississippi Goddam” is set to a jaunty show-tune rhythm, and Simone jokes that it is a show tune for a show not yet written; the cheerful music makes the anger more biting. In “Pirate Jenny”, from Weill and Brecht's Threepenny Opera, she turns a German cabaret song about a servant's revenge into a story set in a Southern town, and the audience's nervous silence is audible.","It was controversial on release and has since been hailed as one of the great political records of the 1960s. “Mississippi Goddam” is now regarded as a key civil-rights anthem. Critics have praised Simone's courage in risking her career, and the album is often ranked among her most important.","Start with “Mississippi Goddam” and listen for the gap between the jolly rhythm and the words. Then play Weill's Threepenny Opera, and hear Simone reclaim its song. Compare with Max Roach's We Insist! and Sam Cooke's A Change Is Gonna Come."],"nina-simone-pastel-blues":["A blues album that moves from quiet laments to a ten-minute storm. The songs include “Be My Husband”, sung with only hand-claps and foot-stomps, “Nobody Knows You When You're Down and Out”, “Trouble in Mind”, “Strange Fruit” and the long closing “Sinnerman”. Simone's piano, guitar and rhythm section build to a driving, repetitive groove in “Sinnerman”, a spiritual about a sinner running from judgement, which ends in a pounding, almost trance-like climax.","Recorded in 1965 for Philips, it followed Nina Simone in Concert and reflected her growing interest in Black musical traditions and the civil rights movement. “Sinnerman” was a traditional spiritual that Simone had learned from her mother's church services. “Strange Fruit”, the anti-lynching song made famous by Billie Holiday, was an act of homage and protest at a time of violence in the South.","“Be My Husband” is performed with only voice and percussion, recalling work songs and field hollers. In “Sinnerman”, listen for how the piano, drums and clapping build layer by layer until the whole band is chanting; it anticipates the hypnotic grooves of later soul and even electronic dance music. The track has been sampled widely in hip-hop.","It was well received and “Sinnerman” has become one of Simone's most famous recordings, used in many films and sampled by producers. Critics praise the album's rawness and range. Some have argued that its “Strange Fruit” is the most harrowing version after Holiday's; others prefer Holiday's original.","Play “Sinnerman” loud, and listen for how the rhythm keeps building. Then go back to “Be My Husband” and “Strange Fruit”. Compare with Billie Holiday's Strange Fruit and Mahalia Jackson's gospel recordings to hear the church and blues traditions Simone drew on and remade."],"edith-piaf-la-vie-en-rose":["A slow, swaying love song, barely three minutes long, in which Piaf's voice rises out of a soft bed of strings and a gently rocking rhythm. The melody moves in small, caressing steps, almost spoken, before opening into a broad refrain that she leans on with her whole weight. The singer describes how the world changes colour when her lover holds her and speaks to her. What you remember is the voice: hard-edged, vibrating quickly, rolling its consonants, never sweet.","Piaf wrote the words in 1945, in a Paris just freed from German occupation and hungry for tenderness. Because she was not yet registered with the French authors' society as a composer, the music was credited to the guitarist and songwriter Louiguy. Other singers performed it first, but Piaf's own recording, released on Columbia in 1947, made it hers. She soon sang it in English on tours of the United States, where it became her calling card.","The song is built on a trick of contrast: a gentle, rosy melody delivered by a voice formed in the streets of Belleville, singing for coins. Listen to how Piaf holds back in the verse, keeping her tone narrow and almost conversational, then unleashes the full vibrato only on the refrain. That tightening and release, rather than the tune itself, is where the emotion lives.","It was an instant success and has never really left the air, recorded by Louis Armstrong, Grace Jones and hundreds of others and used in countless films. Some French critics once saw it as sentimental next to Piaf's darker realist songs. Today it is treated as the song that carried chanson around the world after the war, the tune foreigners hum when they think of Paris.","Listen first only to the voice: notice how she darkens and brightens single words and how little she moves the melody until the refrain. Then hear Louis Armstrong's 1950 version, relaxed and swinging, for a completely different idea of the same song. Piaf's later live recordings at the Olympia show how much rawer she could make it."],"edith-piaf-non-je-ne-regrette-rien":["Two and a half minutes of defiance. A march-like pulse on snare drum and low brass sets up a melody that climbs in short, stubborn phrases, each one pushed higher than the last. Piaf sings of sweeping away the past, good and bad alike, and starting again for a new love. The orchestra swells behind her in blocks, the drums pound, and the final notes are held with a force that sounds like a vow rather than a performance.","Charles Dumont wrote the music and Michel Vaucaire the words, and they brought the song to Piaf in 1960, when she was ill, exhausted and in debt. She took it at once, and it became the centrepiece of her return to the Paris Olympia at the end of that year, a run of concerts that also rescued the struggling hall. She dedicated her recording to the French Foreign Legion, then fighting in the Algerian war.","The legionnaires took the dedication seriously: after the failed generals' putsch in Algeria in 1961, soldiers of a disbanded parachute regiment reportedly sang it as they were marched away, and it has stayed in the Legion's repertoire. Listen, too, for how short the melody's phrases are, each a single breath; it is written for a singer whose lungs were failing, and turns that limit into punch.","It was a huge hit and became Piaf's last great signature before her death in 1963. Some hear in it pure self-assertion, others a darker refusal to face what one has done, and its political uses have kept that argument alive. It has since been sampled, parodied and used in films from war dramas to Inception, where its slowed-down brass becomes part of the score.","Listen to the snare drum and brass under the first verse and notice how march-like the song is; then follow how each phrase is pitched a little higher. Compare it with La vie en rose, thirteen years earlier: the same voice, now rougher and more commanding. Seek out the live Olympia recording for the crowd's roar."],"charles-trenet-ya-d-la-joie":["A bouncing, swinging song in which Trenet, bright-voiced and grinning, reels off a list of absurd, cheerful images: the whole city of Paris waking up drunk on happiness, monuments and machines behaving like children on holiday. A small dance band plays with a jazz lilt, brass punctuating his lines. The tempo never lets up, and the melody hops in wide, playful leaps rather than the sighing steps of older French song.","Trenet first gave the song to Maurice Chevalier, who introduced it in a Paris revue in 1937; Trenet's own recording followed in 1938, just as he launched his solo career after the break-up of his duo with Johnny Hess. France was in the afterglow of the Popular Front, with its first paid holidays, and American swing and Django Reinhardt's Hot Club were changing the sound of Paris nightlife.","Listen to the rhythm under the words: the band is swinging, not marching, which was new in French popular song. Trenet sings slightly ahead of and behind the beat like a jazz singer, and his nonsense images owe as much to the Surrealist poets he admired as to music hall. The happiness is so relentless that some listeners, knowing what was about to happen in 1939, hear an edge of mania in it.","Critics and audiences hailed Trenet as 'le fou chantant', the singing madman, and the song made him a star almost overnight. Later generations of songwriters, Brassens among them, credited Trenet with showing that chanson could be poetic, funny and modern at once. It is sometimes dismissed as a period novelty, but its mixture of swing and whimsy set the template for post-war French song.","Don't try to follow every image; just ride the rhythm and listen to how Trenet bends the tune around the beat. Then play Django Reinhardt's Dinah or Duke Ellington's It Don't Mean a Thing to hear where the swing came from. Trenet's own 1938 recording is the one to find, with its crackle and period dance band."],"charles-trenet-la-mer":["A slow, rocking song about the Mediterranean seen from the shore: the water glinting, white birds, houses soaked in rain, the sea compared to a shepherdess watching her flock of waves. The orchestra rolls under the voice in long swells, strings and harp suggesting light on water, and the melody rises and falls in wide, calm arcs. Trenet sings with less clowning than usual, softly, as if describing something he has just seen from a train window.","Trenet wrote it with his pianist Léo Chauliac, reportedly in 1943 on a train running along the lagoons near the coast of Languedoc, close to his native Narbonne. It was first recorded by another singer, Roland Gerbeau, in 1945; Trenet's own version, released in 1946, became one of the best-selling French records ever. In 1959 Bobby Darin turned it into Beyond the Sea, with new English words.","The English version is not a translation: Beyond the Sea is about a lover waiting across the water, while the French is a pure landscape, a song almost without people. Listen, too, to the orchestra's harp and high strings, which borrow the shimmering textures of Debussy and Ravel, the concert-hall French sea translated into three minutes of popular song.","It was a hit at once and has been recorded, by some counts, several thousand times, which makes it one of the most covered songs of the century. French critics long treated it as lovely but slight next to Trenet's wittier songs; it has since come to be heard as his most perfect, a song that sums up the southern French coast as surely as a Matisse window.","Listen to it with your eyes closed and follow the orchestra rather than the voice: the waves are in the strings. Then play Debussy's La mer for the symphonic version of the same water, and Bobby Darin's Beyond the Sea for the swinging American one. Trenet's 1946 recording is warmer than his later remakes."],"georges-brassens-la-mauvaise-reputation":["Just a voice, a strummed guitar and a plucked double bass keeping a steady, jaunty two-beat. Brassens, in a plain baritone with a southern accent, describes a man who lives quietly by his own rules in a small town and is hated for it: he stays in bed on the national holiday, cheers the wrong people, looks away when others want a lynching. The tune is simple and hummable; the bite is all in the words.","Brassens was thirty and almost unknown when the singer Patachou heard him and put him on stage at her Montmartre cabaret in 1952. The producer Jacques Canetti signed him to Polydor, and this became the title song of his first record. France was conservative, Catholic and still settling wartime scores; Brassens, who had been sent to forced labour in Germany, wrote for the anarchist press and distrusted every uniform.","The accompaniment never changes: Brassens always worked with just guitar and bass, usually his friend Pierre Nicolas, so that nothing could distract from the words. Listen to how he fits long, supple sentences into a tiny tune, with rhymes as intricate as a classical poet's; he admired François Villon and the old ballad forms, and this is a medieval outlaw's song dressed in a cardigan.","Some of Brassens's early songs were kept off French radio as offensive to decency or to authority, which only spread his fame by word of mouth. Critics were puzzled by a singer who did no showmanship at all. Within a decade he was a national institution, and in 1967 the Académie française gave him its poetry prize. This song remains his manifesto.","Listen once for the bass and guitar, which simply keep time like a clock, and once for the words, following the story in a translation. Then hear De André's Italian version or Brassens's own later songs to see how the formula never changed. The 1952 recording, dry and close, is the one."],"georges-brassens-les-copains-d-abord":["A cheerful, rolling song in Brassens's usual lineup of voice, guitar and double bass, with a tune that sounds like it has always existed. It describes a small boat whose crew are friends, and uses that boat to talk about friendship itself: it is no famous ship, it never sails for glory, but it holds together through storms because no one on board pretends to be a hero. The refrain invites a room to sing along.","Brassens wrote it in 1964 for Yves Robert's film Les Copains, a comedy about a gang of friends playing pranks across provincial France, and it appeared that year on his album of the same name. By then he was fixed in his routine: a small house in the south of Paris, cats, pipe, a band of old friends, and long runs at the Bobino music hall where audiences knew his songs by heart.","The song is a web of puns on sailing words and on the phrase in its title, which also means 'on board'. Brassens slips in allusions to famous shipwrecks and to Montaigne's essay on friendship; the grand boats of French history and myth are named only to be dismissed. Under the jokes lies a serious moral: friendship as the one institution he trusted.","The film is half-forgotten; the song became Brassens's most widely sung, taught in schools and bawled at weddings, scout camps and funerals across the French-speaking world. Some critics find it the gentlest thing he wrote, lacking the anarchist bite of his early work. Admirers answer that its craft, the way every line lands on a rhyme and a double meaning, is his art at its fullest.","Listen for the bass, which bounces like a boat on a swell, and for how easily the refrain could be sung by a crowd. Then play Brel's Amsterdam, which puts sailors in a very different mood. Any of Brassens's Bobino concert recordings will let you hear an audience singing along."],"jacques-brel-ne-me-quitte-pas":["A slow plea to a departing lover that grows from near-whisper to near-collapse. A piano sets out a hesitant, circling figure; strings gather; Brel's voice, rough and trembling, piles up promise after promise, offering everything he has and then more, until he is ready to erase himself entirely. The same refrain returns again and again, each time more abject. It is less a song than a nervous breakdown in slow motion.","Brel, a Belgian from Brussels who had come to Paris in 1953, wrote it in 1959 after the end of his affair with the singer Suzanne Gabriello. It appeared that year on his fourth album, arranged by François Rauber, his long-time orchestrator. Brel was then breaking through at the Bobino and Olympia music halls with a style of performance that shocked audiences: sweating, gesturing, singing as if his life depended on it.","Listen to how little the melody itself changes: the drama is carried by Brel's voice and by Rauber's strings, which thicken verse by verse. And the opening melody has often been traced to Liszt's Sixth Hungarian Rhapsody, which Brel probably knew; a nineteenth-century piano tune becomes the skeleton of a modern love song. Brel's 1972 re-recording is slower and more restrained.","It became Brel's most famous song and one of the most covered in French, sung by Nina Simone, Dusty Springfield, Scott Walker and Sting; in English it was softened as If You Go Away. Some listeners, notably feminist critics, hear it as a song of humiliation rather than love, and Brel himself reportedly called it a song about cowardice, not romance. That tension is why it lasts.","Hear the 1959 original first, then Brel's 1972 remake, and notice how differently he paces the build. Then compare Nina Simone's version, which turns the plea into something proud. Try listening without a translation: the sound of the voice alone tells you most of the story."],"jacques-brel-amsterdam":["A song that starts almost murmured and ends in a roar. Over a slow, repeating melody in the orchestra, Brel paints the port of Amsterdam through its sailors: they sleep, they are born and die, they eat fish with their hands, they drink, dance clumsily and go with women for money. With each verse the orchestra grows louder and the tempo presses forward, and Brel's voice goes from description to disgust to a kind of hoarse pity.","Brel never recorded Amsterdam in a studio. It exists in his recording from the Paris Olympia in October 1964, released by Barclay as a live album, where the audience's ovation at the end is part of the track. He was then at the height of his stage career, touring relentlessly, and within three years would give up concerts altogether, exhausted by the physical toll of performances like this one.","Listen to the melody: it closely resembles the old English tune Greensleeves, stretched into a slow, obsessive circle that repeats without ever resolving. Brel builds the whole song as one long crescendo, the same four-line shape again and again, so that the effect comes from pressure rather than development. The applause at the end starts before the last note has faded.","The Olympia recording made it one of Brel's signatures, and it became a staple of English-language cabaret rock through Scott Walker's 1968 version and David Bowie's, recorded in 1971. Some French listeners find the portrayal of the sailors cruel; others hear it as compassionate. Critics generally agree that the live recording is inseparable from the song: this is a performance, not a composition.","Listen with the volume high enough to feel the crescendo, and track how many times the melody repeats before the explosion. Then hear Scott Walker's version, and Bowie's, and notice how much of Brel's fury each one keeps. The 1964 Olympia album is the only version by Brel himself."],"leo-ferre-avec-le-temps":["A slow, bare song for voice and strings about what time does to love: how it wears away faces, voices, the memory of a name, until even the passion that once mattered most becomes a dull indifference. There is no chorus in the usual sense, only a returning phrase, and the strings move in long sustained lines that barely support the voice. Ferré sings very quietly, almost speaking, then lets a few words break open.","Ferré wrote it in 1969, reportedly after the end of his long marriage to Madeleine Rabereau, and recorded it the following year for Barclay. He was then in his fifties, an anarchist poet-songwriter who had set Baudelaire, Verlaine and Rimbaud, and who was moving toward long orchestral pieces he often conducted himself. France was still in the aftershock of May 1968, which had made him a hero to the young.","Listen to what the strings do not do: they never swell into a climax, and the arrangement withholds the release that the melody seems to ask for. The song's desolation comes partly from that refusal. Ferré's diction is also exact; he lands on the consonants of each word as if carving them, a habit from his years reciting poetry.","It became his most famous song and a standard for other singers, from Dalida and Catherine Sauvage to Alain Bashung, as well as translated versions across Europe. Some find it self-pitying; most French critics now place it among the finest songs in the language. Its fame has partly overshadowed Ferré's more radical and political work, which he himself rated more highly.","Listen in a quiet room, late, and follow the strings, which hang like mist around the voice. Notice where Ferré raises his voice and how rarely he does it. Then hear Brel's Ne me quitte pas for the opposite response to lost love, begging rather than numbness."],"charles-aznavour-la-boheme":["An old painter looks back on his youth in Montmartre: a cold studio, hunger, a young woman who modelled for him, nights of work and talk, the feeling that they were poor but alive. The song alternates a lilting, almost waltzing verse with a broad refrain in which Aznavour's thin, reedy tenor swells with nostalgia. The last verse returns to the present, when he goes back to the old street and finds it changed beyond recognition.","Aznavour wrote the music and Jacques Plante the words for a 1965 operetta, Monsieur Carnaval, where another singer introduced it. Aznavour's own recording, released that year on Barclay, became one of his biggest hits. Montmartre by then was a tourist district; the bohemian painters' quarter the song remembers belonged to the early twentieth century, to Picasso and Modigliani, and had long since disappeared.","The title nods to Henri Murger's stories and to Puccini's opera La bohème, which told a similar tale of poor artists in Paris. But Aznavour turns that story into memory: the drama is not the love affair but the gap between then and now. Listen to how the final refrain is sung more slowly and heavily, the same words now weighted by loss.","It became Aznavour's signature, recorded in many languages and sung at concerts until his death in 2018. Critics sometimes found his early voice too frail and his performances too theatrical, but this song won even sceptics. It is now treated as the model of the nostalgia chanson, and a reminder that Aznavour was a craftsman of form as much as a romantic.","Follow the difference between the brisk verses and the slow, swelling refrain, and listen for how Aznavour breaks off at the final line. Then play Puccini's La bohème, or just its first act, to hear the operatic version of the same Paris. Aznavour's later live recordings are slower and more reflective."],"charles-aznavour-comme-ils-disent":["A song sung in the first person by a gay man who lives with his mother in a Paris flat, works as a drag performer in a nightclub, and loves a young man who will never love him back. Over a slow, sad orchestral arrangement, the verses describe his daily chores and nightly act with tenderness and no irony; the refrain turns on the word people use about him, and his insistence that nature alone made him as he is.","Aznavour recorded it in 1972, when homosexuality was still classified as a mental illness in France and a legal age of consent discriminated against gay men. He was a superstar, and his management reportedly feared the song would damage him. He sang it on stage anyway, often to silence and, at first, to hostility, and it became one of his best known songs in France.","Listen to how undramatic the music is: no ironic cabaret touches, no camp, just a sober melody and orchestra, which is exactly what made the song radical. Aznavour gives his narrator the same dignity he would give a lover in any other song. The title means 'as they say', and the whole song is built around that gap between what others say and what the man knows.","It was one of the first songs in mainstream French popular music to present a gay man sympathetically and in his own voice, and it is now regarded as a landmark. Some later listeners find the character too lonely and pitiable, a victim rather than a free person. Others argue that in 1972 compassion was itself a political act, and that its quiet realism has worn better than many louder anthems.","Read a translation first, then listen to how Aznavour shapes the quiet verses and lets the refrain rise. Compare it with his La Bohème, where the same voice sings nostalgia. Then play Bowie's Ziggy Stardust, from the same year, for a very different approach to gender on stage."],"barbara-nantes":["A song about arriving too late. Barbara tells how she received a message that her estranged father was dying in Nantes and travelled there through the rain, only to find he had died before she arrived. The piano moves in slow, steady steps like footsteps on wet stone; her voice, high, clear and fragile, stays almost level until the last verses, when it thins to a whisper. The rain in the song is also in the accompaniment.","Barbara's father, who had left the family years before, died in Nantes in December 1959; she reached the city after his death. She began the song then and finished it only in 1963. It appeared on her 1964 album Barbara chante Barbara, the record on which she stopped interpreting Brel and Brassens and emerged as a writer of her own songs, performing at the piano in black.","In her posthumously published memoirs, Barbara revealed that her father had abused her as a child. That knowledge changes the song: the grief is not simple mourning but something more tangled, a reconciliation that never happened. Listen for the restraint in her voice; nothing in the music tells you what it is withholding, and that is the point.","The song established Barbara as a songwriter of rare intimacy and is still among her most admired. Critics praised its understated craft from the start; after her memoirs appeared, discussion of it deepened. Some regard it as the single finest French song of the 1960s, a model for the confessional songwriting that followed across Europe.","Listen to the piano alone for a minute and hear the footsteps and the rain. Then notice how Barbara keeps her voice level through the story and lets it break only at the end. Compare Joni Mitchell's Blue for another woman at the piano turning private pain into song."],"barbara-l-aigle-noir":["A dream narrated in song. Barbara describes waking, or half-waking, to see a great black eagle descending from the sky, landing beside her, and speaking to her of a lost childhood before taking her up with it. The piano rolls in wide arpeggios and the orchestra gathers like weather, while her voice floats high and eerie. The melody rises as the bird rises, and the song ends suspended, as though still dreaming.","Barbara released it in 1970, and it became by far her biggest-selling record, taking her from Left Bank cabaret to a mass audience. She was then living outside Paris and performing at larger halls. She always said the song came from a dream, and she refused to explain what the eagle meant, which only deepened the public's fascination.","Listen to the piano figures, which circle like wingbeats and never settle. The song mixes the childhood imagery of fairy tales with something darker, and since her memoirs were published many listeners have read the eagle as an image of the father who abused her. Barbara herself kept silent on the question; the song lets both readings live side by side.","It was a huge popular success and one of the best known French songs of its decade. Some early critics found it too vague, a mystical fantasy. Later listeners heard its ambiguity as its strength, and it is now the song most identified with Barbara. Its interpretation remains debated, which is precisely what keeps it alive.","Listen first as if it were only a fairy tale, and enjoy the orchestration and the rising melody. On a second hearing, notice the details of childhood Barbara mentions and how the music darkens. Compare Kate Bush's Wuthering Heights, another high, eerie voice singing from a dream."],"jean-ferrat-nuit-et-brouillard":["A slow, grave song about the deportations of the Second World War: trains crossing Europe at night, the people crammed inside, their names, ages and faiths, the camps at the end of the line. Over a sombre, march-like orchestral arrangement, Ferrat's warm baritone stays steady and restrained, refusing any theatrical outburst. The melody is simple enough to be a folk song; the weight comes from the calm, level delivery.","Ferrat's father, a Jewish immigrant from Russia, was deported from France in 1942 and murdered at Auschwitz. Ferrat took his title from Alain Resnais's 1956 documentary on the camps, which in turn took it from the Nazi decree that made prisoners vanish into 'night and fog'. He recorded it in 1963, with arrangements by Alain Goraguer, at a moment when France and West Germany were signing a treaty of reconciliation.","Listen to what Ferrat does not say: his father is not named, and the song speaks for all the deported rather than for one family. The steadiness of the tempo, never slowing for effect, mirrors the trains themselves. Goraguer, who also arranged for Gainsbourg, keeps the orchestra dark and sparse, so that the voice carries the whole memorial.","French broadcasters were reportedly reluctant to air the song, feeling it reopened wounds at a delicate diplomatic moment, but Ferrat performed it on television and it became his breakthrough. It is now taught in French schools as a memorial text. Critics praise its restraint, and it is often cited as one of the few popular songs to treat the Holocaust with adequate seriousness.","Listen with a translation and let the steady pace carry you. Notice how the voice never rises to a shout. Then play Billie Holiday's Strange Fruit, another song of atrocity delivered without melodrama, and Steve Reich's Different Trains, which remembers the same trains from the other side of the Atlantic."],"jean-ferrat-la-montagne":["A broad, swinging song about the mountains of southern France and the young people who leave them. Ferrat describes villages emptying as the young go to the city for office jobs, cheap flats and processed food, while the old stay behind with their goats and vineyards. The orchestra gives it a sweeping, lyrical lift, and the refrain opens out like a view from a high ridge. His baritone is warm and conversational, not preachy.","Ferrat wrote it after settling in Antraigues-sur-Volane, a village in the Ardèche, where he would live for the rest of his life. France in the 1960s was urbanising fast, with the countryside losing people to Paris, Lyon and the new tower-block suburbs. The song, arranged by Alain Goraguer, was released in 1964 and became one of the biggest French hits of its decade.","Listen to the contrast between the orchestra's sweeping romantic gestures and the plain details of the words; Ferrat sets modern consumer goods, plastic and packaged meals, against the old life in a grand, almost symphonic setting. It is protest disguised as a pastoral postcard, and much of its bite is in the smallest nouns.","It became Ferrat's signature song and an anthem for rural France, still sung in villages today. Some urban critics found it nostalgic and conservative; admirers argue that it anticipated the ecological concerns of later decades. Its reputation rose further as rural depopulation continued, and it is now regarded as one of the great French songs of place.","Listen to the orchestral introduction and the way the refrain opens up; then follow a translation to catch the concrete details. Compare Paolo Conte's Genova per noi, which looks from the land toward the sea with similar unease, and Shirley Collins's Anthems in Eden for English rural loss."],"domenico-modugno-vecchio-frack":["A slow, dreamy song about an elegant old man in tails, top hat and gloves, walking alone through a sleeping city at dawn. Modugno describes the empty streets, the fading street lamps, the man's quiet farewell to the moon, and his final walk toward the river. The melody drifts over a soft, lilting accompaniment, almost a lullaby, and Modugno sings with a hushed, storytelling tenderness quite unlike the booming voice of his later hits.","Modugno wrote it in 1955, and it is often said to have been inspired by the death of the Sicilian aristocrat Raimondo Lanza di Trabia, a famous playboy who died in Rome in 1954, a death widely believed to be suicide. Modugno was then a young actor and singer from Puglia who had been writing songs in southern dialects and was just beginning to write in standard Italian.","The song never says what happens at the river; the ending is left to the listener, and the tune simply fades. Listen to how the melody imitates a slow walk, each phrase a few steps, and how the accompaniment drops away near the close. It is a small tragedy told entirely through gesture and costume, which is why it works like a scene from a film.","It was one of Modugno's first successes in standard Italian and established him as a serious songwriter, not just a folk entertainer. It has been covered by many Italian singers and is often called one of the first true canzoni d'autore, songs in which the singer-writer's personal poetry matters more than the performance. Its fame was soon eclipsed by Volare three years later.","Listen to it as a short film: the dawn, the streets, the man, the river. Notice how quietly Modugno sings compared to Nel blu dipinto di blu. Then watch or listen to Nino Rota's music for Fellini's La strada, from the same years, for another Italian melancholy of the lonely performer."],"domenico-modugno-nel-blu-dipinto-di-blu":["The song everyone knows as Volare. It opens with a slow, dreamy verse in which the singer imagines painting his hands and face blue and being lifted into the sky, then bursts into the famous refrain, which flies upward in a single joyful leap. Modugno's voice is big, open and theatrical, and the orchestra swings with a lightness closer to American big-band pop than to the traditional Italian ballad. The final refrain soars and fades.","Modugno wrote the music and Franco Migliacci the words, reportedly inspired by the floating figures in Marc Chagall's paintings. It won the Sanremo Festival in February 1958, when Modugno flung his arms wide on stage, and then represented Italy at the Eurovision Song Contest. Released in the United States, it went to number one there, and in 1959 it won the first Grammy Awards for record and song of the year.","Listen to the opening verse, which most people have never heard: slow, strange and quiet, a dream before the flight. The famous refrain only works because of that hush. And notice Modugno's gesture in the recording, a sudden shout and release; the arms-outstretched pose at Sanremo became part of the song, and a break with the stiff, formal delivery of Italian singers before him.","It was an international sensation, covered by Dean Martin, the Gipsy Kings, David Bowie and many others, and it is often called the song that dragged Italian pop out of its sentimental, operatic past into the modern world. Some Italian critics disliked its showbiz sheen; most now see it as the birth of modern Italian song and a turning point for Sanremo.","Start at the very beginning and don't skip the slow verse: the whole song is about the change from stillness to flight. Then listen to Modugno's own later live performances and to Dean Martin's American version for contrast. Trenet's La mer shows the older, gentler style Modugno was breaking from."],"mina-il-cielo-in-una-stanza":["A slow, intimate love song about a small room that opens into the whole sky when the beloved is there, the walls vanishing, the ceiling giving way to trees and stars. The orchestra is hushed, with sustained strings and a soft, tolling rhythm, and Mina sings with extraordinary control, holding her huge voice back to a near-whisper before letting it bloom. The melody climbs in long, gradual lines and never hurries.","Gino Paoli wrote it, one of the young Genoese songwriters who were remaking Italian pop at the turn of the 1960s, and Mina recorded it in 1960, when she was twenty and already a television star known for wild, rock-and-roll performances. The song showed a different singer: mature, controlled, capable of the utmost delicacy. It became a number one hit in Italy and the song most associated with her.","Paoli later said the room that inspired the song was in a brothel, a detail that surprises listeners who hear only a pure love song. Listen, too, to Mina's breath control on the long notes: she sustains and swells phrases like an opera singer, but never uses operatic vibrato, a blend of technique and restraint that became her trademark.","It was an immediate hit and has been voted among the finest Italian songs of the century. Critics credited it, along with Paoli's other songs, with inventing a new Italian intimacy distinct from both opera and Sanremo glitz. Some later listeners find the arrangement dated, but Mina's performance is still treated as a benchmark of Italian singing.","Listen for how quietly Mina starts and how gradually she opens her voice; note the moment she finally lets it ring. Then hear Paoli's own recording, thinner and more fragile, for contrast. Compare Françoise Hardy's Tous les garçons et les filles, two years later, for the French side of the same new intimacy."],"mina-se-telefonando":["A song about a love affair ending on the telephone: a single call, and a relationship that seemed immense is suddenly small and over. The music is dramatic, ascending and very modern for 1966: a three-note figure repeats obsessively in the orchestra, rising step by step, while Mina's voice climbs with it. Strings, brass and a wordless choir build a wall of sound, and every refrain lifts higher than the last.","Ennio Morricone composed and arranged it, with words by Maurizio Costanzo and Ghigo De Chiara, as the theme for a variety show on Italian state television in 1966. Morricone was then writing the scores for Sergio Leone's westerns, and brought the same bold, unexpected orchestral gestures to pop. Mina, by then the queen of Italian television, sang it on the programme and recorded it as a single.","Morricone said the obsessive three-note motif came to him from the siren of police cars in Marseille. Listen for how the motif keeps rising a step each time, a device borrowed from classical sequence technique, and for the harmonies that shift underneath in surprising directions. The song's structure is unusual too: it modulates repeatedly rather than returning to a stable refrain.","It was a hit and has since become a favourite of musicians and critics, regularly named among the best Italian pop recordings. Françoise Hardy recorded a French version, and many others have covered it. Morricone's arrangement is now studied as a model of how orchestral ideas can transform pop, and it anticipates the lush, dramatic pop of later decades.","Listen for the three-note siren motif and follow it as it rises; then listen to what the bass and harmony do beneath it. Compare Morricone's The Good, the Bad and the Ugly, from the same year, for the same composer's sense of drama. Dusty Springfield's big Italian-derived ballads show the English side of the style."],"lucio-battisti-emozioni":["A slow, meditative song that moves like a walk through the countryside. Over strummed acoustic guitar and soft strings, Battisti describes simple experiences: riding a bicycle, watching a sunset, standing by a river, and asks whether anyone else feels what he feels. The refrain lifts on a single word, the song's title. His voice is thin, husky and unpolished, and he sings with a directness closer to folk and soul than to Italian bel canto.","Mogol, the lyricist who wrote almost all the words for Battisti's songs in their great decade together, supplied the text, and it was released in 1970 as the title song of a compilation album. The two had just founded their own record label, Numero Uno, to escape the conservatism of the big Italian companies. Italy was in its 'hot autumn' of strikes and student protest; Battisti's songs looked inward instead.","Listen to how unforced the singing is: Battisti rarely raises his voice and often seems to speak the melody, which was radical in a country where singers were judged by power and range. The chords are borrowed from American and British pop, and the song's slow unfolding owes much to soul ballads. Mogol's lyric deliberately avoids grand statements and lets small details carry the feeling.","It became one of Battisti's most loved songs and a classic of Italian pop. Critics at the time were divided; some thought his voice too weak for a star. Later generations have credited him with modernising Italian song, and Emozioni is often chosen to show his gift for simple melody and understatement.","Listen to the acoustic guitar and let the song take its time. Notice how quietly Battisti sings and how the strings sneak in. Then play Nick Drake's Bryter Layter, from the same year, for another soft-voiced singer finding intensity in understatement, and Battisti's Anima latina for where he went next."],"lucio-battisti-anima-latina":["An ambitious, dreamlike album that blurs Italian song, progressive rock and Latin American rhythms into one long flow. Tracks merge into one another, percussion and flutes drift in and out, and Battisti's voice is often buried in the mix, one instrument among many. The songs deal with love, identity and the gap between people, but the sound matters as much as the words: layered, humid, uncertain, closer to a film score than a pop record.","Battisti made it with his lyricist Mogol after travelling in South America, and they released it on their own label, Numero Uno, in 1974. By then Battisti was Italy's biggest-selling star and had stopped giving interviews or concerts, preferring to control everything in the studio. Italian rock was at its most adventurous, with bands such as PFM reaching audiences abroad.","Listen for the low placing of the voice: Battisti deliberately mixed himself back so that the lyrics were hard to hear, forcing listeners into the texture. The Latin influences are less in the rhythms than in the layered percussion and the humid sound of the flutes. Mogol's lyrics are more oblique than ever, sometimes deliberately incomplete sentences.","It sold well on the strength of Battisti's name, but many fans and critics were baffled, finding it formless. Over time it has come to be seen as his masterwork and one of the great Italian albums of the 1970s, admired by musicians across genres. It is now often compared to the experimental records of Caetano Veloso and to British progressive rock.","Listen straight through, without skipping, and treat it as one long piece. Pay attention to the percussion and flutes rather than trying to follow the words. Then play Caetano Veloso's Araçá Azul for a Brazilian experiment of the same years, and Battiato's La voce del padrone for the next step in Italian art pop."],"fabrizio-de-andre-la-canzone-di-marinella":["A gentle, lilting ballad that tells a tragic story as if it were a fairy tale. A young woman named Marinella meets a mysterious, princely stranger, and after their meeting she slips into a river and drowns; the song turns her death into an ascent to the stars. The melody is soft and swaying, the accompaniment simple, and De André's deep, calm baritone narrates with a tenderness that keeps the horror at a distance.","De André wrote it early in his career, when he was an unknown Genoese songwriter recording for a small label. He said it was based on a newspaper story he had read as a teenager about a young woman who had been killed and thrown into a river, and that he wanted to give her a different, gentler ending. The song made little impact until Mina recorded it in 1967.","Listen to how the melody and arrangement echo old ballads and lullabies; the song is built like a folk tale, with repeated phrases and a slow, circular form. De André's great model was Brassens, and the song's calm, almost amused narration of a terrible event is a Brassens technique. The fairy-tale surface is a deliberate refusal to exploit the real crime.","Mina's cover turned it into a hit and gave De André enough money and fame to devote himself to songwriting. It is now one of the most loved Italian songs. Some critics call it sentimental; most admire its poise and its refusal to sensationalise. It is often cited as the first sign of De André's gift for turning outcasts and victims into figures of dignity.","Listen to De André's version first, then Mina's, and notice how differently they tell the story. Pay attention to the repeated phrases and the lullaby rhythm. Then play Brassens's La mauvaise réputation to hear the master De André learned from."],"fabrizio-de-andre-la-buona-novella":["A concept album retelling the life of Jesus through the apocryphal gospels, with the focus on the people around him: Mary's childhood in the Temple, her marriage to the old Joseph, her son's execution seen through the eyes of three grieving mothers and a thief crucified beside him. The songs are gentle, often folk-like, with acoustic guitars, soft strings and a chorus. De André's baritone is calm and sad.","De André recorded it in 1969 and released it in 1970, at the height of the Italian student protests, and he said he saw Jesus as the greatest revolutionary in history. The arrangements were by Gian Piero Reverberi, and the band included musicians who would soon form the progressive-rock group PFM. De André drew on the Protevangelium of James and other texts excluded from the official Bible.","Listen to the song for the three mothers at the cross: it is a popular-song version of the old Stabat Mater, the medieval poem of Mary at the cross, set by Pergolesi and countless others. And in the thief's song, De André goes through the Ten Commandments one by one, showing how each has been broken by those who claim to keep them.","Some Catholic critics were offended, some leftist critics thought a religious album a retreat from politics. Over time it has come to be seen as one of De André's finest works and among the most thoughtful Italian concept albums. It has been performed in churches and theatres, and scholars have written extensively on its sources.","Listen straight through with a translation to hand. Notice how gentle and folk-like the music is, even at the most dramatic moments. Then compare Pergolesi's Stabat Mater or Bach's St Matthew Passion, to hear the old sacred forms De André was answering."],"fabrizio-de-andre-creuza-de-ma":["An album sung entirely in Genoese dialect, with instruments from all around the Mediterranean: Greek bouzouki, Arab oud, Turkish saz, bagpipes, frame drums. The seven songs portray the life of the port of Genoa and the sea beyond it, sailors, fishmongers, prostitutes, a corsair, a war-torn Lebanese city. The sound is dense and ancient, full of drones and odd rhythms, and De André's deep voice rides over it with calm authority.","De André made it with the multi-instrumentalist Mauro Pagani, formerly of PFM, who had travelled around the Mediterranean gathering instruments and tunes. It was released by Ricordi in 1984. De André, now living largely in Sardinia, had turned away from Anglo-American influences toward his own region's language and the older cultures of the Mediterranean, a move almost no Italian pop artist had made before.","The Genoese dialect itself is part of the music: it is full of Arabic and Spanish words from centuries of trade, and De André chose it partly for that reason. Listen for how the rhythms often fall in irregular groupings from Balkan and Middle Eastern music. And the song about the Lebanese city of Sidon was written about the Israeli invasion of 1982, from the point of view of a father with a dying child.","It was a surprise success and was hailed by critics in Italy and abroad; David Byrne named it one of the most important records of the decade. It is now widely regarded as one of the greatest Italian albums and a pioneering work of what would come to be called world music. Some purists question its mixing of traditions, but its influence is undeniable.","Listen to the whole album in one sitting, loudly, and don't worry about the words at first. Focus on the instruments and rhythms. Then read translations. Compare Amália Rodrigues's Barco Negro, or Cesária Évora, for other port cities singing their own languages to the sea."],"paolo-conte-genova-per-noi":["A slow, wry song about people from the inland hills going down to the port city of Genoa: the sea is enormous and frightening, the city dark and unreadable, and they feel like peasants in their Sunday best. Over a lazy, swaying piano and brushes, Conte's hoarse voice half-speaks the words in a tone both amused and melancholy. The melody is simple, almost a shrug, and the mood is of bewildered longing.","Conte, a lawyer from Asti in Piedmont who had been writing hits for other singers, recorded it on his second album in 1975; the Genoese singer Bruno Lauzi recorded it the same year. Conte was only beginning his career as a performer, shy and gravel-voiced, and his songs drew on jazz, tango and the small-town life of the Piedmontese countryside.","Genoa was not just any city for Italian songwriters: it was the home of Paoli, De André, Tenco and Lauzi, the great 'Genoese school' of the 1960s. Conte's song is partly an outsider's tribute to that tradition. Listen to the piano, which is closer to jazz than to pop, and to the rueful pauses Conte leaves between phrases.","It was admired by fellow musicians more than by the public at first, and Conte's fame came slowly. Today it is one of his most loved songs and is taken as a portrait of the Italian provincial's awe before the sea. Critics praise its wit and its precise, understated observations, typical of his art.","Listen with a translation and enjoy the small details. Notice the piano's jazz inflections and the way Conte sings behind the beat. Then play Via con me for the more swinging side of Conte, and Ferrat's La Montagne for a French song of the countryside and the city."],"paolo-conte-via-con-me":["A jaunty, swinging song in which Conte, gravel-voiced and deadpan, invites someone to come away with him, promising nothing much except a good time. The band plays in a 1930s swing style, with bouncing piano, clarinet and muted brass, and Conte punctuates the verses with scat syllables and a kazoo-like mumble. The refrain, partly in English, is catchy and insistent, and the whole thing is over in three minutes.","Conte released it in 1981 on his album Paris milonga. He was then in his forties, a lawyer from Asti who had come late to singing, but his stage career was taking off, especially in France, where audiences loved his mix of jazz, tango and Italian irony. He had loved American swing and jazz since his youth, and the song is a tribute to that music.","Listen to the English phrase in the refrain, sung with a deliberately stiff Italian accent: Conte is playing with the image of the provincial Italian enchanted by American culture. The scat and kazoo effects are not throwaway gags but part of a carefully built pastiche of 1930s swing records. The arrangement is densely crafted beneath its casual surface.","It became Conte's most famous song and has been used in many films and adverts around the world. Some critics found it lightweight compared to his more melancholy songs; most see it as a perfect example of his art of pastiche and irony. It made him an international figure and a cult artist in France and beyond.","Listen for the swing rhythm and the clarinet, and enjoy Conte's deadpan delivery and scat. Then play Django Reinhardt's Minor Swing or Ellington's Mood Indigo to hear the jazz Conte was echoing. Tom Waits's Swordfishtrombones, a few years later, offers another gravel-voiced song-and-dance man."],"franco-battiato-la-voce-del-padrone":["A short, bright, dazzling album of seven songs mixing new-wave synthesizers, rock guitars, classical violin and strange, collage-like lyrics that jump from Sufi mysticism to TV commercials to cheap tourist beaches. The hits, Centro di gravità permanente, Bandiera bianca and Cuccurucucù, are catchy and danceable, but full of odd harmonic turns and cut-up quotations. Battiato's voice is light, nasal and detached, like a narrator observing the modern world from above.","Battiato, a Sicilian who had spent the 1970s making avant-garde electronic records, turned to pop at the end of the decade, and this album, released by EMI in 1981, became the first Italian LP to sell a million copies. His chief collaborator was the violinist Giusto Pio, who co-wrote and arranged. Italy was emerging from the 'years of lead' of terrorism into a brash new consumer culture.","The title means 'His Master's Voice', the famous record-company logo, a sly joke on the album's commercial success and on conformity. The lyrics are cut-up collages: lists of names, languages, songs and ideas in which Gurdjieff's mysticism jostles with pop trivia. Listen for the classical violin lines and for the abrupt changes of rhythm, legacies of Battiato's experimental years.","It was a huge popular success and baffled critics who knew Battiato as an experimentalist. Some accused him of selling out; others hailed it as intelligent pop. Today it is regarded as a classic of Italian art pop and a model of how to combine mass appeal with intellectual ambition. Its hits are still played on Italian radio.","Play it loud and enjoy the hooks first. Then listen again with a translation to follow the collage of references. Notice the violin, synthesizers and odd harmonies. Compare Kraftwerk's Computer World and Talking Heads' Remain in Light, from the same years, for other brainy dance-pop records."],"franco-battiato-la-cura":["A slow, luminous love song in which the singer promises to protect and care for someone: to keep away fear and illness, to travel with them through time and space, to cure them. The melody is simple and rising, and the arrangement grows from soft keyboards to full strings. Battiato's voice is gentle and earnest, stripped of the irony of his 1980s records, and the song ends in a broad, calm swell of strings.","Battiato wrote it with the Sicilian philosopher Manlio Sgalambro, his lyricist from the mid-1990s onward, and released it in 1996 on the album L'imboscata. Battiato was then in his fifties, living near Etna in Sicily, and moving away from pop toward opera, film and meditation. Sgalambro, a pessimist philosopher, supplied a text that could be read either as a love song or as a spiritual vow.","Many listeners hear the song as addressed not to a lover but to the soul, or to a disciple by a teacher; Battiato practised meditation and was interested in Sufism and Eastern thought. Listen to how the strings enter gradually and never overwhelm the voice, and how the melody climbs by small steps like a prayer rather than a pop hook.","It became Battiato's most loved song, frequently voted the greatest Italian love song, and is often performed at weddings and funerals. Critics admired its sincerity after years of ironic pop. Some find it too sentimental, but its fame has only grown since Battiato's death in 2021, when it was sung across Italy in tribute.","Listen in a quiet space and let it unfold. Notice how the strings gradually take over. Then compare Ferré's Avec le temps for another meditation on love and time, and Battiato's La voce del padrone to hear how far he had travelled from the ironic, jumpy pop of fifteen years before."],"adriano-celentano-azzurro":["A bouncy, catchy summer song about being stuck alone in the city while the woman he loves is away on holiday. The verses are playful, with odd images of trains, baobabs and Sunday afternoons; the refrain opens into a sweeping melody that evokes the blue summer sky. Celentano sings with his characteristic loose, rhythmic, almost mocking style, and the arrangement combines strings, brass and a bright pop rhythm section.","Paolo Conte wrote the music, with Michele Virano, and Vito Pallavicini the words; Celentano released it on his own label, Clan Celentano, in 1968. Celentano was then Italy's biggest pop star, a former rock-and-roller turned all-round entertainer. It was a summer hit amid the student protests of 1968, a very different world from the song's lazy afternoons.","Conte's melody is more sophisticated than it seems, with jazz-inflected harmonies and a refrain that shifts unexpectedly. Listen to how the verses and the refrain almost belong to different songs, one playful, one soaring. Conte later recorded his own, much slower and more melancholy version, which reveals the sadness beneath the cheerful surface.","It was a huge hit and has become one of the best-known Italian songs, sung by Italian football fans, especially during the 2006 World Cup. Critics have often dismissed it as light pop, but musicians admire Conte's craft. It is now a national anthem of sorts, and its dual life, in Celentano's and Conte's versions, is itself a lesson in interpretation.","Listen to Celentano's version first, for the bounce and swagger, then to Conte's slower one. Notice how the refrain changes character. Then play Modugno's Nel blu dipinto di blu for another Italian song whose refrain flies into the sky, and Conte's Genova per noi for the songwriter's own voice."],"adriano-celentano-prisencolinensinainciusol":["A funky, hypnotic track built on a relentless groove of bass and drums, over which Celentano delivers a torrent of words that sound like American English but mean nothing at all. His delivery is rhythmic, half-spoken and half-chanted, much closer to rap than to song, while a female chorus answers with a repeated hook. The track never changes key or tempo; its power is in the repetition and the rhythm of the syllables.","Celentano released it in 1972 on his own label. He said he wanted to show how English sounds to Italians who don't speak it, and to make a point about how people fail to communicate. It was initially a flop in Italy but became a hit elsewhere in Europe, especially in Belgium and France, and then back home, helped by comic TV performances in which Celentano acted out the song with a partner.","The gibberish is carefully constructed: Celentano used real English words and fragments mixed with invented ones, sung with American intonation and slang rhythms. Listen for how the vocal line functions as percussion, a technique that would later define hip-hop. The title itself is a nonsense word in the same imaginary language.","For decades it was a novelty song, but in the 2000s it was rediscovered online and hailed as a precursor of rap. Critics and musicians now take it seriously as an experiment in sound and language. Some still see it as a joke; others argue that the joke is precisely the point, a satire of the global dominance of English-language pop.","Listen to the groove first, then to Celentano's vocal rhythm, as if it were a drum. Notice how convincing the nonsense English sounds. Then play Grandmaster Flash's Superrappin', from a few years later, to hear rap in its early form, and Talking Heads' Remain in Light for another experiment in rhythm and voice."],"depeche-mode-just-cant-get-enough":["A bright, bouncing three-and-a-half-minute dance song built almost entirely from cheap synthesizers. A chirping, interlocking keyboard riff opens it and never really stops; a second synth line answers it, a drum machine ticks underneath, and Dave Gahan sings a simple, breathless song about a crush so strong it will not let go. There is no guitar anywhere. The hook is the riff, not the voice, and it repeats until it becomes almost hypnotic.","Depeche Mode were four young men from Basildon, an Essex new town, signed to Daniel Miller's tiny independent label Mute in London. This was their third single, from the debut album Speak & Spell, and it was written by Vince Clarke, the band's first songwriter. Portable synthesizers had just become affordable enough for a working-class band to own, and the Human League, OMD and Soft Cell were already proving that a pop group could manage without guitars.","Almost as soon as this song made Depeche Mode famous, its writer left: Vince Clarke quit at the end of 1981, uneasy with touring and celebrity, and went on to form Yazoo and later Erasure. Martin Gore, who had written only a couple of the band's songs, took over. So this cheerful record is really a Vince Clarke song, and the gulf between it and what Gore wrote next is the story of the whole band.","It was a UK hit and it fixed the early image of Depeche Mode as a lightweight teen-pop act, a reputation they spent the rest of the decade escaping. For years the band treated it with mild embarrassment. Later listeners heard it more kindly, as a perfectly engineered piece of electronic pop, and it became a football-terrace chant in Britain and a staple of every eighties revival.","Listen to how many separate synthesizer parts there are, each doing one simple thing, and how they lock together like clockwork. Then play Yazoo's Only You to hear where Clarke went next, and Depeche Mode's Everything Counts, written by Gore only two years later, to hear how quickly the band grew darker and heavier."],"depeche-mode-everything-counts":["A clanking, metallic synth-pop single about money. Its rhythm is built from sampled sounds that ring like hammered pipes and struck metal, and its best-known melody is played on a nasal, reedy sampled instrument that sounds like a folk shawm or oboe. Martin Gore and Dave Gahan trade the verses, and the chorus, a wordless-seeming sing-along, sits over a bitter song about greed in business, the handshake that hides the grabbing hand.","This was the first big single after Vince Clarke's departure, written by Martin Gore for the album Construction Time Again, much of which was mixed at Hansa studios in West Berlin with the engineer Gareth Jones. Alan Wilder had joined as a musician. The band had discovered samplers, expensive new machines that could record any real-world sound and play it back from a keyboard, and they went out into building sites and scrapyards looking for noises.","The industrial sounds are not decoration; they are the argument. A song about the machinery of commerce is built from recordings of actual machinery, struck, scraped and looped into drum patterns, an idea the band took partly from German industrial groups such as Einstürzende Neubauten. The pretty tune on top and the hard metal underneath are the same contrast the words describe: a friendly surface over a cold transaction.","Reviewers noticed the leap at once: the teen-pop band of Just Can't Get Enough had become politically pointed and sonically adventurous in two years. A live version recorded at the Pasadena Rose Bowl in 1988 became a second hit and the climax of the concert film 101. It is now heard as the hinge of Depeche Mode's career, where Gore's voice as a writer first came through fully.","Listen first to the percussion alone and try to name the objects being struck. Then compare the studio single with the 101 live version, where tens of thousands of people sing the chorus back. Kraftwerk's Computer World, from two years earlier, is the obvious ancestor; New Order's Blue Monday, from the same year, is a sibling."],"depeche-mode-violator":["Nine songs and a few short instrumental links, about forty-seven minutes of dark, spacious, immaculately produced electronic music. The sound is sparse: deep synthesizer bass, programmed drums with the weight of a rock band, and occasional blues-inflected guitar. It includes Personal Jesus, built on a stomping, swamp-blues guitar riff, and Enjoy the Silence, with its melancholy synth pulse and chiming guitar. Dave Gahan's baritone and Martin Gore's high harmonies give the record its unmistakable double voice.","By the end of the eighties Depeche Mode had become a stadium act in the United States and across Europe, a status confirmed by their 1988 Rose Bowl concert. For this album they worked with the producer Flood, recording in Milan and Denmark and finishing in London, and deliberately stripped back the busy sampling of their mid-eighties records. When the band held a record-shop signing in Los Angeles in March 1990, the crowd was so large that police had to intervene.","Enjoy the Silence began as a slow, bare ballad on Martin Gore's demo, sung over a harmonium-like organ. It was Alan Wilder and Flood who heard it as a dance track and rebuilt it around a four-on-the-floor beat; Gore reportedly disliked the change at first. The guitar line that most listeners hum was added late. The record's cleanest moments are the result of arguments about what to leave out.","It was the band's commercial peak and their critical turning point: reviewers who had dismissed synth-pop as disposable began treating Depeche Mode as serious songwriters. Personal Jesus was later covered by Johnny Cash and Marilyn Manson, which says something about its range. Violator is now commonly named among the essential albums of electronic pop and as a bridge between eighties synth-pop and nineties industrial and alternative rock.","Start with Enjoy the Silence, then play Personal Jesus to hear how a blues riff survives inside a machine. Listen on good headphones: much of the drama lies in the space between sounds and in the low end. The instrumental codas between songs are worth hearing rather than skipping. Afterwards try New Order's Technique and Radiohead's Kid A, both electronic records made by bands."],"the-human-league-being-boiled":["A slow, heavy, sinister electronic record. A fat, sliding synthesizer bass line lurches forward with the swagger of a funk groove, a drum pattern made on the synthesizer thuds underneath, and bursts of harsh noise cut across it. Phil Oakey's deep, flat voice delivers a strange lecture about silkworms and the cruelty of boiling them for silk, wrapped in half-understood references to Buddhism. It sounds nothing like the polished pop the same group made three years later.","The Human League began in Sheffield, a steel city hit hard by the decline of heavy industry, where Martyn Ware and Ian Craig Marsh had been making electronic music with cheap synthesizers. They recruited Oakey, a hospital porter with a striking look, as singer. This debut single came out on Fast Product, a small independent label in Edinburgh run by Bob Last, in the thick of punk's do-it-yourself moment. Their heroes were Kraftwerk, Giorgio Moroder and funk records.","The groove owes more to black American funk and Philadelphia soul than to German electronics: Ware and Marsh loved dance music, and the bass line is essentially a funk bass played on a synthesizer. That hybrid, European machines playing American dance music, is exactly what Sheffield would export for the next decade. The track was re-recorded more than once, and the versions differ noticeably in tempo and weight.","It sold modestly, but other musicians heard it: David Bowie reportedly told people he had seen the future of pop after seeing the group live. Critics filed it with the industrial and post-punk underground rather than with pop. It has since become a touchstone for electronic musicians and a reminder that the Human League started as an avant-garde band, closer to Cabaret Voltaire than to Duran Duran.","Listen to the bass line and try moving to it; it is a dance record, however cold it sounds. Then jump straight to Don't You Want Me on the album Dare, three years later, and try to believe it is the same singer. Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express and Donna Summer's I Feel Love are the two records it sits between."],"the-human-league-dare":["Ten songs, about forty minutes, of hard-edged, precise electronic pop. Every sound comes from synthesizers and a drum machine, each part cleanly separated and punchy. Phil Oakey's baritone leads, and two teenage women, Joanne Catherall and Susan Ann Sulley, sing unpolished backing and answer lines. The singles are The Sound of the Crowd, Love Action, Open Your Heart and Don't You Want Me, a duet in which an ex-lover and the woman he made a star tell opposite versions of the same story.","In 1980 the original Human League split: Martyn Ware and Ian Craig Marsh left to form Heaven 17, leaving Oakey and the visual director Adrian Wright with the name and a tour booked. Oakey recruited Catherall and Sulley after seeing them dancing in a Sheffield nightclub, and later added Ian Burden and the guitarist Jo Callis, who played synthesizer here. The producer Martin Rushent, working for Virgin, programmed the album on new digital drum machines and synthesizer sequencers.","Oakey reportedly thought Don't You Want Me was the weakest song on the album and resisted its release as a single; it became the UK Christmas number one in 1981 and a US number one the following year. The amateur quality of the two women's voices is deliberate and crucial, making the gleaming machines sound human. A remixed instrumental version, Love and Dancing, credited to the League Unlimited Orchestra, followed in 1982.","Dare was a huge seller in Britain and broke synth-pop in America. Some early fans mourned the experimental band of Being Boiled, but critics quickly accepted it as a model of what pure electronic pop could be. Its production, Rushent's crisp, programmed precision, became a template for the decade, and it regularly appears on lists of the best British albums of the eighties.","Start with Don't You Want Me and listen to the way the two voices contradict each other. Then play Love Action and notice that the drums never vary; the interest comes from the layering. Go back to Being Boiled for the before, and forward to the Pet Shop Boys and Robyn's Dancing On My Own for the long after."],"pet-shop-boys-west-end-girls":["A slow, brooding dance song, nearly five minutes long, in which Neil Tennant half-raps and half-speaks the verses in a cool, very English voice before sliding into a sung chorus. Underneath, a deep synthesizer bass rolls, programmed drums keep a mid-tempo groove, and sirens and street noise drift in and out. A muted, jazzy trumpet line and a choir-like synth give it a nocturnal atmosphere. It sketches London at night: class, ambition, crowded streets and a sense of being trapped.","Neil Tennant was a journalist at the pop magazine Smash Hits; Chris Lowe was an architecture student. They met in a London hi-fi shop in 1981. A first version of this song, produced in New York by Bobby Orlando in 1984, was a club hit in a few places but flopped in Britain. After signing to Parlophone they re-recorded it with the producer Stephen Hague, and the new version reached number one in Britain and then in the United States.","Tennant has said the rapped verses were inspired by Grandmaster Flash's The Message, the New York hip-hop record about city pressure, and that the fragmented, many-voiced words owe something to T. S. Eliot's The Waste Land. There is also a sly historical reference to Lenin's rail journey to the Finland Station in 1917, slipped into a song about nightlife. The song is a collage, like Eliot's poem.","It was one of the defining British hits of the mid-eighties and launched what became the most successful duo in UK pop history. Critics admired the novelty of an English rap and the combination of club music with literary irony. Its reputation has only grown, and it is now routinely named among the finest British pop singles of the decade, admired as much by songwriters as by dance-floor audiences.","Listen for the moment Tennant switches from speaking to singing, and for the sirens and street sounds in the background. Then play Grandmaster Flash's The Message to hear one of its models. The album Actually shows where the duo went next; the Human League's Dare shows the Sheffield electronic pop they grew from."],"pet-shop-boys-actually":["Ten songs of grand, glossy, ironic electronic pop. The sound is huge: orchestral stabs, choirs, thunderclaps and church bells built from samples, over dance rhythms, with Neil Tennant's light, deadpan voice floating above. The hits are It's a Sin, a melodramatic confession of Catholic guilt; What Have I Done to Deserve This?, a duet with Dusty Springfield; Rent, a cool song about a love that is also a transaction; and Heart. Other tracks, like Shopping and King's Cross, look coldly at Thatcher's Britain.","Actually was the Pet Shop Boys' second album, made at the height of their fame with several producers, among them Stephen Hague and Julian Mendelsohn. Britain in 1987 was in its third term of Margaret Thatcher's government: a City boom, privatisation, sharpening division and the arrival of AIDS, which hung heavily over gay life. Tennant was not yet publicly out, but the album's songs of guilt, secrecy and money are now read in that light.","The duet with Dusty Springfield was the start of her comeback; the duo had admired her sixties records and gave her a hit two decades after her peak. King's Cross, the closing track, describes the station as a place of the lost and the desperate; months after the album appeared, a fire at King's Cross Underground station killed thirty-one people, and the song took on a painful second meaning.","It was a major commercial success in Britain and Europe and critics praised its wit and ambition, though some found the irony chilly. Over time Actually has been reassessed as one of the sharpest political pop records of the decade, and It's a Sin was renewed in 2021 when Russell T Davies named his television drama about the AIDS years after it.","Begin with It's a Sin at loud volume, then the Dusty Springfield duet. After that listen to Shopping and King's Cross carefully, attending to what Tennant is describing rather than to the gloss. Dusty in Memphis is the natural companion; Depeche Mode's Violator is the darker cousin from the same scene."],"eurythmics-sweet-dreams":["A cold, circling, minor-key electronic single. A pulsing synthesizer bass riff, bouncing between low and high notes, loops under a simple drum-machine beat, and Annie Lennox's deep, commanding contralto delivers a short, fatalistic meditation on how everyone is searching for something and how people use and are used by one another. Strings made on a synthesizer swell in the middle. There is almost no chorus in the usual sense; the riff itself is the hook.","Lennox and Dave Stewart had been in the pop group the Tourists, and their romantic relationship had just ended. Broke and depressed, they set up a small home studio above a picture-framing workshop in north London and recorded on cheap equipment. Stewart found the bass riff by accident while experimenting with a drum machine and a synthesizer; Lennox, reportedly lying on the floor in misery, got up and started singing over it. The song was released by RCA in early 1983.","The song is often heard as a celebration, but what it describes is coldly bleak: a world divided into people who exploit and people who are exploited. Lennox has said it reflected her depression at the time. The famous video, with Lennox in a man's suit and cropped orange hair in a boardroom and, oddly, a field with a cow, deliberately played with gender and power.","It reached number one in the United States and near the top in Britain, and the video made Lennox one of the defining images of early MTV. Critics praised its stark minimalism. It has been sampled and covered constantly, from Marilyn Manson's gothic version onward, and remains one of the most recognisable synthesizer riffs ever recorded.","Listen to how little is actually going on: one bass riff, one beat, one voice, some synthesizer strings. Notice how Lennox sings with authority and restraint rather than belting. Then play Donna Summer's I Feel Love, another song built on a single hypnotic bass sequence, and Madonna's Vogue, which borrows its poise and its cool."],"soft-cell-tainted-love":["A minimal, stark electronic version of a sixties soul song. A cold drum-machine beat with hard handclaps, a throbbing synthesizer bass, and a two-note electronic sting, a bink-bink that became famous in its own right, frame Marc Almond's theatrical, slightly wavering voice. The song describes a love that has turned poisonous and a lover who needs to escape. The twelve-inch version runs on into a cover of the Supremes' Where Did Our Love Go.","Soft Cell were Marc Almond and Dave Ball, who met as art students at Leeds Polytechnic. Tainted Love was originally recorded in 1964 by the American singer Gloria Jones, written by Ed Cobb; it had become a favourite on the Northern soul dance scene in England, where rare American soul records were played in all-night clubs. Soft Cell recorded it with the producer Mike Thorne for Stevo's label Some Bizzare, and it reached number one in Britain in 1981.","The choice of song was itself a statement: the duo had heard it in Northern soul clubs, and the record is a Northern soul classic translated into synthesizer pop. Almond's vulnerable, camp delivery also gave a song about a damaging love a new queer subtext in a period when openly gay pop was still risky. The two-note sting was reportedly created on a Synclavier synthesizer brought in by Thorne.","It was a global success and for years held a record for the most consecutive weeks spent on the American singles chart. Some critics dismissed Soft Cell as a novelty act, but the song's reputation has only grown. It has been sampled by Rihanna in SOS and covered by Marilyn Manson, and is now heard as one of the founding records of electronic pop.","Hear Gloria Jones's 1964 original first; it is faster and punchier, with horns. Then play Soft Cell's version and notice what the machines strip away and what Almond's voice adds. Listen to the long twelve-inch mix to hear the segue into Where Did Our Love Go, and follow up with the full album Non-Stop Erotic Cabaret."],"soft-cell-non-stop-erotic-cabaret":["Ten songs, about forty minutes, of seedy, witty, heartbroken electronic pop. Dave Ball's synthesizers are brittle and busy; Marc Almond sings like a torch singer lost in a nightclub. The songs are a tour of the underside of the city: Bedsitter, about a young man's lonely weekend nightlife; Seedy Films, about the porn cinemas of Soho; Frustration, about suburban boredom; and Say Hello, Wave Goodbye, a devastating farewell in a nightclub, with a smoky saxophone.","The album was recorded mostly in New York with the producer Mike Thorne, where Almond and Ball absorbed the club culture and the city's nightlife. It appeared at the end of 1981, just after Tainted Love had made them unexpectedly famous. The early eighties in Britain were a time of recession and high unemployment, and also of flamboyant club scenes where outsiders reinvented themselves; this record belongs to that world.","Almond has spoken of the influence of Jacques Brel, Scott Walker and the cabaret tradition, and the album's title and its characters recall Weimar Berlin as much as eighties London. Behind the synthesizer pop, the songs are really small theatre pieces about lonely people. The track Sex Dwarf was so provocative that its video, shot by Tim Pope, was never officially shown; it is mentioned here only in passing.","It sold strongly and was praised for its wit, though some critics found it too camp or trashy. Its reputation has risen steadily, and it is now considered a key album of British synth-pop and of queer pop, admired especially for Say Hello, Wave Goodbye and Bedsitter. Almond's later career as a torch singer confirmed that the cabaret was never a pose.","Play Say Hello, Wave Goodbye first, then Bedsitter and Seedy Films. Listen for how Almond's dramatic voice rides over the chilly machines. After that hear Scott Walker's Scott 4 and Brecht and Weill's Threepenny Opera, which Almond clearly loved, and the Pet Shop Boys' Actually, where the same city appears a few years later."],"omd-enola-gay":["A fast, cheerful-sounding synth-pop song about the atomic bombing of Hiroshima. A brisk, programmed drum pattern and a bright, bouncing bass line run under a lead melody played on a thin, reedy synthesizer, simple and singable. Andy McCluskey sings about the aircraft and its mission, the timing of the bomb and the question of whether it could ever be undone, but he does it at a skipping tempo that makes the song sound almost joyful.","Orchestral Manoeuvres in the Dark were Andy McCluskey and Paul Humphreys, friends from the Wirral near Liverpool who had been making electronic music since their teens. Enola Gay, released in 1980 on Dindisc, a Virgin subsidiary, was a single from their second album Organisation. It appeared at a time of heightened Cold War fear, with new missiles being deployed in Europe and the anti-nuclear movement growing in Britain.","The Enola Gay was the B-29 bomber that dropped the atomic bomb on Hiroshima in August 1945; it was named after the pilot Paul Tibbets's mother. The Hiroshima bomb itself was called Little Boy, and McCluskey's song glances at both names. The mismatch between the upbeat music and the grim subject was deliberate, and many listeners who danced to it had no idea what it was about.","It was a major hit in Britain and Europe. At the time some misread the title and objected to the song as crude, while others missed its meaning entirely. It has since become the best-loved OMD song and a staple of eighties revival radio, admired for its combination of a catchy hook with an unusual and serious subject.","Enjoy it first as a dance song, then listen again paying attention to the words. Notice the lead synthesizer line, which is really the chorus. Then try Architecture and Morality to hear OMD at their most ambitious, and Kraftwerk's Radio-Activity, another electronic record about nuclear power in a deceptively gentle voice."],"omd-architecture-and-morality":["Nine songs, a little under forty minutes, of lush, melancholy, almost choral electronic pop. The distinctive sound is the Mellotron, a tape-replay keyboard that plays recorded choirs and strings, used here to give the songs a cathedral-like glow. The three singles are Souvenir, sung by Paul Humphreys; Joan of Arc; and Maid of Orleans, a slow, swaying waltz with a folk-like melody. Around them are more abstract pieces with found sounds and industrial noise.","It was OMD's third album, made in 1981 at the peak of their early success and released on Dindisc. The band had been reading about the medieval saint Joan of Arc and listening to sacred choral music, and they wanted to make something grander and more emotional than their earlier records. They had also been influenced by Kraftwerk and by experimental electronic music, and the album mixes those strands.","The Joan of Arc songs grew from a visit McCluskey made to a French cathedral and his fascination with her story; that a song about a medieval martyr could become a hit single in the era of Duran Duran says much about how open pop briefly was. The whispering choir on Souvenir and elsewhere is the sound of old Mellotron tapes, a technology from the sixties repurposed for the eighties.","It sold several million copies across Europe, though the British music press was divided, some finding it pretentious. OMD's next, even more experimental album, Dazzle Ships, flopped. Today Architecture and Morality is widely considered their finest record and a high point of British electronic pop, and it was performed in full on reunion tours.","Start with Maid of Orleans and listen for the waltz time, unusual in pop, and the choir-like Mellotron. Then play Souvenir and Joan of Arc. Afterwards try Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express and Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel for two very different kinds of slow, luminous, electronic-sounding stillness."],"tears-for-fears-mad-world":["A mid-tempo, melancholy synth-pop song. A bouncing, busy drum pattern and a funky synthesizer bass line give it a surprisingly danceable body, while Curt Smith sings in a soft, sad voice about watching the empty routine of the world and dreaming of dying. The chorus, in which the singer finds the world strange, is almost resigned. The song sounds both chilly and warm, with shimmering synth pads and a rich vocal harmony.","Tears for Fears were Roland Orzabal and Curt Smith, teenage friends from Bath. The band's name came from Arthur Janov's book on primal therapy, which Orzabal read as a way of understanding his troubled childhood. Mad World, written by Orzabal and sung by Smith, was their third single and their first hit, in 1982, before appearing on the debut album The Hurting in 1983, a whole record about childhood pain and psychology.","The song's gloomy thoughts are balanced by a danceable arrangement, a tension that runs through Tears for Fears. Two decades later, a stripped-down piano version by Michael Andrews and Gary Jules for the film Donnie Darko revealed how desolate the melody is without its beat, and in 2003 that cover became the UK Christmas number one, climbing higher than the original ever had.","It was a hit in Britain, and critics grouped the band with the new wave of synth-pop acts. Over time the song has come to be seen as one of the defining statements of teenage alienation in the eighties, and the Andrews and Jules cover made it familiar to a whole new generation who never knew the original.","Listen to the original first and notice how bouncy the drums are compared with the sad melody. Then play the Donnie Darko version and hear the song naked. Go on to the whole album The Hurting for Orzabal's psychology, and then Songs from the Big Chair to hear how much bigger the band grew in three years."],"tears-for-fears-songs-from-the-big-chair":["Eight songs, about forty minutes, of big, polished, emotionally searching pop. The synthesizers of the first album are now joined by guitars, piano, saxophone and heavy, echoing drums. Shout is a slow-building protest chant; Everybody Wants to Rule the World glides on a shuffling rhythm and a chiming guitar; Head Over Heels is a grand romantic song. The second half turns darker and more experimental, with The Working Hour, Mothers Talk and the closing Listen.","Made with the producer Chris Hughes and keyboard player Ian Stanley, the album came out in early 1985, when the Cold War, nuclear fear and Thatcher and Reagan's politics coloured British and American pop. The title refers to Sybil, a 1976 television film about a woman with multiple personalities who finds safety in her therapist's big chair; Orzabal's lyrics continue the band's interest in psychology and emotional pain.","Everybody Wants to Rule the World was one of the last songs recorded and was almost left off; Orzabal thought it was too slight. Its unusual rolling shuffle rhythm, rather than a straight rock beat, gives it its gliding quality. Shout, often misheard as a party anthem, is about the need to let out anger and grievances, a lesson from primal therapy turned into a stadium chant.","The album was a huge international success, with Shout and Everybody Wants to Rule the World both reaching number one in America. Critics admired its ambition and craft, though some found it grandiose. It is now regarded as one of the defining British albums of the mid-eighties and a model of sophisticated, emotionally serious pop.","Play Everybody Wants to Rule the World, then Shout, then Head Over Heels, and listen for how carefully each is built up. Then play the album's quieter second half straight through. Compare it with Mad World to hear the band's growth, and with Talk Talk's The Colour of Spring era for another eighties band moving beyond synth-pop."],"duran-duran-rio":["Nine songs, about forty-three minutes, of glossy, danceable new pop. The sound is a band, not just machines: John Taylor's busy, funky bass lines, Roger Taylor's disco drums, Andy Taylor's rock guitar and Nick Rhodes's shimmering synthesizers, with Simon Le Bon's yearning vocals. The singles are the bright title song, with its saxophone solo; Hungry Like the Wolf, with its prowling rhythm; and the ballad Save a Prayer. The album ends with the eerie, atmospheric The Chauffeur.","Duran Duran formed in Birmingham and were house band of sorts at the Rum Runner club, a centre of the New Romantic scene, where fans dressed in extravagant costume. Rio was their second album, produced by Colin Thurston for EMI. Its success in the United States came from the new cable channel MTV, which played their lavish videos, directed by Russell Mulcahy and filmed on location in Sri Lanka and Antigua, again and again.","The album's American breakthrough came only after it was remixed for the US market, with some tracks given a harder, dance-club punch; the original British and the American versions sound noticeably different. The cover painting is by the American illustrator Patrick Nagel. The Chauffeur, the last track, is the band's most experimental song, built on a cold, ticking sequencer pattern rather than on the rhythm section, and it hints at the art-rock ambitions the band rarely showed.","British critics often dismissed Duran Duran as vain, pretty boys, especially compared with more earnest post-punk bands. But Rio sold enormously, and its reputation has steadily grown; musicians later praised the rhythm section's playing, and critics now hear it as one of the most confident pop albums of the decade and a model of the video age.","Start with Hungry Like the Wolf and listen to the bass line, then the title track. Watch the videos: they are part of the work. Save a Prayer shows the band's romantic side, and The Chauffeur their experimental edge. Afterwards, go back to Roxy Music's For Your Pleasure and David Bowie's Heroes, the band's clear ancestors."],"yazoo-only-you":["A gentle, simple, sad love song. A soft, rippling synthesizer arpeggio and a steady drum-machine pulse underpin Alison Moyet's rich, bluesy alto as she sings about a love that leaves her lonely and about the one person who can make things right. The melody is plain and memorable and the arrangement is almost bare, which leaves all the warmth to the voice. The result sounds like a soul ballad played on electronic instruments.","After leaving Depeche Mode at the end of 1981, Vince Clarke placed an advertisement in a music paper for a singer and was answered by Alison Moyet, whom he knew from Basildon, where she had sung in blues and punk bands. Only You was a song Clarke had written earlier; it became Yazoo's first single on Mute Records in 1982 and a British hit. The duo called themselves Yaz in the United States.","The song was reportedly one Clarke had offered to Depeche Mode before he left. In 1983 the a cappella group the Flying Pickets released a version sung entirely by voices, mimicking the synthesizer parts vocally, and it became the UK Christmas number one, a neat demonstration that underneath the machines this is a simple, old-fashioned song.","Critics praised the pairing of Clarke's precise machines with Moyet's earthy voice, an unusual combination that became Yazoo's signature. Only You remains one of the most covered and loved synth-pop ballads. It helped establish that electronic pop could carry genuine tenderness, a point previously in doubt among rock critics.","Listen to the synthesizer arpeggio and notice how steadily it runs under the voice. Then hear the Flying Pickets' vocal version for a different angle. Go on to Upstairs at Eric's to hear the rest of the duo's short career, and play Depeche Mode's Just Can't Get Enough to hear Clarke's earlier, sunnier style."],"yazoo-upstairs-at-erics":["Eleven songs, about forty minutes, pairing Vince Clarke's bright, bubbling synthesizers with Alison Moyet's big, bluesy voice. Don't Go is the fast, stomping single with its bounding synth riff; Only You and Midnight are slower and tender; Bad Connection and Goodbye 70's are playful dance tracks. Among them sits a strange sound collage, I Before E Except After C, made from overlapping spoken voices and taped fragments. The album moves between pop joy and melancholy.","It was the debut album of Yazoo, Vince Clarke's new duo after leaving Depeche Mode, released by Mute in 1982. The title refers to the studio where it was made, owned by the producer Eric Radcliffe, where Clarke and Moyet worked upstairs. Moyet had sung in rhythm-and-blues and punk bands in Essex, and her voice was very different from the cool, detached singers typical of synth-pop at the time.","The odd collage I Before E Except After C, built from recorded voices, shows Clarke's interest in experimental electronic music and tape techniques, a strand of his work often overlooked. Moyet and Clarke reportedly did not get on easily, and Yazoo made only two albums before splitting in 1983; Moyet went on to a solo career, Clarke to Erasure.","It was a big success in Britain and helped make Moyet a star. Critics praised its combination of warmth and precision and saw it as proof that synth-pop could accommodate a soulful singer. Its reputation has held up well; Don't Go and Situation, a dance track added to some editions, became favourites in American clubs and influenced early house music.","Play Don't Go loud and notice the interplay between synth riff and voice. Then listen to Midnight and Only You for the ballads. Try the strange collage track once, straight through. Afterwards hear Frankie Knuckles's Your Love and other early house records, to which Yazoo's club tracks were close cousins."],"a-ha-take-on-me":["A bright, speedy synth-pop song. It opens with a fizzing drum-machine beat and a bouncing, instantly memorable synthesizer riff that runs up and down like a fanfare. Morten Harket's voice leaps across a wide range, ending the chorus in a famous high falsetto note. The words are simple and eager, a young man asking someone to take a chance on him because he will soon be gone. It lasts under four minutes and never relaxes.","a-ha were three young Norwegians, Morten Harket, Magne Furuholmen and Pål Waaktaar, who moved to London to make it in the pop world. Take On Me was released twice in 1984 and flopped both times. Warner Bros. then had it re-recorded with the producer Alan Tarney and paired with a new video; released again in 1985, it reached number one in the United States and near the top across Europe.","The riff was written by Furuholmen when he was a teenager, long before a-ha existed, and it circulated in several of the band's earlier songs before finding its home. The famous video, directed by Steve Barron, mixes live action with pencil-sketch animation drawn over filmed footage, a laborious technique called rotoscoping, and much of its charm lies in that hand-drawn look.","It became one of the defining songs of the mid-eighties and its video won several MTV awards. Critics initially treated a-ha as a teen-idol band, but the song's craft has been increasingly admired; Harket's vocal range is often singled out. The video has passed a billion views online, making it one of the most-watched music videos from before the internet era.","Listen for the riff at the start, then wait for the high falsetto at the end of the chorus. Watch the video, which is as much a part of the song as the music. Afterwards, listen to Duran Duran's Rio and the Human League's Dare to hear other video-age synth-pop, and The Weeknd's Blinding Lights, which borrows the same bright, galloping energy."],"talk-talk-its-my-life":["A moody, mid-tempo synth-pop song. Paul Webb's fretless-sounding bass slides through the song, Lee Harris's drums are crisp and steady, and airy synthesizers shimmer and call like distant birds. Mark Hollis's cracked, pleading voice sings about his right to his own life and his own mind, a quiet declaration of independence. The chorus lifts with a soaring synth line, but the mood stays restless and uneasy.","Talk Talk formed in London around Mark Hollis, with Webb on bass and Harris on drums, and were initially marketed by EMI as a New Romantic synth band in the Duran Duran mould. It's My Life was the title song of their second album, co-written by Hollis and Tim Friese-Greene, the producer and keyboard player who became Hollis's essential collaborator. It was a hit across Europe but did only modestly in Britain on first release.","In the video, Hollis refuses to mime properly, standing sullenly while a cartoon and animal footage run around him, a mocking reply to the video-star role the label wanted him to play. That refusal is the key to the whole song and to Talk Talk's later history: the fierce privacy that would lead Hollis to abandon pop completely within four years.","It was well received in Europe and grew in reputation. In 2003 the American band No Doubt had a major hit with a cover, introducing it to a new audience. Critics now hear it as the point where Talk Talk's craft began to exceed the synth-pop format, a stepping stone toward the extraordinary albums that followed.","Listen for the bird-like synth calls and the sliding bass. Watch the video to see Hollis's silent protest. Then jump directly to Spirit of Eden, only four years later, and try to believe it is the same band. Tears for Fears' Songs from the Big Chair is a useful comparison: another synth band growing up."],"talk-talk-spirit-of-eden":["Six long songs, about forty minutes, of hushed, spacious, mostly acoustic music. Long silences, a quiet organ, a muted trumpet, harmonica, a distant choir, and sudden bursts of loud, distorted guitar alternate with passages that are barely there at all. Mark Hollis sings softly, sometimes almost inaudibly, about faith, addiction, nature and the search for grace. The opening The Rainbow takes minutes to emerge from near-silence; I Believe in You, with a boys' choir, is the emotional centre.","After two synth-pop hit albums, Talk Talk spent about a year in Wessex Studios in London recording hours of improvisation by a large group of session musicians, which Hollis and producer Tim Friese-Greene then edited and shaped into songs. EMI, expecting another hit album, were baffled by the result. Hollis refused to release singles or tour, and the relationship with the label collapsed into legal dispute.","The choir on I Believe in You is from Chelmsford Cathedral, and the song is Hollis's reaction to heroin addiction, which he had seen ruin people around him, including his own brother. Many of the most beautiful moments are edits from hours of improvisation, including musicians' mistakes deliberately kept. Hollis cited Miles Davis's Sketches of Spain and the French composers Debussy and Satie as influences.","It sold poorly, and most critics were puzzled or hostile at first. Over time it has become one of the most revered albums of its era, praised for its courage and its textures, and it is seen as a founding record of what came to be called post-rock. Bands from Radiohead to Sigur Rós have acknowledged its influence.","Listen in a quiet room, at a good volume, all the way through; do not skip the silences. Start with The Rainbow and notice how long it takes to arrive. Then play Miles Davis's In a Silent Way and Morton Feldman's Rothko Chapel to hear other music built on space and patience, and Radiohead's Kid A for what it influenced."],"talk-talk-laughing-stock":["Six songs in about forty-three minutes, even quieter and more skeletal than Spirit of Eden. Guitar notes hang in the air, drums play loose, jazz-like patterns, strings and horns come and go, and Mark Hollis's voice is often barely a murmur. After the Flood builds slowly to a squalling guitar climax; Ascension Day ends abruptly, as if the tape had been cut; New Grass floats on a gentle, rolling drum groove for almost ten minutes. Silence is as important as sound.","After leaving EMI, Talk Talk signed to the Polydor jazz imprint Verve. Only Hollis, the drummer Lee Harris and Tim Friese-Greene remained at the core; the bassist Paul Webb had left. They again recorded improvisations by many musicians at Wessex Studios, reportedly working in near-darkness with candles and an oil-slide projector, then spent months editing. It was the band's last album; Hollis made one quiet solo record in 1998 and then withdrew from music entirely.","The abrupt ending of Ascension Day is deliberate: the song simply stops mid-phrase, a cut in the tape rather than a fade. Much of the album is assembled from separate takes by musicians who did not play together, and the players were sometimes not told what the song was. Listen closely and you hear chairs creaking, breathing and the room itself, kept in the mix.","It was barely noticed on release and sold poorly. Within a few years it became a cult reference point, and the critic Simon Reynolds cited Talk Talk when coining the term post-rock in 1994. Today it is widely admired as one of the purest statements of its kind, the endpoint of a band that walked away from pop success on principle.","Listen from start to finish, preferably on headphones, at night. Start with New Grass if you want a gentler way in. Compare with Spirit of Eden to hear how much further this goes into stillness. Slint's Spiderland, released the same year, and Mark Hollis's solo album are the natural next steps; Arvo Pärt's Für Alina shares its sense of space."],"the-cure-the-lovecats":["A playful, jazzy pop song with a walking double bass, tinkling piano, brushed drums and finger-snaps. Robert Smith sings in a mischievous, yelping voice about two lovers as a pair of cats, prowling and purring and tumbling together through the night. The song feels like a cartoon soundtrack or a cabaret number, with scat-like vocal flourishes and a loose, swinging rhythm entirely unlike the Cure's gloomier records.","In 1982 the Cure had released Pornography, a harrowing, heavy album, and the band nearly fell apart under the strain. Robert Smith responded by deliberately making lighthearted pop singles for their label Fiction, beginning with Let's Go to Bed and The Walk, both electronic. The Lovecats, released in 1983, was a sharp turn to acoustic jazz and became the band's first top-ten hit in Britain.","Smith has said the song was inspired partly by the Disney film The Aristocats, and partly by a drunken night and a story about a house full of cats. It was a deliberate joke on the Cure's gloomy image, but it also revealed Smith's gift for melody. Its jazz band, led by a double bass, has more in common with Tom Waits than with post-punk.","It surprised fans and critics, some of whom thought the band had sold out. But it made the Cure a mainstream pop act and showed that Smith could write successful, lighthearted songs. It is now regarded as one of the charming oddities of eighties pop, and the key to the band's later dual life as both gloom merchants and hit-makers.","Listen for the walking double bass and the piano, then compare with the Cure's Pornography or Siouxsie and the Banshees' Juju for the darker sound Smith was escaping. Just Like Heaven, four years later, shows the pop and melancholy combined. For more jazz-tinged whimsy, try Tom Waits or the Kinks."],"the-cure-just-like-heaven":["A fast, bright, bittersweet song, three and a half minutes long. It begins with an insistent drum pattern and a chiming, rolling bass line, then a cascading keyboard melody and a jangling guitar arrive before Robert Smith sings. The song describes a single dizzy night by the sea, a kiss and a moment of perfect happiness, then ends with a sense of loss, as if the memory might have been a dream. The long instrumental opening is half the song.","It came from the double album Kiss Me, Kiss Me, Kiss Me (1987), made at a studio in the south of France during the band's most commercially successful period. Smith has said the song was inspired by a trip to Beachy Head, the chalk cliffs on the English south coast, with Mary Poole, his girlfriend and later his wife, and the video was filmed there. It became the Cure's first top-forty hit in the United States.","The instrumental opening was reportedly first written for a French television music programme, which used it as a theme, before Smith added a vocal. The song's apparent romance has a sting: Smith has said the ending, where happiness slips away, is the point. Mary Poole appears in the video, dancing with Smith on the cliffs, a rare personal glimpse from a notoriously private band.","It became one of the Cure's most loved songs and a staple of college radio. The American band Dinosaur Jr made a loud, abrupt cover in 1989 that brought it to indie-rock audiences. Critics now often call it the best song Smith wrote, the perfect meeting of the Cure's melancholy and their pop gift.","Listen to the instrumental opening, noticing how each part enters in turn. Then follow the story in the words: the night, the kiss, the loss. Compare it with The Lovecats and with the Smiths' How Soon Is Now? for other British guitar bands of the eighties balancing gloom and pop."],"cyndi-lauper-girls-just-want-to-have-fun":["A bouncing, exuberant new-wave pop song, about four minutes long. Chirpy synthesizers and a skipping, reggae-tinged rhythm open it; guitar and bass fill in; and Cyndi Lauper's piercing, elastic voice sings about a young woman who wants to escape parental scolding, a boyfriend's control and the daily grind, and simply enjoy herself with her friends. The chorus is a chant that sounds like playground singing. Hiccups and squeals in the voice give it a cartoon energy.","The song was written by Robert Hazard, who recorded a demo in 1979 from a man's perspective. Lauper, a New York singer who had spent years in cover bands and a failed group called Blue Angel, rewrote the lyrics to make it a statement of female solidarity. It was the first single from her debut album She's So Unusual, released on Portrait in 1983, produced by Rick Chertoff with members of the Philadelphia band the Hooters.","In Hazard's original the song was about a man boasting of the girls who wanted him; Lauper's rewrite turned it into a song about women's freedom, a change she insisted on. The video, with its crowded, joyous street scenes, features her real mother and the wrestler Captain Lou Albano as her father. Lauper deliberately cast friends and family rather than models.","It reached number two in the United States and became an international hit. The video won an MTV award and made Lauper a star. Critics praised its humour and its message, and the song has since become an enduring feminist anthem, re-recorded by Lauper herself and covered by many. It is often named among the best pop songs of the decade.","Watch the video first; it is essential. Then listen to how Lauper's voice jumps from high squeaks to growls. Compare it with Madonna's Like a Virgin from the next year, another way to be a woman in eighties pop, and follow with Time After Time, Lauper's gentle ballad, to hear her range."],"cyndi-lauper-time-after-time":["A slow, tender ballad of about four minutes. A soft, ticking rhythm that sounds like a clock, gentle synthesizer chords and a chiming guitar make a quiet bed for Cyndi Lauper's voice, here unusually soft and intimate. She sings about a relationship in which one person will always be there for the other, catching them whenever they fall. The chorus rises gently, and the whole song has the sound of a promise kept.","Lauper wrote the song with Rob Hyman of the Hooters during the recording of She's So Unusual; it was one of the last songs finished. The title came from a TV listing for the 1979 film Time After Time, a science-fiction story in which H. G. Wells chases Jack the Ripper through time. The ballad was released in 1984 as the album's second single and became her first number one in the United States.","The ticking rhythm is an echo of the clock image in the title and the film. The song's quiet confidence comes partly from Lauper's restraint: after the hiccuping exuberance of Girls Just Want to Have Fun, she sings here without vocal tricks. In 1985 Miles Davis recorded an instrumental version, which he played in concert for the rest of his life.","It became Lauper's most enduring song, covered by artists from jazz to pop. Critics praised its warmth and simplicity, and Miles Davis's adoption of it gave it a unique place bridging pop and jazz. It is regularly listed among the best ballads of the eighties and has been used in countless films and television programmes.","Listen for the ticking rhythm and the soft guitar, and notice how Lauper keeps her voice restrained. Then hear Girls Just Want to Have Fun to feel the contrast. Find Miles Davis's version from his album You're Under Arrest for a trumpet reading of the melody, and compare with Yazoo's Only You, another tender electronic ballad."],"culture-club-do-you-really-want-to-hurt-me":["A slow, swaying pop song over a lilting reggae rhythm. The bass is deep and rounded, the drums lope rather than drive, and gentle keyboards and guitar fill the gaps. Boy George sings in a soft, smoky, soulful voice about a love affair in which he feels wounded and asks whether his lover truly means to cause him pain. The chorus is simple, quiet and sad, and the whole song feels more like lovers' rock than new wave.","Culture Club formed in London in 1981 around Boy George, a flamboyant figure from the New Romantic club scene, with Mikey Craig, Roy Hay and Jon Moss. Their mix of soul, reggae and pop, and George's androgynous style, made them different from the synth-led bands of the time. This was their third single, from the album Kissing to Be Clever, produced by Steve Levine for Virgin, and it reached number one in Britain in 1982.","The song was written in part about George's secret relationship with the band's drummer, Jon Moss, which was hidden from the public at the time. The reggae rhythm connects Culture Club to London's lovers' rock scene, the romantic British reggae of the late seventies. George's first appearance on Top of the Pops, singing this song, caused a national sensation over whether he was a man or a woman.","It was a huge international hit and made Culture Club one of the biggest bands of the early eighties. Critics praised George's voice and the band's blend of styles. Later the song came to be seen as an important moment in the visibility of queer identity in mainstream pop, and it remains the band's most admired song, alongside Karma Chameleon.","Listen for the reggae rhythm and the soft, deep bass. Notice how George sings quietly rather than belting. Then hear Bob Marley's Catch a Fire for the reggae roots and Smokey Robinson's Tracks of My Tears for the soul lineage. Compare it with Duran Duran's Rio, from the same London club scene, for a flashier approach."],"japan-gentlemen-take-polaroids":["Eight songs of cool, elegant, slow-moving art pop, about forty-five minutes long. The title song glides on a polished groove; Methods of Dance and Swing are sinuous and funky, with Mick Karn's rubbery fretless bass sliding under Steve Jansen's precise drums and Richard Barbieri's synthesizer textures. David Sylvian sings in a deep, smooth croon. The record ends with Nightporter, a slow, sad piano piece with oboe and synthesizers, far from pop.","Japan formed in south London in the mid-seventies as a glam-rock band influenced by David Bowie, Roxy Music and the New York Dolls. By 1980 they had moved to Virgin and turned to a sleeker, more atmospheric electronic sound, produced here by John Punter. They were closely linked to the New Romantic movement but distanced themselves from it. Their sophisticated sound owed much to European electronic music and to Japanese pop and art.","Nightporter was influenced by Erik Satie's Gymnopédies, which Sylvian admired; its slow piano chords are close to Satie's in mood and shape. The track Taking Islands in Africa was co-written with Ryuichi Sakamoto of Yellow Magic Orchestra, beginning a long friendship and partnership between Sylvian and Sakamoto that produced Forbidden Colours in 1983.","The album gave Japan their first chart success in Britain. Critics were divided between those who found them affected and those who admired their taste and musicianship. Today Gentlemen Take Polaroids is seen as a key transitional album from glam to sophisticated eighties art pop, and a bridge between British and Japanese electronic music.","Start with the title track and listen closely to Mick Karn's bass. Then play Nightporter immediately after Satie's Gymnopédie No. 1. Follow with Tin Drum, where the band completed its transformation, and with Yellow Magic Orchestra's BGM and Sakamoto's Merry Christmas, Mr. Lawrence, where Sylvian's collaborator went next."],"japan-tin-drum":["Eight songs, about forty minutes, of spare, angular, rhythmic art pop. Steve Jansen's drums and percussion are tight and full of odd accents; Mick Karn's fretless bass slides and weaves; Richard Barbieri's synthesizers play bright, piercing melodies built from Chinese-sounding scales. David Sylvian sings in a detached croon. Ghosts, the most famous track, is almost without rhythm at all: a bare, eerie song of isolated synthesizer sounds and voice, like a sound sculpture.","It was Japan's fifth and final studio album, released on Virgin in 1981 and produced with John Punter. The band had become fascinated by Chinese culture and politics and by the Cultural Revolution; the cover shows Sylvian in a bare room with a portrait of Mao on the wall, eating with chopsticks. The title comes from Günter Grass's novel The Tin Drum. Tensions within the band led to its break-up the following year.","Ghosts, released as a single, became Japan's biggest British hit, remarkable for a song with no beat and hardly any melody in the usual sense. The Chinese elements are Sylvian's imagination of China, a Western fantasy, which he later acknowledged. Much of the rhythmic complexity comes from Jansen playing unusual percussion and Karn's melodic bass taking the role of lead instrument.","It was the band's most successful album and is now considered their masterwork, admired for its daring and its originality. Critics have praised its fusion of Asian influences with European electronic pop, though some have questioned its exoticism. Musicians from Massive Attack to Sakamoto's circle have acknowledged its influence.","Start with Ghosts and listen to its silences. Then hear Visions of China and Cantonese Boy for the rhythmic, angular side. Compare with Yellow Magic Orchestra's BGM, which approached East–West electronic pop from the other side, and David Bowie's Low, one of Sylvian's key influences."],"gary-numan-are-friends-electric":["A long, slow, ominous electronic rock song of over five minutes. A heavy, grinding synthesizer riff churns under Gary Numan's flat, robotic, alienated voice, backed by bass and a live drummer. The song is structured unusually, with verses followed by long instrumental passages rather than a chorus. Numan sings a story, part science fiction and part loneliness, about a figure visited by a paid companion who may be a machine. The mood is cold, paranoid and dreamlike.","Numan began in the punk band Tubeway Army, which recorded for the independent label Beggars Banquet. Legend has it that during early sessions he found a Minimoog synthesizer left in the studio and was captivated. This song, credited to Tubeway Army, was from the album Replicas and reached number one in Britain in 1979, one of the first singles dominated by synthesizers to do so. Numan was influenced by science-fiction writers such as Philip K. Dick and J. G. Ballard.","The quotation marks in the title are part of the point: are friends actual friends, or machines? The theme of android companions draws on Philip K. Dick's novel Do Androids Dream of Electric Sheep?, later filmed as Blade Runner. In 2002 the producer Richard X combined the song with Adina Howard's Freak Like Me for a mash-up that became a UK number one for the Sugababes.","It was a surprise hit and made Numan a star. Critics were often scornful of his image and his debt to Bowie and Kraftwerk. But the song's impact on British pop was enormous: it proved a synth-dominated record could top the charts. Its reputation has grown steadily, and later musicians, including Nine Inch Nails and many electronic producers, have acknowledged Numan's influence.","Listen to the synthesizer riff and notice how slowly the song unfolds; do not expect a chorus. Then play David Bowie's Low for one of Numan's main influences and The Pleasure Principle for his next step. Vangelis's Blade Runner score, set in the same imaginary future, is an evocative companion."],"gary-numan-the-pleasure-principle":["Ten songs, about forty minutes, played without any guitar at all. Heavy, buzzing synthesizers carry the riffs and melodies, supported by a live bass player and drummer who give the machines a physical punch. Gary Numan's voice is cool and distant. Cars, the famous single, is built on a thick, stomping synth riff; Metal is a dark, grinding song; M.E. is narrated by the last machine on earth; Complex is a slow, gentle ballad with strings.","It was Numan's first album under his own name, released on Beggars Banquet in 1979, just months after Are 'Friends' Electric? had topped the British charts. Numan wanted to prove that a rock album could be made without guitars. The title comes from a painting by the Belgian surrealist René Magritte, and the album reflects Numan's reading of science fiction and his own feelings of isolation and anxiety.","Cars was reportedly inspired by an incident of road rage when Numan was threatened by other drivers and locked himself in his car; the car becomes a safe capsule against the world. M.E. was later sampled by Basement Jaxx for their 2001 hit Where's Your Head At, and Cars has been sampled or covered by countless others.","It topped the British album charts and Cars became Numan's biggest international hit. Critics were often hostile, dismissing him as a Bowie imitator. Since then its reputation has risen steadily, and it is recognised as one of the most influential electronic rock albums, a key step from Kraftwerk to industrial rock and electronic dance music.","Start with Cars and listen to the synthesizer riff. Then play Metal and M.E. for the darker side. Notice how the live drums give the machines their punch. Compare with Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine, a cooler German counterpart, and OMD's Enola Gay for the British synth-pop that followed."],"mariah-carey-vision-of-love":["A slow ballad in a rocking triple sway, closer to a late-fifties doo-wop record than to anything else on the radio in 1990. Carey begins almost conversationally, low and breathy, and the song climbs by degrees: gospel runs in the second verse, a choir of her own overdubbed voices behind the bridge, and finally a flight into the whistle register, notes so high they sound more like a bird or a kettle than a human singer. That last ascent is how everyone recognises it.","Carey was twenty and had been singing backup in New York when her demo tape reached Tommy Mottola, head of Columbia Records, who signed her and launched her debut album with an expensive campaign. She co-wrote the song with Ben Margulies, her early writing partner, in the months before the deal. Columbia wanted an answer to Whitney Houston at Arista, and the pop charts of 1990 were ready for a big-voiced ballad singer who also wrote her own material.","Listen to the background vocals: nearly all of them are Carey herself, stacked in layers, so the call-and-response with the 'choir' is really one singer answering herself. The whistle notes at the end are not an effect or a studio trick; she used them live, which is why her MTV Unplugged appearance in 1992 mattered so much to her reputation. Notice too how late the drums commit to a full beat.","The single went to number one in America and won Carey two Grammys, including Best New Artist. Some critics complained of oversinging, a charge that followed her for a decade, and sceptics suspected studio help until her live performances silenced them. Its real legacy is the melisma it licensed: a whole generation of singers, from Christina Aguilera to the talent-show contestants of the 2000s, learned to decorate every phrase with runs because of this record.","Listen once just for the arc: count how many distinct vocal colours Carey uses between the first line and the last, from husky whisper to full chest voice to whistle. Then compare it with Whitney Houston's debut album, the model Columbia had in mind, and hear how Carey's phrasing is more gospel-church and less Broadway. The original album version is the one to hear."],"mariah-carey-fantasy":["A bright, bouncing pop song built on a loop lifted from Tom Tom Club's 1981 dance hit Genius of Love: the springy bassline and the chirping synth figure run underneath the whole track. Over it Carey sings a daydream about a crush, lightly, almost playfully, saving the big voice for the ad-libs at the end. The official remix goes further, stripping the track down and handing the opening to the Wu-Tang Clan rapper Ol' Dirty Bastard, whose ragged, half-sung verse collides with her polish.","Carey was by now Sony's biggest star and married to its chief, Tommy Mottola, who preferred her as a ballad singer. On the Daydream album she pushed toward hip-hop, co-producing Fantasy with Dave Hall and commissioning a remix from Sean 'Puffy' Combs's Bad Boy camp. Pairing a pop diva with a hardcore rapper was then a genuine risk; label executives reportedly doubted it. Carey also directed the song's video herself, filmed at an amusement park.","The loop has a history: Genius of Love had already been sampled by hip-hop pioneers in the early eighties, including Grandmaster Flash, so Carey was not raiding a new-wave record so much as reaching into hip-hop's own record crate. Listen to how the verse melody sits in the gaps of the sampled synth riff rather than on top of it, as if the song had been written to answer the loop.","Fantasy debuted at number one on the Billboard Hot 100, only the second single ever to do so and the first by a woman. The remix mattered more in the long run. Critics now credit it with inventing the pop-meets-rap feature that became standard practice by 2000, the template for Beyoncé with Jay-Z and countless others. Carey has said she felt she had to fight her label to make it.","Hear the album version first for the pure pop pleasure of the loop and the lightness of her voice, then play the Bad Boy remix and notice how much more space there is, and how odd and thrilling the two voices sound together. After that, find Tom Tom Club's original to hear what the sample was before it was hers."],"mariah-carey-all-i-want-for-christmas-is-you":["It opens as if it were a slow gospel ballad: a free-time vocal over soft bells and keyboard, before snapping into a brisk, shuffling beat with sleigh bells, chimes, a piano pounding out even chords and a wall of backing voices. The singer says she does not care about presents or decorations, only about the person she loves, and the song races forward on that single idea with a joy that barely pauses for breath until the final held note.","Carey wrote the song with her producer Walter Afanasieff in 1994 for Merry Christmas, a holiday album that her label initially treated as a sideline. Afanasieff built nearly the entire arrangement from keyboards and drum programming, imitating the live bands of Phil Spector's 1963 Christmas album. The nineties were full of seasonal records that sank within a year; the aim here was to write a new standard that sounded as though it had always existed.","The chords are more sophisticated than they seem. The song borrows the moving harmonies of forties Christmas standards, with one chord in the chorus that seems to sag and brighten at the same time, a colour that belongs to Tin Pan Alley rather than to nineties pop. The 'band' is almost entirely programmed, yet it convinces as a live Spector session because of the swing in the drum pattern.","Reviews in 1994 were warm rather than rapturous. The song's life has been posthumous in the way of folk carols: it returns to the charts every December, and in 2019, twenty-five years after release, it finally reached number one on the Billboard Hot 100, returning to the top in later seasons. It is now widely treated as the last Christmas song to enter the permanent canon.","Notice the moment the free-time introduction breaks into the beat; that switch is the whole trick of the record. Listen for the chimes and the piano chords, then play the Ronettes' and Darlene Love's tracks from A Christmas Gift for You to hear the source. The original 1994 mix is better than the later re-recordings."],"celine-dion-deux":["Twelve French-language songs, nearly all written and produced by the French singer-songwriter Jean-Jacques Goldman, in an idiom far plainer than Dion's English records: acoustic guitars, soft drums, close-miked voice. The opener, Pour que tu m'aimes encore, is a mid-tempo plea built on a strummed figure and a slowly rising melody. Elsewhere there are ballads, a little rock, and a closing song about the everyday life of a star. What you notice first is how quietly she sings.","By 1995 Dion, a Québécoise who had been singing in French since childhood, was an international English-language star after The Power of Love. Her French albums had mostly served Quebec. Goldman, the biggest songwriter in France, offered to write her an entire record; he wanted to show a different Céline. The album was aimed squarely at France and the francophone world, and was later issued abroad as The French Album.","Goldman deliberately wrote in her lower and middle voice and asked her to hold back the power she was known for, so several songs never reach a belted climax at all. Listen for the lyrics' self-portraiture: Goldman built several songs around what he knew of her life and career, so the album works partly as his portrait of her rather than her own statement.","D'eux became the best-selling French-language album in history, spending many weeks at number one in France, and Pour que tu m'aimes encore was a huge single there. French critics, often sniffy about Quebec pop, admired its restraint, and many who find her English ballads overwrought hold this up as her most honest record. Goldman and Dion later made further French albums together.","Start with Pour que tu m'aimes encore and compare it with any of her English power ballads of the same years: the voice is the same, the approach almost opposite. Listen for the acoustic guitars and the dry, close sound of the voice. If you like it, go next to Goldman's own records and to the French chanson tradition behind them."],"celine-dion-its-all-coming-back-to-me-now":["A seven-and-a-half-minute Gothic power ballad written and produced by Jim Steinman, the architect of Meat Loaf's Bat Out of Hell. It starts with a lone piano and a hushed voice remembering a lost lover, and builds in waves: drums, a choir, a thundering band, then sudden drops back to near-silence. The story is of desire that returns physically at the lover's touch, however hard she tried to forget. The piano motif at the start is the thing you remember.","Steinman wrote the song in the late eighties and first recorded it with his studio girl group Pandora's Box in 1989, where it went almost unnoticed. Meat Loaf wanted it; Steinman held out for a female singer and gave it to Dion for Falling into You, a 1996 album designed to make her a global adult-contemporary giant. Steinman reportedly said he had Wuthering Heights in mind: passion that reaches beyond the grave.","The song is really a series of false endings: each time the band crashes to a climax, it retreats to quiet piano and starts building again, a structure borrowed from rock opera more than from pop. Listen for the moment late on when the choir enters behind her, and for how Steinman keeps changing the key upward, so the final chorus sits noticeably higher than the first.","It was a major hit on both sides of the Atlantic and helped Falling into You win the Grammy for Album of the Year. Critics of the time mocked its excess; later writers have reclaimed it as the peak of Steinman's operatic pop, a deliberately camp and completely sincere record at once. Meat Loaf finally released his own duet version in 2006.","Play the full album version, never the radio edit: the length is the point. Listen for the architecture of quiet and loud, and count the climaxes. Then hear Meat Loaf's Bat Out of Hell, or Queen's Bohemian Rhapsody, for the tradition of pop as operatic melodrama that Steinman believed in. Kate Bush's Wuthering Heights makes a fine companion."],"celine-dion-my-heart-will-go-on":["The love theme from Titanic, turned into a pop ballad. A tin whistle plays the tune alone over soft synthesizer pads, recalling the film's Irish steerage scenes; Dion enters quietly and sings of love that outlasts distance and death. After the second chorus the drums arrive, the whole song jumps up a key, and the orchestra swells beneath her full voice. The Celtic whistle and that key change are what everyone remembers.","The composer James Horner wrote the melody as a recurring theme in his Titanic score, with lyrics added by Will Jennings. James Cameron did not want a pop song over his credits, so Horner quietly recorded a demo with Dion, whose husband and manager René Angélil persuaded her to do it, and later played it for Cameron, who relented. The film became the highest-grossing ever released, and the soundtrack on Sony Classical sold in the tens of millions.","Dion sang the song essentially once, in a demo session, and that take was kept; the orchestra and production were built around it afterwards. Listen in the film score itself for the same melody sung wordlessly by the Norwegian singer Sissel Kyrkjebø, and for how Horner's whistle and pipes play it in dozens of fragments before Dion's version ever appears.","It won the Academy Award for Best Original Song and the Grammys for Record and Song of the Year, and was one of the best-selling singles in history. Its sheer ubiquity produced a lasting backlash, and it became shorthand for sentimental excess. Dion herself has said she did not initially care for it. Later listeners have been kinder to its craft, especially the arrangement's restraint in the first half.","Listen to the first minute as a piece of arranging: whistle, pads and voice, nothing else. Mark the exact moment the drums enter and the key lifts, and notice how much of the song's emotion is simply that gear change. Then listen to Horner's score, where the theme is shared among pipes, choir and orchestra, to hear its classical life."],"britney-spears-baby-one-more-time":["Three hammered piano notes, a low grunt of a vocal, and then a mid-tempo groove that sounds like a sad song dressed for a party. Over thudding drums, a wah-wah guitar and a bass that bounces between two notes, a teenage girl sings of loneliness after a breakup and asks for another chance. The verses are sultry and low; the chorus leaps up and punches. That three-note opening is one of the most recognisable introductions in pop.","Spears, a former Mickey Mouse Club performer from Louisiana, was sixteen when Jive Records sent her to Cheiron Studios in Stockholm, where the Swedish writer-producer Max Martin had been building hits for the Backstreet Boys. Martin wrote the song, reportedly with TLC in mind; they passed. The record came out in late 1998 with a video, set in a school corridor, whose uniform styling was reportedly Spears's own idea.","Max Martin's English was his second language, and he chose words for how their vowels and consonants sit on the beat as much as for their meaning; that is why some phrases feel faintly odd yet stick. Listen, too, for Spears's low, nasal growl on certain words, a mannerism Martin kept and emphasised. The minor key is unusual for a teen hit: it is essentially a torch song over a dance beat.","It went to number one in the United States and Britain and began the teen-pop boom of the late nineties. Critics dismissed it as manufactured bubblegum, and the video set off years of argument about the sexualisation of a teenager. Judgment has since shifted strongly: it now routinely appears on lists of the greatest pop singles, and Max Martin's method has come to be seen as a craft as disciplined as any Tin Pan Alley shop.","Listen first to the construction: piano intro, low verses, pre-chorus that climbs, chorus that explodes, and a bridge that pulls the rug out before the last chorus. Then hear it next to ABBA's Dancing Queen, the Swedish ancestor of everything Cheiron did. Finally put on the Backstreet Boys' I Want It That Way to hear the same workshop in a different mood."],"britney-spears-toxic":["A high, sliding string figure, like a Bollywood film orchestra caught in a loop, runs through the whole song, answered by a twanging surf-rock guitar that sounds borrowed from a spy film. Underneath, a hard electronic beat. Spears sings breathily about a lover she knows is bad for her and cannot resist, comparing him to a drug in neutral, cartoonish terms. The string hook, rising and falling in a spiral, is what makes it instantly recognisable.","The song was made in Stockholm by Bloodshy & Avant, the Swedish producing duo of Christian Karlsson and Pontus Winnberg, with the British songwriter Cathy Dennis and Henrik Jonback. It appeared on In the Zone, a 2003 album on which Spears, now twenty-one, moved toward club music and away from teen pop. Kylie Minogue's camp was reportedly offered the song first and let it go.","The swooping strings are widely reported to derive from a Hindi film song of the early eighties by the composing duo Laxmikant–Pyarelal, reworked rather than simply lifted. Listen to how the track shifts gear in the bridge: the beat thins, the strings climb higher, and the whole song briefly hovers before crashing back. The surf guitar is quietly doing most of the rhythmic work.","Toxic won Spears her only Grammy, for Best Dance Recording, and quickly became the most respected record of her career. It has been covered by indie musicians, jazz singers and orchestras, which says something about how well built it is. Critics now often name it among the finest pop singles of the 2000s, and it is frequently cited as the point where the decade's pop turned toward global, genre-crossing production.","Follow the strings: they never stop, and the whole song is arranged around them. Notice the switch between the cool, whispered verses and the more urgent chorus. Then play Kylie Minogue's Can't Get You Out of My Head, also co-written by Cathy Dennis, and Beyoncé's Crazy in Love from the same year, both built on one irresistible borrowed-sounding hook."],"britney-spears-blackout":["Twelve tracks of cold, dense, club-oriented electronic pop: distorted bass, clattering drums, synthesizers that grind and wobble, and Spears's voice pitched, filtered and cut up until it becomes another instrument. Gimme More opens on a spoken tag and a sleazy, slow-rolling pulse; Piece of Me is a flat, sardonic answer to the tabloids; Radar and Break the Ice are bright and propulsive. It is less a set of songs than a sustained texture of nightlife.","The album was recorded in 2006 and 2007, a period of public collapse for Spears: a divorce, a custody battle, relentless paparazzi coverage, and the episodes that preceded the court conservatorship of 2008. Most of the production came from Danja, Timbaland's protégé, with Bloodshy & Avant and others. Spears did almost no promotion, and a much-criticised MTV Awards performance of Gimme More coloured the release.","Spears is credited as executive producer, and those who worked with her have said she chose tracks and shaped the vocal treatment herself, even amid the chaos. Listen to how rarely her natural voice appears unprocessed. In Piece of Me she lists the tabloid versions of herself in a deadpan monotone, and the deep, pitched-down voices in the chorus turn her into a crowd mocking its own image.","Reviews in 2007 were mixed, and the album was overshadowed by the headlines. Its reputation has grown steadily: writers now describe it as one of the most influential pop records of its decade, the template for the hard electropop of Lady Gaga and many who followed, and a strange document of an artist turning surveillance into art. The 2021 end of her conservatorship prompted a further re-reading.","Play it loud, straight through, as a club set rather than an album of singles. Listen to what is done to her voice track by track: pitch shifts, stutters, vocoder, whispers. Then hear Lady Gaga's The Fame Monster and Charli XCX's Pop 2 to trace where the sound went, and Janet Jackson's The Velvet Rope for an earlier star's dark, electronic self-portrait."],"backstreet-boys-i-want-it-that-way":["A mid-tempo ballad that opens on a softly picked acoustic guitar and a single voice, then gathers the other four into tight, close harmony. Each member takes a line or two of the verses, passing the tune around like a relay, before all five meet on a chorus that climbs in steps and resolves with enormous satisfaction. The feeling is of a couple kept apart by distance; the harmonies and the guitar figure are its signature.","The Backstreet Boys were assembled in Orlando in 1993 by the businessman Lou Pearlman and became stars first in Europe before breaking America. For their third album, Millennium, Jive sent them again to Max Martin in Stockholm; Martin wrote the song with Andreas Carlsson and produced it with Kristian Lundin. Millennium sold more than a million copies in its first week in the United States, a record at the time.","The lyric famously does not quite add up: listen closely and the pronouns and the logic of what the singer wants contradict each other. The band and label reportedly recorded a rewritten version with more sensible words, and everyone agreed it sounded worse, so the original stayed. Martin's rule that the sound of the syllables outranks sense has rarely been clearer.","It became one of the biggest singles of 1999 around the world and has settled in as the defining boy-band song. Critics who dismissed boy bands in the moment later granted its craft, and its afterlife has been long: a much-shared police-lineup scene in the sitcom Brooklyn Nine-Nine in 2018 introduced it to a new generation, and it remains a sing-along standard.","Follow the vocal relay: try to identify which member sings each line and hear how the producers use their different colours, the grainy one, the high one, the smooth one. Notice the tidy modulation before the final chorus. Then listen to ABBA's The Winner Takes It All for the Swedish ballad tradition behind it, and NSYNC's Bye Bye Bye for the rival band's harder answer."],"nsync-bye-bye-bye":["A tense, stabbing dance-pop track: short, staccato synthesized string chords, a hard, clipped beat and five voices snapping out phrases in tight rhythmic unison. The singer is walking away from a relationship that has used him, and the chorus repeats the title as a dismissal. The stuttering vocal edits and the jabbing strings give it a nervous energy far from the soft ballads boy bands were known for.","NSYNC, also formed in Orlando under Lou Pearlman, had spent 1999 in a lawsuit to escape his management and their original label contract, moving to Jive. Bye Bye Bye, written by Kristian Lundin, Jake Schulze and Andreas Carlsson of the Stockholm Cheiron circle, was the first single from No Strings Attached, whose title and puppet-strings video made the point. The album sold about 2.4 million copies in its first week in the United States.","The song works as a coded message: ostensibly about a girlfriend, it was heard at the time as the band's farewell to Pearlman, and the video, with the five manipulated by a giant puppeteer, underlines that reading. Listen to the edits in the vocal: syllables are chopped and repeated by machine, an early mainstream use of the stutter effects that would become standard in 2000s pop.","The album's first-week sales stood as the American record for fifteen years, until Adele's 25 in 2015. The song's choreography, with its hand gestures and sharp hits, became a cultural reference in itself. Critics granted that NSYNC's records were edgier than their rivals', and the single is now remembered as the peak moment of the boy-band arms race, and the launch pad for Justin Timberlake's solo career.","Listen to the rhythm of the vocals rather than the words: the five voices behave almost like drums. Notice the string stabs and how the track leaves gaps for the dance hits. Then compare with the Backstreet Boys' I Want It That Way, from the same Swedish workshop a year earlier, and with Timberlake's Justified to hear where this edge went next."],"spice-girls-wannabe":["Under three minutes of shouted, giggling, bouncing pop. A laugh and a stomping piano open it, then five voices trade lines half-sung, half-rapped, telling a would-be boyfriend that he must accept her friends if he wants her. The chorus rests on a nonsense word that nobody has ever convincingly defined. It is rough, fast and deliberately unpolished, more playground chant than ballad, and the introductions of each singer's personality are built into the song.","The Spice Girls were formed in London in 1994 through an audition advertised in a trade paper, then broke away from their first managers and signed with Simon Fuller and Virgin Records. The song was written with the producers Matt Rowe and Richard Stannard in a quick early session. In the Britpop era of guitar bands and lad culture, they sold a brash, jokey idea of female solidarity, packaged as Girl Power.","The video was shot in a single continuous take in the old Midland Grand Hotel at St Pancras, the five running through rooms and startling guests; its rough energy was part of the pitch. Listen to how little the song resembles a conventional verse and chorus: the rap-like sections, the chants and the shouted interjections make it closer to a playground game than to a pop ballad.","Wannabe spent seven weeks at number one in Britain and four in the United States, and reached number one in dozens of countries, becoming one of the best-selling singles by a female group. Critics sneered at the manufacture; later writers have argued over whether Girl Power was real feminism or marketing, and have come to see the song as a genuine moment in how young girls were spoken to by pop.","Try to pick out each of the five voices; the song is built to introduce them. Notice the piano, rough and rolling like a pub upright, and how the rhythm keeps stopping for shouted asides. Then play TLC's Waterfalls or the Shangri-Las for other girl groups that sold an attitude, and Destiny's Child's Survivor for the harder solidarity anthem that followed."],"spice-girls-spice":["A ten-song debut that moves between brash dance-pop and soft balladry. Wannabe opens it; Say You'll Be There follows with a slinky, mid-tempo groove, a whistling synth and a harmonica solo; 2 Become 1 is a hushed love ballad; Mama is a tender tribute to their mothers; Who Do You Think You Are is a disco stomp. The five voices trade leads throughout, and the production is clean, bright and radio-shaped, with R&B rhythms under a British pop sensibility.","Released by Virgin in November 1996, after Wannabe had already made the group famous, the album was produced mainly by Stannard and Rowe and by the duo Absolute, with Eliot Kennedy contributing. It arrived during a British pop boom and a period of intense media interest in personality; each member had already been given a tabloid nickname that became part of the brand.","Listen to 2 Become 1: amid the romance there is a line about safe sex, a small, deliberate message to a very young audience. Say You'll Be There borrows the loping feel of American West Coast R&B of the time, the kind of groove that sits behind G-funk, rather than anything British. Mama, a gentle song to their mothers issued as a double A-side single, shows the brash group briefly tender.","Spice sold more than twenty million copies worldwide and became the best-selling album by a female group. Reviews were grudging; the group was treated as a phenomenon rather than a band. Later writers have been kinder, noting the quality of the craft behind the singles, and in Britain it is now remembered as the sound of a particular optimistic moment of the late nineties.","Skip Wannabe on this listen and start with Say You'll Be There: notice the groove, the harmonica and the pacing of the verses. Then 2 Become 1 for the ballad side. Compare with TLC's CrazySexyCool, the American girl group whose R&B sound Spice partly borrowed, and with PJ Harvey's Rid of Me for an opposite version of women's rock in the nineties."],"christina-aguilera-genie-in-a-bottle":["A cool, minor-key mid-tempo song over a clipped R&B beat, a spidery synthesizer figure and a guitar that flicks in and out. Aguilera's voice is low and restrained in the verses, then breaks into gospel runs on the chorus. The lyric, about wanting to be treated right before giving her heart, uses the genie as an extended metaphor. The contrast between the sleek track and the huge, ornamented voice is the whole identity of the record.","Aguilera, like Spears and Timberlake, was an alumna of the Disney Mickey Mouse Club. In 1998 she recorded Reflection for Disney's Mulan, which helped secure her RCA deal. Genie in a Bottle, written by Steve Kipner, David Frank and Pam Sheyne, was the lead single of her 1999 debut album, arriving months after Spears's first hit and immediately setting up a rivalry the press enjoyed.","Listen to the ad-libs on the final choruses: short, rapid melismatic runs of the kind Aguilera learned from Whitney Houston, Mariah Carey and older soul singers such as Etta James. The track itself is very sparse; notice how often the drums drop out and leave only the synth figure and the voice. The producers' restraint is what lets the voice sound so large.","The song spent five weeks at number one in the United States and topped charts across Europe. In 2000 Aguilera beat Spears to the Grammy for Best New Artist. Critics recognised early that her voice was of a different order from most teen pop; some complained she oversang, the same charge once made against Carey. The song remains one of the defining singles of 1999.","Listen first for the voice, then for the gaps in the track around it. Compare with Britney Spears's ...Baby One More Time from months earlier: both are minor-key teen songs with a hint of R&B, but here the emphasis is on the singing. Then play Mariah Carey's Vision of Love to hear the lineage of runs she inherited."],"christina-aguilera-stripped":["A long, sprawling second album of twenty tracks that swings between hip-hop, rock, Latin, soul and piano balladry. Dirrty is a hard, brash club track with the rapper Redman; Fighter is a rock anthem with a stomping guitar riff; Beautiful is a spare piano ballad about self-worth sung close and quiet until its climax. Short spoken interludes stitch it together. The range is the point: it is a deliberate portrait of a voice able to do anything.","Aguilera, then twenty-one, had broken with her early management and wanted to shed the teen-pop image of her debut. She worked with a wide range of producers, including Linda Perry, former singer of 4 Non Blondes, who wrote Beautiful, and Scott Storch and Rockwilder. The provocative styling of the Dirrty video caused a scandal, and the album cast her as a candid, adult artist in a period of intense scrutiny of young women in pop.","The vocal on Beautiful is reportedly largely from an early recording session, which is why it has a rawness and a few small imperfections unusual in her work. Listen too to the interludes: in the opening track and between songs, she answers her critics and sets out the album's argument about authenticity, a technique borrowed from hip-hop albums of the period.","Reviews were divided on release: some found it bloated and overeager, others praised its ambition. Beautiful won a Grammy and became an anthem for gay audiences and for anyone struggling with self-image, and Fighter has had a long life as an empowerment song. The album now reads as a key example of the early-2000s reinvention record, the move from teen star to self-defined adult.","Do not try it all at once. Start with Beautiful, then Fighter, then Dirrty, to hear three completely different singers in one voice. Notice how in Beautiful she waits until the end to use her full power. Then compare with Janet Jackson's Control, the original adult-reinvention record, and with Justin Timberlake's Justified from the same season."],"justin-timberlake-justified":["A solo debut in the lineage of Michael Jackson's Off the Wall: falsetto-heavy dance pop, crisp and funky, produced mostly by the Neptunes. Like I Love You is built on a flamenco-flavoured acoustic guitar and a guest verse by Clipse; Rock Your Body is a disco strut with a dry, clattering beat; Señorita is a live-sounding, handclap-driven jam. Cry Me a River, produced by Timbaland, is darker: a dramatic song of betrayal with choir, harpsichord-like keyboards and a beatboxed rhythm.","With NSYNC on hiatus in 2002, Timberlake went solo at twenty-one, making the album mainly with Pharrell Williams and Chad Hugo of the Neptunes, the most sought-after producers in hip-hop and R&B. His breakup with Britney Spears was a tabloid event, and Cry Me a River, with its video featuring a Spears lookalike, was universally heard as a comment on it. Rock Your Body was reportedly first intended for Michael Jackson.","On Cry Me a River, the percussive clicks and breaths underneath are Timbaland beatboxing, woven into the programmed drums, and the chanted voices that sound like a monastic choir are layered vocals. Listen to how much of the album sits in falsetto: Timberlake barely uses his chest voice, a direct homage to Jackson and to the soul falsetto of Curtis Mayfield and Prince.","Justified won the Grammy for Best Pop Vocal Album and established Timberlake as a credible solo artist rather than a boy-band escapee. Critics noted, sometimes pointedly, how closely it borrowed from Jackson, and later debates about white pop stars' use of Black musical idioms have often cited it. Cry Me a River is widely held to be its finest track.","Start with Cry Me a River and listen to the production detail: beatbox, choir, rain-like textures and the stop-start drama. Then Rock Your Body for the lighter Neptunes sound. Put on Michael Jackson's Off the Wall afterward and listen for the falsetto and the disco guitars, and Missy Elliott's Get Ur Freak On for Timbaland's other face."],"justin-timberlake-futuresex-lovesounds":["A dense, adventurous second album produced almost entirely by Timbaland and his protégé Danja. SexyBack is a stripped, distorted electro-funk track, the voice run through heavy filtering over a bare, pounding beat. My Love uses synthesized staccato chords and a verse from T.I.; What Goes Around... Comes Around is a long, cinematic ballad. Many tracks split in two, a song then an interlude or a second song, so the album flows like a continuous mix.","Timberlake had been out of music for several years, acting in films. He recorded largely at Timbaland's studio in Virginia Beach, reportedly working without written lyrics and building songs by improvising at the microphone over Timbaland's tracks. It came out in 2006, a moment when Timbaland also produced Nelly Furtado's Loose, and together the two albums defined the sound of American pop radio that year.","Listen for the two-part structures: LoveStoned, for example, turns midway into I Think She Knows, a slow, guitar-driven coda that sounds like a completely different record. On SexyBack the voice is so distorted that it approaches the sound of the synthesizers around it; the effect is deliberately robotic, a homage to Prince's electronic funk and to eighties electro.","SexyBack and My Love both reached number one in America, and the album was widely praised as bold and inventive, with many critics ranking it above Justified. It has since been seen as a high point for Timbaland's pop production and for the mid-2000s idea of the mainstream album as a continuous, experimental listen. Some find its length and its sexual swagger tiring.","Play it continuously on headphones. Start with SexyBack to hear the bare distorted funk, then My Love for its interlocking synths, then the long What Goes Around... Comes Around. Listen for the joins between tracks. Compare with Prince's Sign o' the Times, a clear model, and with Nelly Furtado's Loose, made at the same time by the same producers."],"kylie-minogue-confide-in-me":["Nearly six minutes of slow, brooding atmosphere. A sweeping string line with an Arabic, film-orchestra flavour sets the scene, over a deep bass pulse and a loping, trip-hop-inflected beat. Minogue sings low and close, offering to be a confidante who will hear any secret, and the song circles and deepens rather than building to a big chorus. The strings, sliding and swirling, are what you remember: it sounds like a film noir scored in Cairo.","Minogue had spent the late eighties as the bright, bubbly face of the Stock Aitken Waterman hit factory in London. In 1993 she left their label PWL and joined Deconstruction, a British dance label, looking for credibility. The song was written and produced by Brothers in Rhythm, Steve Anderson and Dave Seaman, with Owain Barton. It appeared in 1994 as the first single from her self-titled album.","Listen to the verses: Minogue's voice is almost spoken, half-whispered, a far cry from the bright upper register of her earlier hits. The strings play a figure in a scale with an unusual step that gives the Middle Eastern colour, and they return between every section like a refrain. The song's structure breathes: there are long passages with no voice at all, only strings and groove.","The single topped the Australian charts and was a hit in Britain. Critics who had dismissed Minogue as a teen idol were surprised and often impressed. It began what fans call her 'indie Kylie' phase, which led to the more experimental Impossible Princess, and it is now widely regarded as her finest single and a key piece of mid-nineties sophisticated dance-pop.","Listen for the strings first and follow their line through the song. Then notice how quietly she sings and how little the song climaxes. Compare with Madonna's Ray of Light and Björk's Post from the same years, where pop singers also turned to electronica and strings, and with her own Can't Get You Out of My Head seven years later."],"kylie-minogue-cant-get-you-out-of-my-head":["A cool, minimal electronic dance track: a hypnotic, pulsing synth bass, a clean, steady beat, a few synthesizer chords and a hook sung on wordless syllables that is as catchy as any lyric. Minogue sings in a light, breathy, almost detached voice about an obsession she cannot shake. There is hardly any change of texture over four minutes; the song works by repetition and restraint, and the circling bassline is its core.","By 2001 Minogue had moved again, to Parlophone, and was enjoying a successful comeback with the disco-leaning Light Years. The song was written by Cathy Dennis and Rob Davis, a former glam-rock guitarist, and was reportedly turned down by other artists before Minogue took it. It was the lead single from Fever, and its video, with Minogue in a white hooded jumpsuit in a futuristic city, set the look of the era.","Listen to how much of the hook is not words at all: wordless syllables carry the tune, which is why it translated so easily across languages. The bassline owes a debt to Giorgio Moroder's work with Donna Summer. At the 2002 Brit Awards Minogue performed a mash-up with New Order's Blue Monday, making plain its family tree in electronic dance music.","The song reached number one in Britain, Australia and a string of other countries, and broke into the American top ten, rare for her. It is widely considered her signature and one of the defining singles of the 2000s; critics praised its restraint in an era of maximal production. The Blue Monday mash-up is often cited as a turning point for mash-up culture going mainstream.","Listen once for the bassline alone, then for the vocal. Notice that nothing much changes: the tension is in holding still. Then play Donna Summer's I Feel Love and New Order's Blue Monday back to back with it to hear its lineage, and Robyn's Dancing on My Own to hear where the cool, sad-disco idea went next."],"shakira-donde-estan-los-ladrones":["A Spanish-language pop-rock album of eleven songs built on crunching guitars, a rock rhythm section and Shakira's distinctive voice, with its sudden yodel-like breaks between registers. Ciega, Sordomuda is a brisk, mariachi-horned pop song; Tú is a rock ballad; Inevitable a slow-burning, guitar-heavy love song; Ojos Así brings in Middle Eastern percussion and melody, drawing on her Lebanese heritage. The overall feel is of an alternative-rock singer-songwriter with a Latin pop sense of melody.","Shakira, from Barranquilla, Colombia, had broken through across Latin America with Pies Descalzos in 1995. For this follow-up she worked in Miami, with Emilio Estefan as executive producer, and co-produced much of it herself with Luis Fernando Ochoa and Lester Mendez. The late nineties were the moment of the so-called Latin explosion in the United States, with Ricky Martin and Jennifer Lopez crossing over; Shakira stayed in Spanish, for now.","The album's title comes from a real incident: a suitcase containing handwritten lyrics was reportedly stolen at an airport, and she turned the loss into a question about who the real thieves are. Listen to Ojos Así for her voice's Arabic ornamentation, the quick melismatic turns she learned from music of her father's family, and for the darbuka drum under the chorus.","It became one of the best-selling Spanish-language albums of its time and earned a Grammy nomination; her MTV Unplugged the next year, drawn largely from these songs, won a Grammy. Critics compared her to Alanis Morissette, a comparison that flattered and irritated. Many Latin American listeners still regard it as her best work, before English crossover smoothed her sound.","Start with Ojos Así to hear the Arabic side, then Ciega, Sordomuda for the pop side, then Inevitable for the rock ballad. Listen to the way her voice flips suddenly into a higher, harder register. Compare with Alanis Morissette's Jagged Little Pill for the rock singer-songwriter model, and with Umm Kulthum for the ornament she echoes."],"shakira-whenever-wherever":["A bright, driving pop song that opens with Andean panpipes and the strummed charango, the small Andean guitar, before a rock beat and a thick guitar riff arrive. Shakira sings of a devotion that will cross any distance, with her trademark yodel-like leaps. The chorus is huge and simple, but the texture keeps flavours of the Andes and of Colombian coastal rhythm alongside the rock guitars. The panpipe figure is the one sound everyone remembers.","The song, released in Spanish as Suerte and in English as Whenever, Wherever, was the lead single from Laundry Service, Shakira's first English-language album, released by Epic in 2001. It was co-written and co-produced with Tim Mitchell. Her crossover had been carefully prepared, with English lessons and an image change, and the song's video, featuring belly-dancing in natural landscapes, introduced her dancing to a global audience.","The panpipes and charango are drawn from the Andean music of Bolivia and Peru, not from her own Caribbean-coast Colombia; the record builds a pan-Latin American collage rather than a single national sound. Listen for how the rock guitars sit beneath the Andean instruments rather than replacing them. Her English lyrics are full of unusual, idiosyncratic images, deliberately kept.","The single was a worldwide number one in dozens of countries and a big American hit. English-language critics sometimes made fun of her lyrics' quirky imagery; others found them refreshing. The song became the model for a new kind of Latin crossover in which folk instruments and dance were kept rather than smoothed away, preparing the ground for her even bigger Hips Don't Lie.","Listen for the panpipes at the start and notice when they return. Then track the charango's quick strumming through the verses, under the guitars. Play the Spanish version, Suerte, too; she sings it differently. Afterward listen to Celia Cruz's La vida es un carnaval for another late-nineties Latin anthem, and Kylie's Can't Get You Out of My Head from the same season."],"shakira-hips-dont-lie":["A bright trumpet riff kicks off a dance song that mixes cumbia, reggaeton, Caribbean rhythms and hip-hop. Wyclef Jean opens with a rapped, chatty invitation to the dance floor; Shakira answers with verses about the honesty of the body in motion, sung partly in Spanish and partly in English. Latin percussion, shakers and a looped brass line drive the whole song, and the call-and-response between the two voices gives it a party feel.","Wyclef Jean of the Fugees produced the song, reworking a track of his own, Dance Like This, recorded a couple of years earlier. It was added to the reissue of Shakira's 2005 album Oral Fixation, Vol. 2 in 2006. That summer she performed it at the closing ceremony of the FIFA World Cup in Berlin, beginning her long association with football anthems. The Latin pop market in the United States had grown hugely by then.","The trumpet riff is borrowed, by interpolation, from a salsa ballad by the Puerto Rican singer Jerry Rivera, Amores como el nuestro, a detail most listeners outside Latin America never noticed. Listen to how Wyclef name-checks places and figures across the Caribbean and Colombia, knitting the song into a pan-Caribbean map. The percussion includes rhythms associated with Colombian coastal cumbia.","It reached number one in America and dozens of other countries and set a record for radio plays in a single week in the United States. It was one of the biggest songs of the decade anywhere. Critics saw it as the peak of Latin crossover, a genuinely hybrid record rather than an English pop song with Latin seasoning, and it is still a staple of weddings and stadiums.","Follow the trumpet riff in and out, then listen for the two voices trading places. Try to pick out the different rhythmic layers: percussion, shakers, programmed beat. Then hear the Fugees' The Score for Wyclef's own world, and Sean Paul's Get Busy for the Caribbean dance crossover a few years earlier."],"nelly-furtado-whoa-nelly":["An eclectic debut that pulls together folk-pop guitars, trip-hop drum loops, bossa nova, hip-hop scratching and touches of Portuguese folk song. I'm Like a Bird is a gentle, lilting song about a restless spirit who cannot stay in one place; Turn Off the Light is jittery and funky; Shit on the Radio is a wry look at fame. Furtado's voice is light and flexible, moving between singing and half-rapped patter, with a slight catch in the tone.","Furtado grew up in Victoria, British Columbia, the daughter of Portuguese immigrants from the Azores, and played several instruments as a child. The album was made in Toronto with the production duo Track & Field, Gerald Eaton and Brian West, and released by DreamWorks in 2000. It arrived amid the teen-pop boom but offered a singer-songwriter with an ear for global and hip-hop sounds, a rarer figure in mainstream pop then.","Listen for the Portuguese influences: some vocal ornaments and melodic turns echo fado and the folk music of the Azores that she heard at home, and she sings in Portuguese on at least one song. The drum loops and turntable scratches are close to trip-hop and hip-hop production, but the songs underneath are written like folk songs on acoustic guitar.","I'm Like a Bird was a big international hit and won the Grammy for Best Female Pop Vocal Performance. Critics praised the record's range and freshness, often comparing her with Lauryn Hill and Björk for the mix of pop, folk and hip-hop. It sold millions, and is now remembered as a distinct, slightly overlooked moment of eclectic mainstream pop before the Timbaland reinvention of 2006.","Start with I'm Like a Bird for the melody, then Turn Off the Light for the rhythmic side. Notice the loops and scratches under the guitars. Then play Lauryn Hill's Miseducation and Björk's Debut, both kindred mixtures of pop and global sound, and Amália Rodrigues for the fado behind her phrasing."],"nelly-furtado-loose":["A complete reinvention: dark, synthesizer-driven dance pop and R&B, produced mainly by Timbaland and Danja. Promiscuous is a teasing call-and-response duet with Timbaland over a sparse, bouncing beat; Maneater is a pounding, distorted electro-stomp; Say It Right is a haunting, spacious song with a slow, heavy pulse and floating synth pads. The album also includes Spanish songs and a ballad with Chris Martin of Coldplay. Throughout, the beats are unusual, full of odd sounds and gaps.","After a quieter second album, Furtado sought a new direction and went to Miami to work with Timbaland, the producer then defining the sound of American pop and R&B. The album came out in 2006 on Geffen and Timbaland's Mosley label, the same year as Timberlake's FutureSex/LoveSounds. Its image and sound were more overtly sensual and club-oriented, a shift that divided her early fans.","Listen to Say It Right: the rhythm is built partly from a low, pulsing synth and chanted vocals, and the song circles rather than climaxes, giving it its trance-like quality. Timbaland's own voice appears across the album, interjecting and ad-libbing; he is effectively a second performer. Several tracks reportedly grew out of loose studio jam sessions, which may explain their raw edges.","Promiscuous and Say It Right both reached number one in America, and Maneater topped the British charts. Critics were largely positive, praising the boldness of the production, though some early admirers regretted the move away from her folk-pop roots. It is now considered one of the defining pop records of the mid-2000s and a peak of Timbaland's reach.","Start with Say It Right and listen for the space in the mix, then Maneater for the hard, distorted beat, and Promiscuous for the duet. Notice how often Timbaland's voice appears. Compare with Timberlake's FutureSex/LoveSounds and with Missy Elliott's Get Ur Freak On, Timbaland's earlier landmark, to hear the family sound."],"rihanna-umbrella":["A crisp, heavy drum loop, a slow, looming synthesizer line and a short rapped introduction from Jay-Z, then Rihanna sings an offer of shelter and loyalty through hard times, using the umbrella as the image. The chorus's stuttering repetition of the last syllable of the title became one of the most imitated hooks of the decade. The production is simple and massive, with a slow build of synth layers to a big final chorus.","Rihanna, from Barbados, had been signed to Def Jam at seventeen by Jay-Z, then the label's president, and had enjoyed hits in a light dancehall-pop style. Umbrella, written by Christopher 'Tricky' Stewart, Terius 'The-Dream' Nash, Kuk Harrell and Jay-Z, was the lead single from Good Girl Gone Bad in 2007, which remade her as a darker, edgier star. The song was reportedly offered to Britney Spears's team first.","The drum loop is widely reported to have begun as a stock pattern supplied with Apple's GarageBand software, a detail that says much about how home technology shaped pop production. Listen to how little the arrangement changes: most of the drama comes from the vocal and the way the synth pads thicken, not from new instruments. Rihanna's Barbadian accent colours the vowels in ways a studio singer might have smoothed out.","It spent ten weeks at number one in Britain during a famously wet summer, and several weeks at the top in America; it won a Grammy for Best Rap/Sung Collaboration. Critics praised its simplicity and strength, and it made Rihanna a major star. It is now regarded as one of the defining singles of the 2000s, and its stuttering hook was endlessly copied.","Listen for the drums first: notice their heaviness and how they never change. Then follow the synth line that slowly thickens. After, play Beyoncé's Crazy in Love, another Jay-Z-assisted breakthrough, and Destiny's Child's Say My Name for the R&B background. Rihanna's much later Anti shows how far she travelled."],"rihanna-anti":["A moody, loose and adventurous album that avoids big pop hooks. Work, with Drake, is a slow dancehall groove with Rihanna singing in heavy Bajan patois; Kiss It Better is a smoky eighties-style ballad with a wailing electric guitar; Love on the Brain is a doo-wop and soul ballad in a slow triple time; Same Ol' Mistakes is a complete cover of a Tame Impala psychedelic song. Throughout, Rihanna's voice is rougher and more varied than on her singles.","After nearly a decade of hit-driven albums, Rihanna took more than three years to make Anti, now on her own Westbury Road label through Roc Nation. Its release in January 2016 was chaotic: it leaked, was issued first on the Tidal streaming service, and was given away free to a million people through a sponsorship deal. The era of streaming had begun to change what an album release meant.","Same Ol' Mistakes is Kevin Parker's song New Person, Same Old Mistakes, taken almost unchanged; Rihanna sings over a track very close to the Tame Impala original, a striking choice for a pop star. Listen to the opener, Consideration, with SZA, for a declaration of independence from what others expect. The patois in Work is the language of her Barbados childhood, not a stylistic gesture.","Reviews were mixed at first, with some critics finding the album unfocused. Work spent nine weeks at number one in America, and the album's reputation has grown steadily: it is now often called her best album and one of the finest records of its decade, praised for its looseness and range. Fans have long waited, so far in vain, for a follow-up.","Listen straight through; it moves by mood more than by hits. Start with Kiss It Better, then Love on the Brain, then Work. Notice how differently she uses her voice across the three. Compare with Sade's Love Deluxe and D'Angelo's Black Messiah for the slow, textured R&B around it, and Beyoncé's Lemonade from the same spring."],"katy-perry-teenage-dream":["A glossy, sunny, candy-coloured pop album of twelve songs. California Gurls, with Snoop Dogg, is a bright, bouncy tribute to West Coast beach culture; the title track is a shimmering, nostalgic love song over a steady, pulsing guitar; Firework is a thumping, uplifting anthem; E.T. is dark and heavy, with a pounding beat; Last Friday Night is a comic account of a wild party. The sound is polished, loud and tuned for radio.","Perry, the daughter of evangelical pastors, had recorded a Christian album as a teenager before a pop breakthrough in 2008. For Teenage Dream she worked with the leading hitmakers of the period, including Dr. Luke, Max Martin, Benny Blanco and Stargate, on Capitol Records. It was released in 2010, at the height of the digital-singles era, when individual tracks mattered more than albums.","Listen to the title track: the chords never settle on the home key, and the harmony keeps circling without resolving, which is why the song feels suspended and longing rather than triumphant; the musician Owen Pallett wrote a much-read analysis of this. The album also nods to the Beach Boys, especially in California Gurls, whose title echoes their California Girls.","It produced five number-one singles on the Billboard Hot 100, matching the record set by Michael Jackson's Bad and making Perry the first woman to do so. Reviews were mixed; many critics found it shallow, though the title track has since been widely praised as a near-perfect pop song. Later controversies around one of its producers have complicated the album's legacy for some listeners.","Start with the title track: listen for the unresolving harmonies and the pulsing guitar. Then California Gurls and Firework for the anthem side. Compare with the Beach Boys' California Girls for the ancestor, Taylor Swift's 1989 for the Max Martin sound at its peak a few years later, and Michael Jackson's Bad, whose record it tied."],"robbie-williams-angels":["A slow-building ballad that begins with piano and voice and grows into a full band with strings and electric guitar. Williams sings about finding comfort and protection in a guardian figure, love or the sacred, when he feels lost. The chorus is broad and anthemic, made for crowds to sing. The song's gradual build, from intimate verse to huge, soaring final chorus, is its signature shape.","Williams had left Take That, Britain's biggest boy band, in 1995 amid acrimony, and his solo career began uncertainly. With the songwriter and producer Guy Chambers he made Life thru a Lens, released in 1997 on Chrysalis. The Britpop era favoured guitar bands and swagger; Williams used both, but Angels was a ballad, and its success rescued the album and turned his career around.","The Irish songwriter Ray Heffernan has long said he wrote an early version of the song with Williams and was paid for his share; the credited version is by Williams and Chambers. Listen to how the arrangement adds one element per section, piano, then band, then strings, so that the song climbs steadily without an obvious key change. Williams's voice cracks a little on the high notes, part of the appeal.","It became a British standard and in 2005 was voted the best British single of the previous twenty-five years at the Brit Awards. It is regularly reported to be among the most requested songs at British funerals. Critics have often found it sentimental, but its hold on British audiences is unmatched, and it has become a defining song of his live shows.","Listen for the gradual build: note when each instrument joins. Then hear it live, where the crowd sings the chorus for him. Compare with Whitney Houston's I Will Always Love You for the anthem-ballad tradition, and with Radiohead's OK Computer, from the same year, for the opposite face of British music in 1997."],"robbie-williams-swing-when-youre-winning":["An album of swing standards with a full big band and strings, in the manner of Frank Sinatra's Capitol records of the fifties. Williams sings songs associated with Sinatra, Bobby Darin and Dean Martin, including Mack the Knife and Beyond the Sea. There are duets with Nicole Kidman on Something Stupid and with the actor Jane Horrocks, and a technologically assisted duet with Sinatra himself on It Was a Very Good Year.","By 2001 Williams was one of the biggest stars in Britain and Europe. The album was recorded largely at Capitol Studios in Hollywood, where Sinatra had made many of his classic records, and was produced by Guy Chambers and Steve Power. Its release coincided with a lavish concert at the Royal Albert Hall in London, filmed for television, and Something Stupid became the British Christmas number one.","The Sinatra duet is built from an archival vocal recording, with Williams's voice added to create a conversation between the two singers across decades. Listen to the arrangements: they are close pastiches of the classic Nelson Riddle and Billy May charts rather than modernisations, with muted trumpets, saxophone sections and strings used exactly in the old manner.","The album sold millions and was a huge success in Britain and Europe. Critics were divided, some admiring Williams's charm and commitment, others calling it karaoke. It helped launch a wave of pop stars recording standards albums in the 2000s, and it remains his best-selling record. It is still a gateway into the Great American Songbook for many British listeners.","Start with Something Stupid for the duet, then Mack the Knife for the big-band swing. Listen for the brass sections and how closely they imitate the originals. Then go straight to Sinatra's Songs for Swingin' Lovers! and Sinatra at the Sands to hear the models, and notice the difference in phrasing and timing."],"alanis-morissette-jagged-little-pill":["Twelve songs of confession, anger, wit and self-examination over a sound that mixes alternative rock guitars, hip-hop-inflected drum loops and folk-rock melody. You Oughta Know is a furious, raw account of a breakup; Hand in My Pocket is a laid-back list of contradictions over a loping groove; Ironic is a wry list of life's misfortunes; You Learn and Head over Feet are warmer. Morissette's voice cracks, yelps and swoops, with an unusual, idiosyncratic phrasing.","Morissette had been a teenage dance-pop star in Canada before moving to Los Angeles, where she met the songwriter and producer Glen Ballard. They wrote and recorded the album quickly in his home studio, often finishing a song in a day, with many of the demo vocals kept. It was released on Maverick, the label co-founded by Madonna, in 1995, in the wake of grunge and Nirvana's success.","You Oughta Know features Flea of the Red Hot Chili Peppers on bass and Dave Navarro on guitar, added after the demos were done. There is a hidden a cappella track after a stretch of silence at the end, a common nineties CD trick. Listen for the harmonica, which gives some songs a folk-rock edge, and for how many vocals are first or early takes.","It became one of the best-selling albums of all time, with sales over thirty million, and won the Grammy for Album of the Year. Critics praised its honesty, though some dismissed it as calculated. The debate over whether the events in Ironic are really ironic became a running joke. A Broadway musical built on its songs opened in 2019.","Start with You Oughta Know for the fury, then Hand in My Pocket and Head over Feet for the other sides. Listen to her voice: its breaks and swoops are expressive, not flaws. Compare with Joni Mitchell's Blue and Patti Smith's Horses for confessional forebears, and PJ Harvey's Rid of Me for a rawer take."],"u2-war":["Ten songs that sound like a young band clenching its fists. The record opens with 'Sunday Bloody Sunday', built on Larry Mullen's military snare tattoo and a sawing electric violin, and 'New Year's Day' rides Adam Clayton's stalking bass line and a cold piano figure. The Edge's guitar is sparse, chiming and full of echo rather than riffing, and the album ends with '40', a hushed setting of a psalm that became the band's concert farewell for years.","U2 were four Dublin schoolfriends who had released two albums on Island by 1981, the second, October, shaped by their evangelical Christian circle and nearly torn apart by doubts about whether faith and rock could coexist. War was recorded at Windmill Lane in Dublin with the producer Steve Lillywhite in late 1982, while the Troubles filled the news and the Solidarity movement was being crushed in Poland. It became their first British number one album.","'Sunday Bloody Sunday' refuses the side-taking its title seems to promise: the song is a plea against sectarian killing, not a rebel anthem, and Bono introduced it on stage for years as not a rebel song. The closing '40' was written and recorded in a rush at the very end of the sessions, its words taken almost straight from Psalm 40, which is why it sounds so unlike anything else on the record.","Some British critics found War earnest to the point of pomposity, and the white-flag-waving stage show that followed invited mockery. But in America it broke U2 on college radio and MTV, and the live film Under a Blood Red Sky, shot at Red Rocks in Colorado, made their reputation as a stadium band in waiting. It is now heard as the moment post-punk seriousness was turned outward into arena-sized protest.","Start with 'New Year's Day' and follow the bass line, which carries the whole song while the guitar only colours it. Then play 'Sunday Bloody Sunday' and notice how much of its force is drums and violin rather than guitar. Finish with '40' and hear how the band that shouts for most of the record chooses to end on a quiet hymn."],"u2-the-joshua-tree":["Eleven songs that move from open-sky exhilaration to dust and dread. 'Where the Streets Have No Name' begins with an organ swell and a pulsing, delay-drenched guitar that seems to spin forever before the drums arrive; 'I Still Haven't Found What I'm Looking For' is a gospel song in rock clothes; 'With or Without You' builds for four minutes over a single bass figure. The second side darkens into 'Bullet the Blue Sky', 'Running to Stand Still' and 'Mothers of the Disappeared'.","After The Unforgettable Fire, U2 kept Brian Eno and Daniel Lanois as producers and recorded mostly in Dublin through 1986. Bono had travelled to Ethiopia after Live Aid and to Nicaragua and El Salvador, and the band had fallen for American roots music, from blues to Johnny Cash. The record is their argument with America: in love with its myths and landscapes, appalled by its foreign policy. Anton Corbijn photographed them in the California desert for the sleeve.","The shimmering, endlessly sustaining tone in 'With or Without You' comes from the Infinite Guitar, a prototype device lent to the Edge by the Canadian musician Michael Brook. 'Where the Streets Have No Name' went through so many versions that, by a story both producers have told, Eno at one point wanted to erase the tape and start again. 'Mothers of the Disappeared' was written for the women who marched for the missing in Argentina and Chile.","It went to number one in Britain and America, won the Grammy for Album of the Year, and put U2 on the cover of Time. Critics largely embraced it at the time; later, the band itself grew wary of the earnestness and mythmaking it had unleashed and spent the next record dismantling it. It remains the benchmark for rock that reaches stadium size without losing the sense of a band playing in a room.","Play the opening minute of 'Where the Streets Have No Name' loud and notice how long the band withholds the beat. Then set 'I Still Haven't Found What I'm Looking For' against a gospel choir recording and hear the call-and-response underneath. On a second listen, stay for the last three songs, which most people skip and which hold the album's real darkness."],"u2-achtung-baby":["Twelve songs in which U2 buries its familiar chime under distortion, drum loops and fuzz. 'Zoo Station' opens with a grinding, detuned guitar that sounds like a machine starting up; 'The Fly' is all sneering falsetto and industrial crunch; 'Mysterious Ways' borrows a loose-limbed dance groove. At the centre sits 'One', a slow, bruised ballad about a relationship that has to go on despite everything, and the record ends in the ghostly murmur of 'Love Is Blindness'.","After the grand Americana of the late 1980s, U2 felt they had become a parody of themselves. In late 1990 they went to Hansa Studios in newly reunified Berlin, where Bowie had made Low, hoping to reinvent themselves amid the city's upheaval. The sessions went badly and the band nearly split, before the song 'One' emerged from an improvisation. Eno, Lanois and the engineer Flood finished the album in Dublin, with ears on industrial music and the Manchester dance-rock scene.","The title is a borrowed catchphrase, a German warning turned into a joke, and the whole record plays with irony and masks in a way the earnest band had never allowed itself. Listen to how many of the vocals are treated, doubled or pitched up: Bono is playing characters rather than preaching. 'One', the most direct song, was the piece that pulled the fractured band back together in Berlin.","Fans who feared a betrayal found instead a record that won over sceptical critics, topped the American chart and launched the Zoo TV tour, a satirical multimedia spectacle about television and celebrity. It is now often ranked above The Joshua Tree as U2's best work, and it is the rare case of a band at the peak of its fame successfully changing its sound.","Listen to 'Zoo Station' first, preferably straight after 'Where the Streets Have No Name', to hear how deliberately the band tore up its own template. Then give 'One' your full attention and notice how little happens musically; the song lives in the vocal. For the Berlin background, follow it with Bowie's Low."],"the-cure-pornography":["Eight songs of enormous, tom-tom-heavy drums, droning guitars and Robert Smith's voice pushed to the edge of screaming. 'One Hundred Years' opens the record with a grinding drum machine and a wall of flanged guitar; 'The Hanging Garden' pounds like a war dance; the title track ends everything in a murk of distorted voices and noise. There is almost no relief: the album is one sustained, claustrophobic mood of dread and self-disgust.","The Cure came from Crawley, a new town south of London, and by 1982 had made two increasingly bleak albums, Seventeen Seconds and Faith. Pornography, recorded with the producer Phil Thornalley, completed that trilogy at a time when Smith was, by his own later account, in a very dark state of mind. The band was fraying under touring and exhaustion; after a fight on the following tour, the bassist Simon Gallup left for a while.","Much of the album's weight comes from Lol Tolhurst's drums, which are recorded huge and roomy and often carry the melody's role while the guitars simply drone. Smith has said he expected Pornography to be the last Cure record, and the title track, stitched together with collaged voices, sounds like a deliberate ending. Its final moments, though, turn toward a wish to fight on rather than surrender.","Reviews at the time were often hostile, and the record's relentless gloom baffled some listeners. It nevertheless reached the British top ten. Over the following decades it was reassessed as a founding text of gothic rock and a direct influence on metal, industrial and shoegaze bands, and Smith himself later returned to it, performing it in full alongside the albums that followed.","Put on 'One Hundred Years' loud and let the drums and guitar wash over you rather than listening for a tune. Then compare it with Joy Division's Closer and Siouxsie's Juju, its closest cousins. If it feels too much, try 'The Hanging Garden', the album's single, which shows how danceable this darkness could be."],"the-cure-disintegration":["Twelve long songs that drift like weather. Each tends to open with minutes of instrumental layering, chiming guitar, slow bass melodies and banks of synthesiser strings, before Robert Smith's voice arrives. 'Plainsong' opens with wind chimes and a vast wash of keyboards; 'Pictures of You' circles for more than seven minutes; 'Lovesong' is a compact, ardent declaration; 'Lullaby' creeps along on plucked strings. The mood is melancholy and immersive rather than pounding.","By 1989 the Cure had become a huge international band on the strength of bright singles, and Smith, approaching thirty and unhappy with that success, set out to make a record as heavy in feeling as their early work. It was recorded at Hookend Manor in Oxfordshire with the producer David M. Allen. During the process the founding member Lol Tolhurst, whose contributions had dwindled, was dismissed. The record company reportedly feared commercial suicide.","'Lovesong', the most direct song here, was written as a wedding present for Mary Poole, Smith's long-time partner, and became the band's biggest American hit. Listen to how much melody Simon Gallup and Smith put in the bass registers, often using a six-string bass, so that the tunes sit low and the guitars and keyboards form a ceiling above them. The long introductions are the point, not padding.","The label's fears proved wrong: the album reached the British top three and sold millions worldwide, and 'Lovesong' reached number two in the United States. Critics have steadily raised its standing, and it now often appears near the top of lists of the decade's albums. Smith has called it the most complete Cure record, and the band still plays it in full on special occasions.","Give it a dark room and the full seventy minutes; it is designed as one mood. Start with 'Plainsong' and notice how long you wait for the voice. Then hear 'Pictures of You' as a study in layering, picking out each guitar and keyboard as it enters. Follow it with Cocteau Twins' Heaven or Las Vegas for a kindred dream."],"echo-and-the-bunnymen-heaven-up-here":["Eleven songs of taut, restless tension. Will Sergeant's guitar spirals and slashes rather than strumming, Les Pattinson's bass drives in long lines, and Pete de Freitas plays the drums like a jazz drummer with a grudge, all toms and cymbal washes. Over it Ian McCulloch croons and howls like a Liverpool Jim Morrison. 'A Promise', 'Over the Wall' and the title track share one atmosphere: grey, windswept and romantic, with no ballads to soften it.","Echo & the Bunnymen formed in Liverpool in 1978, named after the drum machine they used before de Freitas joined. Their debut, Crocodiles, had made them contenders; this second album, produced by Hugh Jones and released on Korova, a Warner offshoot, in 1981, was made while the band was growing in confidence and ambition. Liverpool's post-punk scene, centred on Eric's club, also produced the Teardrop Explodes, and a friendly rivalry pushed both bands on.","The sleeve, a photograph by Brian Griffin of the band silhouetted on a wet beach in South Wales at dusk, sets the album's visual mood so exactly that it is hard to hear the record without it. Listen to how rarely Sergeant plays a conventional chord: his guitar parts are mostly single lines and textures, which leaves the drums space to carry the drama.","Critics received it warmly, and it reached the British top ten, one of the first signs that dark, difficult post-punk could sell. Many long-time fans regard it as the band's most complete statement, less polished than the orchestral Ocean Rain that followed but more urgent. Its standing has grown alongside that of its contemporaries by the Cure and Joy Division.","Start with 'Over the Wall' and follow the way the song builds from quiet menace to full storm. Listen to the drums as much as the voice. Then compare it with the Doors' Strange Days to hear what McCulloch took from Morrison, and with the Cure's Pornography to hear the same era turning inward."],"echo-and-the-bunnymen-the-killing-moon":["A slow, swaying song that sounds like a romantic ballad sung at the edge of a cliff. Plucked acoustic and electric guitars trace a circling, almost Russian-sounding melody, the bass moves in grave steps, and strings swell underneath. Ian McCulloch sings about fate and surrender in a low croon that rises to a cry in the chorus. The mood is grand, fatalistic and strangely tender, and the arrangement never hurries.","Released as a single in January 1984, the song reached the British top ten and became the centrepiece of Ocean Rain, which the band promoted, with some tongue in cheek, as the greatest album ever made. Much of that record was recorded in Paris with a string section. By now Echo & the Bunnymen were at their peak in Britain, touring with elaborate lighting and a growing reputation as the most romantic of the post-punk bands.","McCulloch has said the song's opening words came to him in his sleep, and that he found the chord sequence by playing Bowie's 'Space Oddity' backwards, which is why the harmony feels both familiar and upside down. The balalaika-like tremolo in the guitars gives the song its faintly Russian colour, a deliberate touch of exoticism the band enjoyed.","The band has repeatedly called it their best song, and critics agree. Its reputation grew again in the 2000s, when it was used prominently in the film Donnie Darko and introduced to a new generation. It is regularly cited as one of the finest British singles of the decade, and it shows post-punk drifting toward lush, orchestral romanticism.","Listen first for the guitars, which play the melody before the voice ever does. Notice how the song keeps the same slow pulse throughout, letting the strings and McCulloch's rising voice supply all the drama. Then play Bowie's 'Space Oddity' to hear the ghost of its chords, and the album version of Ocean Rain for the whole orchestral setting."],"the-police-message-in-a-bottle":["A trio song that sounds bigger than three people. Andy Summers opens with a rippling guitar figure of wide-spaced notes that loops through the whole track; Stewart Copeland's drums skitter around it with hi-hat splashes and off-beat accents; Sting's bass locks in and his high, urgent voice tells the story of a castaway who sends a message and finds he is not alone. The chorus repeats the title as a chant until it becomes a mantra.","The Police were a London trio, an American drummer, a Newcastle bassist and a guitarist who had played in the 1960s, who rode the punk wave while playing with far more technique than punk allowed. The song opened their second album, Reggatta de Blanc, recorded cheaply at Surrey Sound with Nigel Gray, and became their first British number one in 1979. Their blend of rock and reggae was their signature.","The guitar riff looks simple but is awkward to play, stretching the fingers across the neck, and Summers has described how the part took shape in rehearsal. Listen to Copeland rather than Sting: his drumming never settles into a plain rock beat, and the constant little accents on the off-beats are the reggae inheritance that gives the song its tension. The song's story takes a quiet turn in the final verse.","It topped the British chart and remains one of the band's most admired songs among musicians, often cited as Sting's best lyric and a peak of the trio's interplay. Critics then and now have argued about how deeply the Police understood the reggae they borrowed, but the song itself is rarely attacked. It is a model of how to make a three-piece band sound like an orchestra.","Listen to the first thirty seconds for the guitar alone, then start again and follow only the drums. Notice how the song shifts from the verse's tension to the chorus's release without changing its pulse. Then play Bob Marley's Catch a Fire to hear the reggae the Police were translating into new wave."],"the-police-synchronicity":["The Police's final studio album splits into two halves. The first side is restless and experimental: the jittery title track, Andy Summers' queasy 'Mother', Copeland's 'Miss Gradenko', and the storming 'Synchronicity II'. The second side holds the hits: 'Every Breath You Take', built on a single repeating guitar figure; 'King of Pain'; 'Wrapped Around Your Finger'; and ends with the desolate, desert-hazy 'Tea in the Sahara'. Space and restraint define the sound.","By 1983 the Police were one of the biggest bands in the world and could barely stand each other. The album was recorded at George Martin's AIR studios on Montserrat with the producer Hugh Padgham, reportedly with the three musicians often playing in separate rooms. Sting was reading widely, and the title comes from Carl Jung's idea of meaningful coincidence. After the tour that followed, the band stopped working together.","'Every Breath You Take', often played at weddings, is really about obsessive, controlling surveillance, as Sting himself has pointed out. 'Tea in the Sahara' retells a story from Paul Bowles' novel The Sheltering Sky. And the guitar part in 'Every Breath You Take' was, by Summers' account, found late in the sessions, after the song had sat unfinished without a guitar identity.","It topped the American chart for many weeks and 'Every Breath You Take' became the biggest single of the year. Critics admired its ambition and polish, though some found the first side self-indulgent. Its status as a last word, a band splitting at its peak, has only added to its mystique, and it remains one of the decade's best-selling rock records.","Listen to 'Every Breath You Take' with the story in mind and notice how the gentle music makes the words more unsettling. Then try 'Tea in the Sahara' and listen to how little is played. For contrast, go back to 'Message in a Bottle' and hear how much tighter and more jubilant the band sounded four years earlier."],"dire-straits-sultans-of-swing":["A relaxed, rolling song that tells the story of a pub jazz band playing to an indifferent crowd on a rainy south London night. Mark Knopfler sings in a dry, half-spoken voice and answers each line with clean, fingerpicked Stratocaster phrases, no plectrum, that twist and cascade. The rhythm section is light and swinging. It climaxes in a long guitar solo that builds through fast runs to one of the most recognisable codas in rock.","Dire Straits formed in Deptford in 1977 when pub rock and punk ruled London clubs. Knopfler, a former journalist and teacher, recorded a demo of the song that was played on Charlie Gillett's BBC Radio London show, which led to a record deal. The single, first released in 1978, was slow to catch on in Britain but became a hit in the Netherlands and then America before returning home to the top ten.","Knopfler's sound comes from playing with his fingers rather than a pick, which makes each note snap and gives him the fluency you hear in the solo. The Stratocaster's in-between pickup setting gives that hollow, glassy tone. Listen to the verses: the song is an affectionate portrait of amateur musicians who play because they love it, a quiet rebuke to both punk's sneer and prog's bombast.","Its cool restraint was an odd fit for 1978, but it found audiences who were tired of both punk and progressive excess. Guitarists have treated its solo as a rite of passage ever since. Some critics wrote the band off as pub-rock traditionalists; the song's durability has largely answered them.","Follow the guitar answers between the vocal lines, then wait for the final solo and trace how it climbs. Compare the studio version with a live one, where the solo stretches out. Then play J. J. Cale or Bob Dylan to hear where Knopfler's laid-back voice and phrasing came from."],"dire-straits-brothers-in-arms":["Nine songs spanning smooth late-night ballads and a few big rockers. 'So Far Away' and 'Your Latest Trick' drift on soft keyboards and a saxophone; 'Money for Nothing' opens with a long synthesiser build before a crunching guitar riff; 'Walk of Life' bounces on a jaunty organ; the title song, closing the album, is a slow lament for soldiers with a keening, bent-note guitar solo. The production is spacious, clean and quiet.","Dire Straits had become a stadium band by 1985. The album was recorded at AIR studios on Montserrat with Mark Knopfler and Neil Dorfsman producing, on a digital multitrack machine, which was new. The compact disc had just arrived, and this record's silent backgrounds and long running time suited it perfectly; it became one of the first albums to sell a million copies on CD. The title song reflects on the recent Falklands War.","The high voice singing the chant behind 'Money for Nothing' is Sting, who had a co-writing credit because the melody echoes a Police song. Its computer-animated video, then startlingly new, became an MTV staple. Listen to how quietly the record is mixed: much of it is soft enough that the dynamic range, not loudness, is the effect, a deliberate showcase for the new digital medium.","It topped charts worldwide and became one of the best-selling albums in British history. Critics praised the craftsmanship but some found it too tasteful and bland. It is remembered as much as a technological landmark, the album that sold people CD players, as for its songs, and debate continues over whether it is middle-of-the-road or a subtle work of sound design.","Play the title song last and listen to the guitar as if it were a voice; each phrase is shaped like singing. Then try 'Money for Nothing' and note the long build-up before the riff. Hear it on a good CD or lossless master, where the quiet passages matter, and follow it with 'Sultans of Swing' to hear the band's modest beginnings."],"tom-petty-damn-the-torpedoes":["Nine tight, loud songs with the Heartbreakers playing as one machine. 'Refugee' opens with Benmont Tench's churchy organ and Mike Campbell's snarling guitar and builds to Tom Petty's defiant chorus; 'Here Comes My Girl' alternates spoken verses with a soaring chorus; 'Don't Do Me Like That' is a nimble pop hit; 'Even the Losers' is an anthem for underdogs. The Byrds' ringing guitars meet punk's energy and southern soul's organ.","Petty, from Gainesville, Florida, had moved to Los Angeles with his band and released two albums. When his contract was sold to MCA without his consent, he refused to record, fought the label in court and filed for bankruptcy to escape the deal. The album was made with the producer Jimmy Iovine at the end of that struggle, and its defiance reflects it. The title quotes a Civil War admiral's order.","Iovine and the engineer Shelly Yakus spent weeks chasing the drum sound, and Stan Lynch's snare on 'Refugee' is the result of many takes. Listen for Tench's organ, which underpins nearly every song; his church-and-soul keyboard style is the Heartbreakers' quiet signature. 'Refugee' was apparently written about the legal fight rather than about a person.","It reached number two in America and made Petty a star. Critics admired its blend of sixties craft and new wave directness; he was grouped with Springsteen and later with heartland rock, though he disliked the label. It is still considered one of the essential American rock albums of its time.","Play 'Refugee' loud and follow the organ under the guitars. Then 'Here Comes My Girl' for how the verse and chorus contrast. For lineage, go back to the Byrds' 'Mr. Tambourine Man' and forward to Petty's Full Moon Fever to hear the jangle refined."],"tom-petty-full-moon-fever":["Twelve compact, polished songs built on bright acoustic strumming, crisp drums and stacked harmonies. 'Free Fallin'' glides over a simple chiming guitar pattern; 'I Won't Back Down' is a steady, stubborn anthem; 'Runnin' Down a Dream' drives forward on a propulsive riff. There is a jaunty cover of the Byrds' 'I'll Feel a Whole Lot Better'. The sound is clean and warm, with each instrument sitting precisely in place.","Petty made his first solo album without most of the Heartbreakers, working with Jeff Lynne of the Electric Light Orchestra and Mike Campbell, partly in Campbell's home studio in Los Angeles. At the same time Petty and Lynne were members of the Traveling Wilburys with George Harrison, Bob Dylan and Roy Orbison, and that friendly spirit fed the record. MCA reportedly first turned it down, hearing no hits.","George Harrison plays and sings on 'I Won't Back Down', and Lynne's production, with its compressed acoustic guitars and tight, dry drums, is a deliberate homage to 1960s pop and to the Beatles in particular. 'Free Fallin'' was reportedly written in a single afternoon, as a joke to amuse Lynne, before it became Petty's signature song.","It became Petty's best-selling album and produced several of his most enduring songs. Some critics found Lynne's gloss too smooth, and the Heartbreakers' absence was felt, but the record's songwriting is widely admired, and 'I Won't Back Down' has become a much-used anthem of resilience.","Start with 'Free Fallin'' and listen to how the guitars sparkle; it's a production lesson in a pop song. Then compare 'I'll Feel a Whole Lot Better' with the Byrds' version. And play Damn the Torpedoes after, for how much rougher the full band sounded ten years before."],"the-replacements-let-it-be":["Eleven songs that swing between ramshackle punk and aching ballads. 'I Will Dare' opens with a bouncing, near-pop jangle; 'Unsatisfied' builds from acoustic strum to Paul Westerberg's raw, cracking howl; 'Androgynous' is a quiet piano waltz; 'Answering Machine' ends the album with Westerberg alone against a wash of guitar. There are joke songs and a scrappy cover of Kiss's 'Black Diamond' alongside. The playing is loose and the feeling is naked.","The Replacements came from Minneapolis, the same city as Hüsker Dü, and were known for drunken, chaotic shows. Their third album was made for the tiny local Twin/Tone label with the band's manager Peter Jesperson and the engineer Steve Fjelstad. The title, taken from the Beatles, was a cheeky dismissal of rock's sacred cows. Westerberg's songwriting was maturing fast, and the album announced him as a real writer.","The guitar solo on 'I Will Dare' is played by Peter Buck of R.E.M., who was a friend and fan. Listen to how much the album relies on Bob Stinson's lead guitar, which is wild and imprecise and makes the tender songs feel exposed. 'Androgynous' is an unusually gentle song for its time and place, a sympathetic sketch of a gender-nonconforming couple.","It was modestly received at release but became a college-radio touchstone and is now widely treated as one of the definitive American indie records of the 1980s. Critics praise its mixture of vulnerability and noise. The band never had great commercial success, and its legend rests on albums like this.","Start with 'Unsatisfied' and hear how the song grows from strumming into a full-throated plea. Then play 'Androgynous' and 'Answering Machine' for Westerberg's range. For context, follow with Hüsker Dü's New Day Rising and R.E.M.'s Murmur, their neighbours in 1980s college rock."],"the-replacements-alex-chilton":["A bright, driving, joyous rock song, built on a jangling guitar riff and a fast, steady beat, that pays homage to Big Star's singer and songwriter. Paul Westerberg imagines a world in which Chilton is famous and beloved, and sings his praise with delight. It is catchy and loose, with a chorus that shouts the name and harmonies that nod to the power pop Big Star practised.","It appeared on Pleased to Meet Me, the Replacements' second major-label album, recorded in Memphis at Ardent Studios with Jim Dickinson, who had produced Big Star's Third. By then Bob Stinson had left and the band was a trio. Chilton had become a cult hero to a generation of American indie musicians, and the song is a thank-you to him.","The recording location is part of the tribute: Ardent was where Big Star had made their records. Chilton himself is credited with guitar on another song on the album. Westerberg's words cleverly flip rock's idea of fame, imagining a world where a neglected cult figure receives the adoration usually given to stars.","It was a modest single but is now one of the band's best-known songs and a key document of how Big Star's reputation grew in the 1980s. Fans and critics treat it as a fond love letter; it helped send new listeners back to Big Star's albums.","Play it, then immediately play Big Star's 'September Gurls' to hear the source it celebrates. Listen for the guitars chiming like Big Star's, and the way Westerberg's voice cracks with pleasure on the chorus. Then go back to Let It Be, three years earlier, to hear how the band's ragged punk had grown into clean, confident power pop."],"simple-minds-new-gold-dream":["Nine songs of shimmering, pulsing, dreamlike rock. Mick MacNeil's keyboards form glittering layers, Derek Forbes' bass keeps up a melodic, propulsive line, and Charlie Burchill's guitar chimes and swirls rather than riffing. Jim Kerr sings in hushed, incantatory phrases. 'Someone Somewhere in Summertime', 'Promised You a Miracle' and 'Glittering Prize' are the hits; 'Hunter and the Hunted' features a synthesiser solo by Herbie Hancock.","Simple Minds came out of Glasgow's punk scene in the late 1970s and moved through art-rock and electronic experiments before this record. It was produced by Peter Walsh, and it was the band's breakthrough, a top-three album in Britain. It sits between the spiky European post-punk they had admired and the arena rock they soon embraced, which makes it a hinge between two eras.","Herbie Hancock's contribution came through a shared connection to the producer's circle and was, by the band's telling, recorded quickly and without much fuss. Listen to the bass on nearly every song: Forbes' lines are the real melodies, with Kerr's vocals floating over the top. The numbers in the title evoke a hope for the future rather than a literal chronology.","It was well received and has held its reputation as the band's most beautiful album, often preferred by critics to their later stadium work. It is cited as an influence on dream pop and on later bands seeking atmosphere without abandoning songs.","Begin with 'Someone Somewhere in Summertime' and follow the bass rather than the voice; it carries the tune. Then play 'Hunter and the Hunted' and wait for Hancock's solo, a jazz musician's shimmer inside a Glasgow rock band. Compare with U2's War, released the next year, to hear two bands heading toward the same arena from different directions."],"simple-minds-dont-you-forget-about-me":["A mid-tempo anthem with a booming drum beat, a strutting bass line and a chiming guitar. Jim Kerr sings of a relationship that might be forgotten and asks to be remembered, and the chorus opens up with a big, sing-along sweep. It ends with a wordless chant that audiences took up in stadiums. It is polished, radio-friendly and instantly memorable.","The song was written by the producer Keith Forsey and the guitarist Steve Schiff for John Hughes' film The Breakfast Club, a teen comedy-drama. Several artists turned it down before Simple Minds reluctantly agreed. It became their only American number one and made them international stars, though it was not written by the band and did not appear on their albums of the time.","The band's reluctance is audible if you know to listen: Kerr added his own ad-libs and that final wordless chant to make it theirs. Listen to how much of the arrangement comes from Forsey's production style, with its heavy drums and gated reverb. It shows how film soundtracks could reshape a band's career in the MTV era.","It is one of the most recognised songs of the decade and a staple of eighties nostalgia. Some longtime fans see it as a turning point where Simple Minds' art-rock gave way to stadium anthems, and the band had mixed feelings about it for years. It remains ubiquitous.","Listen for Kerr's ad-libs in the verses and the wordless chant at the end, the parts that made someone else's song his own. Then play New Gold Dream to hear how much the band's sound had changed in three years, from dreamy art-rock to arena punch. It is best of all heard over the film's final scene, where it belongs."],"inxs-kick":["Twelve tight, groove-heavy songs that fuse rock with funk and dance music. 'Need You Tonight' is almost nothing but a scratchy guitar riff, a drum-machine pulse and Michael Hutchence's close, purring vocal; 'Devil Inside' swaggers; 'New Sensation' rides a saxophone break; 'Never Tear Us Apart' is a slow, dramatic waltz with swelling strings and a sudden saxophone cry. The sound is lean, danceable and sensual.","INXS came from Sydney and built their following on relentless touring in Australia and America through the early 1980s. Kick was produced by Chris Thomas and written mostly by Andrew Farriss and Hutchence. The band's American label reportedly found it too uncommercial and wanted changes, but it became their breakthrough, producing four American top-ten hits. MTV made Hutchence a pin-up.","The riff for 'Need You Tonight' is said to have come to Andrew Farriss as he waited for a taxi, and he recorded a rough version immediately. Listen to how much space the song leaves: the minimal arrangement is what makes Hutchence's voice so intimate. 'Never Tear Us Apart' borrows a waltz time unusual for rock.","It sold millions and made INXS one of the biggest bands in the world. Critics praised the fusion of funk and rock; some found it slick. Its reputation has held, and 'Never Tear Us Apart' in particular is widely loved, especially in Australia, where it is almost a national anthem of feeling.","Start with 'Need You Tonight' and count how few elements it uses: one guitar riff, a drum pulse, a voice, and silence doing the rest. Then play 'Never Tear Us Apart' and wait for the saxophone's sudden cry after the pause. For lineage, play Prince's 'Kiss', a close cousin in minimal funk, and Roxy Music's Avalon for the suave glamour."],"midnight-oil-diesel-and-dust":["Ten songs of spacious, pounding rock with a political edge. 'Beds Are Burning' opens with punchy brass-like keyboards and a strutting groove; 'The Dead Heart' is built on a chant-like chorus; 'Put Down That Weapon' is darker and slower; 'Warakurna' and 'Sometimes' drive hard. Peter Garrett's lanky, jerky presence and forceful baritone dominate, and Rob Hirst's drums sit big and clear. The sound is tough but open, like the landscape it describes.","Midnight Oil came from Sydney's pub circuit, loud, political and energetic. In 1986 they toured remote Aboriginal communities in Central Australia with the Warumpi Band, an Aboriginal rock group, and the album grew from what they saw: displacement, poverty and the struggle for land rights. It was produced by Warne Livesey. Garrett later became a politician, serving as a federal minister.","'The Dead Heart' was written earlier for the handback of Uluru to its traditional owners, the Anangu, and features the idea of Aboriginal endurance long before European arrival. 'Beds Are Burning' concerns the return of land to the Pintupi people. Listen for the way Hirst's drums and the rhythm guitar suggest ceremonial rhythm without imitating Aboriginal music directly.","It became a major international success and brought Aboriginal land rights to a global audience. Some critics questioned whether a white band should carry the message, but the band was careful to point toward Aboriginal voices. It remains one of the most important Australian rock albums, and the band's performance at the 2000 Sydney Olympics closing ceremony, in clothes bearing the word 'Sorry', revived its message.","Start with 'Beds Are Burning' and feel how the groove drives the message. Then 'The Dead Heart' for the chant. For context, read about the Blackfella/Whitefella tour, and compare with U2's The Joshua Tree, another 1987 record about a vast landscape."],"the-jesus-and-mary-chain-psychocandy":["Fourteen short songs in which sweet, sixties girl-group melodies are buried under screeching guitar feedback. 'Just Like Honey' opens the record with a thudding, slow beat and a dreamy, sugary tune; 'Never Understand' and 'You Trip Me Up' swirl in shrieking distortion; 'Taste of Cindy' is a two-minute blast. Jim Reid sings in a flat, bored murmur. Underneath the noise, nearly every song is a simple, catchy pop tune.","The Reid brothers, Jim and William, came from East Kilbride near Glasgow. Their early gigs in London were short and chaotic, sometimes ending in riots, and they became notorious. Alan McGee signed them to his label Creation, then they moved to a Warner offshoot, Blanco y Negro. Bobby Gillespie, later of Primal Scream, played a stripped-down kit standing up. The album's mixture of pop and noise shocked listeners.","The drum pattern at the start of 'Just Like Honey' is a direct nod to the Ronettes' 'Be My Baby', produced by Phil Spector, whose 'wall of sound' the band was reimagining in feedback. Listen to how basic the rhythm section is: the drums are minimal, almost naive, so that all the richness comes from the guitar noise. Take the noise away and you are left with a girl-group record.","Critics immediately recognised its importance, and it made many best-of-year lists. It was divisive, loved for its audacity and derided as a gimmick, but it shaped much of the alternative rock that followed, especially shoegaze, and it is now treated as one of the essential British albums of the decade.","Start with 'Just Like Honey', then play the Ronettes' 'Be My Baby' and hear the resemblance. Next try 'Never Understand' and listen for the pop tune beneath the noise. Follow with My Bloody Valentine's Isn't Anything to hear where the idea went."],"cocteau-twins-treasure":["Ten songs, each named after a person or mythic figure, where the sound is more important than the words. Robin Guthrie's guitar shimmers through chorus and echo, layering into vast, glittering washes; Simon Raymonde's bass moves underneath in melodic lines; a drum machine ticks softly. Over it Elizabeth Fraser sings in ecstatic, swooping, often wordless syllables, overdubbed into choirs of her own voice. 'Lorelei', 'Ivo' and 'Persephone' are highlights.","Cocteau Twins came from Grangemouth, a Scottish industrial town, and signed to 4AD, Ivo Watts-Russell's label whose ethereal aesthetic they helped define. Treasure was their third album and the first with Raymonde as a full member. They made it largely themselves, in a time when producers ruled. Their sound seemed to come from nowhere, unlike anything else in British rock.","Fraser often sang not in English but in invented syllables or words chosen for their sound; she described them as sounds rather than meanings. The track titles are names of people, including one nod to the label boss, Ivo. The band, especially Guthrie, has spoken dismissively of the album since, which surprises fans who love it.","It divided critics at the time, who found it either ravishing or precious, and some mocked the invented words as affectation. It reached the British top thirty all the same. It has since become a landmark of dream pop and an inspiration to shoegaze bands, ambient musicians and film composers, and its reputation has grown steadily, whatever the band itself thinks of it.","Stop trying to understand the words and listen to Fraser's voice as another instrument. Start with 'Lorelei' and hear how the layers build. Then play Brian Eno's Ambient 2 with Harold Budd, with whom Cocteau Twins later collaborated, to hear the connection."],"cocteau-twins-heaven-or-las-vegas":["Ten songs, brighter and more tuneful than anything the band had made before. 'Cherry-coloured Funk' drifts on warm guitar haze; 'Iceblink Luck' is almost a pop song, bouncing along; the title track swells with a gorgeous, rising chorus; 'Fifty-fifty Clown' shimmers. Elizabeth Fraser's voice, still a swooping instrument, is clearer and more intelligible than before, and the rhythm section is firmer, giving the dream a pulse.","It was the band's last album for 4AD. Fraser and Robin Guthrie, partners, had just had a daughter, and the record's lighter mood reflects that; at the same time the band's life was strained, with Guthrie struggling with drug problems. They recorded at their own studio in London. The album reached the British top ten, their best result.","Many of the songs are about Fraser's new daughter and the complicated joys around her, although the lyrics remain oblique. Listen to the bass: Simon Raymonde's melodic lines are more prominent here, anchoring the dreaminess and giving the songs a pop shape. The band made it with more discipline than ever before.","It was well received and has grown into the band's most loved album, often named one of the best records of 1990. Its influence runs through later dream pop and indie. It is often the first Cocteau Twins record new listeners hear.","Start with 'Cherry-coloured Funk' and let the guitar wash over you, then listen for the moment the bass gives it shape. Play the title track for its rising chorus. Compare with Treasure to hear the band's move toward pop, with the Cure's Disintegration for a darker kin, and with My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for where the haze went next."],"the-smiths-hand-in-glove":["A fast, urgent song that fades in out of nowhere on a wailing harmonica and a churning, ringing guitar figure. Johnny Marr's guitar chimes rather than crunches, Andy Rourke's bass runs in busy melodic lines, and Mike Joyce drives the drums hard. Morrissey sings of a secret, defiant love between two outsiders who expect the world to be against them, and suspects it will end badly anyway. It is raw and almost breathless, and it fades out as abruptly as it arrived.","It was the Smiths' debut single, released on Rough Trade in May 1983, about a year after Marr knocked on Morrissey's door in Stretford, Manchester, and proposed they write songs together. British guitar music was then being eclipsed by synthesisers and glossy videos, and the Smiths deliberately set themselves against both. The single did not reach the national chart, but it made them favourites of the independent scene and of John Peel, whose radio sessions spread the word.","The sleeve, a photograph of a naked man seen from behind, announced the band's sensibility before a note was heard, and it set the pattern of borrowed images of cult figures that ran through all their sleeves. The song was later re-recorded with the 1960s singer Sandie Shaw, one of Morrissey's heroes, backed by the Smiths, and that version reached the charts. The version on the band's debut album is a different mix.","Critics singled it out at once, and it quickly became a fan favourite and a live staple. Its failure to chart has since become part of the legend: the first step in a run of singles that many regard as the finest by any British band of the decade. It is now heard as the moment British indie found its own voice, literate, romantic and proudly un-macho.","Listen to how the song fades in, as if you had walked into a room where the band was already playing, and how the harmonica sets a mood of early-sixties Merseybeat. Then play 'This Charming Man', released a few months later, to hear how quickly the band sharpened its sound. For contrast, look up Sandie Shaw's version with the Smiths behind her."],"the-smiths-panic":["A short, stomping glam-rock song, barely over two minutes long. Johnny Marr's guitar crunches a boogie riff, the drums march in a heavy, even thump, and the band plays with a swagger unusual for them. Morrissey imagines panic spreading through the streets of British towns and cities, and blames the radio disc jockeys who play music that says nothing about ordinary lives. The song builds to an ending in which a chorus of children chants the refrain over and over.","Released in July 1986, a month after The Queen Is Dead and with the band at the height of its British fame, it reached number eleven. Marr and Morrissey reportedly conceived it after hearing a radio presenter follow a bulletin about the Chernobyl disaster with a cheerful pop song, which struck them as absurd. Craig Gannon had just joined as a second guitarist, briefly making the Smiths a five-piece.","Marr has acknowledged that the riff borrows from T. Rex's 'Metal Guru', a playful homage to the glam rock of the early 1970s that he and Morrissey had grown up on. Listen for the children's voices at the end, which turn the song's angry demand into something like a playground chant, both funnier and more unsettling than the adult voice alone.","Some critics accused the song of hostility toward black dance music, a charge the band denied; the argument has never quite gone away, and the song is now read in the light of Morrissey's later provocations. Others hear only an attack on bland daytime radio. Musically it is one of the band's most direct and catchy singles, and it remains a favourite in indie discos.","Notice how short it is and how much it packs in: riff, story and chant in little more than two minutes. Then play T. Rex's 'Metal Guru' and hear the borrowing. Finally set it beside 'How Soon Is Now?', from two years earlier, to hear how wide the Smiths' range was, from hypnotic drone to glam stomp."],"xtc-making-plans-for-nigel":["A jittery, angular new wave song built on a drum pattern that circles the tom-toms and never settles into a plain rock beat. The guitars stab short, chiming chords into the gaps, and the bass bounces underneath. Colin Moulding sings, in a nasal, deadpan voice, as a pair of parents who have their son's future all worked out, a steady job at British Steel, and insist he is happy about it. The tune is catchy; the mood is quietly suffocating.","XTC came from Swindon, a railway town west of London, and had made two frantic, keyboard-led albums before the organist left and the guitarist Dave Gregory joined. The song, written by Moulding, opened the side of Drums and Wires in 1979 and reached the British top twenty, their first real hit. It was produced by Steve Lillywhite with the engineer Hugh Padgham at the Town House in London, where both were experimenting with big, dry, powerful drum sounds.","The drum part is the song's secret: Terry Chambers plays the rhythm around the toms and avoids the usual snare hits, so the song feels as if it is holding its breath. The work on this album's drum sound fed into techniques Padgham soon made famous with Peter Gabriel and Phil Collins. The British Steel reference was sharply topical; the nationalised industry was heading into a strike and mass redundancies.","It gave XTC their commercial breakthrough and remains their best-known song, though it was written by the band's second songwriter rather than Andy Partridge. Critics praised its originality and its satire of a society that decides young people's lives for them. It is regularly listed among the essential British new wave singles, and cover versions have kept it in circulation.","Listen to the drums first; they are the engine, and once you hear that they avoid the snare you cannot unhear it. Then follow the guitars, which answer each other in short bursts. Compare with Wire's Chairs Missing, a cousin in angularity, and then jump ahead to XTC's Skylarking to hear how far the same band travelled in seven years."],"xtc-skylarking":["A song cycle that follows a summer day, the turning year and a human life, from waking through love, marriage, work, ageing and death. The songs flow into one another without gaps. 'Summer's Cauldron' opens with insect buzz and heavy heat; 'Grass' is lazy and sensual; '1000 Umbrellas' is set against a mournful string arrangement; 'Season Cycle' nods openly to the Beach Boys; 'Earn Enough for Us' is bright, anxious pop. The whole sound is pastoral, lush and very English.","XTC had stopped touring in 1982 when Andy Partridge suffered crippling stage fright, and became a studio band. Their sales were slipping, and Virgin insisted they work with the American producer Todd Rundgren, who proposed arranging the songs as a cycle and recorded them at his Utopia Sound studio in Woodstock, New York, and in San Francisco. The sessions were tense: Partridge and Rundgren clashed constantly over control of the sound.","'Dear God', a song addressing a deity the singer doubts exists, was left off the original album and released as a B-side; American college radio adopted it, it became a hit, and it was added to later editions, which changes the cycle's shape. Listen to how Rundgren's running order carries the album from morning to night and from birth to the final bonfire of 'Sacrificial Bonfire'.","Its first reception was respectful rather than ecstatic, and 'Dear God' drew protests in parts of America. Over time it has become the XTC album most often named as their best, and Partridge, despite his fights with Rundgren, came to speak more warmly of it. A later reissue corrected a phase error in the original mastering, which had thinned the sound for years.","Listen straight through, in order, as a cycle rather than a set of songs. Start with 'Summer's Cauldron' and let the heat and insects set the scene. Then play the Beach Boys' Pet Sounds and the Kinks' Village Green Preservation Society to hear its two great ancestors, and seek out the corrected reissue for the fullest sound."],"big-star-third-sister-lovers":["A fragile, disordered album of broken ballads, sour rockers and eerie orchestral pieces. 'Kanga Roo' drifts in a haze of feedback and mellotron; 'Holocaust' is desolate piano and voice; 'Thank You Friends' swells with a mock-gospel chorus; 'Stroke It Noel' has strings; 'Jesus Christ' is a strange, bright carol. There are covers of the Velvet Underground's 'Femme Fatale' and the Kinks' 'Till the End of the Day'. The mood is private and unravelling.","Big Star, from Memphis, had made two albums that sold poorly because of distribution failures. By 1974 only Alex Chilton and the drummer Jody Stephens remained. They recorded with the producer Jim Dickinson at Ardent Studios, with strings arranged by Carl Marsh, but no label would take the result and it was shelved, finally appearing in 1978. The subtitle refers to the sisters Chilton and Stephens were then dating.","There is no definitive track listing: different editions order the songs differently, because the band never finalised the album, and each order tells a slightly different story. Listen for how Dickinson let Chilton's disorder into the sound rather than hiding it; many songs sound deliberately unfinished, with false starts, odd balances and instruments that drop in and out as if the room were falling apart.","Ignored on its belated release, it became a cult record and then a canonical one, cherished by R.E.M., the Replacements and a whole generation of American and British indie musicians. This Mortal Coil covered 'Kanga Roo' and 'Holocaust' on 4AD. It is now routinely ranked among rock's great lost albums, though some still hear it as a document of collapse rather than a finished work.","Start with 'Kanga Roo' and 'Holocaust' to hear the album at its most haunted, then 'Thank You Friends' for its crooked joy. Play the Velvet Underground's 'Femme Fatale' next to Chilton's cover. For the afterlife, follow with the Replacements' 'Alex Chilton', recorded in the same Memphis studio with the same producer, and Nick Drake's Pink Moon, its closest cousin in solitude."],"big-star-september-gurls":["Under three minutes of chiming, bittersweet power pop. Layered guitars, including a ringing twelve-string, open with a bright, descending figure; the bass moves melodically beneath; Jody Stephens' drums are crisp and light. Alex Chilton's high, slightly strained voice sings of longing for someone who has let him down, the girls of the title both desired and out of reach. Harmonies swell into a chorus that feels joyful and sad at the same moment, and then it is over.","The song appeared on Big Star's second album, Radio City, released in 1974 on the Memphis label Ardent, which depended on Stax for distribution just as Stax was collapsing. Critics loved the record, but it was almost impossible to find in shops. Chris Bell had left after the first album, and Chilton led a trio. Their British-influenced pop was out of step with both Memphis soul and the heavy rock of the day.","The Bangles covered it in 1986, which brought Chilton royalties and pointed a new audience back to the original. Listen to how the guitar parts overlap to create the chime: no single guitar plays the whole figure, a layering trick learned from the Byrds and the Beatles. The words are more wounded than the sunny sound suggests.","It was not a hit, but it is now routinely named one of the greatest power pop songs ever recorded and a touchstone for indie and alternative bands from R.E.M. onward. Its obscurity on release has become part of its legend, the prime example of a record that failed commercially and won the argument over time.","Play it loud and listen to the chime of the guitars in the first seconds, then to the harmonies in the chorus. Compare it with the Byrds' 'Mr. Tambourine Man' to hear where the jangle came from. For its afterlife, go to the Bangles' cover and the Replacements' 'Alex Chilton', which celebrates its writer by name."],"minutemen-double-nickels-on-the-dime":["Forty-five short songs across four sides, most of them under two minutes. D. Boon's trebly, scratchy guitar, Mike Watt's busy, melodic bass and George Hurley's jazzy, skittering drums interlock like a funk band playing punk at speed. Songs range from political rants to jokes, spoken poems and diary entries, with covers of Creedence Clearwater Revival, Steely Dan and Van Halen. 'History Lesson – Part II' is a tender, plain-spoken account of the band's own story.","The Minutemen came from San Pedro, a working port town in Los Angeles, and were a core band of SST Records and the West Coast hardcore scene. The album was recorded cheaply and quickly in 1984 and released as a double, at the same moment as their label-mates Hüsker Dü's double Zen Arcade; the two records were a friendly competition in ambition. D. Boon died in a van accident in December 1985, ending the band at its peak.","The title mocks Sammy Hagar's song complaining about the fifty-five mile an hour speed limit: the Minutemen's joke was that they would drive at exactly fifty-five, on the dime. The sides are named after each member, with the fourth made of leftovers. 'History Lesson – Part II' later gave a well-known history of 1980s American indie rock its title.","It was admired on release and is now one of the most celebrated American indie albums, often ranked with Zen Arcade as the peak of the SST era. Critics treat it as proof that punk could absorb jazz, funk, folk and poetry without losing its urgency, and its do-it-yourself ethic, jamming econo in the band's phrase, became a creed for a generation.","Do not try to take in all forty-five songs at once. Start with 'History Lesson – Part II' and 'Corona', then 'Viet Nam' and 'This Ain't No Picnic'. Listen to Watt's bass, which carries the melodies while the guitar comments. Then compare with Hüsker Dü's Zen Arcade and Gang of Four's Entertainment!, whose funk-punk the Minutemen admired."],"meat-puppets-meat-puppets-ii":["A short album that mixes punk energy with country twang, psychedelic guitar and desert haze. Curt Kirkwood's guitar picks and bends like a cowboy's, his voice wavers slightly off key, and his brother Cris's bass and Derrick Bostrom's drums keep things loose and rolling. 'Lake of Fire' is eerie and gleeful; 'Plateau' is a sparse, puzzling ballad about a mysterious mesa; 'Aurora Borealis' is a dreamy instrumental. It sounds like sunstroke set to music.","The Meat Puppets came from Phoenix, Arizona, and their first album on SST was a blur of screamed hardcore. For this second record, recorded in 1983 and held back until 1984, they turned toward the country music and psychedelia they had grown up on, confusing the punk audiences who had embraced them. The desert landscapes of Arizona, far from the urban scenes of Los Angeles or New York, seep into every song.","Kurt Cobain loved this album: Nirvana performed 'Plateau', 'Oh Me' and 'Lake of Fire' with the Kirkwood brothers on MTV Unplugged in 1993, and millions heard these songs first in that form. Listen to the guitar's country licks and bends, a sound almost no punk band dared at the time, and to how often a song simply stops rather than resolving.","Initially overlooked outside the punk underground, it is now seen as a key record in the development of both alternative rock and alt-country, a bridge between hardcore and Americana years before either term was common. Nirvana's covers gave it a second life, and the band's reputation rests largely on this album and its successor, Up on the Sun.","Start with 'Plateau', then 'Lake of Fire', and then listen to Nirvana's MTV Unplugged versions to hear how faithfully Cobain treated them. For the roots, play Hank Williams and the Byrds' Sweetheart of the Rodeo. For the peers, set it beside Minutemen's Double Nickels on the Dime, released the same year on the same label."],"the-stone-roses-the-stone-roses":["Eleven songs that marry the chiming guitars of sixties West Coast pop to a loose, rolling drum groove. 'I Wanna Be Adored' fades in from silence on a single bass line before John Squire's guitar arrives; 'She Bangs the Drums' and 'Made of Stone' are bright, sunlit pop; 'Waterfall' is followed by 'Don't Stop', the same track played backwards with new vocals. The record closes with 'I Am the Resurrection', whose final four minutes are an instrumental jam that speeds and swells.","Recorded in 1988 and early 1989 in London and Wales with producer John Leckie for Silvertone, a small offshoot of the Zomba group, the album arrived as acid house was transforming Manchester's Haçienda club and British youth culture. Ian Brown, John Squire, Mani and Reni had been gigging since the mid-eighties. Their swagger, baggy flares and huge outdoor show at Spike Island in May 1990 made them the figureheads of what the press called Madchester.","Listen to the drummer. Reni plays with a swing and a lightness closer to a funk or jazz player than to indie rock, and most of the groove lives in his hi-hat and ghost notes rather than the guitars. Squire's cover painting, splashed with lemons in the manner of Jackson Pollock, refers to the Paris student riots of May 1968, when protesters used lemons against tear gas; 'Bye Bye Badman' takes up the same subject.","It sold slowly at first and reviews were warm rather than ecstatic; its reputation grew for years, and by the late nineties British readers' and critics' polls regularly placed it at or near the top of all-time lists. The band's legal battle to leave Silvertone froze them for five years, and the delayed follow-up, Second Coming (1994), disappointed many. Some hear a thin, slightly dated production; others hear the template for Oasis and all of Britpop.","Start with 'I Wanna Be Adored' and wait for the full band to arrive, then jump to 'I Am the Resurrection' and ride the long coda. Listen to the bass and drums together as you would to a soul rhythm section. Afterwards try the Byrds' Mr. Tambourine Man to hear where the chiming guitars came from, and Oasis's Definitely Maybe to hear who learned from them."],"the-stone-roses-fools-gold":["Nearly ten minutes of hypnotic groove. A looped funk drum break and Reni's own shuffling kit lock together under a deep, circling bass line from Mani; John Squire's guitar scratches and squeals through a wah-wah pedal, sometimes rhythmic, sometimes melting into feedback. Ian Brown half-sings, half-whispers a story about greed and treasure-hunters who turn on each other. There is hardly a chorus; the track is built to be danced to, not sung along with.","Released in November 1989 as a double A-side with 'What the World Is Waiting For', it was the moment the Stone Roses turned fully toward the dance floor of the Haçienda and the warehouse raves of that year. The same week they appeared on Top of the Pops alongside Happy Mondays, a broadcast now remembered as Madchester's coronation on national television. It became their biggest hit to date and was added to the American edition of their debut album.","The drum loop underneath is widely identified as a break from a James Brown-circle funk record by Bobby Byrd, the same well of breakbeats hip-hop producers were mining at the time. Notice that the live drums play slightly around the loop rather than exactly on it, which gives the groove its lazy sway. The lyric's greedy prospectors borrow the plot of the film The Treasure of the Sierra Madre.","At the time some rock writers heard it as an indie band chasing a club trend; within a year it was clear it had helped dissolve the wall between guitar music and dance music in Britain. It is often called the Stone Roses' finest recording, and the long version rather than the radio edit is now the one people mean. Debate continues over whether it pointed to a future the band never followed.","Play the full ten-minute version loud and focus first on the bass, which barely changes, then on the guitar, which never repeats itself exactly. Compare it with James Brown's Funky Drummer to hear the breakbeat tradition it borrows from, and with Primal Scream's 'Loaded' to hear where the idea went next."],"happy-mondays-pills-n-thrills-and-bellyaches":["Ten tracks of loose-limbed funk-rock and house rhythm, with Shaun Ryder's slurred, sardonic vocals sitting over grooves that owe as much to seventies soul as to the Haçienda. 'Kinky Afro' opens with a lazy funk strut; 'Step On', a cover of John Kongos's 1971 song 'He's Gonna Step On You Again', rides a piano riff and hand percussion; 'God's Cop' and 'Loose Fit' are thick, swaying club records. Rowetta's soaring backing vocals lift several choruses.","Made in London in 1990 with the dance producers Paul Oakenfold and Steve Osborne, who had remixed the band's earlier singles for club play, the album was released on Tony Wilson's Factory Records at the height of Madchester. The Mondays were a gang of Salford friends, including a dancer, Bez, whose only instrument was maracas. Their chaotic lifestyle became tabloid news, and within two years the sessions for their next album helped push Factory itself into bankruptcy.","Shaun Ryder's lyrics are collages: half-remembered film lines, nursery rhymes, pub talk and street slang stitched together so that the sense slides away just as it forms. 'God's Cop' was aimed at James Anderton, the Greater Manchester chief constable who claimed to speak with God and campaigned against the club scene. Listen for how far back in the mix the guitars sit; Oakenfold and Osborne built the record around bass and percussion, as on a dance twelve-inch.","It was the band's commercial peak, reaching the UK top five, and critics praised it as the most successful fusion yet of indie rock and rave. Ryder was hailed by some as a street poet in the line of Mark E. Smith, dismissed by others as a lucky chancer. The film 24 Hour Party People (2002) made the Mondays folklore, and the record's reputation has held up better than its makers' conduct.","Begin with 'Step On' and 'Kinky Afro', the two hits, and notice how the grooves never hurry. Listen to Ryder less for meaning than for rhythm and character, like a rapper. Then put on the Stone Roses' 'Fools Gold' and New Order's Technique to hear the rest of the Manchester dance-rock conversation of 1989 and 1990."],"oasis-definitely-maybe":["Eleven songs of loud, layered guitars and huge, simple choruses. 'Rock 'n' Roll Star' opens with a wall of overdubbed guitar and Liam Gallagher's nasal, drawn-out vowels; 'Supersonic' lopes along at mid tempo; 'Cigarettes & Alcohol' borrows a stomping glam-rock riff; 'Slide Away' is a long, aching love song. The sound is thick and compressed, every instrument pushed to the front, with Noel Gallagher's guitar solos soaring over the top.","Oasis were a Manchester band from the council estates of Burnage, signed by Alan McGee to Creation Records after a now-legendary support slot in Glasgow in 1993. Early sessions sounded thin and were abandoned; the album was finally rescued by the engineer Owen Morris, who mixed it hot and loud. Released in August 1994, it became the fastest-selling debut album in British history to that point, arriving just as grunge was fading.","The record's riffs openly advertise their sources: 'Cigarettes & Alcohol' is built on the same boogie as T. Rex's 'Get It On', and 'Shakermaker' borrowed a melody from a famous soft-drink advertising song, which led to a settlement. Listen for the sheer number of guitar overdubs Morris stacked on 'Columbia' and 'Rock 'n' Roll Star', which is why the sound feels like a single roaring mass rather than separate instruments.","Critics loved its confidence and its refusal of grunge's gloom; some already grumbled about borrowed tunes and lyrics that rhymed for the sake of it. It is still, for many, Oasis's best album, rawer and hungrier than its famous successor. The argument over whether Noel Gallagher was a great songwriter or a great magpie of the Beatles and T. Rex has never ended.","Play 'Live Forever' first, then 'Slide Away', and notice how the songs build by adding guitars rather than changing chords. Hear the Stone Roses' debut beforehand to understand the Manchester lineage, and the Beatles' Revolver for the melodic model. Turn it up; it was mixed to be heard loud."],"oasis-live-forever":["A mid-tempo anthem of about four and a half minutes. It opens on a plain, thudding drum beat, then Liam Gallagher sings a slow-climbing melody about the wish to outlast ordinary life and the refusal to give in. The chorus lifts on massed guitars, and Noel Gallagher's long, singing guitar solo, bent and sustained, carries the second half to its close. It is the simplest possible arrangement, built on a handful of chords.","Noel Gallagher wrote it in Manchester around 1991, while working as a storeman for British Gas, and has said it was an answer to the self-loathing of American grunge, especially Nirvana. Released as the third single from Definitely Maybe in August 1994, it became Oasis's first UK top-ten hit. Its sleeve photograph shows the Liverpool house where John Lennon grew up, a deliberate statement of lineage.","Listen to how little the drums do: a flat, almost mechanical beat that lets the melody breathe. The melody itself repeatedly rises then falls back, never quite settling, which gives the chorus its yearning quality. The song's optimism is argued rather than naive; it grew out of Gallagher's reaction to hearing grunge's despair on the radio, and it answers that mood point by point without ever naming it.","Widely regarded as one of Oasis's finest songs and frequently named among the greatest British singles of the decade, it is often cited as the moment Britpop's mood of confidence overtook grunge's. Some critics found its sentiment adolescent; its defenders answer that adolescence is the point. It became a funeral and memorial favourite in Britain, a use its writer did not foresee.","Listen straight through to the guitar solo and notice how it sings the melody rather than showing off. Then play Nirvana's 'Smells Like Teen Spirit' to hear the mood it was written against, and the Beatles' 'Strawberry Fields Forever' to hear the tradition of northern English longing it joins."],"oasis-whats-the-story-morning-glory":["Twelve tracks, slower and broader than the debut, built for stadium singalongs. 'Wonderwall' strums on acoustic guitar and cello; 'Don't Look Back in Anger', sung by Noel Gallagher, opens on a piano figure and climbs to a massive chorus; 'Some Might Say' and 'Roll with It' are brisk guitar rock; the closing 'Champagne Supernova' stretches past seven minutes, with Paul Weller adding guitar, and ends in a haze of feedback and waves.","Recorded quickly at Rockfield Studios in Wales in spring 1995 with Owen Morris and Noel Gallagher producing, and a new drummer, Alan White, the album came out in October 1995, weeks after Oasis lost the so-called Battle of Britpop, a chart race between their single 'Roll with It' and Blur's 'Country House'. Its sales dwarfed Blur's, and in August 1996 Oasis played to about 250,000 people over two nights at Knebworth.","The piano introduction of 'Don't Look Back in Anger' deliberately recalls John Lennon's 'Imagine', and the song's lyric salutes Lennon too. Listen at the very end of 'Champagne Supernova' for the rushing sound of sea surf, which frames the album with the fragments of it that appear between tracks. The cover photograph was taken in Berwick Street, Soho, a record-shop street, just after dawn.","British reviews in 1995 were mixed, several finding it bloated and lazy after the debut; the public disagreed, and it became one of the best-selling albums in British history. Its reputation among critics rose steadily after that. It is now seen as the high-water mark of Britpop's commercial power, and sometimes as the moment the movement tipped into complacency.","Begin with 'Don't Look Back in Anger' and 'Wonderwall', then hear 'Champagne Supernova' as a slow psychedelic ending in the manner of late Beatles. Notice how many songs rest on four chords and a chorus that invites a crowd to sing. Compare Blur's Parklife from the year before for the other side of the argument."],"blur-modern-life-is-rubbish":["Fifteen songs of sharply observed English suburban life, with music-hall melodies, jangling and scratchy guitars, brass and harpsichord touches. 'For Tomorrow' opens with a swelling singalong about a couple in a grey London; 'Chemical World' and 'Sunday Sunday' sketch commuters and family routines; 'Colin Zeal' portrays a punctual office worker. Short instrumental interludes break up the running order. Damon Albarn sings in a deliberately London, unglamorous accent.","Blur returned from a miserable American tour in 1992 in debt, sick of grunge and determined to write about England. Albarn, Graham Coxon, Alex James and Dave Rowntree recorded mostly with producer Stephen Street for Food Records; the label reportedly thought the first version lacked singles, and 'For Tomorrow' and 'Chemical World' were added. Released in May 1993, it was a deliberate manifesto, and with Suede's debut it is often named the starting gun of Britpop.","Listen for Coxon's guitar parts, which scrape, squeal and play odd counter-melodies against the tunes, pulling the songs away from pastiche. The cover painting of a steam locomotive, the Mallard, was chosen as an image of a lost national pride. The album's models are English: the Kinks, Madness, the Jam and the music hall, rather than any American rock.","It sold modestly and reviews were respectful rather than excited, but it rescued the band's career and set up everything that followed. Later critics often prefer it to Parklife for its bitterness and oddity. The charge of nostalgic little-Englandism, which dogged Britpop throughout, was first made against this record, and it is still debated whether the album mocks or celebrates the nation it describes.","Start with 'For Tomorrow', then 'Chemical World' and 'Colin Zeal', and listen as you might to a set of short stories. Keep an ear on the guitar rather than the tune. Hear the Kinks' Village Green Preservation Society alongside it to hear the lineage, and then Parklife to hear the idea turned into hits."],"blur-parklife":["Sixteen tracks that tour English life like a variety show. 'Girls & Boys' is a disco-pop song on a pumping synthesizer bass about package-holiday hedonism; the title track has the actor Phil Daniels narrating a day in the park in a cockney voice over a brass-band bounce; 'End of a Century' is a wistful waltz; 'To the End' is a lush French-style ballad; 'This Is a Low' closes with a slow, grand guitar song built on shipping-forecast place names.","Recorded in London in late 1993 with Stephen Street, and released by Food in April 1994, the album arrived as Britain was ready to hear itself described. Its huge success, with four Brit Awards in 1995, made Blur household names and gave the press its word, Britpop. Phil Daniels had starred in Quadrophenia, the 1979 film of the Who's mod opera, and his presence tied the record to an earlier English youth culture.","'This Is a Low' borrows the names of sea areas from the BBC shipping forecast, a radio ritual heard by millions, and turns them into a map of the country. On 'To the End', Laetitia Sadier of the Anglo-French band Stereolab sings in French alongside Albarn. The greyhound on the cover points to the working-class leisure the album both loves and satirises, and many listeners still cannot decide which.","Critics and public were almost unanimous in 1994, and it is often named the defining Britpop album. Later judgment has been cooler about the novelty songs and the mockney accent, and Albarn himself distanced the band from Britpop within three years. Yet 'This Is a Low' and 'End of a Century' are now heard as some of the finest songs of the decade, and the record's range remains impressive.","Play 'Girls & Boys', then jump to 'End of a Century' and 'This Is a Low' to hear the band's range from satire to tenderness. Notice the small arrangement touches: brass, harpsichord, electronic bleeps. Compare Pulp's Different Class for a sharper take on the same country, and Oasis for the opposite temperament."],"blur-song-2":["Two minutes and two seconds of stop-start noise. A clattering drum beat and quiet, slack verses alternate with sudden explosions of heavily distorted guitar and bass, and a wordless whooping shout that crowds instantly pick up. There are only a few lines of nonsense lyric about feeling heavy and dizzy. The quiet-loud contrast is so extreme that it sounds like a band switching a wall of amplifiers on and off.","Released in April 1997 from the album Blur, recorded partly in Reykjavík, it came from the band's deliberate turn away from Britpop toward American lo-fi and indie rock, championed especially by Graham Coxon, who loved Pavement. The joke was that a band tired of Britain's charts made a grunge parody; it became their biggest hit in the United States and a staple of sports arenas, advertisements and video games.","The title was a working name: it was simply the second song on the demo list, and nobody bothered to change it. Listen to the verses: the drums and bass are recorded in a small, dry room, which makes the choruses feel even bigger when the distortion arrives. The shape is a compressed parody of the quiet-verse, loud-chorus design Nirvana and the Pixies had made famous.","Intended partly as a joke, it was taken straight by American radio, which made the joke on the band's critics complicated. British reviewers saw the album as a brave reinvention and a clean break from Britpop. The song is now so ubiquitous in stadiums that it can be hard to hear as music, but its economy remains a lesson in how much energy can fit in two minutes.","Listen once for the whoop, then again for the quiet parts, and notice how tight and restrained they are. Play the Pixies' 'Where Is My Mind?' and Nirvana's 'Smells Like Teen Spirit' before it to hear the loud-quiet model, then Parklife to measure how far Blur had travelled in three years."],"pulp-different-class":["Twelve songs of sex, class and small-town escape, sung by Jarvis Cocker in a voice that moves from breathy whisper to yelp. 'Mis-Shapes' is a rallying call for the misfits; 'Common People' builds from a keyboard pulse to a frantic climax; 'Disco 2000' is a bouncing pop song about a childhood crush; 'I Spy' is a melodrama of revenge with swooping strings; 'Something Changed' is a gentle love song. Candida Doyle's cheap-sounding synths and Russell Senior's violin give the band its colour.","Pulp had struggled in Sheffield for over a decade before success arrived with His 'n' Hers in 1994. Different Class, produced by Chris Thomas for Island and released in October 1995, came after 'Common People' made them stars and after their triumphant Glastonbury headline slot, stepping in for the Stone Roses. In 1996 it won the Mercury Prize, and Cocker became a national figure after his protest stage invasion during Michael Jackson's Brit Awards performance.","The sleeve came with a set of interchangeable photographs showing cardboard cut-outs of the band placed in ordinary scenes, such as a wedding, and buyers could choose their own cover. Listen to the album's running order as an argument about class: it begins with a call to the outsiders and ends with 'Bar Italia', a weary dawn in Soho after a night out. The single 'Sorted for E's & Wizz' caused a tabloid outcry over drug references, which the band insisted were ambivalent.","Received with near-universal praise, it is often ranked as the finest Britpop album, the one that asked hard questions about class that Oasis and Blur mostly avoided. Cocker's lyrics, witty, voyeuristic and sad, are widely compared to Morrissey's and to Ray Davies's. Critics still argue whether its satire of middle-class slumming was affectionate or merciless.","Start with 'Common People', then 'Disco 2000' and 'I Spy', and listen to Cocker's delivery, which moves between talking and singing like an actor. Pay attention to the strings and cheap synths, which give the songs their cinematic glamour. Compare Blur's Parklife and the Smiths' The Queen Is Dead."],"pulp-common-people":["A song of nearly six minutes that grows from a single ticking keyboard note into a frenzied climax. Jarvis Cocker tells the story of a rich art student who wants to live like working-class people, and his narrator, increasingly angry, explains why she never can. The verses accumulate, the drums and guitars pile in, the synthesizer rises in pitch, and the last minutes become a thrashing, almost hysterical dance around the same chords.","Cocker based the story on a Greek student he met while studying film at Saint Martin's College in London in the late 1980s. Released in May 1995, it reached number two in the UK and made Pulp stars after years of obscurity; weeks later they headlined Glastonbury, replacing the Stone Roses. It arrived during Britpop's peak and the last years of a Conservative government, when class was again a live subject.","The song never changes its basic chord pattern; all its drama comes from arrangement, tempo and Cocker's rising vocal intensity, a lesson in building a song like a crescendo in Ravel's Boléro. The famous synthesizer line was played on a cheap keyboard by Candida Doyle. In 2004 William Shatner recorded a spoken cover with Ben Folds, which revealed how much of the song is really narration.","It is widely named one of the greatest British singles of the nineties and the finest single of the Britpop years. Some heard its attack on class tourism as snobbish in reverse; most heard it as bitingly accurate. Its anger has made it a protest-march and festival anthem ever since, and it has been adopted in political campaigns across the spectrum, an irony its writer has noted.","Listen to the whole six minutes without skipping and track how the energy grows while the chords stay the same. Pay attention to the drums, which begin sparsely and end in a gallop. Then play the Kinks' 'Waterloo Sunset' for a gentler English song about ordinary lives, and 'Disco 2000' for Pulp's lighter side."],"pulp-this-is-hardcore":["Twelve long, dark songs about fame, ageing, fear and the emptiness of desire. 'The Fear' opens with a woozy, horror-film guitar; the title track, over six minutes, is built from a slow, grandiose orchestral sample and a crushing piano, like the soundtrack of a seedy film; 'Help the Aged' is a wry anthem about getting older; 'Dishes' is a quiet hymn to ordinariness; the album ends with 'The Day After the Revolution', which trails into a long, repeated fade.","After Different Class made them famous, Pulp found fame corrosive; violinist and guitarist Russell Senior left in 1997, and Jarvis Cocker has spoken of how depleted the period left him. Recorded again with Chris Thomas and released by Island in March 1998, the record appeared just as Britpop collapsed. Its sleeve and poster imagery, art-directed by Peter Saville, drew complaints and were defaced on the London Underground.","The title track's lush orchestral backing comes from a sampled easy-listening record by the Peter Thomas Sound Orchestra, turned from kitsch into menace by slowing and repetition. Listen for how the album's structure mirrors a hangover: it begins in panic and ends in exhausted calm. 'Dishes' presents its singer as an ordinary man who is not Christ, a quiet answer to rock-star messiah poses.","It sold far less than Different Class, and many reviewers in 1998 found it gloomy and indulgent. Its reputation has grown enormously since, and many critics now call it Pulp's finest album and the definitive document of Britpop's hangover. The disagreement over whether it is a masterful post-mortem or simply a bleak record continues, though the former view is now more common.","Start with 'Help the Aged' and then the title track, heard in the dark and at volume. Notice how the strings and piano are made to sound both glamorous and sick. Hear Scott Walker's Scott 4 for the ancestor of this orchestral gloom, and compare Pulp's bright Different Class to feel the fall."],"suede-suede":["Eleven songs of glamorous, sleazy London romance. Bernard Butler's guitar is the engine: glittering chords, sharp riffs and long, bending solos, drawn from glam rock and the Smiths. Brett Anderson sings in a high, theatrical, androgynous voice. 'So Young' opens with a surge; 'Animal Nitrate' is a hard-driving hit; 'Metal Mickey' is swaggering glam; 'Sleeping Pills' and 'The Next Life' are fragile ballads, the second with only piano.","Before they had released an album, Suede appeared on the cover of Melody Maker in 1992 as the best new band in Britain. Recorded with producer Ed Buller and released by the independent label Nude in March 1993, the album became the fastest-selling debut in Britain in almost a decade and won the first Mercury Prize given in the Britpop era. Anderson and Butler's songs were set in a seedy, suburban London of high-rise flats and cheap thrills.","The cover photograph shows two figures kissing whose sex is left ambiguous, and Anderson's lyrics play the same game, one of the first mainstream British rock records to court sexual ambiguity since glam. Listen to how Butler's guitar fills the gaps between Anderson's lines like a second voice, in the manner of Johnny Marr. The sound is deliberately thin in places, with the vocal far forward, recalling early Bowie production.","The press hype made the album a target, and some felt it could not live up to it; still, it won the Mercury Prize in 1993 and is widely credited, alongside Blur's Modern Life Is Rubbish, with starting Britpop. Later critics tend to prize its sequel more, but the debut's energy and oddness still impress, and it opened space for queerer, darker Britpop voices.","Begin with 'Animal Nitrate' and 'So Young', then 'The Next Life' to hear the other side. Listen to the guitar as much as the voice. Play David Bowie's Ziggy Stardust and the Smiths' The Queen Is Dead first to hear the two ancestors, then Dog Man Star to hear Suede go further."],"suede-dog-man-star":["Twelve songs of grand, decadent melancholy, many far longer than Suede's pop singles. 'Introducing the Band' opens with a chant-like drone; 'We Are the Pigs' is a stomping vision of urban riot; 'The Wild Ones' is a sweeping love song; 'The Asphalt World', over nine minutes, builds long, stormy guitar passages; 'Still Life', the closing song, ends in a huge orchestral swell. Bernard Butler's guitar and Brett Anderson's voice both push toward extremes of beauty and excess.","Recorded in 1994 while Anderson lived reclusively in Highgate, reading and writing in drug-fuelled isolation, the sessions were torn apart by conflict between Butler and producer Ed Buller. Butler left the band before the album was finished, and his parts were completed without him; the teenage Richard Oakes replaced him on tour. Released in October 1994, it arrived as the cheerful Britpop of Parklife was dominating the charts.","Listen to 'Still Life': the full orchestra, with its swooping strings, was the kind of excess Butler and Anderson had argued over, and the result sounds like a Hollywood ending for a record about loneliness. The album owes as much to Scott Walker's lush orchestral records and to Bowie's Diamond Dogs as to anything in 1994. Anderson's lyrics are full of bedsits, cars and suburban fantasy.","It sold disappointingly and was overshadowed by sunnier Britpop, and the band's split seemed fatal. Its reputation has risen steadily since, and it is now often named Suede's best record and one of the great British albums of the decade. Debate focuses on its excess: some hear overwrought melodrama, most now hear ambition that Britpop too rarely allowed itself.","Play 'The Wild Ones' first, then set aside ten minutes for 'The Asphalt World', listening to the guitar build and break. End with 'Still Life' at volume. Compare Pulp's This Is Hardcore, a later and wearier album of glamour gone sour, and David Bowie's Low for another record of retreat."],"supergrass-i-should-coco":["Thirteen short, breakneck songs of teenage exuberance. 'I'd Like to Know' opens at a gallop; 'Caught by the Fuzz' is a two-minute punk rush about a youthful run-in with the police; 'Mansize Rooster' bounces on a piano; 'Alright', the big hit, is a jaunty piano-pop celebration of being young, with Danny Goffey's clattering drums and Gaz Coombes's voice. The guitars are fuzzy, the tempos fast, the choruses immediate.","Supergrass were three young men from Oxford, the singer and guitarist Gaz Coombes still a teenager when the band was signed to Parlophone. Recorded with producer Sam Williams and released in May 1995, the debut went to number one in Britain and 'Alright' became one of the summer songs of Britpop's golden year. The band's sideburns and cartoon energy led, reportedly, to interest from Steven Spielberg in a Monkees-style television show, which they declined.","Listen past the cheerfulness: the lyrics often take the edge off youth rather than celebrating it plainly, and 'Caught by the Fuzz' is an unglamorous account of a humiliating arrest. The musicianship is remarkably tight for such a young band, with the three players locking together at speeds close to punk. The piano-driven 'Alright' owes a debt to the bouncing pub-pop of Madness and the Small Faces.","Praised in 1995 as a breath of fresh air, it was later sometimes dismissed as a novelty, especially as 'Alright' became an advertising jingle. The band's later albums, In It for the Money above all, won more critical respect. Still, I Should Coco is often remembered as the purest distillation of Britpop's optimism, with none of its pretensions.","Start with 'Caught by the Fuzz' and 'Alright', then play the whole thing in one go; it lasts barely forty minutes. Listen to the drums, which never sit still. Compare the Who's My Generation for the older model of teenage energy and the Buzzcocks for its punk ancestors."],"the-verve-urban-hymns":["Thirteen songs, some sweeping ballads, some long psychedelic drifts. 'Bitter Sweet Symphony' opens on its famous string loop; 'Sonnet' and 'Lucky Man' are big, warm acoustic-and-strings anthems; 'The Drugs Don't Work' is a slow, grieving ballad; 'Catching the Butterfly' and 'Come On' let Nick McCabe's guitar swirl in layers of effects. Richard Ashcroft sings in a pleading, drawling voice that turns simple melodies into confessions.","The Verve, from Wigan, had broken up in 1995 after two acclaimed but little-sold psychedelic albums. Ashcroft reformed the band in 1997, first without and then with McCabe, and recorded Urban Hymns in London with producers including Youth and Chris Potter for the Hut label. Released in September 1997, weeks after the death of Princess Diana and as New Labour began, it became one of the biggest-selling British albums of the decade.","Listen to the tension between Ashcroft's plain songs and McCabe's guitar, which dissolves them into clouds of reverb and feedback; the band's earlier albums were almost all McCabe's textures. The long, hidden instrumental at the end of 'Come On' echoes the band's past. 'The Drugs Don't Work' was written, in Ashcroft's account, partly about his father's death years earlier, and its title is literal rather than a slogan.","A huge success with critics and public, it won the Brit Award for best British album in 1998. The band split again within two years. Later critics are more divided: some see it as the last great Britpop record, others as a slick compromise that buried the psychedelic ambition of A Storm in Heaven and A Northern Soul. Its strongest songs have never lost their hold.","Begin with 'Bitter Sweet Symphony' and 'The Drugs Don't Work', then play 'Catching the Butterfly' to hear McCabe's side of the band. Notice how often the strings and guitars share the melodic line. Hear Spiritualized's Ladies and Gentlemen, from the same summer, to compare two very different kinds of psychedelia."],"the-verve-bitter-sweet-symphony":["Nearly six minutes built on a single looping string figure, swaying and ominous, over a heavy, steady drum beat. Richard Ashcroft sings about the grind of life and the struggle to change, his voice rising and falling as the loop repeats. Guitars and a rising organ-like wash add layers, and the song gathers strength without ever changing its core pattern. The strings are what everyone remembers; the song never lets them go.","The strings come from a 1965 orchestral version of the Rolling Stones' 'The Last Time' by the Andrew Oldham Orchestra. The Verve had licensed a sample, but the Stones' former manager Allen Klein argued they had used more than agreed; the result was that songwriting credit and royalties passed to Mick Jagger and Keith Richards. Only in 2019 did Jagger and Richards hand the rights back to Ashcroft.","The string arrangement was rebuilt and extended by Wil Malone, who had arranged the strings on Massive Attack's 'Unfinished Sympathy'. The video, in which Ashcroft walks down a London pavement in a single take, barging into passers-by and never deviating, is a knowing homage to the continuous-shot walk of the 'Unfinished Sympathy' video. The tune of the Stones' original is barely recognisable in the loop.","It was a global hit and was nominated for a Grammy, and it is widely regarded as one of the defining songs of the nineties. Its legal story became a standard case study of sampling and copyright, often cited as a warning. The irony of a song about the trap of money losing its royalties was not lost on anyone.","Listen first to the loop alone, then to how the song's other layers come and go around it. Seek out the Rolling Stones' 'The Last Time' to hear the source, and Massive Attack's 'Unfinished Sympathy' for the strings and walk that inspired it. Hear Steve Reich's looping music for another kind of power in repetition."],"manic-street-preachers-the-holy-bible":["Thirteen tense, angular songs of sharp post-punk guitar and densely packed words. James Dean Bradfield sings over his own jagged riffs, racing to fit in lines that were often never written to scan. 'Yes' opens with a surge; 'Faster' is a snarling statement of self-will; 'Archives of Pain' and 'The Intense Humming of Evil' confront violence and history; '4st 7lb' describes anorexia from the inside. Spoken samples from films and documentaries introduce many tracks.","Recorded quickly and cheaply at a small studio in Cardiff in early 1994 and released in August on Epic, the album was written mostly by the band's lyricist and rhythm guitarist Richey Edwards, whose own health was then deteriorating. The band had deliberately stepped back from the mainstream as Britpop brightened. Edwards disappeared on 1 February 1995 and has never been found; he was declared presumed dead in 2008.","Listen to how Bradfield turns Edwards's crammed, unrhymed lines into melody by squeezing and stretching syllables; the strain is part of the effect. The spoken samples, including testimony and archival voices, frame the record like a documentary. The music draws from the sharp post-punk of Wire, Magazine and Siouxsie and the Banshees rather than from the Britpop around it.","It sold poorly on release and many found it unbearable; its reputation has since grown into cult reverence, and it is often named among the greatest British albums of the nineties. Critics admire its seriousness; others find it hard to separate from the tragedy that followed, and the debate over how to hear it without mythologising Edwards continues.","Start with 'Faster' and 'Revol' for the band's energy, then 'Archives of Pain' if you are ready for the heavier material. Read about the subjects before listening rather than during. Compare Joy Division's Closer and Wire's Pink Flag to hear the post-punk models, and Blur's Parklife, the same year, for the mood it rejected."],"manic-street-preachers-a-design-for-life":["A four-minute anthem that opens on a sweeping, melancholy string line before James Dean Bradfield's voice arrives, strong and plain. The song salutes working-class self-education and pride while mocking the way the working class is treated as a mere market. The chorus swells with strings and guitars into a huge, hymn-like refrain. Everything about it is broad and ceremonial, deliberately far from the tense clutter of The Holy Bible.","Released in April 1996, it was the first single after Richey Edwards's disappearance, recorded by the remaining three members with producer Mike Hedges and lyrics by bassist Nicky Wire. It reached number two in the UK and led the album Everything Must Go, which revived the band commercially. The band from Blackwood in South Wales had grown up among mining communities devastated in the 1980s.","The song begins from the image of the public library as a monument to working-class self-improvement, a thread in South Welsh history of miners' institutes and libraries. Listen to the string part carefully: it echoes the grandeur of Phil Spector and Scott Walker, not Britpop guitar. The song's pride sits uneasily with bitterness, and the sarcasm of its lyric was often missed by audiences who sang it as a straight celebration.","Widely praised on release, it is now regarded as one of the defining songs of the band and of 1990s Britain. Some critics hear a triumphant rebirth after tragedy; others note that its irony was flattened by its use as a crowd anthem. Its place in Welsh cultural life is secure, sung at rugby and football matches.","Listen first to the string introduction, then to how the chorus lifts. Pay attention to the words, spoken plainly in your own paraphrase, about dignity and contempt. Compare The Holy Bible to hear the change of voice after Edwards's loss, and Pulp's 'Common People' for another nineties song about class."],"spiritualized-ladies-and-gentlemen-we-are-floating-in-space":["Twelve tracks that range from hushed, overlapping hymns to walls of free-jazz noise. The title track opens with layered vocal rounds; 'Come Together' is a roaring, brass-driven rocker; 'Broken Heart' is a slow orchestral lament; 'Cop Shoot Cop...', seventeen minutes long, begins as a blues and collapses into a storm of feedback and horns. A gospel choir, string quartet and brass section surround Jason Pierce's thin, unassuming voice. The effect is one continuous drift between calm and chaos.","Jason Pierce, known as J. Spaceman, had led the drone-rock band Spacemen 3 in Rugby before forming Spiritualized in 1990. The album, recorded over many months and released by Dedicated in June 1997, features the London Community Gospel Choir, the Balanescu Quartet and New Orleans pianist Dr. John. It is widely heard as a breakup album following the end of Pierce's relationship with keyboardist Kate Radley. It came out within days of Radiohead's OK Computer.","The title track originally quoted 'Can't Help Falling in Love', sung by Elvis Presley; permission was refused and the quotation was removed, before being restored on a later reissue. The first edition's packaging was designed as a prescription pharmaceutical blister pack, which fits the album's portrait of love and medicine as the same thing. Listen for the gospel choir, which appears most unexpectedly at the album's quietest points.","It was named album of the year by NME in 1997, ahead of OK Computer, and is widely considered one of the finest British records of the decade. Some find its excess tiring, and its seventeen-minute centrepiece divides listeners. Its fusion of gospel, free jazz and drone-rock still sounds singular, and it has aged as well as anything of its moment.","Play the title track first, with headphones, and listen to the overlapping voices. Then 'Broken Heart' for the album's emotional core. Save 'Cop Shoot Cop...' for last and hear it through. Compare the Velvet Underground's White Light/White Heat for the noise, and gospel records for the choir."],"primal-scream-screamadelica":["Eleven tracks that move from Rolling Stones-style gospel rock to euphoric house and dub. 'Movin' On Up' is a joyful rock song with a gospel choir; 'Loaded' rides a slow, loping breakbeat and a borrowed horn riff; 'Come Together' is ten minutes of rolling house piano and preaching; 'Higher Than the Sun' is a floating, dub-drenched ballad; 'Don't Fight It, Feel It' is a pounding acid-house track with Denise Johnson's vocals. The album ends in the slow-motion haze of 'Shine Like Stars'.","Primal Scream, a Glasgow-born indie band led by Bobby Gillespie, were drawn into the acid-house scene in 1989 and 1990. The DJ Andrew Weatherall remixed their song 'I'm Losing More Than I'll Ever Have' into 'Loaded', a surprise hit that turned them into a dance act. Released by Creation in September 1991, the album was produced by Weatherall, the Orb's circle and the Rolling Stones' former producer Jimmy Miller. It won the first Mercury Prize in 1992.","'Loaded' opens with a spoken sample of Peter Fonda from the 1966 biker film The Wild Angels, announcing the wish to be free to have a good time. Listen to how little of the original band remains in that track: Weatherall stripped it to a drum loop, a bass line and horns. 'Slip Inside This House' covers a song by the Texas psychedelic band the 13th Floor Elevators, tying rave to sixties psychedelia.","Hailed immediately as a landmark, it is widely regarded as the album that fused rock and dance music in Britain most completely. Some critics credit Weatherall more than the band, and that argument continues. Gillespie's later turns to hard rock confused many, but Screamadelica's reputation has only grown, and its cover, a sunburst by Paul Cannell, is one of the famous images of British pop.","Start with 'Movin' On Up' and 'Loaded', then 'Higher Than the Sun' for the dub side. Play it loud and let the long tracks run. Compare the Rolling Stones' Sticky Fingers for the rock model, King Tubby for the dub, and Happy Mondays for the Manchester parallel."],"portishead-dummy":["Eleven slow, smoky songs. Beth Gibbons sings in a fragile, aching voice over slow hip-hop drum loops, scratched vinyl, spy-film guitar, organ and eerie electronic whines like a theremin. 'Mysterons' opens with a wavering electronic tone; 'Sour Times' rides a tense, twanging loop; 'Roads' is a slow, devastating ballad with strings; 'Glory Box' closes on a sultry, sampled groove. The sound is dusty, vintage and cinematic, as if from an old spy film.","Geoff Barrow, a young Bristol producer who had worked as a tape operator at the studio where Massive Attack made Blue Lines, formed Portishead with the singer Beth Gibbons and the guitarist Adrian Utley, a veteran jazz player. The band took their name from a small town near Bristol. Released by Go! Beat in August 1994, Dummy won the Mercury Prize in 1995 and brought the Bristol sound, soon called trip-hop, to a vast audience.","Barrow built many sounds by recording live instruments, pressing them on vinyl and sampling them back, to get the crackle of old records. 'Glory Box' is built on a sample from Isaac Hayes's 'Ike's Rap II', and 'Sour Times' on one from Lalo Schifrin's spy-film music. Listen to how the scratching is played as an instrument in its own right, a legacy of hip-hop turntablism.","Praised on release, it became one of the defining records of the decade, played at dinner parties as much as in clubs, which some critics mocked. The band disliked the trip-hop label and its easy-listening reputation. Its unease, its grief and its technical craft have kept it fresh; it is still widely named one of the finest debuts of the nineties.","Start with 'Roads' in a dark room, then 'Sour Times' and 'Glory Box'. Listen for the crackle and scratch as parts of the music, not noise. Then play Isaac Hayes's Black Moses to hear the source of 'Glory Box', and Massive Attack's Blue Lines to hear the Bristol ancestor."],"portishead-third":["Eleven harsh, unsettling tracks that abandon the vintage hip-hop crackle of the band's early albums. 'Silence' opens with a spoken Portuguese introduction and a rattling, motorik drum pulse; 'The Rip' begins with a delicate guitar and turns into a pulsing synthesizer loop; 'Machine Gun' is built on a drum machine that sounds like gunfire; 'Deep Water' is a brief ukulele song; 'We Carry On' drives relentlessly on a German-style beat. Beth Gibbons's voice floats over sounds that are cold and industrial.","Released by Island in April 2008, Third came eleven years after the band's second album. Barrow, Utley and Gibbons had spent years in Bristol trying and abandoning directions, determined not to repeat the trip-hop sound they had popularised and that had been copied everywhere. The result drew more on krautrock, Silver Apples, Suicide and the experimental electronics of the 1960s and 70s than on hip-hop.","Listen to 'We Carry On': its throbbing pulse is a deliberate debt to the minimal drive of Suicide and Can. The spoken words that open 'Silence' are in Portuguese, a disorienting doorway into an English-language record. Notice how the drums on 'Machine Gun' are left raw and unprocessed, a deliberate refusal of the polished loops of their early work. The album avoids the melancholy lushness of Dummy almost completely.","Critics received it with near-unanimous praise, many calling it the band's finest record and an object lesson in refusing one's own formula. Some listeners who loved Dummy found it cold and hostile. It is now widely regarded as one of the most important British albums of the 2000s and a rare comeback that surpassed its origins.","Begin with 'The Rip', which bridges the old and new Portishead. Then 'Machine Gun' and 'We Carry On' at volume. Compare Dummy first, then Suicide and Can's Tago Mago to hear the new ancestors. Headphones reveal how raw the textures are."],"massive-attack-blue-lines":["Nine tracks of slow, deep grooves built from sampled soul, funk and reggae records, with laid-back rapping from 3D, Daddy G and Tricky and soulful singing from Shara Nelson and the reggae veteran Horace Andy. 'Safe from Harm' opens with a menacing bass loop; 'Unfinished Sympathy' soars on a real string orchestra; 'Be Thankful for What You've Got' covers a 1970s soul song; 'Hymn of the Big Wheel' closes on Horace Andy's gentle voice. Tempos are slow, bass heavy, mood nocturnal.","Massive Attack grew from the Wild Bunch, a Bristol sound-system collective of DJs, rappers and graffiti artists in the 1980s. Robert Del Naja (3D), Grant Marshall (Daddy G) and Andrew Vowles (Mushroom) made Blue Lines with producers Jonny Dollar and Cameron McVey, released by Circa in April 1991. During the Gulf War they briefly used the name Massive to avoid seeming to comment on the conflict. The album fused Jamaican sound-system culture, hip-hop and soul in a British city.","Listen to 'Safe from Harm': its sinuous bass line is lifted from the jazz-fusion drummer Billy Cobham's 'Stratus'. The album's slow tempos, far below the hip-hop of the day, came from the sound-system habit of playing records late at night to a stoned, swaying crowd, and from the reggae and dub the members grew up on. Tricky's whispered rapping here is the first appearance of a major voice.","Critics recognised it as something new, and it is widely regarded as the founding album of what would later be called trip-hop, though the band always rejected the label. Its influence spread through Portishead, Tricky, Björk and a whole school of downtempo music. It is now routinely named among the greatest British albums ever made.","Begin with 'Unfinished Sympathy', then go back to 'Safe from Harm' and listen through. Pay attention to the bass and the slow drums, and to how the voices trade places. Compare King Tubby's dub records for the space and echo, and Eric B. & Rakim for the hip-hop of the same era."],"massive-attack-unfinished-sympathy":["Five minutes of soaring soul over a slow breakbeat. Shara Nelson sings a lament of unrequited love, her voice rising over a full string orchestra that swoops and sighs. A high, repeated electronic ping and a loose drum groove hold the song steady while the strings carry its emotion. The song's power lies in the contrast between the cool, mechanical rhythm and the warm, surging orchestra.","Released in February 1991 as a single from Blue Lines, it reached the UK top twenty and became Massive Attack's signature song. The strings, arranged by Wil Malone, were recorded with a real orchestra, a rare expense for a young Bristol act on a small label. The single was credited to 'Massive' during the Gulf War.","The video, directed by Baillie Walsh, is a single continuous shot following Shara Nelson walking down a Los Angeles street, singing and ignoring everything around her; it became one of the most copied videos ever, notably by the Verve for 'Bitter Sweet Symphony'. Listen to how the strings enter: not as sweetening but as the song's main voice, answering the singer.","It is regularly named one of the greatest singles of all time by British critics and publications. Its union of an orchestra with sampled hip-hop drums seemed daring in 1991 and was widely imitated. The only debate is how much of its power belongs to Nelson, who left the band soon after, and how much to the production.","Listen first to the strings, then to the drums, and then to how Nelson's voice moves between them. Watch the video once. Compare Isaac Hayes's orchestral soul and the Verve's 'Bitter Sweet Symphony', which learned from it. Seek a good speaker; the bass matters."],"massive-attack-protection":["Ten tracks of slow, luxurious downtempo. The title track, nearly eight minutes, features Tracey Thorn of Everything but the Girl singing a tender song of loyalty over a slow, echoing breakbeat; 'Karmacoma' is a hazy duet of whispered raps between 3D and Tricky; 'Spying Glass' is a reggae dub with Horace Andy; 'Better Things' is another hushed Thorn ballad; Craig Armstrong's piano and strings add a cinematic sheen. The album closes with a live cover of the Doors' 'Light My Fire'.","Released in September 1994, weeks after Portishead's Dummy, Protection arrived just as the Bristol sound was becoming a national trend. It was produced with Nellee Hooper, who had worked with Soul II Soul and Björk. Shara Nelson had left; the band recruited Thorn, the singer Nicolette and Horace Andy. In 1995 the dub producer Mad Professor remixed the whole album as No Protection.","Listen to 'Karmacoma': its sinuous groove would be reworked by Tricky on his own album as 'Overcome'. The strings and piano on several tracks are by Craig Armstrong, a Scottish composer later famous for film scores. The whole album sits a notch slower and softer than Blue Lines, a deliberate retreat from hip-hop's hardness toward ambient space.","Critics found it beautiful but less revolutionary than Blue Lines, and it sold well. Over time it has been re-evaluated as a subtle, influential album whose title track is one of the finest songs of the Bristol scene. The companion No Protection is often preferred by dub lovers.","Start with the title track, heard late at night. Then 'Karmacoma' and 'Spying Glass'. Notice the space between sounds. Compare King Tubby's dub albums and Everything but the Girl's own records, then play Blue Lines to hear the difference in weight."],"massive-attack-mezzanine":["Eleven dark, heavy tracks where slow beats meet distorted guitar. 'Angel' opens with a throbbing bass line and Horace Andy's soft voice before exploding into guitar noise; 'Risingson' is a paranoid rap by 3D and Daddy G; 'Teardrop', sung by Elizabeth Fraser of the Cocteau Twins, is a delicate lullaby over a heartbeat-like pulse and harpsichord; 'Inertia Creeps' slithers on distorted rhythm. The whole sound is darker, heavier and more guitar-based than before.","Recorded in Bristol with co-producer Neil Davidge and released in April 1998, the album was made amid bitter disagreements within the band; Andrew Vowles left soon afterwards, disliking its dark, rock-led direction pushed by Del Naja. Massive Attack streamed the whole album online before release, an early experiment in internet distribution. It became their biggest commercial success.","Listen to 'Risingson': it samples the Velvet Underground's 'I Found a Reason', turning a gentle 1970 song into a looming loop. 'Teardrop' was reportedly offered first to Madonna, and Elizabeth Fraser wrote her lyric partly in grief after the death of her friend Jeff Buckley. The song later became famous worldwide as the theme music for the television series House.","Critics praised it as a bold reinvention, and it is now often named the band's finest album and one of the best of the decade. Those who loved the band's early warmth found it chilly and oppressive. Its use of guitar noise and paranoia influenced a new generation of electronic and rock artists.","Start with 'Teardrop', then 'Angel' at volume and wait for the guitars to arrive. Listen for the bass throughout. Play the Velvet Underground's Loaded to hear the source of 'Risingson', and Portishead's Third for a later dark reinvention from the same city."],"tricky-maxinquaye":["Twelve claustrophobic tracks of murky beats, eerie samples and two voices: Tricky's hoarse whisper and Martina Topley-Bird's cool, clear singing, often trading lines or singing his words. 'Overcome' reworks the groove of Massive Attack's 'Karmacoma'; 'Hell Is Round the Corner' drifts on a slow, sampled soul loop; 'Black Steel' covers a Public Enemy song as distorted rock; 'Aftermath' is a hazy, hypnotic slow burn; 'Pumpkin' features Alison Goldfrapp. The mood is paranoid, sensual and unsettling.","Adrian Thaws, known as Tricky, grew up in Knowle West, Bristol, and had rapped on Massive Attack's first two albums. Maxinquaye, released by Island's Fourth & Broadway label in February 1995, was made with Martina Topley-Bird, a Bristol teenager he met singing outside his house. The title honours his mother, Maxine Quaye, who died when he was four. NME named it album of the year.","Listen to 'Hell Is Round the Corner': it samples the same Isaac Hayes track, 'Ike's Rap II', as Portishead's 'Glory Box', released months earlier; the two uses sound completely different. Tricky often gave Martina his lyrics to sing, blurring gender and voice, and making their meanings strangely detached. 'Black Steel' turns Public Enemy's prison-escape narrative into a wall of guitar.","Hailed on release for its claustrophobic darkness, it is still regarded as trip-hop's most original album, though Tricky rejected the label and his later records confused many. Critics praised its sonic invention and emotional murk; others found it hard to live with. Its influence, on artists from Radiohead to Björk, remains considerable.","Start with 'Aftermath' and 'Hell Is Round the Corner'. Then 'Black Steel', after first hearing Public Enemy's original. Listen with headphones to the murky detail. Compare Portishead's Dummy and Massive Attack's Protection to hear the Bristol family's different moods. Notice how often the two voices sing the same words a fraction apart."],"elastica-elastica":["Sixteen short, sharp songs in about forty minutes. Wiry, choppy guitars, taut bass and brisk drums back Justine Frischmann's cool, deadpan vocals. 'Connection' rides a jagged, stuttering riff; 'Waking Up' bounces on a new-wave groove; 'Line Up' mocks rock groupies with a sneer and a squawk; 'Stutter' is a punchy two-minute rush. The songs are frank, often funny and sexually candid, and rarely stretch past three minutes.","Frischmann had been a founding member of Suede and was then the partner of Blur's Damon Albarn, which put Elastica at the centre of Britpop's gossip. With Donna Matthews, Annie Holland and Justin Welch, they released their debut on the small Deceptive label in March 1995; it became the fastest-selling debut album in Britain since Oasis's Definitely Maybe the year before, and also sold well in the United States.","Listen to 'Connection' after Wire's 'Three Girl Rhumba' from Pink Flag; the riff is so close that Wire's publishers secured a settlement, and another song led to a settlement with the Stranglers. Elastica's sound comes almost entirely from late-1970s post-punk and new wave rather than from 1960s pop, which sets them apart from most Britpop.","Warmly received for its energy and wit, it was also criticised for its obvious borrowing. Critics now see it as a short, sharp classic, a rare Britpop album led by women, and one that kept punk economy alive. The band's slow collapse over the following five years, amid drug problems and lineup changes, cut off the promise it showed.","Play it through in one sitting; it takes barely forty minutes. Start with 'Connection' and 'Stutter'. Then play Wire's Pink Flag and the Buzzcocks to hear the sources. Compare Supergrass's I Should Coco for the same year's other burst of youthful speed."],"saint-etienne-foxbase-alpha":["Twelve tracks that stitch together house beats, dub bass, sixties pop melodies and snippets of film dialogue into a dreamy collage of London. 'Only Love Can Break Your Heart' turns Neil Young's ballad into a slow house song; 'Nothing Can Stop Us' is sunny, sample-based soul pop; 'London Belongs to Me' is a gentle song about a day in the city; interludes of speech, radio and street noise connect the songs. Sarah Cracknell's airy voice floats over everything.","Saint Etienne were formed by two former music journalists and record collectors, Bob Stanley and Pete Wiggs, who wanted to combine the club culture of acid house with their love of 1960s pop. Early singles used guest singers before Sarah Cracknell joined. Released by Heavenly Records in 1991, the album is often seen as one of the first Britpop records, in its fascination with a mythic London.","'Nothing Can Stop Us' is built on a sample of a Dusty Springfield song, and the album is full of borrowed sounds, from film clips to vintage pop, many chosen for their associations with a lost London. Listen to how the interludes work like the scenery changes in a film; Stanley and Wiggs thought of the album as a soundtrack. Their record-collector's affection for the past became a template for much later music.","Critics praised its charm and originality, though it sold modestly. It is now regarded as a pioneering album that joined indie pop to dance music and anticipated Britpop's fascination with English pop history. Some find it slight; others hear a quiet revolution in how pop could be made from records.","Start with 'Only Love Can Break Your Heart', then 'Nothing Can Stop Us'. Play it on a walk through a city. Compare Neil Young's After the Gold Rush for the source, and Dusty Springfield's records for the pop tradition Saint Etienne adored."],"bark-psychosis-scum":["A single piece of about twenty-one minutes, issued on its own as a single. It begins in near-silence, with scattered guitar harmonics, a softly repeated bass figure and shimmering keyboards, and drifts for long stretches before tension gathers in the drums and a guitar begins to growl. The piece rises and falls in long waves rather than verses, with occasional murmured vocal phrases barely surfacing. What you remember is the space: every note seems to hang in a large, resonant room and take a long time to die.","Bark Psychosis were very young east Londoners who had begun in the noisy indie scene but were increasingly drawn to Talk Talk's late records and to jazz. For Scum they set up in St John's Church in Stratford, near their homes, and recorded the piece live in the building to capture its natural reverberation. Releasing a single over twenty minutes long, with no chorus and little singing, was a pointed rejection of the British music press's hunger for three-minute indie anthems in the early 1990s.","The church is the most important instrument. Listen to how the snare hits bloom and decay and how the bass seems to come from the floor itself; none of that is artificial reverb. The quietest passages carry the ambient noise of the building and the traffic outside. The piece is essentially one long performance, so the small hesitations and swells are real-time decisions by players listening to the room rather than edits.","Scum was admired in the weekly music press but heard by few at the time. Its reputation rose in retrospect, once Bark Psychosis's album Hex prompted the term 'post-rock' and critics looked back for early examples. It is now often cited, alongside Talk Talk's late albums, as proof that British bands were exploring this territory independently of the American scene around Slint and Tortoise.","Treat it like a long ambient work: lie back, turn off the lights, and resist the urge to wait for a song to start. Follow the bass figure as a thread through the whole piece. Afterwards, hear Brian Eno's Discreet Music and then Talk Talk's Laughing Stock to feel how Scum sits between ambient and rock."],"bark-psychosis-hex":["Seven long, softly lit songs, including 'The Loom', 'A Street Scene', 'Absent Friend' and 'Big Shot'. Graham Sutton's voice is a hushed half-whisper, often mixed below the instruments, and the arrangements lean toward jazz: brushed drums, upright-sounding bass, muted trumpet, piano chords that are allowed to decay, vibraphone-like shimmer and faint strings. There are no big climaxes. Instead the music moves by small shifts of colour and texture, like light across a wall, and the whole album has the muffled calm of a city heard from an upstairs window late at night.","Bark Psychosis spent a long time on Hex, working in several studios with an obsessive attention to sound that reportedly exhausted the band and emptied the budget of their label, Circa, a subsidiary of Virgin. It appeared in February 1994, just as Britpop was taking over the music press, and could hardly have sounded less like it. Reviewing it in Mojo, the critic Simon Reynolds used the phrase 'post-rock' to describe music using rock instruments for non-rock ends, and he developed the idea soon afterwards in The Wire.","Listen to how often the drums are not keeping time but adding colour, with brushed cymbals and rim clicks placed like punctuation. Many of the sounds were built up and then stripped away, as on Talk Talk's records, so a trumpet or a piano chord often appears once and never returns. The title of the opening track, 'The Loom', hints at the method: separate threads woven into a single fabric rather than a song with backing.","Hex was well reviewed but sold little, and the band split soon after its release. Its fame rests largely on Reynolds's review, which gave a name to a whole field of music. Later critics have argued about whether post-rock was a real movement or a label stuck on disparate bands, but Hex is widely admired on its own terms as one of the most carefully made British records of the 1990s.","Start with 'A Street Scene' at night, on headphones, and listen to the urban quiet the band paints with a few instruments. Pay attention to the bass, which carries most of the melody. Then place it between Talk Talk's Laughing Stock, which it learned from, and the lounge-cool of Tortoise's Millions Now Living Will Never Die, released two years later."],"godspeed-you-black-emperor-f-sharp-a-sharp-infinity":["Three long movements, each built from several sections: 'The Dead Flag Blues', 'East Hastings' and 'Providence'. The album opens with a slow, spoken voice describing a ruined, burning city over a mournful drone, then a lone slide guitar and a violin take up a funereal melody. 'East Hastings' starts with a street preacher on tape and a bagpipe-like drone before a guitar line climbs through a long, slow crescendo to a roaring climax. The whole record moves like a film score without a film: field recordings, glockenspiel, strings and massed guitars rising and collapsing.","The collective formed in Montreal in the mid-1990s in a scene of cheap lofts, warehouse shows and anarchist politics. This album was first issued on vinyl in a tiny run by the local label Constellation in 1997, then reissued on CD in 1998 by Chicago's Kranky with longer, reworked content. The band's refusal of interviews, the anonymous members and the hand-drawn packaging all rejected the music industry's usual machinery, at a moment when the post-rock label was just beginning to travel.","The title refers to the vinyl edition, which ended with a locked groove that repeats forever, the 'infinity' of the title. The CD and vinyl versions differ considerably, with different sections and a hidden track, so fans argue about which is the real album. The field recordings of preachers, trains and street noise were made by band members in Montreal and elsewhere, and act like a narrator's voice-over in a documentary.","Word spread slowly through import shops and fanzines, and by the time the CD reached Britain and America in 1998 the band had become a cult. Critics praised its ambition and apocalyptic atmosphere; some found the politics vague or the crescendos formulaic. The use of 'East Hastings' in Danny Boyle's film 28 Days Later (2002) brought it to a far larger audience, and it is now considered one of the defining post-rock albums.","Hear 'East Hastings' first, and give it the full length: the payoff depends on the long, patient build. Listen for the moment the drums finally enter with force. Then go back to the spoken opening and hear it as a prologue. Compare the slow orchestral lament of Górecki's Third Symphony, and the massed guitars of Glenn Branca, both close cousins."],"godspeed-you-black-emperor-lift-your-skinny-fists-like-antennas-to-heaven":["A double album of four movements, each around twenty to twenty-three minutes: 'Storm', 'Static', 'Sleep' and 'Antennas to Heaven'. 'Storm' opens with a bright, triumphant theme for guitars, strings and horn that might have come from a Western film, then passes through mournful and violent sections. 'Static' begins with a recording of a street preacher and slides into desolate drone. 'Sleep' starts with an elderly man remembering Coney Island in his youth, before an enormous slow crescendo. The final side closes with a children's chant and a fading, hymnlike coda.","Recorded in Montreal after years of touring, the album showed the collective at its largest and most ambitious. It appeared in October 2000, at the height of the post-rock boom and just before the attacks of 2001 made its apocalyptic mood seem prophetic to many listeners. The band remained anti-commercial, releasing through small independent labels, giving no singles and framing the album with diagrams and notes about capital and control.","The old man in 'Sleep' was a New Yorker named Murray Ostril, recorded talking about how the pleasures of old Coney Island had vanished; his lament about a lost public world is the key to the whole album. The four movements share motifs that reappear transformed, most clearly the opening theme of 'Storm'. The cover's Hebrew lettering and the inner sleeve's hand-drawn diagrams are part of the argument, not decoration.","Reviewers greeted it as the band's high point, and it is now widely named as one of the greatest post-rock albums and one of the most important independent records of 2000. Critics who find the genre overlong point to its duration and melodrama. The collective went on hiatus in 2003 and returned in 2010 to a larger audience than they had ever had, with this record as the centre of their legend.","Take it one side at a time, and begin with 'Storm', whose opening theme is the most instantly rewarding thing the band made. Listen for how the violin and cello thicken the guitar chords at each climax. Then compare the monumental repetitions of Reich's Music for 18 Musicians, and Mogwai's Young Team, the British counterpart."],"mogwai-young-team":["The debut album, ten tracks and over seventy minutes, almost entirely instrumental. 'Yes! I Am a Long Way from Home' sets the template: quiet, chiming guitars circle a simple figure for minutes, then the band stamps on its distortion pedals and the music becomes a wall of noise. 'Like Herod' is the most extreme case, lurching between near-silence and deafening explosions. 'Tracy' is soft and glockenspiel-lit, and the closing 'Mogwai Fear Satan' rides a single build for sixteen minutes, with a flute floating over the storm.","Mogwai were in their early twenties, part of a Glasgow scene around the Chemikal Underground label, which also released Arab Strap and The Delgados. The album was recorded in Glasgow with the producers Paul Savage and Andy Miller and released in October 1997, when Britpop dominated the charts. The band made their disdain clear, famously selling T-shirts mocking Blur, and presented themselves as heirs instead to Slint, Sonic Youth and My Bloody Valentine.","'Tracy' opens with a recording of a prank telephone conversation between band members, a piece of Glasgow humour at odds with the music's melancholy. 'R U Still In 2 It' is the only real vocal track, sung by Aidan Moffat of Arab Strap. The loud sections work because the quiet ones are mixed so low: the dynamic range of the original CD is extreme, and later loud remasters flatten some of the shock.","British critics were quickly won over, and the album became an indie touchstone of 1997. Detractors called the loud-quiet method a trick, and the band themselves later played with that reputation. Over time it has been accepted as a founding British post-rock record, with 'Mogwai Fear Satan' and 'Like Herod' as live staples. A 2008 reissue added unreleased material and renewed attention.","Listen to 'Like Herod' at a volume that makes the quiet passages just audible; the eruption should make you jump. Then play 'Mogwai Fear Satan' from start to finish and follow the flute through the noise. Compare Slint's Spiderland, the American source, and Sonic Youth's Daydream Nation for the guitar textures."],"mogwai-come-on-die-young":["The second album, and a deliberate turn inward. Most of its twelve tracks are slow, clean and hushed: 'Cody', with a rare murmured vocal, 'Christmas Steps', which builds from a quiet bass figure into one of the band's heaviest climaxes, 'Ex-Cowboy', which borrows the lonesome twang of a Western soundtrack, and 'Chocky', built on piano. The sound is warmer and more detailed than the debut, with organ and strings, and the explosions are rarer and more earned. Much of the album sounds like music for an empty landscape at dusk.","Having made their name with the loud-quiet dynamic, Mogwai went to the American producer Dave Fridmann's Tarbox Road Studios in upstate New York, known for work with Mercury Rev and The Flaming Lips. The album's slowness reflected the band's growing admiration for American slowcore, particularly Low and Codeine, and for spaghetti-Western scores. The album was released in March 1999, less than two years after the debut, and many of its pieces had been worked out slowly on tour.","The opening track, 'Punk Rock:', sets a recorded television interview with Iggy Pop, in which he defends punk as a word used against the young, over a delicate guitar; the voice acts as the record's manifesto. 'Christmas Steps' reworks 'Xmas Steps', a piece the band had already issued on an EP, slowed and stretched until its climax lands with far greater weight. Listen for the lone trumpet in 'Ex-Cowboy', the clearest sign of Morricone's influence.","At first some reviewers found it slow and anticlimactic after Young Team, missing the noise. Its standing has risen considerably, and many fans and critics now prefer it for its restraint and atmosphere. It is often cited as the moment post-rock and slowcore met, and as proof that Mogwai could sustain a mood across a whole album without relying on explosions.","Start with 'Christmas Steps', which holds the whole album's arc in ten minutes, then listen from the beginning. Notice how long the band waits before raising the volume at all. Pair it with Low's I Could Live in Hope and Codeine's Frigid Stars to hear its slowcore sources, and Morricone's Once Upon a Time in the West for its Western shades."],"sigur-ros-agaetis-byrjun":["Ten tracks and over seventy minutes, from the backwards-sounding shimmer of the 'Intro' to the drifting 'Avalon'. 'Svefn-g-englar' is the calling card: a sonar-like ping, a slow bass pulse and Jónsi's falsetto rising and falling over a guitar played with a cello bow. 'Starálfur' adds a string quartet, 'Viðrar vel til loftárása' swells from piano to full brass, and the title track is almost a pop song. Words are sung in Icelandic and sometimes in an invented, wordless tongue, so voices function as another instrument.","Sigur Rós were little known outside Iceland when they recorded this second album in Reykjavík with the producer Ken Thomas, adding Kjartan Sveinsson on keyboards and a small string section. It came out in June 1999 on the Icelandic label Smekkleysa (Bad Taste), the independent that had launched The Sugarcubes and Björk. An international release in 2000, and support from Radiohead, who took the band on tour, made them global names within two years.","The title means 'a good beginning', reportedly from a remark overheard after the band first played the album to someone who thought it a decent start. Listen to the guitar in 'Svefn-g-englar': the bowing produces a long, swelling attack with no pluck, closer to a viola than a rock guitar. 'Viðrar vel til loftárása' ('good weather for airstrikes') reportedly takes its title from a phrase heard in a weather forecast, a dark irony beneath its gentle surface.","Reviews in Britain and America were rapturous, and the album is now regularly ranked among the best records of its decade. Some critics worried that its grandeur was too easily borrowed by advertisers and documentary makers, and it has indeed been used in countless trailers and in Cameron Crowe's film Vanilla Sky. That ubiquity has not dulled its reputation as the band's most complete statement.","Start with 'Svefn-g-englar' through headphones and let its ten minutes unfold without distraction. Don't try to follow the words; treat the voice as a melodic instrument. Then hear 'Viðrar vel til loftárása' for its brass-lit climax. Compare Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel for its stillness and Sibelius's Tapiola for a Nordic sense of vast space."],"sigur-ros-untitled":["Eight untitled tracks in two halves of four, separated by about thirty-six seconds of silence. The first half is lighter and prayerful, built on piano, glockenspiel and soft organ; the second half is darker and heavier, ending with the long, crushing climax of the eighth track, known to fans as 'Popplagið' ('the pop song'). Every word is sung in Vonlenska, a wordless tongue made of vowel sounds, so there are no lyrics to translate. The tempos are slow, the structures long, and the mood alternates between hush and weight.","After their international breakthrough, Sigur Rós recorded the album in their own studio, Sundlaugin, a converted swimming pool in the village of Álafoss outside Reykjavík. Many songs had been developed on tour, and the band chose to release them without names. It appeared in October 2002 through the British label Fat Cat and the American major MCA, a sign of their new commercial reach, and went into the charts in several countries.","The booklet is blank: the band left empty pages for listeners to write their own words, a direct invitation to hear the voice as pure sound. The eight tracks were later given working titles by the band, but the official release keeps them nameless. The silence in the middle is a structural hinge, and it is meant to be heard, not skipped; the second half mirrors the first in darker colours.","Some critics called it the band's most beautiful work, others found it self-indulgent and too slow, and the parentheses of the title were mocked as pretension. Opinion has settled around it as a bold, unified album whose second half contains some of the heaviest music Sigur Rós recorded. The eighth track remains the traditional closer of their concerts.","Listen in one sitting and keep the silence in the middle. In the first half, attend to the piano and the breath of the voice; in the second, feel the drums grow heavier. The last eight minutes of the final track are the album's summit. Compare Górecki's Third Symphony, another slow, wordless-seeming lament that became an unexpected bestseller."],"tortoise-millions-now-living-will-never-die":["Six instrumentals, dominated by the opening 'Djed', which runs about twenty-one minutes. 'Djed' begins with a warm, pulsing bassline and electronic chirps, settles into a motorik groove, then is cut, spliced and rearranged, with vibraphone and marimba patterns, dubby echoes and a sudden, deliberately glitchy tape break in the middle before new sections arrive. The shorter pieces, among them 'Glass Museum' and 'Along the Banks of Rivers', move between cool jazz, dub bass and minimalist mallet patterns, and the whole album feels crafted in the studio more than performed.","Tortoise came out of Chicago's close-knit indie scene, where musicians crossed freely between punk, free jazz and experimental music, and where the engineer and drummer John McEntire had his studio. Released in January 1996 on Thrill Jockey, the album was assembled by recording, sampling and splicing tape and hard-disk edits, drawing on Jamaican dub, krautrock and Steve Reich's phase pieces. It arrived just as critics were adopting Simon Reynolds's term 'post-rock', and became its American flagship.","The title is a slogan from a 1920 Jehovah's Witness lecture by Joseph Rutherford, predicting the end of the world, an odd frame for such unhurried music. In 'Djed', listen for the audible edits: at one point the music seems to stumble and skip like damaged tape, and a new section takes over. That seam is intentional, a nod to dub and to musique concrète, making the editing process itself part of what you hear.","Critics hailed it as a new direction for rock, and it sold surprisingly well for such a record. Some rock writers found it cold or overly cerebral, a complaint that has followed post-rock ever since. Its reputation has held firm: 'Djed' is regularly named one of the defining post-rock tracks, and the album is seen as the moment American indie rock opened its doors to minimalism, dub and electronic music.","Give 'Djed' its full twenty minutes and listen for the seams where one section is spliced to the next. Notice how the vibraphone and marimba make patterns that shift against each other, as in Reich. Afterwards, hear Neu!'s first album for the groove, and Reich's Drumming for the mallets."],"tortoise-tnt":["Twelve tracks, most between three and eight minutes, and a lighter, more varied record than Millions Now Living Will Never Die. The title track opens with a loose drum pattern and a jazz guitar line from Jeff Parker; 'Swung from the Gutters' is breezy and melodic; 'Ten-Day Interval' is a gentle vibraphone pattern that could sit beside Steve Reich. Elsewhere come hints of Brazilian music, cool jazz, electronica and dub. There is no singing, and the album flows like a long, varied suite of interconnected ideas rather than a collection of separate pieces.","Recorded in Chicago with John McEntire at the controls and released in March 1998, TNT was one of the first rock albums made primarily on a hard-disk recording system, allowing edits and loops impossible with tape. The guitarist Jeff Parker, a player on Chicago's jazz scene, had joined, and his clean, harmonically rich guitar tilted the band toward jazz. McEntire was also producing Stereolab at this time, and ideas passed between the two bands.","Because it was built on computer, many passages are assembled from separate improvisations and layered afterwards, so a groove may have been recorded in fragments. Listen to how 'Ten-Day Interval' and 'Four-Day Interval' echo each other like companion interludes. Titles such as 'In Sarah, Mencken, Christ and Beethoven There Were Women and Men' hint at the band's taste for oblique literary jokes.","Reviews were warm but more divided than for Millions: admirers praised its breadth and polish, detractors found it too smooth, like background music for coffee houses. It has come to be seen as a key record in the broadening of post-rock toward jazz and electronica, and Jeff Parker's later career as a jazz leader has cast its guitar playing in a new light.","Start with the title track and 'Ten-Day Interval' to hear the two faces of the band, jazz and minimalism. Listen to the album as a single piece, attending to how moods pass from track to track. Then compare Miles Davis's In a Silent Way and Reich's Music for 18 Musicians, and Stereolab's Dots and Loops, made nearby."],"stereolab-transient-random-noise-bursts-with-announcements":["Stereolab's second full album is their most krautrock-driven. Songs such as 'Tone Burst', 'Our Trinitone Blast' and 'Pack Yr Romantic Mind' ride steady, metronomic drumming, droning Farfisa organ and buzzing guitars, with Lætitia Sadier's cool, even vocal on top. The centrepiece, 'Jenny Ondioline', runs about eighteen minutes, moving from a bright pop chorus to a long, hypnotic middle section of repeating chords and back. The sound is thick, fuzzy and repetitive, built from a small number of cycling ideas stretched out until they become a trance.","Tim Gane and Lætitia Sadier founded the band in London in 1990 after Gane's group McCarthy split, and released records on their own Duophonic label, a partner arrangement kept through a later deal with the American major Elektra. They loved Neu!, Can, Faust and the early Velvet Underground as well as 1960s easy listening and library music, and collected cheap vintage organs and synthesisers when others were using samplers. The album came out in 1993 amid Britpop's rise.","The title reads like the label of an old hi-fi test record, a hint at the band's love of obsolete technology. In 'Jenny Ondioline' the two chords of the long middle section never change, and the interest comes entirely from timbre and texture, exactly as in Neu!'s motorik pieces or La Monte Young's drone music. Sadier's lyrics throughout carry political content about consumerism and alienation that the sweetness disguises.","The album was admired in the British music press and helped establish Stereolab as one of the most distinctive independent bands of the 1990s. Critics were divided on whether its repetition was hypnotic or monotonous, a debate that krautrock had already had. Today it is recognised as a key document in krautrock's revival and an important link between that music and post-rock.","Put on 'Jenny Ondioline' and don't wait for a change: let the repetition carry you. Listen for how small shifts in organ or guitar tone keep the ear awake. Then play Neu!'s Neu! 75 and the Velvet Underground's White Light/White Heat to hear the two main sources, and Emperor Tomato Ketchup to hear where the band went next."],"stereolab-emperor-tomato-ketchup":["Thirteen songs that loosen the band's krautrock drive into something funkier and lighter. 'Metronomic Underground' rides a slinky, sampled-sounding groove for nearly eight minutes, 'Cybèle's Reverie' sets Sadier's French vocal over sweeping strings and a lilting 1960s pop arrangement, and 'Percolator' is short and bouncy. Farfisa and Moog organs, vibraphone and interlocking voices ('ba-ba-ba' counter-melodies from Sadier and Mary Hansen) stay central. The record sounds at once retro and futuristic, like a 1960s film soundtrack reimagined through Neu! and dub.","Released in March 1996, the album was made partly in Chicago with Tortoise's John McEntire, who engineered and co-produced some tracks and brought vibraphone and a lighter rhythmic touch. The title comes from the Japanese director Shūji Terayama's 1971 experimental film about children overthrowing adults, and the lyrics mix love songs with political reflection. It was Stereolab's most successful album to date, reaching a wide international audience.","The rhythm of 'Metronomic Underground' echoes the funk loops of hip-hop and the groove of Can, and the long repetitions give way to tiny shifts in the organ patterns rather than chord changes. Listen for Mary Hansen's counter-melodies, which often move against Sadier's line and make the vocals a kind of two-part counterpoint. The warm, dubby low end came partly from McEntire's Chicago approach.","Reviews were strong, and the album is widely seen as Stereolab's peak, bridging post-rock and pop. Some critics complained of style over substance, a charge often levelled at the band's retro eclecticism. It now appears on many lists of the best albums of the 1990s and remains a touchstone for bands mixing vintage keyboards with experimental structure.","Begin with 'Cybèle's Reverie' for the pop side and 'Metronomic Underground' for the groove. Listen for the interplay of the two female voices, and let the organs wash over you. Afterwards try Can's Ege Bamyasi and Serge Gainsbourg's Histoire de Melody Nelson, both cousins of this sound."],"stereolab-dots-and-loops":["The softest and most polished Stereolab album, where krautrock drone gives way to bossa nova, jazz-inflected pop and electronica. 'Brakhage' opens with a gentle, gliding groove, 'Miss Modular' bounces on a Burt Bacharach-style horn chart, and 'The Flower Called Nowhere' floats on strings and harpsichord-like keyboards. The long 'Refractions in the Plastic Pulse' runs about seventeen minutes, moving through several contrasting sections like a suite. Guitars are often in the background; keyboards, vibraphone, horns and the two women's voices lead.","Stereolab split the recording between Chicago, again with John McEntire, and Düsseldorf, with Andi Toma and Jan St. Werner of the electronic duo Mouse on Mars. The combination of Chicago post-rock craftsmanship and German electronic editing gives the album its crisp, detailed sound. Released in September 1997, it was their most commercially successful release in Britain, while some older fans missed the noise of the early records.","'Brakhage' is named after the American experimental filmmaker Stan Brakhage, whose hand-painted films influenced the band's thinking about abstraction and texture. Listen to how the edits in 'Refractions in the Plastic Pulse' jump from one section to the next without transition, like cuts in film. The Mouse on Mars tracks contain small electronic glitches and clicks that sit inside the retro arrangements like a modern signature.","Critics were largely enthusiastic, praising the album's warmth and sophistication, though some thought the band had become too smooth. Over time it has been increasingly admired as a crossover record, drawing in listeners who came to it from electronic and lounge music, and it is frequently named alongside Emperor Tomato Ketchup as Stereolab's finest work.","Put on 'Brakhage' and let the gentle groove set the mood. Then take 'Refractions in the Plastic Pulse' as a suite, noticing each section change. Compare Tortoise's TNT, made in the same Chicago studio, and the bossa nova and exotica records the band loved; then Yellow Magic Orchestra's BGM for another retro-futurist blend."],"low-i-could-live-in-hope":["Low's debut album: eleven songs, each at a walking pace or slower, including 'Words', 'Lullaby', 'Slide', 'Down' and a closing, heavily slowed cover of the old standard 'You Are My Sunshine', here titled 'Sunshine'. Alan Sparhawk's guitar is clean and reverberant, John Nichols's bass sits low and still, and Mimi Parker plays a tiny kit with brushes, often just a snare and cymbal. The two voices sing in close harmony, often on the same syllable, so that the music seems suspended. Nothing is hurried and there are no solos.","Sparhawk and Parker, married and practising Mormons in Duluth, Minnesota, formed Low partly as a reaction against the loud grunge that dominated American rock in the early 1990s. The album was recorded with the producer Kramer at his Noise New Jersey studio, known for work with Galaxie 500, and released in 1994 by Vernon Yard, a Virgin imprint. The press quickly grouped Low with Codeine and Red House Painters under the new label of slowcore, a term the band disliked.","The close harmony is often not a third or a sixth apart, as in most pop, but an open interval that sounds bare and ancient, closer to plainsong or Appalachian shape-note singing than to rock. Listen to Parker's drumming: the brushes seem to push the tempo back rather than forward. The cover of 'Sunshine', a children's song of love and loss, reveals how sad its words are when slowed right down.","The album received respectful but modest attention, some critics mocking the slowness as a gimmick. Over the years it has come to be seen as the defining statement of slowcore, and the band's long career has made its opening gesture look like a manifesto. Later musicians from Mogwai to Robert Plant, who recorded their songs, have spoken of its influence.","Listen late at night, with no other sound, and allow each song to take its time. Notice how the two voices breathe together. Start with 'Words', the first track, then jump to 'Sunshine'. Afterwards compare Nick Drake's Pink Moon, another hushed and bare record, and Arvo Pärt's Für Alina for a similar spiritual stillness."],"low-things-we-lost-in-the-fire":["Twelve songs, fuller and warmer than Low's early records while remaining slow. 'Sunflower' opens with bright guitar and a haunting, story-like harmony; 'Dinosaur Act' is louder and more insistent, driven by distorted guitar; 'Laser Beam' is a short, shivering vocal by Mimi Parker; 'Whitetail' and 'Closer' are delicate and intimate. Strings, piano, trumpet and organ colour many songs, and the bassist Zak Sally gives the band a thicker bottom end. The album ends with 'In Metal', a lullaby over a warped tape loop.","Recorded in Chicago with Steve Albini, known for the raw sound of Nirvana's In Utero and Slint's Spiderland, and released in January 2001 by Kranky. Albini's approach — microphones in the room, minimal effects — gave the album a clarity and presence that suited Low's harmonies. By now the band had toured widely and built a devoted audience, and Sparhawk and Parker had become parents, which shaped several songs.","'In Metal' was written about their young daughter, and the song's weary tenderness reflects the strain of touring life with a baby; listen for the looped, wavering accompaniment that makes the lullaby seem fragile. Albini's recording captures the tiny sounds of fingers on strings and breathing between phrases.","Reviews were strong, and many critics named it Low's best work, praising the balance of intimacy and weight. It brought the band a wider audience and remains a fan favourite. Some older admirers missed the extreme sparseness of the early records, but over time its songs have become among the most covered in Low's catalogue.","Start with 'Sunflower', then 'Dinosaur Act' to hear how loud Low can be at a slow pace. Close with 'In Metal' and listen to the tape loop behind the voice. Compare Neil Young's Tonight's the Night for raw emotional honesty, and Elliott Smith's Either/Or for intimacy in close focus."],"low-double-negative":["Eleven tracks in which Low's voices are buried in digital static, clipping and pulsing distortion. 'Quorum' opens with a sound like a speaker being torn apart, the harmonies barely surfacing; 'Dancing and Blood' is built on a throbbing low pulse; 'Fly' lets Mimi Parker's voice emerge almost clean, like a hymn through bad radio reception; 'Always Trying to Work It Out' and 'Disarray' let melody break through. The album plays continuously, with little separation between songs, like a single broadcast from a collapsing world.","Low made it in Eau Claire, Wisconsin, with the producer BJ Burton, known for work with Bon Iver, who pushed the band to treat recording and processing as composition. Released in September 2018 by Sub Pop, it reflected the anxieties of American political life in those years. It came twenty-five years into the band's career, a rare case of a veteran group making its most radical album so late.","The distortion is not a surface effect but the structure: many songs began as simple vocal harmonies, then were pushed through digital processes until the processing itself became the arrangement. Listen for moments when the voices come through clearly; they are placed as the emotional turning points. The result sits close to Burial's crackling textures and William Basinski's disintegrating tape loops, though Low arrived there from the opposite direction, starting with song.","Critics greeted it with astonishment and some of the best reviews of the band's career; it appeared near the top of many year-end lists for 2018. Some longtime fans found it hard going, missing the clean harmonies. After Mimi Parker's death in 2022, the album and its follow-up, Hey What (2021), came to be seen as a final, courageous reinvention.","Hear it complete, loud, on good headphones. Don't fight the noise; listen for the harmonies underneath, like voices in a storm. 'Fly' is the tender centre. Then play Burial's Untrue and Radiohead's Kid A, and go back to I Could Live in Hope to feel how far the band travelled."],"swans-children-of-god":["A double album that sets two opposites side by side. Tracks such as 'New Mind' and 'Sex, God, Sex' are brutal and slow, a hammering bass and drums repeating a single figure while Michael Gira chants; others, such as 'In My Garden' and 'Blood and Honey', are quiet, acoustic and hymnlike, with Jarboe's voice and piano, almost like sacred song. The album moves between violence and devotion, and the repetitive, ritual structures make the listener feel inside a ceremony rather than a rock record.","Swans had begun in the no-wave aftermath of early-1980s New York, making some of the loudest, slowest music of the decade. By 1987 the band had grown to include the singer and keyboardist Jarboe and had softened its sound. The album was made against a background of American televangelism and Christian conservatism, and it explores religion, power and submission. It was released in Britain on Product Inc. and in America on Caroline.","Religious imagery runs throughout, but the band neither mocks nor endorses faith; many songs turn the language of devotion toward human relationships and power. Listen for the shift in 'Sex, God, Sex' where the mood swings from pounding force to near-silence. The acoustic songs were a surprise at the time, and the alternation of loud and quiet across the album is as structured as any post-rock record that followed.","Critics were impressed by the album's range, calling it a turning point from the noise of the early records. Some early fans resented the gentler songs. Over time it has been recognised as one of the key records of the American underground, a bridge between no wave and the long-form, ritual rock of Swans' later career.","Start with 'New Mind' to feel the force, then 'In My Garden' for the other side. Listen for how Gira's voice is used like a drone, and how the repetition gradually overwhelms you. Compare Glenn Branca's The Ascension for massed guitar drone, and Joy Division's Closer for a darker, similar gravity."],"swans-soundtracks-for-the-blind":["A double CD of more than two hours, twenty-six tracks mixing songs, long drones, found recordings and live material. Some pieces are hypnotic, pounding rock such as 'The Sound' and 'I Was a Prisoner in Your Skull'; others are collages of tape loops, voices and ambient noise. Jarboe and Michael Gira share the vocals. The album moves like a dream or a feverish journey through memory, with nothing predictable, and the long, droning crescendos anticipate the music that post-rock bands were then making.","Gira intended it as Swans' final album, and the band did break up after the accompanying tour in 1997. It was released on his own Young God label and assembled from years of recordings, including home tapes, live recordings and studio sessions. By the mid-1990s post-rock was emerging, and Swans' record took part in that conversation from a different, darker direction.","Among the collaged fragments are personal and found tapes from Gira's and Jarboe's archives, so private memory is buried inside the noise. Listen for voices appearing briefly and then dissolving into drones. The album was assembled somewhat like a film soundtrack, as the title suggests, meant to create images in the listener's mind without pictures.","The album was admired but little known on release, and its length and difficulty kept it a cult item. Its reputation grew as Swans' reunion in 2010 made the band's catalogue newly relevant. It is now often cited as their most ambitious work before the reunion, and as a bridge between their early noise and the later epic albums.","Take it in parts, one disc at a time, ideally at night. Begin with 'The Sound' for the pounding core, then let the collage pieces carry you. Compare Brian Eno's On Land for ambient memory-scapes, and Godspeed You! Black Emperor's F♯ A♯ ∞ for another use of found tape."],"swans-the-seer":["Two hours of music in eleven tracks, the centrepiece the title piece, which runs about thirty-two minutes. It builds from a single repeated chord and chanting into a vast, pounding ritual, with bagpipe-like drones and tuned percussion, before collapsing. 'Lunacy' opens the album with harmonies from Low's Alan Sparhawk and Mimi Parker; 'Song for a Warrior' is a quiet ballad sung by Karen O; 'A Piece of the Sky' and 'Apostate' are long, shifting pieces. The music is monumental, patient and relentless, built on a few chords stretched over enormous spans.","Michael Gira re-formed Swans in 2010 after more than a decade away, with a line-up including Norman Westberg, Christoph Hahn, Thor Harris and Phil Puleo. Their concerts became famous for their volume and length. The Seer, released in August 2012 on Young God, was the second album of the reunion and was partly funded by a limited, hand-made live album sold to fans in advance. It found a new, younger audience.","Many passages grew out of long live improvisations and were then shaped in the studio, so the album is half performance and half composition. The chanting and repetitions draw on minimalism: listen for how the repeated chord in 'The Seer' slowly changes in weight as instruments are added, the same principle as Terry Riley's In C. Jarboe, who had left the band, returns for a vocal on the album, linking it to the older Swans.","Critics received it with near-unanimous acclaim, and it was named among the best albums of 2012. Its length divided some listeners, but it is now regarded as the high point of the Swans reunion and one of the major rock records of its decade. Many writers see it as the moment Swans became a true experimental orchestra rather than a rock band.","Start with 'Lunacy' for the harmonies, then give 'The Seer' its full half-hour, preferably loud. Listen to how the chords grow heavier without changing. Compare Godspeed You! Black Emperor's Lift Your Skinny Fists, Terry Riley's In C for the principle of repetition, and La Monte Young's drones."],"explosions-in-the-sky-the-earth-is-not-a-cold-dead-place":["Five long instrumentals: 'First Breath After Coma', 'The Only Moment We Were Alone', 'Six Days at the Bottom of the Ocean', 'Memorial' and 'Your Hand in Mine'. The opening track begins with a heartbeat-like guitar pulse and builds through interlocking tremolo lines into a cathartic surge. The sound is bright and clean, with three guitars weaving melodies over a steady bass and snare-roll drums. 'Your Hand in Mine', the closer, is gentle and hopeful, the band's most widely heard piece, and the album as a whole moves from tension toward release.","Explosions in the Sky formed in Austin, Texas, in 1999 and released their third album in November 2003 on the small independent label Temporary Residence. Post-rock had become a recognised genre, and the band made a more accessible, emotional version of it without the political framing of Godspeed or the jazz of Tortoise. The following year their music scored the film Friday Night Lights, which brought them a much larger audience.","The opening track's pulse is often described as a heartbeat, suggesting a body waking, and the album's arc from coma to the joined hands of the final track is a narrative of recovery. Listen for how the three guitars divide roles: one sustains, one plays tremolo, one plays melody, so the texture remains transparent even at its loudest. The drums rely on military snare rolls, giving the climaxes a processional quality.","The album was widely praised by fans and critics, who found it affecting and direct. Others criticised it, and the band's later work, for turning the post-rock crescendo into a formula, and the genre's critics often use the band as their example. Nonetheless it is regarded as one of the most beloved post-rock albums and a gateway for many listeners.","Start with 'First Breath After Coma' and follow each guitar line as it enters. Notice how the build is achieved by adding layers, not speeding up. End with 'Your Hand in Mine'. Compare Mogwai's Young Team, the model for the dynamics, and Max Richter's On the Nature of Daylight, a later piece with similar emotional aims."],"boredoms-super-ae":["An album of long, driving, chant-filled pieces with titles built around the word 'Super'. Pounding drums, often several kits playing together, set up a trance-like pulse while guitars and electronics roar and Yamantaka Eye yells and chants. The music moves in cycles, building and dissolving, and the effect is less a set of songs than a single ritual journey. Compared with the band's earlier chaos, the album is more focused and hypnotic, with long passages of repetition leading to ecstatic peaks.","Boredoms had built a reputation through the late 1980s and early 1990s as wild noise rockers, touring with Nirvana and Sonic Youth. By the late 1990s they had become interested in psychedelia, trance and ritual, influenced by Can and Neu! as well as by drumming traditions. Super æ was released by Warner Japan in 1998 and found an international audience through the growing interest in Japanese experimental rock.","The repetitive rhythms are built on the same kind of motorik pulse that Neu! and Can used, but multiplied through several drummers, so the rhythmic texture is dense and slightly unstable. Listen for how the drummers slip in and out of sync. Eye's shouts are not lyrics but vocal percussion and chanting, so the voice functions as another drum.","Critics outside Japan treated it as a surprising reinvention, praising its energy and trance. Some fans of the band's chaotic early records missed the humour. It is now regarded as a key record in the band's shift toward ritual drumming and as a bridge between noise rock and psychedelic trance.","Play it loud and let the drumming carry you. Listen for when new layers enter and when the pulse shifts. Compare Can's Tago Mago and Neu!'s Neu! for the groove, then Boredoms' own Vision Creation Newsun for the next step, and Reich's Drumming for the classical cousin."],"boredoms-vision-creation-newsun":["A continuous suite of pieces, most titled with symbols rather than words, the whole album moving like a single piece of music. Bright, repeating guitar figures and electronic drones sit over a bed of pounding, interlocking drums. The music climbs toward ecstatic peaks, then drifts into shimmering, ambient passages. Compared with Super æ, it is warmer and more melodic, with a glowing, sunlike quality in the sound. Listeners recognise it by the joyful, cycling guitars and the sense of the whole band moving together in a trance.","Released by Warner Japan in 1999 and later in the United States, it capped Boredoms' turn from noise toward trance and ritual. Yamantaka Eye had become fascinated by the sun as a symbol and by ideas of cosmic energy, and the band played increasingly long, drum-driven shows. The album reflects late-1990s interest in rave culture, trance and psychedelia, filtered through the Japanese underground.","The symbolic track titles, circles, stars and arrows, are meant to be read as pictures rather than words, and suggest the music's visual, cosmic imagery. Listen for tape loops and edits that splice sections together, a technique borrowed from Can's and Faust's studio methods. The joyful cycling guitars echo Terry Riley's A Rainbow in Curved Air in their bright repetition.","Critics outside Japan praised it as the band's peak, and it appears often on lists of the best experimental rock albums. Some found it too long or unfocused, but most regard it as a unique fusion of noise, psychedelia and trance. It influenced bands such as Animal Collective and helped build international interest in Japanese experimental music.","Play it all the way through, loud, and let the sections blur into each other. Listen for the moment the drums lock in and the guitars begin to cycle. Compare Terry Riley's A Rainbow in Curved Air for bright repetition, and Can's Future Days for flowing, sunlit groove."],"codeine-frigid-stars":["Ten slow, heavy songs, among them 'D', 'Gravel Bed', 'Pickup Song', 'New Year's' and 'Cigarette Machine'. The sound is spare: a distorted guitar chord struck and left to hang, a bass line moving slowly underneath, drums placed sparingly, and Stephen Immerwahr's voice, weary and close, singing of small defeats and loneliness. The songs are built on tension and release, with long pauses between chords, and the effect is claustrophobic and melancholic. A first-time listener remembers the gaps between sounds as much as the sounds.","Codeine formed in New York in 1989 and released their debut first on the German label Glitterhouse in 1990, then on Sub Pop in America in 1991, at the moment Sub Pop was becoming famous for grunge. Codeine were the opposite of grunge's energy, and the press soon grouped them with Low and Red House Painters under the name slowcore. The album was made cheaply, with minimal overdubs.","The songs use loud and quiet in a similar way to Slint, who were working in Louisville at the same time, but slower. Listen for how the drummer lets silences fall between beats, giving each chord its full weight. The lyrics describe everyday failures and emotional numbness without melodrama, and the delivery is deliberately flat.","The album was little noticed at the time, but it gained a cult following and is now regarded as a founding slowcore record. Critics praised its emotional honesty and heaviness. A 2012 reissue by the Numero Group brought it to a new audience and confirmed its status as a lost classic.","Listen in a quiet room and focus on the space between the notes. Start with 'D' and 'Pickup Song'. Compare Slint's Spiderland, released the next year, and Low's I Could Live in Hope for the gentler version of the same idea, and Neil Young's Tonight's the Night for raw sorrow."],"codeine-the-white-birch":["The second and final album, slower and more delicate than Frigid Stars. Songs such as 'Sea', 'Loss Leader', 'Washed Up' and 'Tom' place gentle, clean guitar next to crushing distortion, with Stephen Immerwahr's voice close and tired. Doug Scharin's drumming is more varied than before, with subtle cymbal work and moments of power. Piano appears on some tracks. The mood is bleak, reflective and wintry, and each song unfolds with great patience, sometimes pausing for long stretches before the next chord arrives.","Recorded after the drummer Chris Brokaw left to focus on his band Come, and released by Sub Pop in 1994. The band was worn down by touring and internal tension, and split soon after the album's release. The album appeared at the height of grunge's commercial success, and its restraint and slowness made it an outlier on Sub Pop's roster.","Listen for how the distorted chords are not played quickly but allowed to decay fully before the next chord, turning the natural sustain of the amplifier into part of the composition. The silence between phrases is carefully timed, much like Talk Talk's late records. The title evokes a stark winter image, and the whole album has a quality of coldness and stillness.","The album was praised by the few critics who noticed it, and it has since become a cult classic. Many consider it Codeine's finest record, deeper and more varied than the debut. The Numero Group reissue in 2012 prompted new appreciation, and it is often cited as a key influence on later slowcore and post-rock bands.","Start with 'Sea', then let the album play through in order, in a quiet room. Listen for how the drums shape each song's slow build, and how long each chord is allowed to ring before the next. Compare Talk Talk's Laughing Stock for a similar use of silence, and Mogwai's Come On Die Young, which learned from it."],"red-house-painters-down-colorful-hill":["Six long songs: '24', 'Medicine Bottle', 'Down Colorful Hill', 'Japanese to English', 'Lord Kill the Pain' and 'Michael'. Mark Kozelek's voice is low and unhurried, singing of lost love, the passing of time and old friends, over slowly ringing electric guitars and restrained drums. The songs take their time, some lasting nine or ten minutes, and the arrangements are spare, with little change in dynamics. The mood is sorrowful and reflective, like listening to someone think aloud.","Kozelek, originally from Ohio, had moved to San Francisco and formed Red House Painters. Mark Eitzel of American Music Club passed their demo tape to Ivo Watts-Russell of the British label 4AD, known for Cocteau Twins and Dead Can Dance. 4AD released the demos almost as they were in 1992, which gives the album its raw, intimate sound. The press grouped the band with Low and Codeine under slowcore.","The album was taken from demo recordings, so its intimacy is partly an accident of the process. Listen to how Kozelek's voice is placed close to the microphone, making the listener feel near to him. The song '24' reflects on growing older while still young, a theme that returns throughout his later work. The long guitar codas let the mood settle rather than reaching a climax.","The album was admired in Britain, where 4AD had a strong following, and it established Kozelek as a significant songwriter. Some critics found it too slow and self-absorbed, but it is now regarded as a key slowcore record. Kozelek's later behaviour has complicated his reputation, but the album's standing remains.","Start with '24' and follow the long, slow guitar lines. Listen late at night and let the songs unfold. Compare Nick Drake's Pink Moon for intimacy, Leonard Cohen's Songs of Leonard Cohen for confessional depth, and Low's I Could Live in Hope for slowcore kinship."],"red-house-painters-rollercoaster":["The band's first self-titled album, known as Rollercoaster from its cover photograph of a fairground ride. It holds fourteen songs in over seventy minutes, including 'Grace Cathedral Park', 'Katy Song', 'Mistress', 'Funhouse', 'New Jersey' and 'Things Mean a Lot'. Kozelek's songs are long, slow and intensely personal, dealing with love, betrayal, childhood and loneliness. The sound is fuller than the debut, with piano and richer guitar layering, but still spare, with long codas that let the emotion settle.","Released by 4AD in May 1993, a few months before a second self-titled album known as Bridge, both drawn from the same batch of recordings. Kozelek was living in San Francisco, and many songs reflect the city and his relationships there. The album brought the band wider attention and is often considered its peak, arriving as slowcore was being recognised as a genre.","Listen to how the long songs, such as 'Katy Song', use repetition to build emotional weight rather than dynamic change. 'Grace Cathedral Park' names a real place in San Francisco, grounding the song in the city. The album's length is part of its effect: it asks for patience, and rewards it with a sense of immersion in someone's inner life.","Critics praised it as a major work of 1990s songwriting, and it remains the album most fans name first. Some reviewers found its length and unrelieved sadness excessive, and the decision to release two albums under the same title in one year confused buyers. It is now regarded as one of the defining slowcore records and a clear influence on later indie-folk and sad-rock songwriters, from Bon Iver to Phoebe Bridgers.","Start with 'Katy Song', eight minutes of slowly accumulating heartbreak, then 'Grace Cathedral Park'. Listen for how Kozelek holds back his voice and lets the guitar codas do the emotional work. Play the rest in order on an evening with time to spare. Compare Joni Mitchell's Blue for confessional intimacy and Elliott Smith's Either/Or for another inward-looking songwriter of the same decade."],"do-make-say-think-winter-hymn-country-hymn-secret-hymn":["An instrumental album of warm, jazz-inflected post-rock, with pieces such as 'Fredericia', 'Classic Noodlanders', 'Horns of a Rabbit' and 'Auberge le Mouton Noir'. Two drummers play together, building loose, rolling grooves under chiming guitars, with trumpet and saxophone adding colour. The pieces build gradually, often to joyful peaks, and the sound is organic and communal, with tape hiss and room ambience. It feels less like a studio construction than a group of friends playing together in a room.","Do Make Say Think formed in Toronto in the mid-1990s and released their records on Montreal's Constellation, the same label as Godspeed You! Black Emperor. This fourth album was released in 2003, when post-rock was at its commercial height. The band's members were involved in the wider Toronto scene, including Broken Social Scene, and the album reflects a spirit of collaboration and openness.","The two drummers often play slightly different patterns, creating a rich, shifting rhythmic texture; listen for how they interlock and diverge. Horns enter as colour rather than lead, like a big band softly in the background. The album's title suggests three kinds of hymn, and the music has a devotional, communal quality without being religious.","Critics praised the album as one of the band's best, and it is often recommended as the entry point to their catalogue. Some reviewers found it too gentle and meandering beside the intensity of Godspeed You! Black Emperor. It has since come to stand for the warmer, jazzier side of post-rock, and for the Toronto scene that also produced Broken Social Scene.","Start with 'Fredericia' and follow the two drummers, one in each speaker, as they lean against each other. Then let the horns and guitars of the longer pieces wash over you, ideally outdoors or on a long drive. Compare Tortoise's TNT for jazz-inflected post-rock, and Miles Davis's In a Silent Way for a similar blend of groove, patience and atmosphere."],"bardo-pond-amanita":["An album of slow, heavy psychedelic drones, where guitar feedback piles into thick, molten walls of sound and Isobel Sollenberger's voice and flute drift through the fog. The pieces are long and repetitive, built on a few chords stretched out and saturated with distortion, and the effect is hypnotic, like being immersed in warm sludge. The flute is the recognisable element, a thin, airy line floating over roaring guitars, lending a pastoral quality to the noise.","Bardo Pond formed in Philadelphia in the early 1990s around the brothers John and Michael Gibbons. They signed to Matador, an important independent label, and released Amanita in 1996, their second album. The record belongs to a wave of American neo-psychedelia and drone rock, influenced by Spacemen 3, Hawkwind and Sonic Youth. Its title refers to the hallucinogenic mushroom, a neutral clue to the band's psychedelic interests.","The band's name joins the Tibetan Buddhist 'bardo', the state between death and rebirth, with a pond; the music, suspended and hazy, reflects this idea of an in-between state. Listen for how Sollenberger's flute plays long, simple phrases that echo the guitar drones. The heavy distortion was achieved with amplifiers pushed to their limits, creating overtones that sound like additional instruments.","Critics admired the album's heaviness and atmosphere, and it built a loyal cult following through college radio and the Matador catalogue. Some reviewers found the long pieces undifferentiated and the fog too thick to see through. It is now regarded as a key record of 1990s American drone rock, and a quiet influence on later heavy psychedelic and doom-drone bands.","Play it loud and let the drones envelop you rather than waiting for songs to arrive. Follow the flute as a thread through the noise, and notice how the guitars thicken and thin like weather. Compare My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for walls of guitar, Sonic Youth's Daydream Nation for noisy textures, and La Monte Young for drone in its purest form."],"green-day-dookie":["Fourteen songs in about forty minutes, almost none longer than three and a half. Billie Joe Armstrong sings in a nasal, half-sneering East Bay voice over his own buzzing guitar; Mike Dirnt's bass runs melodic lines rather than root notes; Tré Cool drums with a snare that cracks like a whip. 'Longview' opens on a lone walking bass, 'Basket Case' races through a chord pattern borrowed from the oldest pop, and 'When I Come Around' slows to a loping mid-tempo shuffle.","Green Day had grown up in the Berkeley punk scene around the 924 Gilman Street club and made two cheap records for the local Lookout! label. Signing to Reprise and working with producer Rob Cavallo counted as betrayal at Gilman, which shut its doors to them. Released in February 1994, two months before Kurt Cobain's death, the album arrived just as MTV and alternative radio were hungry for guitars that were loud but not gloomy, and it sold in the tens of millions.","'Basket Case', which sounds like the album's brightest song, is Armstrong describing his own panic attacks before he knew what they were; the bounce is a disguise for dread. 'Welcome to Paradise' is a re-recording of a song from the band's Lookout! album, sharpened by a bigger studio. After the closing track 'F.O.D.' a hidden extra song, sung by Tré Cool, turns up once the record seems to have ended.","Punk purists called it a sell-out and a cartoon of the Ramones and Buzzcocks, and many critics filed it as disposable teenage fun. It won a Grammy for best alternative performance and turned pop-punk into a mass genre that shaped a decade of American guitar music. Judgment has since warmed: it is now heard as a model of economical songwriting, and the argument about whether punk can survive a major label now looks settled by its example.","Start with 'Longview' and follow Dirnt's bass, which carries the tune while the guitar only punches in. Then play 'Basket Case' beside Buzzcocks' 'Ever Fallen in Love' to hear where the speed and the self-pity come from. Listen to how every song ends almost the moment its idea is spent; the brevity is the craft. The original CD mix is loud and dry, which suits it."],"green-day-american-idiot":["A punk rock opera in thirteen tracks. It follows a character called the Jesus of Suburbia, who leaves a dead-end town for the city, meets a destructive alter ego named St. Jimmy and a rebellious woman called Whatsername, and comes home defeated. Two nine-minute suites, 'Jesus of Suburbia' and 'Homecoming', each stitch five short songs together like movements. Between them sit the hits: the furious title track, the trudging 'Boulevard of Broken Dreams', the anthemic 'Holiday'.","Green Day were in their thirties and commercially fading when they began a different album; the master tapes of that project reportedly went missing, and the band started over with Rob Cavallo. The United States was in the middle of the Iraq war and heading into the 2004 election, and the album's anger at television news and national complacency was pointed. Released in September 2004, it won the Grammy for best rock album and later became a Broadway musical in 2010.","The idea of the suites came from the band trading half-minute song fragments as a joke, which they then joined into one continuous piece in the manner of the Who's mini-opera 'A Quick One'. 'Wake Me Up When September Ends' is not about the story at all but about Armstrong's father, who died of cancer when the singer was ten; it was folded into the narrative only afterwards.","Critics who had written the band off were startled; many ranked it among the best records of the decade and credited it with giving mainstream rock a political voice under George W. Bush. Sceptics found the story thin and the anthems overblown, and the Broadway version reopened the argument about punk becoming spectacle. It remains the band's biggest late success and the model for a wave of concept records by younger pop-punk and emo bands.","Play 'Jesus of Suburbia' straight through and count the five sections; notice how the key and tempo change at each seam. Then compare it with the Who's Tommy and Pink Floyd's The Wall, the two big rock operas it openly admires. Hear 'Boulevard of Broken Dreams' as the story's low point rather than as a single, and the album gains a shape."],"blink-182-enema-of-the-state":["Twelve songs in thirty-five minutes, bright and compressed. Mark Hoppus (bass) and Tom DeLonge (guitar) trade lead vocals, Hoppus warm and flat, DeLonge pinched and nasal; new drummer Travis Barker plays fast, intricate fills that turn simple punk beats into something close to showmanship. 'What's My Age Again?' opens on a single clean guitar riff, 'All the Small Things' is built on a playground chant, and 'Adam's Song' drops the jokes for a slow, bleak study of loneliness.","Blink-182 came from the suburbs of San Diego and had spent years on skate-punk tours and small labels. For their third album they signed to MCA, hired producer Jerry Finn for a glossy, radio-ready sound, and brought in Barker from the Aquabats. Released in June 1999, at the height of teen pop and MTV's Total Request Live, it sold in the millions; the video for 'All the Small Things' mocked the very boy bands it was competing against.","Behind the toilet humour sits careful pop craft: Finn double-tracked the guitars and shaped the vocals like a pop record, which is why the album sounds nothing like the band's earlier, scrappier work. 'Adam's Song' was written about the isolation of long tours and deals with suicidal thoughts; its tone shift is the album's real hinge. Listen to Barker's drumming on 'Dumpweed', the opener: he is already outplaying the songs.","Critics largely dismissed it as juvenile and its cover photograph as a cheap joke, and punk scenes regarded the band as a mall-rock product. Yet it defined how American suburban adolescence sounded at the millennium, and a generation of emo and pop-punk bands, and later pop stars, cite it as a first love. Recent writing takes its melodic craft and Barker's playing seriously, while still arguing over whether its humour has aged.","Start with 'All the Small Things' and notice how little is in it: two voices, a chant, a relentless eighth-note pulse. Then play 'Adam's Song' to hear the band's other face. Follow Barker's fills at the end of each phrase, the most musically inventive thing on the record. Compare with Green Day's Dookie, five years older, to hear how much cleaner pop-punk had become."],"blink-182-blink-182":["The band's untitled fifth album is darker, slower and far more varied than anything they had made. Guitars are layered into dense walls, drums are sometimes looped or treated, and keyboards and atmospheric textures fill the gaps. 'Feeling This' opens with Hoppus and DeLonge singing overlapping parts; 'I Miss You' replaces distortion with acoustic bass, brushed drums and piano; 'All of This' features Robert Smith of the Cure; 'Always' borrows the glossy synths of 1980s pop.","After years of joke videos, the three members had drifted into side projects and wanted to be taken seriously. With Jerry Finn again producing, they rented a house near San Diego and spent much of 2003 building songs in the studio rather than rehearsing them first. Released that November on Geffen, it was a hit, but tension over the new direction and DeLonge's other interests led to an indefinite hiatus in early 2005.","The influence here is not punk at all but British post-punk and goth: the Cure, Joy Division and the atmospheric side of 1980s alternative rock, which is why Robert Smith agreed to appear. 'I Miss You' borrows its imagery from Tim Burton's The Nightmare Before Christmas, a gothic-cartoon reference that sums up the album's mood. Listen, too, for Travis Barker's drum parts, which lean on hip-hop grooves rather than punk beats.","Reviewers who had mocked the band were surprised, and many called it a leap in maturity. Fans were divided between those who missed the jokes and those who heard a new seriousness. Over time 'I Miss You' became one of the band's most loved songs, and the album is often credited with bridging pop-punk and the more theatrical emo that dominated the mid-2000s. It is now widely considered their most adventurous record.","Play 'I Miss You' first and notice the absence of distorted guitar; the song is built on bass and brushes. Then 'Feeling This' for the old energy with new layers. Listen to 'All of This' next to Joy Division's 'Atmosphere' and hear where the gloom comes from. Treat it as the midpoint between Enema of the State and My Chemical Romance."],"weezer-blue-album":["Ten songs of heavy, fuzzy guitar pop. Rivers Cuomo's high, earnest voice rides over crunching distortion, and the band stacks backing harmonies that owe more to the Beach Boys than to punk. 'My Name Is Jonas' opens with fingerpicked acoustic guitar before the fuzz crashes in; 'Buddy Holly' pairs a doo-wop chorus with a crunching riff; 'Say It Ain't So' is built on a quiet, lazy verse and an explosive chorus; 'Only in Dreams' closes with an eight-minute slow build.","Weezer formed in Los Angeles in 1992 and signed to Geffen's DGC label. The album was recorded in New York in 1993 with Ric Ocasek of the Cars producing, who pushed for clean, punchy pop arrangements. Released in May 1994, when grunge still dominated rock radio, it offered something unexpected: nerdy, self-deprecating songs about sweaters, garages and Dungeons and Dragons, played with total sincerity. Spike Jonze's videos made the band stars on MTV.","Spike Jonze's video for 'Buddy Holly' spliced the band into footage from the 1970s sitcom Happy Days, and it was included on the Windows 95 installation CD, so millions of people first saw it on a new home computer. 'Say It Ain't So', which sounds like a laid-back slacker song, is in fact about Cuomo's fear of alcohol in his own family. Listen for how many songs end on long, overlapping vocal rounds.","Initially seen as a novelty act, the band were soon recognised for their melodic craft, and the album became a multi-platinum hit. It is now ranked among the defining records of 1990s alternative rock, and its blend of big guitars, geeky confession and pop harmony shaped emo and pop-punk for a generation. The band's later records have divided fans, which has only increased the Blue Album's standing.","Start with 'Say It Ain't So' and listen to the dynamic swing from lazy verse to roaring chorus; then 'Only in Dreams' for the long guitar climax. Play 'Buddy Holly' beside the real Buddy Holly's 'Peggy Sue' and hear the homage. Notice the stacked harmonies throughout, the thread back to Pet Sounds."],"weezer-pinkerton":["Ten raw, loud songs in thirty-five minutes, recorded with far less polish than the band's debut. The guitars are abrasive and overdriven, the drums thunder, and Rivers Cuomo's voice cracks and strains. 'Tired of Sex' opens with howling feedback; 'El Scorcho' lurches between a shambling verse and a shouted chorus; 'Across the Sea' responds to a letter from a young fan in Japan; the album ends with 'Butterfly', a quiet acoustic song of apology.","After the Blue Album's success, Cuomo abandoned a planned science-fiction rock opera, underwent painful leg surgery and enrolled at Harvard, where he felt isolated and wrote most of these songs. The band produced it themselves, wanting a live, unpolished sound. Released in September 1996, it sold poorly, received mixed reviews, and Cuomo later described it as a humiliating mistake. Its title refers to Lieutenant Pinkerton, the American naval officer who abandons his Japanese bride in Puccini's Madama Butterfly.","The whole album is loosely framed by Madama Butterfly: Cuomo saw himself in Pinkerton, the careless man who uses and discards women, and the closing song 'Butterfly' directly echoes the opera's ending. The Japanese imagery in 'Across the Sea' and on the cover, a woodblock print by Hiroshige, belongs to the same theme. Knowing this turns the confessional songs into an uncomfortable self-portrait rather than simple heartbreak.","Rolling Stone readers voted it one of the worst albums of 1996, and the band nearly broke up. Over the following decade it became a cult object, passed between fans and cited by emo bands as foundational; critics reversed themselves, and it is now often ranked above the Blue Album. Recent listeners also question its treatment of women and fans, making it a test case for how to hear confessional rock.","Play 'El Scorcho' and 'The Good Life' first for the album's wild energy, then 'Butterfly' to hear the mask come off. Listen to it once after reading a synopsis of Madama Butterfly, and the album's frame becomes clear. The original mix is deliberately harsh; do not expect the Blue Album's polish."],"jimmy-eat-world-clarity":["Thirteen songs, over an hour, moving between loud guitar anthems and quiet, textured pieces. Jim Adkins sings with clear, unforced sincerity; the band adds strings, chimes, drum loops and electronic textures to the basic rock lineup. 'Table for Glasses' opens with a hushed build; 'Lucky Denver Mint' is a punchy single; 'Just Watch the Fireworks' is a slow, swelling seven-minute piece; the album ends with 'Goodbye Sky Harbor', a sixteen-minute track built from a repeating, gradually transforming loop.","Jimmy Eat World came from Mesa, Arizona, and had signed to Capitol after years of small-label releases. Working with producer Mark Trombino, they took their time with an ambitious, layered album. Released in February 1999, it was poorly promoted and sold little; Capitol dropped the band soon after. Its fortunes turned slowly through word of mouth and file-sharing, and it became a touchstone of the late-1990s emo underground.","'Goodbye Sky Harbor', named after Phoenix's airport, was inspired by John Irving's novel A Prayer for Owen Meany. Its long, looping second half, where one short vocal and guitar figure repeats while textures accumulate, owes more to minimalism than to punk; listen to how little changes while the feeling keeps rising. 'Lucky Denver Mint' appeared in the film Never Been Kissed before the album had much of an audience.","The album was barely reviewed on release, and its commercial failure seemed to end the band's major-label career. In the years afterward, critics and musicians reassessed it as one of the defining emo records, praising its textural ambition and emotional directness. It is often named alongside American Football and Pinkerton as a cornerstone of the genre, and the band's later success sent many listeners back to it.","Start with 'Table for Glasses' for the quiet opening and then play 'Goodbye Sky Harbor' straight through, letting the loop work on you as a repetitive minimalist piece would. Compare it with Steve Reich's gradual processes. Then hear 'Lucky Denver Mint' for the band's pop side. Listen on headphones; the layered textures are easy to miss on small speakers."],"jimmy-eat-world-bleed-american":["Eleven tightly built songs with big, clean guitars and clear choruses. Jim Adkins sings with direct warmth, and the production by Mark Trombino gives the band the punch of radio rock without losing emotional detail. The opening title track slams in on a crunching riff; 'The Middle' is a buoyant two-and-a-half-minute pop-rock song; 'Sweetness' builds a wordless chant into a stadium chorus; 'Hear You Me' is a quiet ballad of loss.","After Capitol dropped them, the band paid for the recording themselves, working day jobs to fund it, then signed to DreamWorks. The album came out in July 2001. After the September 11 attacks, the label reissued it simply as Jimmy Eat World and renamed the title track 'Salt Sweat Sugar', fearing the original title might be misread. 'The Middle' became a major hit in 2002, bringing emo-inflected rock into mainstream radio.","'The Middle' was written as encouragement to a fan who had written to the band about feeling excluded by the punk scene; its reassurance, that things will be all right, is the song's whole message. 'Hear You Me' is a tribute to Mykel and Carli Allan, two sisters who ran a fan club for the band and died in a car accident in 1996. 'A Praise Chorus' openly quotes other songs within its bridge.","Reviewers praised its craftsmanship and directness, and it became the band's commercial breakthrough, selling over a million copies in America. It opened mainstream doors for emo bands in the early 2000s. Some underground fans saw the polish as a retreat from Clarity's ambition, but the record is widely regarded as a model of clean, emotionally direct guitar pop, and 'The Middle' remains one of the era's most recognisable songs.","Play 'The Middle' first for the song's compact pop structure and the moment the chorus arrives; then 'Sweetness' for the chant. Finish with 'Hear You Me' to hear the band's quieter heart. Compare with Weezer's Blue Album for the melodic lineage and with Clarity to hear what was gained and lost in the move to directness."],"my-chemical-romance-three-cheers-for-sweet-revenge":["Thirteen songs, loud and theatrical, loosely built around a story of two lovers separated by death, in which the man strikes a bargain to win back his beloved. Gerard Way's voice swings from croon to scream; Ray Toro's guitar solos and harmonised leads borrow from heavy metal; the band's twin guitars and fast drums come from punk and hardcore. 'Helena' is a grand, crashing requiem; 'I'm Not Okay (I Promise)' is a sardonic anthem; 'The Ghost of You' is a slow-burning ballad.","My Chemical Romance formed in New Jersey after Gerard Way watched the September 11 attacks from across the Hudson and decided to make music rather than comics. After one indie album, they signed to Reprise and worked with producer Howard Benson. Released in June 2004, it sold over a million copies in America, carried by MTV videos, Warped Tour appearances and a devoted online following on sites such as MySpace.","'Helena' is for Way's grandmother Elena, who taught him to sing and paint and died shortly before the recording; the music video, staged as a funeral with dancers in a church, sets the band's gothic imagery. The loose storyline links the album to their first record, and bits of it reappear in the videos. The ghostly spoken introduction and theatrical transitions show the rock-opera impulse that flowered two years later.","Reviews were mixed to positive, but fans embraced it fiercely, and it became the defining record of the mid-2000s emo wave. Critics debated whether the band were a serious act or a fashion; the theatrical make-up drew both mockery and imitation. The album is now recognised for bridging punk, metal and theatre, and many younger musicians cite it as a formative influence.","Start with 'Helena', noticing how the opening quiet gives way to a crash of guitars. Then 'I'm Not Okay (I Promise)' for the band's sarcasm and humour, and 'The Ghost of You' for the slow build. Listen for Ray Toro's harmonised guitar leads, a thread back to Queen and 1980s metal."],"my-chemical-romance-the-black-parade":["A concept album about the Patient, a man dying of cancer, for whom death arrives as a memory of watching a parade as a child. The sound is theatrical: pianos, marching drums, brass, strings, choirs and screaming guitars. 'Welcome to the Black Parade' opens on a single repeated piano note before swelling into a march and then a full rock anthem; 'Mama' becomes a cabaret waltz; 'Famous Last Words' closes the story in defiant, crashing rock.","Produced by Rob Cavallo, who had just made Green Day's American Idiot, the album was recorded in Los Angeles in 2006, reportedly in a house the band believed haunted. Released that October, it was a major commercial success; the band performed in black marching-band uniforms as their alter ego, the Black Parade, much as the Beatles had become Sgt. Pepper's band. In Britain it arrived at the centre of a tabloid panic about emo culture.","The influences are 1970s rock theatre: Queen's layered guitars and choral harmonies, Pink Floyd's The Wall, David Bowie's Ziggy Stardust, and the Beatles' Sgt. Pepper framing. Liza Minnelli sings a short part on 'Mama', a link to the cabaret tradition the band loved. The album opens not with music but with the beep of a hospital heart monitor on 'The End.', placing us at the Patient's bedside before the parade begins.","Critics were more impressed than they had been by the band's earlier work, many comparing it to Queen and The Wall. It sold millions and made the band stars far beyond emo. Detractors found it overblown and derivative, and it was caught up in moral panic in the British press. It is now widely regarded as the genre's grandest statement, and its title song is often ranked among the great rock anthems of the century.","Play 'Welcome to the Black Parade' first and listen for the transitions: piano, march, anthem. Then hear the album in order to follow the Patient's story. Compare with Queen's Bohemian Rhapsody and The Wall to hear the lineage. Notice the use of brass and choir, instruments emo had rarely touched."],"fall-out-boy-from-under-the-cork-tree":["Thirteen songs of punchy, hook-crammed pop-punk. Patrick Stump's soulful, agile voice, influenced by R&B singers, carries melodies full of leaps and runs; Joe Trohman's guitars and Andy Hurley's drums keep things tight and fast; Pete Wentz's lyrics are dense, sardonic and self-dramatising. 'Sugar, We're Goin Down' is built on a stuttering verse and an irresistible chorus; 'Dance, Dance' has a bouncing bassline; many songs have absurdly long titles that parody emo's earnestness.","Fall Out Boy came out of the Chicago suburban hardcore scene. Their second album, produced by Neal Avron and released in May 2005, went double platinum, driven by MTV and the band's use of MySpace and online communities. Wentz, the bassist and lyricist, became a tabloid celebrity. The title comes from The Story of Ferdinand, a children's book about a peaceful bull who prefers to sit under a cork tree rather than fight.","Stump, the band's singer and composer, has said he loved Michael Jackson, Prince and soul singers, which explains the R&B phrasing that separates Fall Out Boy from other pop-punk bands. Wentz wrote the lyrics, often long, clause-heavy sentences that Stump had to fit into melodies, which gives the songs their breathless rhythm. Listen for how often the vocal melody fights the beat and then snaps back.","Critics were divided; some found the band glib and image-driven, others praised Stump's voice and the album's craft. It made pop-punk's mid-2000s wave a mainstream force, and 'Sugar, We're Goin Down' remains one of the era's defining songs. Retrospective writing has credited the band with anticipating the blending of rock and pop that followed, as Stump's melodic sensibility has grown in reputation.","Start with 'Sugar, We're Goin Down' and listen to the way the verse stutters before the chorus lets go. Then 'Dance, Dance' for the bassline. Notice Stump's vocal runs, closer to R&B than punk. Compare with Blink-182's Enema of the State to hear how pop-punk's melodies became more sophisticated."],"paramore-riot":["Eleven fast, crisp songs built around Hayley Williams's powerful, precise voice, which cuts through dense guitars with a clarity rare in the genre. The band plays tight pop-punk with bursts of emo intensity. 'Misery Business' opens on a sharp riff and a rapid-fire vocal; 'crushcrushcrush' has a stop-start groove; 'That's What You Get' bounces; 'Hallelujah' builds to a soaring chorus. The production is polished but lively, with guitars stacked for maximum punch.","Paramore formed in Franklin, Tennessee, when Williams was a teenager; she had been signed by Atlantic as a solo artist but insisted on fronting a band. Riot!, their second album, was produced by David Bendeth and released in June 2007. 'Misery Business' became a hit, and the album went platinum. Williams became one of the few female leaders in a male-dominated scene, and the band's success opened doors for others.","Williams wrote 'Misery Business' about a real conflict from her teenage years; in 2018 she stopped performing it because she regretted a line judging another girl, and when she brought it back it was framed as a conversation with her younger self. Listen for Zac Farro's drumming, which gives the songs their bounce, and for how often Williams sings rhythmically against the beat rather than simply on it.","Reviews were generally positive, praising Williams's voice and the band's energy. Some critics dismissed it as teen fare, but it became a defining record for a generation of listeners, particularly young women who saw themselves in a genre that rarely centred them. Williams's later reflections on the album's lyrics have made it a case study in how artists revisit their youthful work.","Start with 'Misery Business' and listen to the precision of Williams's delivery, then 'Hallelujah' for the band's soaring side. Notice how the guitars stay tight and clean rather than muddy. Compare with Fall Out Boy's From Under the Cork Tree to hear a different voice in the same scene, and then jump to After Laughter to hear how far the band travelled."],"paramore-after-laughter":["Twelve songs of bright, 1980s-inspired new wave pop: chiming clean guitars, marimbas, synthesizers, funky bass and crisp drums. The music sounds sunny and danceable, but Hayley Williams's lyrics describe depression, exhaustion and grief. 'Hard Times' opens with a marimba riff over a bouncing beat; 'Told You So' has a skittering, tropical guitar; 'Fake Happy' begins as a fragile ballad before bursting into a disco chorus; 'Tell Me How' closes on quiet piano.","By 2017 Paramore had lost members, fought legal disputes, and seen Williams struggle with depression and a divorce. Drummer Zac Farro returned, and guitarist Taylor York co-produced the album with Justin Meldal-Johnsen, making it with a small crew in Nashville. Released in May 2017, it confirmed the band's shift from pop-punk to broader pop. It is a record about what happens after success and youth.","The sound draws on Talking Heads, Blondie, and the African guitar pop that influenced them, as well as 1980s synth pop. The marimba and clean, interlocking guitars in 'Hard Times' echo the polyrhythms of Remain in Light. The contrast between cheerful music and despairing words is the album's central trick; listen to 'Rose-Colored Boy' and notice how bouncy the music stays while the singer refuses to cheer up.","Critics praised the album as the band's most mature work and a successful reinvention. Some older fans missed the guitars, but most welcomed the change. It is often cited as an example of the 2010s turn toward honesty about mental health in pop music, and Williams's candour in interviews about depression added to its significance.","Start with 'Hard Times' and listen to the marimba and clean guitars interlocking; then 'Fake Happy', noticing the shift from ballad to dance. Play it beside Talking Heads' Remain in Light to hear the debt. Pay attention to the lyrics' darkness against the music's brightness, the gap is the point."],"the-white-stripes-white-blood-cells":["Sixteen short songs by a two-piece: Jack White's slashing, overdriven guitar and yelping voice, Meg White's primitive, pounding drums. No bass. The songs move between garage rock, blues and country-tinged folk. 'Dead Leaves and the Dirty Ground' opens with a crunching riff; 'Fell in Love with a Girl' is under two minutes of fury; 'Hotel Yorba' is a jaunty acoustic stomp; 'We're Going to Be Friends' is a gentle children's song.","The White Stripes came from Detroit's garage-rock scene. Their third album was recorded in under four days at a studio in Memphis and released in July 2001 on a small independent label. It became their breakthrough, helped by Michel Gondry's Lego-animated video for 'Fell in Love with a Girl'. The duo presented themselves as brother and sister, dressed only in red, white and black, and they were one of the bands, with the Strokes, credited with a rock revival.","The duo were in fact former spouses, not siblings, a fact revealed by journalists; the sibling story was part of their carefully built mythology. The strict limits, three colours, two players, few takes, were a deliberate method; Jack White believed restriction forced creativity. Listen to 'We're Going to Be Friends', recorded with just acoustic guitar and voice, as the plain centre of the album.","British critics championed the band first, and the album was widely hailed as a return to raw rock. Some critics wondered whether the image overshadowed the music, but most praised the songwriting. It is now seen as a foundation of the garage-rock revival and one of the important rock records of the 2000s.","Start with 'Dead Leaves and the Dirty Ground' for the raw sound, then 'Fell in Love with a Girl' for speed and 'We're Going to Be Friends' for gentleness. Notice there is no bass; listen to how the guitar fills the space. Compare with the Stooges' Fun House and with Son House to hear what they are drawing on."],"the-white-stripes-elephant":["Fourteen songs, heavier and bluesier than the duo's earlier work. Jack White's guitar ranges from grinding riffs to lengthy slide-blues solos; Meg White's drums remain simple and pounding. 'Seven Nation Army' opens the album; 'The Hardest Button to Button' is built on an insistent stop-start; 'Ball and Biscuit' is a seven-minute blues jam; there is a cover of Burt Bacharach's 'I Just Don't Know What to Do with Myself', and Meg sings 'In the Cold, Cold Night'.","Recorded in about two weeks at Toe Rag Studios in London, which used vintage analogue equipment, the album was released in April 2003 on a major label after the band's surge of fame. The liner notes boasted that no computers were used in its making. It won the Grammy for best alternative album and made the White Stripes international stars. The period saw a wave of British and American guitar bands influenced by 1960s garage rock.","The eight-track tape and old equipment were a statement against digital production at the moment Pro Tools was taking over studios. 'Ball and Biscuit' is a direct homage to the Delta blues Jack White loved, and the album's dedication and packaging continue the red, white and black mythology. Listen for how many songs are built on just one or two riffs repeated with changing intensity.","Critics hailed it as the band's best album, and it topped many year-end lists. Some felt the band had become more a brand than a band, but most praised the balance of rawness and craft. It is now considered one of the essential rock albums of its decade, and its opening song became one of the most recognisable pieces of music of the century.","Start with 'Seven Nation Army' and 'The Hardest Button to Button' for the riffs, then give 'Ball and Biscuit' its full seven minutes for the extended blues soloing. Notice Meg White's simplicity: she never overplays, and the space she leaves is part of the sound. Compare with Led Zeppelin's third album and with Robert Johnson's recordings to hear the blues lineage, and seek out the vinyl if you can."],"the-white-stripes-seven-nation-army":["A four-minute song built on a single riff: seven notes descending and rising, played by Jack White on a guitar pitched down an octave so it sounds like a bass. Meg White's drums start with a steady, simple beat and build. The verses are sparse; the chorus doubles the riff with distorted guitar at normal pitch. The riff is so simple that anyone can hum it, and it drives the song almost without variation to its end.","The opening track of Elephant, released as a single in 2003, it won the Grammy for best rock song. Jack White wrote the riff during a soundcheck in Australia and saved it, he said, in case he was ever asked to write a James Bond theme. The title comes from his childhood mishearing of the Salvation Army. The song became the band's biggest hit and made them household names.","There is no bass guitar: the low line is White's semi-acoustic guitar run through a pitch-shifting pedal set an octave down. Listen carefully and you can hear the slight wobble of the effect. The riff was later taken up by football crowds, first reportedly by Club Brugge fans in 2003 and then by Italian fans at the 2006 World Cup, and it has since become a global stadium chant.","Critics praised the song's economy and power, and it has appeared on many lists of the best songs of its decade. Its afterlife as a chant, sung by crowds who may never have heard the original, has made it one of the most widely known riffs in rock. Some argue that its simplicity is its triumph; others see it as the apotheosis of the White Stripes' limited approach.","Listen first for the fake bass: notice the sound's odd texture, not quite a bass. Then count the riff's seven notes and hear how the song builds entirely on them. Try to imagine it chanted by a stadium. Compare with the Kinks' 'You Really Got Me' and with Black Sabbath's 'Paranoid' to hear other one-riff songs."],"queens-of-the-stone-age-rated-r":["Eleven songs of heavy, groove-based rock with surprising turns. Josh Homme's guitars are thick and hypnotic, his voice a cool falsetto; Nick Oliveri's bass drives hard and he screams on the punk tracks. 'Feel Good Hit of the Summer' repeats a short list of intoxicants over a pounding riff; 'The Lost Art of Keeping a Secret' is a sleek, catchy single; 'Better Living Through Chemistry' is a long psychedelic journey; horns, vibraphone and piano appear unexpectedly.","Queens of the Stone Age grew out of Kyuss, the desert rock band from Palm Desert, California, where bands played generator parties in the desert. Homme wanted a band that was heavy but danceable, a sound he called robot rock. Rated R, their second album and first for a major label, was released in June 2000. It featured guests including Mark Lanegan and a brief backing vocal from Rob Halford of Judas Priest.","The band's approach came from Homme's Desert Sessions, loose recording collaborations with shifting guests, which explains the album's varied textures and cast. Listen for the repetition in the riffs, closer to the German krautrock of Can and Neu! than to conventional metal; Homme has said he wanted riffs that lock like machinery. 'Feel Good Hit of the Summer' is a deliberate provocation, a single made of nothing but a list.","Critics praised it as a fresh take on heavy rock, and it became a cult favourite and a bridge between metal and alternative rock. Its playful mix of heaviness, pop and psychedelia distinguished it from nu-metal, which dominated the charts. It is now considered a landmark of the desert and stoner rock tradition and one of the best hard rock records of its decade.","Start with 'The Lost Art of Keeping a Secret' for the pop side, then 'Better Living Through Chemistry' for the psychedelic depths. Listen to the riffs' repetition and the groove. Compare with Black Sabbath's Master of Reality and Can's Tago Mago to hear the two traditions it fuses."],"queens-of-the-stone-age-songs-for-the-deaf":["Fourteen songs framed as a drive from Los Angeles through the California desert, with the radio flicking between stations. Between songs, fake DJs talk, and the sound of the car radio links the tracks. Dave Grohl's drumming is thunderous and precise. 'No One Knows' has a hypnotic riff and a stop-start rhythm; 'Go with the Flow' is a driving, chiming anthem; 'First It Giveth' swings; 'A Song for the Dead' is a relentless, grinding assault.","Released in August 2002, the album featured Dave Grohl, formerly of Nirvana and leader of the Foo Fighters, on drums, and Mark Lanegan, formerly of Screaming Trees, sharing vocals with Josh Homme and Nick Oliveri. It was produced by Eric Valentine with the band. It became their commercial breakthrough, helped by striking videos and heavy MTV rotation; the lineup did not last, and Oliveri was fired in 2004.","The radio frame is the album's hidden structure: the drive from the city into the desert maps the band's own origins in the desert rock scene, and the spoken interludes, voiced by friends and collaborators of the band, parody the patter of commercial rock radio. After the album seems to end, a hidden track, 'Mosquito Song', arrives with acoustic guitars, strings and horns, completely unlike everything before it.","Critics hailed it as one of the best rock albums of the decade, praising the musicianship, especially Grohl's drumming, and the album's cohesion as a single journey. It became a benchmark for heavy rock that was both muscular and melodic, and its influence spread across stoner, alternative and hard rock. Some found the radio skits tiresome on repeat listening, but few dispute the songs.","Play it in the car, if you can, from start to finish. Listen for the radio interludes linking the songs. Start with 'No One Knows' for the riff and 'Go with the Flow' for the drive; then 'A Song for the Dead' for Grohl at full force. Compare with Nirvana's Nevermind to hear Grohl's evolution."],"yeah-yeah-yeahs-fever-to-tell":["Eleven songs by a trio with no bass: Karen O's voice veers between yelps, screams, whispers and croons; Nick Zinner's guitar is jagged, noisy and inventive; Brian Chase's drums are fast and dynamic. Most songs are raw and explosive: 'Rich' and 'Date with the Night' are furious; 'Pin' and 'Black Tongue' are fast and weird. Then the album slows: 'Maps' is a heartfelt ballad, and 'Modern Romance' closes quietly.","The Yeah Yeah Yeahs formed in New York in 2000 and became part of the city's rock revival alongside the Strokes and Interpol. After two EPs, their debut album was released in April 2003, produced by David Sitek of TV on the Radio and the band. Karen O's stage presence, her costumes and wild performances, made the band one of the scene's most exciting live acts.","The album's first half is deliberately abrasive; the shift to 'Maps' and the final songs is a sudden unmasking, and the album's structure depends on the contrast. Zinner often uses loops and effects to make his guitar sound larger, filling the space where a bass would be. Listen to how Karen O's voice changes from song to song, a range of personas rather than a single character.","Critics praised the energy and Karen O's charisma, and the album was widely named among the year's best. Its reputation has grown, especially because of 'Maps', and it is now considered one of the defining records of New York's 2000s scene. Its blend of art-punk and vulnerability influenced many later bands.","Start with 'Date with the Night' for the noise, then jump to 'Maps' for the heart, and only then play the album in order to feel the shift. Listen to Karen O's shifting voice and to Zinner's guitar filling the space where a bass would be. Compare with Patti Smith's Horses and Siouxsie and the Banshees' The Scream to hear the lineage of female-led punk."],"yeah-yeah-yeahs-maps":["A four-minute ballad. Nick Zinner's guitar plays a chiming, repeating figure; Brian Chase's drums rumble steadily beneath; Karen O sings a simple plea to a lover not to leave, her voice raw and exposed. The song builds slowly, the guitar growing noisier and the drums more insistent, until the final chorus arrives with a burst of distortion. It is the most direct and vulnerable song the band had made.","Released as a single from Fever to Tell in 2004, 'Maps' became the band's breakthrough hit. Karen O wrote it about Angus Andrew of the band Liars, her boyfriend at the time, who was often away on tour. The video, a simple performance shot, captured her crying on camera, which fans took as a sign of the song's sincerity.","Karen O has said that the tears in the video were real: Andrew was late for the shoot, and she was afraid he would not come. The title is often said to be an acronym for a plea to him to stay, a story the band has never fully confirmed. Listen for how Zinner's guitar never moves through a conventional chord progression; it circles one figure while the drums push the song forward.","Critics praised 'Maps' as one of the best songs of the decade, and it has appeared on many best-of lists. It has been covered by many artists, from folk singers to indie rock bands, and it became a template for the emotional rock ballad of the 2000s. Its reputation as a love song of stark honesty has only grown.","Listen to it quietly first, then loud. Notice how the guitar figure repeats and grows, and how the final chorus breaks open into distortion after so much restraint. Watch the video to see the song performed almost without staging. Compare with Joy Division's 'Love Will Tear Us Apart' for another plain, desperate love song, and with the Smiths' 'How Soon Is Now?' for a guitar that circles."],"lcd-soundsystem-losing-my-edge":["An eight-minute spoken monologue over a cheap drum machine, a throbbing bass and slowly accumulating synthesizers. James Murphy plays an ageing hipster who boasts that he was present at every legendary scene in music history and frets that younger, cooler kids are now overtaking him. The beat is relentless and minimal. The track ends with Murphy reciting a long list of obscure and famous bands, a litany of record-collector taste.","Murphy was a New York sound engineer and DJ who co-founded the DFA label, which fused dance music and punk. 'Losing My Edge' was his debut single under the name LCD Soundsystem, released in 2002. It became an underground hit and a statement of the dance-punk movement, which was reviving the sounds of late 1970s and early 1980s New York, where post-punk bands met disco.","Murphy, then in his early thirties, was satirising himself: the anxieties of the ageing record collector were his own, and the list of bands at the end is a list of his influences. The irony cuts both ways, mocking the hipster while loving the music. Listen for the gradual building of the synthesizers, a technique from minimal techno and from Steve Reich's process music.","Critics hailed it as one of the defining singles of the decade, a witty dissection of music culture that was also a dance track. It launched Murphy's career and the DFA sound. Its themes of taste, obsolescence and nostalgia seemed prescient in the internet age, when music history became instantly accessible.","Listen to the monologue first as a comedy, catching the absurd boasts; then listen again only for the beat and the synthesizers building beneath the voice. Try to catch the names in the final list and count how many you know. Compare with Talking Heads' Fear of Music and with Suicide's first album to hear the New York lineage it both mocks and adores."],"lcd-soundsystem-sound-of-silver":["Nine long songs fusing dance music and rock. 'Get Innocuous!' opens with a pulsing, Kraftwerk-like groove; 'North American Scum' mocks American identity; 'Someone Great' is a gentle, synth-driven song about grief; 'All My Friends' builds a relentless piano figure into a cathartic anthem; 'New York, I Love You but You're Bringing Me Down' closes the album with a slow ballad about the city. Each track builds gradually, adding layers until the song explodes.","James Murphy recorded the album largely alone in the DFA studio in New York, playing most instruments, and it was released in March 2007. It was LCD Soundsystem's second album, and it confirmed Murphy's shift from satire to sincerity. Themes of ageing, loss and friendship reflected his own life in his late thirties and the changing city of New York.","'Someone Great' is a song about grief that many listeners first took for a break-up song; Murphy has reportedly connected it to the death of someone close to him, and the glockenspiel melody running through it gives the sorrow a childlike, fragile quality. Listen for the influence of Brian Eno and David Bowie's Berlin albums, especially in the slowly building synths and the mix of human warmth and machine precision.","Critics hailed it as one of the best albums of the decade, and it was nominated for a Grammy. It is often praised for balancing irony and emotion, and it is considered the best statement of the dance-punk movement. It deepened Murphy's reputation as one of the most important musicians of his generation.","Start with 'All My Friends' and 'Someone Great', then play the album in order and notice how nearly every song builds from a simple rhythm to dense layers before releasing. Compare with Talking Heads' Remain in Light and Bowie's Low to hear the lineage, and with Steve Reich for the patient repetition. Play it loud, ideally on speakers with some bass."],"lcd-soundsystem-all-my-friends":["An eight-minute song built on a single, relentless piano figure that repeats from the first second to the last. Around it, drums, bass, guitar and synthesizers accumulate, each adding energy. James Murphy's voice starts almost conversational and grows more urgent. There is no conventional chorus; the song climbs and climbs. Its subject is getting older: the nights out, the friends who have drifted, the question of where they all are now.","Released as a single from Sound of Silver in 2007, 'All My Friends' became James Murphy's signature song. It was issued with B-sides featuring cover versions by John Cale of the Velvet Underground and by Franz Ferdinand, a sign of how quickly musicians of every generation took to it. It became a staple of indie clubs and a climax of LCD Soundsystem's farewell concert at Madison Square Garden in 2011.","The piano figure, a steady pulse of repeating notes, sounds like a direct descendant of Steve Reich and Philip Glass: a loop that never changes while everything around it does, exactly the technique of Reich's Music for 18 Musicians. The song's power comes from that patience, the refusal to break the loop or give the listener a conventional chorus.","Critics have ranked it among the best songs of the century, praising its emotional weight and its structure. It has been described as a perfect song about ageing, and it is widely covered and quoted. Its combination of dance music and heartfelt lyrics made it a template for the indie music of the following decade.","Listen once only for the piano: follow it from the first second to the last and notice how the other layers pile on around it. Listen again for the voice, which moves from almost-speech to shouting. Then hear the opening of Reich's Music for 18 Musicians and notice the same patience. Play it loud; the build needs volume to work."],"tame-impala-lonerism":["Twelve songs of layered psychedelic pop, recorded largely by Kevin Parker alone. The sound is thick and swirling: phased guitars, fuzzy synthesizers, crashing drums, and Parker's high, dreamy voice, often treated so it sounds like John Lennon. 'Be Above It' opens with a whispered chant; 'Elephant' has a stomping glam riff; 'Feels Like We Only Go Backwards' is a gentle, looping ballad; 'Apocalypse Dreams' builds to a dense climax.","Tame Impala is essentially Kevin Parker, a musician from Perth, Western Australia, who records alone and tours with a band. Lonerism, the second album, was recorded in Perth and Paris and released in October 2012. Its theme is isolation, the feeling of being alone among people. It was widely acclaimed and made Tame Impala one of the most influential rock acts of its decade.","The cover photograph shows the Luxembourg Gardens in Paris through a metal fence, an image of the album's theme: watching people from outside. Parker recorded most of the instruments himself, layering them in his home studio, which gives the album a dense, private texture. Listen for the drums, heavily compressed in the manner of 1960s recordings by the Beatles.","Critics hailed it as a landmark of modern psychedelia, and it topped many year-end lists, including some of the most influential music publications. It was praised for combining 1960s psychedelia with modern production and pop hooks. Its influence spread widely across indie rock and beyond, and Parker became a sought-after producer and collaborator for pop and hip-hop artists.","Start with 'Feels Like We Only Go Backwards' and 'Elephant', then play the album in order on headphones, where the panning and layering make most sense. Notice how the guitars and synths blur into one texture. Compare with the Beatles' Revolver and 'Tomorrow Never Knows' to hear the psychedelic lineage, and with the Zombies for the pop melody."],"tame-impala-currents":["Thirteen songs that trade guitars for synthesizers and grooves. The sound is smooth and danceable, with heavy bass and lush synths. 'Let It Happen' opens with an eight-minute song with a looping section that sounds like a skipping record; 'The Less I Know the Better' has a funky, disco bassline; 'Eventually' is a slow ballad about ending a relationship; 'New Person, Same Old Mistakes' closes the album with a heavy, slow groove.","Parker made the album alone in Perth and released it in July 2015; it is widely heard as a record about a break-up and about personal change. The shift from guitars to synthesizers surprised fans, but it brought Tame Impala to a much wider audience. The album reflects Parker's love of R&B, disco and pop, and it anticipated his later work with pop and hip-hop stars.","In 'Let It Happen', the stuttering section in the middle, where the music seems to skip like a scratched disc, is a deliberate effect rather than a fault. Rihanna covered 'New Person, Same Old Mistakes' on her 2016 album Anti, a sign of Parker's crossover. Listen for how the album is sequenced as a story of transformation, with short interludes marking stages.","Critics praised it as a bold reinvention, and it became Tame Impala's best-selling album. Some fans missed the guitars, but most welcomed the change. It is now seen as a pivotal album in the blending of rock and pop in the 2010s, and its influence on bedroom pop and psychedelic R&B has been wide.","Start with 'Let It Happen' and wait for the skipping section; then 'The Less I Know the Better' for the bass. Play the album in order to follow its story of change, ideally on good headphones. Compare with Daft Punk's Discovery to hear the dance influence and with Frank Ocean's Blonde for another inward pop record of the decade."],"vampire-weekend-vampire-weekend":["Eleven short, bright songs. Ezra Koenig's clean, high-pitched guitar plays quick, looping figures influenced by African guitar pop; Rostam Batmanglij adds harpsichord, organ and string arrangements; Chris Baio's bass is melodic; Chris Tomson's drums are crisp. 'Mansard Roof' opens with a skipping organ; 'A-Punk' is a two-minute burst; 'Oxford Comma' is a wry, easygoing song; 'Cape Cod Kwassa Kwassa' takes its name from a Congolese dance.","The band formed at Columbia University in New York and released their debut album on XL in January 2008, after a buzz online. Their preppy image and lyrics about Ivy League life, grammar and architecture set them apart from other indie bands. The album became a hit and an influential example of indie rock that looked beyond British and American rock for its sound.","Rostam's arrangements add baroque touches: harpsichord in 'M79' and string quartet parts elsewhere, drawing on classical chamber music. The African influence, soukous, highlife and South African township pop, came partly through Paul Simon's Graceland, a debt the band has discussed openly. Listen for how many songs are built on a single skipping guitar or keyboard pattern that never quite settles.","Critics praised the album's freshness and craft, and it was named among the year's best. It also drew criticism for cultural appropriation and for its image of privilege, debates the band addressed in later interviews. Its influence on indie rock was substantial, encouraging other bands to explore global sounds.","Start with 'A-Punk' and 'Mansard Roof', then listen to the string arrangement in 'M79' and notice the harpsichord. Follow Koenig's clean, bright guitar, which is doing the job an African highlife guitarist would. Compare with King Sunny Adé's Juju Music and with Talking Heads' Remain in Light to hear the global influence and how differently each band handles it."],"vampire-weekend-modern-vampires-of-the-city":["Twelve songs, darker and more reflective than the band's earlier work. The sound is richer, with organs, choirs and pitch-shifted voices. 'Obvious Bicycle' opens with a gentle piano; 'Step' has a harpsichord-like melody; 'Diane Young' is a frantic, distorted rocker; 'Hannah Hunt' builds from a quiet ballad to a pounding climax; 'Ya Hey' features pitch-shifted voices singing a chorus that plays on the name of God. The themes are mortality, faith and ageing.","The band's third album, co-produced by Rostam Batmanglij and Ariel Rechtshaid and released in May 2013, won the Grammy for best alternative album. It was the last with Rostam as a full member; he left in 2016. Its cover is a news photograph of New York shrouded in smog in 1966, capturing the album's sense of a city haunted by time and by its own past.","'Step' borrows from a 1990s hip-hop song by Souls of Mischief, and the credits acknowledge it. The album's religious questioning runs through several songs, especially 'Ya Hey' and 'Unbelievers'. Listen for the choir and organ in 'Ya Hey', echoing church music, and for the pitch-shifted voices that make the singer sound both childlike and ancient.","Critics hailed it as the band's best album and one of the best of the decade, praising its depth and craft. It won the Grammy and topped many year-end lists. It is seen as a mature statement, taking the band beyond their preppy image into meditations on mortality.","Start with 'Step' and 'Hannah Hunt'; then listen to the album in order, noticing how the mood darkens toward the end. Listen for the organ and choir, and for the pitch-shifted voices in 'Ya Hey'. Compare with Arcade Fire's Funeral for another album about mortality and the city, and with the Beach Boys' Pet Sounds for studio-built sincerity."],"interpol-turn-on-the-bright-lights":["Eleven songs of dark, atmospheric rock. Paul Banks's deep, detached voice sings over Daniel Kessler's precise, chiming guitar, Carlos Dengler's melodic, prominent bass and Sam Fogarino's driving drums. 'Untitled' opens with a slow, reverberating guitar; 'Obstacle 1' is tense and urgent; 'PDA' is fast and driving; 'NYC' is a slow, melancholy tribute to the city; 'Stella Was a Diver and She Was Always Down' builds to a climax; 'Leif Erikson' closes quietly.","Interpol formed in New York in the late 1990s and released their debut album on Matador in August 2002. It was recorded in Connecticut with producer Peter Katis. It became part of the New York rock revival alongside the Strokes and the Yeah Yeah Yeahs. Its release less than a year after September 11 gave its songs about the city a particular resonance.","Listen for Carlos Dengler's bass, which often carries the melody while the guitars chime above it, a technique borrowed straight from post-punk. The guitar parts are deliberately simple and interlocking rather than showy. The band dressed in suits and presented a cool, stylish image that became part of the album's identity, and the lyrics mix precise city detail with oblique, almost surreal phrases.","Critics praised it as one of the best albums of the year and a key record of the New York revival. Some dismissed it as derivative of Joy Division, but most recognised its originality and emotional power. Its reputation has grown, and it is now considered one of the essential albums of the 2000s.","Start with 'NYC' and 'Obstacle 1', then listen to the bass in 'PDA' and notice how it drives the song. Pay attention to the interplay between the two guitars and the bass, which is where the album's tension lives. Compare with Joy Division's Unknown Pleasures and Television's Marquee Moon to hear the lineage, and with the Strokes for the other face of the same scene."],"the-killers-hot-fuss":["Eleven songs of glossy, synth-driven rock. Brandon Flowers's theatrical voice sings over Dave Keuning's chiming guitar, Mark Stoermer's bass and Ronnie Vannucci's crisp drums, with synthesizers everywhere. 'Jenny Was a Friend of Mine' opens with a driving bass; 'Mr. Brightside' is a relentless song of jealousy, all verse and no let-up; 'Somebody Told Me' is a fast, glittering single; 'All These Things That I've Done' builds to a gospel-style chorus.","The Killers formed in Las Vegas and signed first to a British label, and their debut album was released in June 2004. It became a huge hit, particularly in Britain, where the band's love of British bands such as New Order and the Smiths was welcomed. The band took their name from the fictional band in New Order's video for 'Crystal'.","'Mr. Brightside' became one of the longest-charting songs in British chart history, staying in the Top 100 for years after its release. 'Jenny Was a Friend of Mine' is part of a so-called murder trilogy of songs, in which a narrator is questioned about a woman's death. Listen for the synthesizers, borrowed from British new wave, that set the band apart from other American guitar bands.","Critics praised the album's hooks and energy, and it sold millions worldwide. Some found it derivative of its British heroes, but most recognised Flowers's gift for a chorus. 'Mr. Brightside' has become an anthem sung at weddings, sports grounds and closing time in clubs, and the album is now considered a defining record of the 2000s guitar revival.","Start with 'Mr. Brightside' and 'Somebody Told Me', then listen to 'All These Things That I've Done' for its gospel-style build. Notice how prominently the synthesizers sit in the mix, unusual for an American guitar band in 2004. Compare with New Order's Power, Corruption and Lies and the Smiths to hear the British influence, and with Springsteen for the Americana to come."],"american-football-american-football":["Nine songs of quiet, intricate music. Mike Kinsella's soft, wavering voice sings over two clean electric guitars that tap and pick interlocking patterns in unusual tunings; Steve Lamos's drums shift through odd time signatures and he adds a mournful trumpet. 'Never Meant' opens with a shimmering guitar figure; 'Honestly?' builds to a noisy climax; 'The Summer Ends' features trumpet; 'Stay Home' is an eight-minute slow build; 'The One with the Wurlitzer' closes quietly.","American Football formed at the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign and released their only album of the era on the local label Polyvinyl in September 1999. The band split soon after, as members graduated. The album sold slowly but steadily and became a cult classic through word of mouth and the internet, particularly among fans of emo and math rock. The band reunited in 2014.","The cover photograph shows an ordinary house in Urbana, which has become a place of pilgrimage for fans. The guitar parts use unusual open tunings and tapping techniques, and the interlocking patterns recall both jazz guitar and the phased loops of minimalism. Listen for the odd time signatures, which feel natural rather than showy, and for the trumpet, an instrument almost unheard of in emo.","The album was little noticed on release, but it has since become one of the most beloved and influential emo albums. Critics now praise its intricacy and emotional honesty, and it has inspired a generation of bands. The reunion in 2014 brought the band a much larger audience than they had ever had.","Start with 'Never Meant' and listen to how the two guitars interlock; then 'The Summer Ends' for the trumpet. Play it quietly, ideally at dusk or in autumn, which is the season it seems to describe. Compare with Slint's Spiderland and with Steve Reich's Electric Counterpoint to hear its two lineages, and with Jimmy Eat World's Clarity for the other face of late-1990s emo."],"animal-collective-feels":["Nine songs that sound like a party heard through a warm fog. Guitars are strummed in dense, chiming clusters, drums crash in loose waves, and Avey Tare's voice jumps from a coo to a yelp inside a single phrase. The first half is exuberant: Did You See the Words and Grass bounce and shriek with a kind of playground joy. The second half slows into long, drifting pieces such as Bees and the eight-minute Banshee Beat, where a simple guitar figure repeats until it becomes a trance.","Released on FatCat in 2005, Feels came after the half-acoustic Sung Tongs and was the first Animal Collective record to feature all four members fully as a band. It was recorded in Seattle with the producer Scott Colburn, far from the Baltimore suburbs where the friends had grown up. At the time the press was lumping them in with a so-called freak-folk scene of bearded, barefoot acoustic musicians; Feels was louder, more electric and more ecstatic than that label suggested.","Listen to how the instruments sit slightly apart in pitch: the guitars and piano are layered so that their pitches shimmer and beat against each other, creating a wobbling halo around every chord. It is less sloppiness than a deliberate texture, closer to the beating tones of a gamelan or a church organ than to a rock mix. The love songs hiding under the noise, especially The Purple Bottle, are also more direct than the yelping suggests.","Critics who had found earlier Animal Collective records impenetrable heard Feels as the moment the noise turned into songs, and it placed high on many 2005 lists. Some still found the vocals grating, and the band's habit of screaming where others would sing remains the dividing line for listeners. Its reputation has been somewhat overshadowed by Merriweather Post Pavilion, but many fans hold it as the band's warmest and most human record.","Start with Grass and The Purple Bottle, which show the manic, joyful side, then give Banshee Beat its full eight minutes with nothing else going on. Listen for the moment the guitars lock into a repeating pattern and the vocals start to float above rather than push against them. Compare the Beach Boys' Smile sessions for the idea of pop built from fragments, and Terry Riley's In C for the pleasure of pure repetition."],"animal-collective-merriweather-post-pavilion":["Eleven songs built almost entirely from samplers and electronics, with barely a strummed guitar in sight. Deep, rubbery bass pulses underpin choruses of stacked, echoing harmonies from Avey Tare and Panda Bear, so the whole record sounds like a Beach Boys vocal session dropped into a dance club. My Girls opens with a bubbling arpeggio that repeats for over a minute before the voices arrive; Summertime Clothes and Brother Sport are built on chants that swell and swell. The sound is round, humid and relentlessly bright.","Recorded in 2008 in Oxford, Mississippi, with the producer Ben Allen and released by Domino in January 2009, without Deakin, who had taken a break from the band. The album was named after an outdoor concert amphitheatre in Columbia, Maryland, near where the members grew up. Panda Bear's solo album Person Pitch of 2007 had shown how sample loops could carry melodic pop; MPP scaled that method up. The release was brought forward after the music circulated online early.","Beneath the celebratory surface are domestic and even grieving subjects. My Girls is about wanting nothing more than a solid house for your family, a strikingly modest wish for a psychedelic record. The closer, Brother Sport, was written by Panda Bear after his father's death and urges his brother to let his grief out rather than hold it in. Listen to how the relentless rhythm there is a kind of encouragement, a push to keep moving.","It arrived to a rare consensus: many publications, Pitchfork prominent among them, named it the best album of 2009, and it became the band's commercial peak. The debate since has been whether it was the high point of a brief moment when indie rock embraced dance music and sampling, or a beginning. Some older fans missed the acoustic chaos of earlier records; newer listeners took it as the gateway, and much 2010s electronic indie pop traces back to it.","Play My Girls loud, on good speakers or headphones with real bass, and wait through the long introduction for the moment the voices enter and the beat drops. Then hear Brother Sport as the finale it is meant to be. Notice how little guitar there is and how the harmonies do the work guitars once did. Compare the Beach Boys' Good Vibrations for vocal architecture and Kanye West's 808s and Heartbreak, also a 2008 recording that rebuilt pop from electronics."],"fleet-foxes-fleet-foxes":["Eleven songs, under forty minutes, sung mostly by Robin Pecknold's clear tenor and then echoed by a chorus of his bandmates drenched in cavernous reverb. Acoustic guitars ring, drums thump like a heartbeat, and melodies sound as if they came from old hymnals and English ballads. White Winter Hymnal is built as a round, voices entering one after another on the same tune. Ragged Wood and Blue Ridge Mountains swing between hushed verses and sudden, galloping choruses. The world of the lyrics is woods, mountains, rivers and family.","Released by Sub Pop in June 2008, shortly after the Sun Giant EP, and produced by Phil Ek in Seattle, the city of grunge. Pecknold was only in his early twenties. The late 2000s were full of loud, ironic indie rock and dance-punk, and this sincere, pastoral record went against that grain. Its models were the Beach Boys' harmonies, Crosby, Stills and Nash, the Byrds, and British folk-rock bands such as Fairport Convention and Pentangle.","The cover reproduces Pieter Bruegel the Elder's 1559 painting Netherlandish Proverbs, a crowded village scene illustrating more than a hundred sayings. It is a clue to the record's intent: something communal, old, crowded with small stories rather than one confessional voice. Listen to how much of the vocal sound is room echo rather than studio effect; the harmonies were designed to sound as if sung in a church or a canyon, and that space is half the arrangement.","It was named album of the year by several major publications in 2008, including Pitchfork and Uncut, and sold far beyond indie expectations. It also sparked a wave of bearded, harmonising folk acts that critics grew weary of within a few years. Some found its pastoral innocence too pretty or escapist. Its songwriting and arranging have held up better than most of its imitators, and it remains a touchstone for choral indie folk.","Start with White Winter Hymnal and listen to each voice enter the round, then to the drums bursting in underneath. Hear Ragged Wood for the way the song changes tempo and character halfway through, as if it were two songs stitched together. Compare the Beach Boys' Pet Sounds for vocal stacking and Fairport Convention's Liege and Lief for the folk ancestry. Headphones reveal the space around the voices."],"fleet-foxes-helplessness-blues":["Twelve songs, longer and more unsettled than the debut. Robin Pecknold's voice is more exposed, the harmonies used more sparingly, and acoustic guitars, twelve-strings, fiddle and flute weave around each other in intricate patterns. The title track begins as a fast, fingerpicked confession and then gathers into a huge, open-hearted chorus. The Shrine / An Argument runs eight minutes, moving from fragile verse to shouted climax and ending in a squall of free-jazz saxophone. Montezuma opens the record with a question about getting older.","After the enormous success of the debut, Pecknold spent a long, costly and often frustrated period making the follow-up in Seattle, scrapping sessions and ending a relationship along the way. It was released by Sub Pop in May 2011. The drummer at the time was Josh Tillman, who would soon leave and become the solo artist Father John Misty. The pressure of following a much-praised debut, and of being an idealistic young songwriter, shapes the record's mood.","The title song is a small philosophical argument set to music. It weighs the modern idea that each person is unique and special against a longing to be an anonymous, useful part of something larger, and ends with the singer imagining a simple life working an orchard. Listen to how the music enacts the argument: a knotted, solo, wordy first half gives way to a broad, choral second half, as if the song itself joined the crowd.","Reviews were very warm, and it was nominated for a Grammy for Best Folk Album, but some listeners felt it lacked the immediate sweetness of the debut. Over time many fans have come to prefer it for its ambition and honesty. The squealing saxophone at the end of The Shrine divided listeners, some hearing it as a brave rupture and others as an intrusion into a pastoral world.","Begin with the title track, then Montezuma, then give The Shrine / An Argument its full length and let the ending jar you. Listen for the twelve-string guitars chiming like a harpsichord behind the voice. Compare Joni Mitchell's Blue for the exposed confessional voice, and Crosby, Stills and Nash-style harmony records. The contrast with the debut is the point: the choir has become a single worried person."],"bon-iver-for-emma-forever-ago":["Nine songs, thirty-seven minutes, made almost entirely by one man. Justin Vernon's falsetto is layered on itself until it becomes a quiet, cracked choir; acoustic guitar is strummed hard, drums are thumped like a cabin wall, and there is space, silence and room noise everywhere. Flume opens the record, Skinny Love became its best-known song with its percussive strumming and pleading, and re: Stacks closes it with little more than guitar and voice. The words are often blurred, closer to sounds than sentences.","Vernon made most of it over the winter of 2006 to 2007 in his father's hunting cabin in northern Wisconsin, after his band broke up, a relationship ended and he had been ill. He first self-released it in 2007; Jagjaguwar reissued it in February 2008, and word of mouth made it a phenomenon. The home-studio technology of the time let one person with a microphone and a laptop produce an album that could travel the world.","Many of the lyrics were written by singing sounds first and then finding words that fitted the vowel shapes, which is why they feel so emotionally clear and yet so hard to decipher. Listen to The Wolves (Act I and II), where the song's pleading turns into a massed, shouted chant, the multitracked voices stacking into something like a congregation. The name Bon Iver itself is a deliberately misspelled borrowing of the French for good winter.","The story of the cabin became famous, and some critics grew suspicious of how it was used, but the music itself was almost universally admired, landing on many lists of the best albums of the 2000s. Its influence was vast: soft, falsetto, cabin-folk and multitracked vulnerability became a template for countless followers, and Kanye West recruited Vernon for his next record. Vernon himself has said the cabin myth overshadows the actual music.","Listen alone, ideally late, with headphones: the record rewards attention to the grain of the voice and the air around it. Start with Flume, then Skinny Love, and notice the moment the voices double up. Then hear The Wolves for its massed ending. Compare Nick Drake's Pink Moon, another stripped record by one man, and follow with Kanye West's My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy to hear Vernon's voice transplanted into hip-hop."],"bon-iver-bon-iver-bon-iver":["Ten songs, many named after places, in a sound that has grown from a cabin into a landscape. Justin Vernon's falsetto is still at the centre, but around it now are pedal steel guitar, layered saxophones, brass, strings and two drummers. Perth opens with a guitar figure like a military tattoo and builds to a march; Holocene is a quiet, luminous song about being small against the world; Calgary pulses with synthesisers. The final song, Beth/Rest, turns to glossy 1980s electric piano and saxophone, like a lost soft-rock ballad.","Recorded over about three years at April Base, a studio Vernon built in a former veterinary clinic in Fall Creek, Wisconsin, and released by Jagjaguwar in June 2011. In between, Vernon had sung on Kanye West's My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy and worked with many collaborators. The album was deliberately a band and ensemble record, a reaction against the lonely-cabin myth. It won the Grammys for Best New Artist and Best Alternative Music Album in 2012.","The place names are a mixture of real and invented: some songs point to towns in Wisconsin or cities abroad, others to places that do not exist or exist only as feelings. They are not travelogues; each is an emotional location. Listen closely to the voices in Holocene and Perth, where Vernon's singing is layered with tiny pitch and timbral differences, a halfway step toward the machine-processed voice of his next album.","Reviews were largely rapturous and it placed on most year-end lists. The loudest argument was over Beth/Rest, whose unashamedly 1980s sound some heard as a daring embrace of unfashionable soft rock and others as a baffling misstep. Winning Best New Artist years after his breakthrough provoked jokes. It is now often ranked with the debut, and some prefer its breadth, while others feel the intimacy of the cabin was lost.","Begin with Holocene, alone and quiet, then Perth played loud to hear the drums and brass build. Listen for the pedal steel sliding under the voice, and then decide for yourself about Beth/Rest. Compare For Emma, Forever Ago for the before-and-after of a sound expanding, and Fleetwood Mac's Tusk for another band that followed a huge success by going somewhere stranger. A good vinyl pressing flatters the horns."],"bon-iver-22-a-million":["Ten songs in thirty-four minutes, with titles written in numbers, symbols and scrambled letters. Justin Vernon's voice is shredded, pitch-shifted, chopped and stacked through electronic processors, sometimes becoming a dense chord of himself; samples stutter and loop; saxophones arrive in sudden, blaring clusters. 715 - CRΣΣKS is voice alone, turned by a harmoniser into an eerie a cappella choir. 29 #Strafford APTS is almost a straight folk song, and 8 (circle) swells into gospel-like grandeur. The record feels broken apart and then reassembled.","Released by Jagjaguwar in September 2016, five years after the previous album, during which Vernon had struggled with anxiety and depression and nearly stepped away from Bon Iver. It was made with a group of collaborators, notably the engineer Chris Messina, who built a device, nicknamed the Messina, that harmonised a live voice in real time. By then Vernon had worked extensively with Kanye West, whose Auto-Tune and processed vocals shaped the album's language.","The strange titles are a private numerology rather than a random gimmick: numbers stand for dates, ages, addresses and recurring symbols of duality in Vernon's thinking, and many songs ponder faith, doubt and the self divided. Listen to how the harmoniser is used on 715 - CRΣΣKS: every note Vernon sings is instantly surrounded by computer-chosen harmonies, so a solitary confession is heard as a crowd. The record also borrows fragments of older recordings, folded so deep they sound like ghosts.","It was acclaimed and bewildering in roughly equal parts. Admirers called it a bold rebirth in which folk sincerity met digital fracture, and it placed high on 2016 lists; sceptics found the glitches and typography a distraction from songs. Its influence shows in the wide use of harmonised, processed vocals across indie and pop since. Today it is often heard as Bon Iver's most personal and most daring record.","Start with 715 - CRΣΣKS on headphones, then 8 (circle) for the most straightforwardly beautiful moment. Notice the silences and sudden cuts: the rough edges are part of the design. Compare Kanye West's 808s and Heartbreak for the processed voice as an emotional instrument, and Radiohead's Kid A for a band breaking its own sound. Short enough to hear in one sitting, and it should be."],"the-national-boxer":["Twelve songs in a hushed, adult register: Matt Berninger's low baritone murmurs over piano, guitars and the tight, inventive drumming of Bryan Devendorf, with strings and brass rising behind. Fake Empire opens the record on a hypnotic piano figure and builds into a brass-band swell. Mistaken for Strangers drives hard on a pounding drum pattern; Slow Show, Green Gloves and Apartment Story are tender and anxious. The world of the album is apartments, parties, office life and a marriage under quiet pressure, sung by a man in a suit.","The National were a Brooklyn band from Cincinnati who had built slowly through the early 2000s. Boxer, released by Beggars Banquet in May 2007 after the breakthrough of Alligator, was made with the producer Peter Katis. Arrangements were shaped by Padma Newsome of the chamber group Clogs, and Sufjan Stevens played piano on part of it. Fake Empire, a portrait of a comfortable society ignoring its troubles, was used the following year in Barack Obama's presidential campaign.","The piano that opens Fake Empire is playing in a cross-rhythm, with patterns of three set against a pulse of four, which is why the song feels both steady and slightly dizzy. Bryan Devendorf's drumming throughout is the band's secret engine: listen to Squalor Victoria and Mistaken for Strangers, where the drums play busy, intricate patterns while everything else is still, keeping the songs tense even at their quietest.","Reviews were very strong, and Boxer steadily became a word-of-mouth favourite rather than a sudden hit, eventually landing on many lists of the best albums of the decade. Early critics sometimes found Berninger's delivery monotonous; that restraint is now understood as the point. Its reputation grew through the band's later success, and it is often cited as the record that proved indie rock could be grown-up without being dull.","Start with Fake Empire and count along to feel the piano pulling against the beat, then hear the brass enter at the end. Follow with Mistaken for Strangers for the drumming and Slow Show for the tenderness. Listen to it at night, ideally in a city. Compare Leonard Cohen's I'm Your Man for the low, wry male voice, and Joy Division's Closer for tension held under the surface."],"the-national-high-violet":["Eleven songs, darker, louder and grander than Boxer. Terrible Love opens in murk, a distorted guitar and a stumbling drum pattern that finally breaks into a roar. Bloodbuzz Ohio rides a thundering tom-tom beat under Matt Berninger's baritone, singing of debts and a home state. Sorrow is slow and hymn-like; England and Vanderlyle Crybaby Geeks end the record in swelling brass and massed voices. The mood is dread and exhausted love, domestic anxiety scaled up to cathedral size.","Much of the album was made in a garage studio behind Aaron Dessner's house in Brooklyn, where the band worked obsessively, rerecording songs many times. Released by 4AD in May 2010, it became their commercial breakthrough, reaching the top five of the American chart. The twins Aaron and Bryce Dessner were by then also active in contemporary classical music, and brass and string arrangements carried that side of their lives into the band.","The song Sorrow had an extraordinary afterlife: in 2013 the Icelandic artist Ragnar Kjartansson had the band perform it over and over, without a break, for six hours at MoMA PS1 in New York, as a work called A Lot of Sorrow. Listen to the song after knowing that and its slow circling form sounds made for repetition. Notice too how muddy and saturated the album's opening is; the band intended that distortion as a mood.","Received with very strong reviews and big sales, High Violet made The National festival headliners. Some critics felt its grandeur smoothed away the intimacy of Boxer; others heard it as the band's peak of ambition. Its success helped shape a wave of brooding, anthemic indie rock in the early 2010s, and the Dessners' later work as producers, especially for Taylor Swift, has sent new listeners back to it.","Play Bloodbuzz Ohio loud and follow the drums; then play Terrible Love and notice how long the band holds back before releasing. End with Vanderlyle Crybaby Geeks, sung by a crowd in concert as often as by the band. Compare Arcade Fire's Funeral for communal grandeur and Steve Reich's Electric Counterpoint for the Dessners' other musical world of interlocking, repeating guitar patterns."],"st-vincent-strange-mercy":["Eleven songs in which Annie Clark's cool, precise singing is repeatedly interrupted by guitars that sound broken: fuzzed, gated, chopped into staccato bursts or smeared into a buzzing drone. Chloe in the Afternoon opens with a lurching, distorted riff; Cruel pairs a sweet melody with a snarling, choppy guitar hook; Cheerleader builds from a whisper to a massive, defiant chorus; Surgeon ends in a spiralling, frantic synthesiser and guitar solo. The songs are about self-control, repression and the desire to break loose.","Clark wrote the album in isolation in Seattle, stepping away from New York to concentrate, and made it with the producer John Congleton. It was released by 4AD in September 2011, her third album. She had toured with the Polyphonic Spree and in Sufjan Stevens's band, and was known as a technically dazzling guitarist in a scene that rarely valued virtuosity. Strange Mercy made her a critic's favourite and a live draw.","Chloe in the Afternoon takes its title from an Éric Rohmer film about a married man tempted by an old friend, one of several quiet cinematic references. The title song addresses someone who is locked up and powerless, and Clark later connected it to her own father's imprisonment. Listen to how the guitar sounds on Surgeon: much of the sound is built from a guitar run through effects until it resembles a synthesiser.","Reviews were enthusiastic, and many singled out her guitar playing as the most inventive in indie rock. Some found the record chilly and hard to warm to; others heard emotional violence in its controlled surfaces. It is now often cited as the album where St. Vincent's full artistic identity arrived, and Cheerleader and Cruel are among her most performed songs.","Start with Cruel for the contrast of honeyed voice and nasty guitar, then Cheerleader for its explosion, then Surgeon for the solo. Listen for where each guitar sound starts: often it enters mid-note, clipped. Compare Kate Bush's The Dreaming for another woman using the studio as a weapon, and Talking Heads' Remain in Light for angular, nervous art rock. Hear it loud on headphones."],"st-vincent-st-vincent":["Eleven songs, sharper and funkier than anything St. Vincent had made before. Rattlesnake opens with a pattering synthesiser and a tale of wandering naked in the desert; Birth in Reverse is a speeding, wiry guitar song; Digital Witness is built on a stabbing horn riff and asks what we become when we perform our lives for screens. Prince Johnny and I Prefer Your Love are slow, tender ballads. The guitar is still distorted and angular, but the rhythm now owns the record.","Released in February 2014 on Loma Vista, a new label backed by a major, after Clark had spent a year touring with David Byrne on their joint album Love This Giant. Byrne's influence shows in the brass and in the dancing, nervous rhythms. The record arrived as smartphones and social media were reshaping public life, and Clark's stage show recast her as a cool, silver-haired, almost robotic cult leader. It won the Grammy for Best Alternative Music Album.","The album is a sly essay on the digital self. Digital Witness satirises the urge to share every experience online; Huey Newton, named after the Black Panther co-founder, drifts through a dream haze connected to a sleeping pill before breaking into a heavy riff. Listen for how the guitar is used as punctuation rather than accompaniment: a stab, a squeal, a buzz in the gaps, like a machine interrupting a conversation.","Many critics called it her best album and several placed it at the top of 2014 lists. The Grammy win reinforced her move from indie darling to mainstream art-pop figure. Some admirers of her earlier records felt the persona had become more interesting than the songs; others heard exactly that as its subject. It has since been read as an unusually early and accurate record about life online.","Begin with Digital Witness and listen to the brass lock with the drums, then Rattlesnake, then the quiet Prince Johnny. Watch a video of her live show from this period, where the choreography is half the work. Compare Talking Heads' Remain in Light and Prince's Purple Rain for the fusion of guitar and funk, and David Bowie's Low for the cool, fragmented persona."],"grizzly-bear-veckatimest":["Twelve songs of layered harmony, chiming guitars and restless drums. Two Weeks is built on a bouncing piano pattern and a falsetto chorus with the feel of 1950s doo-wop; Southern Point opens the album in a rush of acoustic guitar and pounding drums; While You Wait for the Others swells into overlapping vocal lines; Foreground ends the record in near stillness. Clarinets, flutes, strings and a boys' choir are woven in so carefully that they seem part of the band. The mood is elegant, anxious and slightly dreamlike.","Recorded in several places, including a house on Cape Cod and studios in upstate New York and Brooklyn, and released by Warp in May 2009. The composer Nico Muhly arranged strings and choir parts. It was the height of the Brooklyn indie boom, when bands from a few blocks of Williamsburg drew international attention, and Jay-Z and Beyoncé famously attended one of their shows that year.","The title is the name of a small uninhabited island off Cape Cod, used as a kind of private emblem. Victoria Legrand of Beach House sings backing vocals on Two Weeks, one of several threads connecting the two bands. Listen to how the drumming of Christopher Bear, trained in jazz, pulls against the stately harmonies, and how the choir enters so subtly on some songs that you hear it only on a second listening.","Veckatimest was widely acclaimed and reached the top ten in America, very unusual for such an intricate record. Some critics found its perfectionism cool or emotionally distant. Two Weeks became their signature and a staple of film and television. The album is now seen as a peak of the late-2000s Brooklyn sound, and of a particular kind of chamber-pop craftsmanship.","Start with Two Weeks and follow the falsetto harmonies against the piano, then While You Wait for the Others for the interplay of two lead voices. Headphones reveal the woodwinds and the choir. Compare the Beach Boys' Pet Sounds for harmonic ambition and the Zombies' Odessey and Oracle for the doo-wop-inflected chamber sound. Then hear Animal Collective's Merriweather Post Pavilion, released the same year, for a louder cousin."],"beach-house-teen-dream":["Ten songs, each moving at a slow, rocking pace, like a lullaby with a pulse. Victoria Legrand's deep, androgynous voice floats over organ and synthesisers, while Alex Scally's guitar plays glinting, circling arpeggios and slide lines. Zebra opens the album with a harp-like figure; Norway warbles with a detuned, sliding vocal hook; Walk in the Park and Used to Be grow from simple patterns into big emotional crests. Everything is washed in reverb, but the songs are clear and strongly built.","Beach House's third album, and first for Sub Pop, released in January 2010 with a DVD of videos for every song. It was recorded with the producer Chris Coady in a converted church in upstate New York. Earlier Beach House records had used cheap drum machines and a thrift-store organ; Teen Dream kept that intimacy but added real drums and a fuller production. It became their breakthrough.","The warble on Norway is not a studio trick but a slide effect on both guitar and voice, bending pitch up and down like a record on a wobbly turntable. Listen to how often the drum machine and live drums overlap, giving the songs a slightly mechanical sway. Legrand is the niece of the French film composer Michel Legrand, and something of that cinematic sense of harmony is audible in the chords.","Reviews were superb and Teen Dream placed high on 2010 lists. It turned dream pop from a niche revival into a mainstream indie genre. Some critics said all the songs sounded alike, a charge that followed the band for years; admirers argued the point was depth rather than variety. Its influence on the moody, reverb-heavy pop of the 2010s, from indie to R&B, is broad.","Play it after dark and do not skip: the album's effect builds. Start with Zebra, then Norway, then Used to Be, and notice how every song climbs to one big moment. Headphones help you hear the organ under the guitar. Compare Cocteau Twins-style dream pop and My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for texture, and Kate Bush's Hounds of Love for a woman's voice at the centre of a vast sound."],"beach-house-bloom":["Ten songs, grander and more luminous than Teen Dream. Myth opens with a shimmering guitar figure and a huge, slow crescendo; Wild and Lazuli are propelled by pulsing drum machines and organ, building to overlapping vocal lines; Wishes and Other People glide at a mid-tempo sway. The last song, Irene, closes with a long, repeating coda that slowly thickens like fog. The sound is wider and cleaner, the guitars more ringing, Victoria Legrand's voice more commanding.","Recorded with Chris Coady at Sonic Ranch, a studio complex among pecan orchards near the Texas-Mexico border, and released by Sub Pop in May 2012. After Teen Dream the band had toured constantly and grown from a cult act into a festival draw. The pressure was to follow a breakthrough; their answer was to refine rather than reinvent. It debuted in the top ten of the American chart.","After Irene ends there is a long silence, and then a hidden song, Wherever You Go, appears as a secret coda, easily missed if you switch off. Listen too for how the album was built on patterns: many songs set a drum-machine pulse and a repeating organ chord and then layer voices and guitar on top, so each track grows like a flower opening, as the title suggests.","Reviews were very positive, though some felt it repeated Teen Dream. Pitchfork made it a Best New Music pick, and its sales confirmed Beach House as one of the big indie bands of the period. It has since been heard as the band's most complete statement of their style, and Myth is among their most loved songs. The debate over sameness has faded as the catalogue has grown.","Listen to the whole record in one go, and let Irene run to its end and beyond, waiting for the hidden song. Start with Myth, then Wild, then Lazuli. Notice how the drum machine and organ lock into a trance. Compare Teen Dream for the before and after, and Brian Eno's Another Green World for the sense of a record as a place you visit."],"mitski-puberty-2":["Eleven songs in about half an hour, lurching between near silence and full distortion. Happy opens with a drum machine and a mournful saxophone; Your Best American Girl builds from a quiet, doo-wop-style verse into a crushing, fuzz-guitar chorus; Fireworks is small, cold and calm; A Burning Hill closes the record with voice and guitar alone. The songs deal with love, depression, the wish to belong, and the fear that happiness always leaves a mess behind it.","Mitski made the album with her longtime producer and collaborator Patrick Hyland, and released it on Dead Oceans in June 2016. She had self-released two albums while studying composition at SUNY Purchase, and a third on an independent label. Puberty 2 was her first widely distributed record and turned her into one of the most discussed songwriters of her generation.","Your Best American Girl is about a relationship across cultural lines, in which the singer realises she can never be the kind of girl her partner's world expects. Musically, the song enacts this: the verse borrows a clean, all-American rock ballad style, and then the chorus drowns it in distortion, as if the smooth surface could no longer hold. Listen to how the saxophone on Happy returns at the end, like an intruder.","Pitchfork and many others praised it lavishly, and Your Best American Girl became one of the most celebrated indie songs of the decade. Some listeners found the abrupt shifts jarring, which was exactly her design. Mitski's rise has also sparked debate about intense fan cultures and the pressures on artists who write so personally; she has spoken about both.","Start with Your Best American Girl and brace yourself for the chorus. Then Happy for its saxophone, then A Burning Hill for the quiet ending. Short enough for a single sitting. Compare Nirvana's Nevermind for the loud-quiet structure and PJ Harvey's Rid of Me for a woman singer weaponising distortion. Then follow with Be the Cowboy to hear how much she changed in two years."],"mitski-be-the-cowboy":["Fourteen songs in about thirty-three minutes, most under two and a half minutes, many ending just as you expect a chorus. Geyser opens with an organ drone and explodes into a roar of distorted sound; Nobody is a lonely disco song with a bright piano and a cool vocal; Two Slow Dancers ends the record on a slow, piano-led waltz. There are synthesisers, horns and strings, glam-rock stomp and country shuffle. Songs feel like short scenes from a film rather than conventional tracks.","Released in August 2018 by Dead Oceans, again produced with Patrick Hyland. Mitski had been touring heavily, supporting Lorde and building a devoted young following. She has said she wrote the songs through a persona: a lonely, controlled woman, sometimes married, who longs for connection but holds back. The title encouraged her to take on the confidence of a cowboy, the swaggering white male figure she said she did not have access to.","Many songs are cut so short that a second verse or chorus never arrives, and the effect is to leave the listener wanting precisely what the characters want. Nobody sounds like a happy dance record, but it is about crushing loneliness; listen to how the backing vocals echo the word of the title until it sounds absurd. Me and My Husband sketches a marriage that stays together because the alternative is worse.","Pitchfork named it the best album of 2018, and it appeared at or near the top of many lists. Nobody became a streaming hit and later went viral again. Some critics felt the persona device was a clever way of dodging the confessional label; others still insisted on reading it as autobiography. It is now widely considered one of the defining albums of the 2010s.","Listen straight through: at thirty-three minutes it plays like a short story collection. Start with Geyser for the burst, then Nobody for the paradox, and end with Two Slow Dancers. Notice how many songs stop abruptly. Compare Kate Bush's Hounds of Love for persona and drama, and Lorde's Melodrama, from the same period, for pop that dramatises loneliness."],"phoebe-bridgers-stranger-in-the-alps":["Eleven songs, soft-spoken and wry, in which a close, double-tracked voice sings of loss, depression, strange dreams and anger over gentle guitar, piano and strings. Smoke Signals opens the record in a haze of reverb; Motion Sickness, the best-known song, is a mid-tempo rocker full of bitter jokes; Funeral imagines singing at the funeral of a boy her own age; Scott Street wanders through a lost friendship and ends with brass. The music is quiet, but the sentiments are often blunt and funny.","Released by Dead Oceans in September 2017, produced by Tony Berg and Ethan Gruska in Los Angeles. Bridgers had busked in Pasadena as a teenager and been encouraged early by established musicians. Motion Sickness is widely understood to be about a relationship with an older male musician, which she later discussed publicly as part of wider accusations against him. The debut quickly found a devoted following.","The title comes from a joke: in the television edit of the film The Big Lebowski, a crude line was redubbed with a nonsense phrase about a stranger in the Alps, and Bridgers liked the absurdity. Listen for such dry humour throughout, often hidden inside the saddest lines. The album also includes a cover of Mark Kozelek's You Missed My Heart, and a duet with Conor Oberst, Would You Rather.","Critics admired its craft and its voice, though some initially filed it as pleasant sad-folk. Its reputation grew as Bridgers's career did, and Motion Sickness became an anthem for a generation of listeners. It is now heard as the start of a songwriting style that dominated indie music in the following years: deadpan, confessional, specific and funny.","Start with Motion Sickness, then Funeral, then Scott Street, and pay attention to the words, which reward it. Listen for how close the voice is to the microphone; it is as if she is sitting next to you. Compare Elliott Smith's Either/Or, a direct influence, for whispered confession and double-tracked vocals, and Joni Mitchell's Blue for the exposed heart."],"phoebe-bridgers-punisher":["Eleven songs, from a short instrumental overture to a final apocalypse. Garden Song floats on a murmured, close-miked voice and a warbling synthesiser; Kyoto bounces on bright horns and a driving beat; the title track is a slow, piano-led tribute; Chinese Satellite and Moon Song move from quiet to overwhelming. The closer, I Know the End, begins as a whisper and ends in a storm of horns, drums and screaming. The sound is more expansive and more cinematic than her debut.","Recorded in Los Angeles with Tony Berg and Ethan Gruska and released by Dead Oceans in June 2020, during the first months of the pandemic, when its themes of isolation and the end of the world seemed prophetic. Bridgers had by then formed boygenius and Better Oblivion Community Center. Punisher earned four Grammy nominations, including Best New Artist, and made her one of the defining voices of 2020s indie.","The title song is about Elliott Smith, who had lived near where Bridgers grew up in Los Angeles; she imagines walking past his old house and the awkwardness of being an over-eager fan, the kind musicians call a punisher. Listen for his influence throughout, especially in the doubled voice. On I Know the End, the screaming at the close was recorded with friends and collaborators, a cathartic group scream.","Reviews were rapturous and it topped many 2020 lists. The dramatic ending of I Know the End became a live ritual. Some sceptics found the sad-girl persona overexposed in the press, and Bridgers herself mocked it. The album is now regarded as her masterwork to date and one of the most important indie albums of the pandemic era.","Hear Garden Song and Kyoto first, then Punisher, then I Know the End at full volume to the very end. Listen for the way the arrangements expand from bare voice to full ensemble within songs. Compare Elliott Smith's XO for the model, and Radiohead's OK Computer for a record that ends in a kind of apocalypse. Pair with boygenius's the record for the friendship behind it."],"big-thief-ufof":["Twelve songs, mostly hushed, in which Adrianne Lenker's small, cracking voice sings of animals, strangers, bodies and an unidentified friend from somewhere else. Acoustic guitars are fingerpicked in circling patterns, drums brush and tap, and electric guitars seep in like weather. The title track floats on a hypnotic riff; Cattails is a gentle country waltz; Contact builds to a sudden scream; Century and Orange feel like private prayers. The whole record sounds as if it were made in a wooden room in a forest.","Recorded live, with minimal overdubs, at Bear Creek Studio in Woodinville, Washington, surrounded by woods, and released by 4AD in May 2019. It was Big Thief's third album and first for 4AD. The band called it the celestial twin of a second album, Two Hands, recorded soon after in the Texas desert and released in the same year as its earthy twin. Both were produced by Andrew Sarlo.","U.F.O.F. stands for unidentified flying object friend. Several songs, including Terminal Paradise, had first appeared on Lenker's solo records and were reworked here with the band, so the album is also a dialogue with her solo work. Listen to how often the band's playing is left raw: breathing, creaks and fingers sliding on strings stay in the mix. That is intentional, a choice of intimacy over polish.","Reviews were superb and it placed high on many 2019 lists. Critics praised Lenker's lyrics, frequently compared to Joni Mitchell and Neil Young, and the band's quiet intensity. Some found the album too subdued compared with the louder Two Hands. It is now often considered the purest expression of the band's hushed, folk-rooted style.","Listen in a quiet room, ideally through good headphones. Start with Cattails for its warmth, then the title track, then Contact for the release. Pay attention to the details of sound in the room. Compare Nick Drake's Five Leaves Left for fingerpicked mystery and Neil Young's After the Gold Rush for a small voice cracking with feeling. Then hear Two Hands for the other twin."],"big-thief-dragon-new-mountain-i-believe-in-you":["Twenty songs, eighty minutes, recorded in four very different places, which gives the album the feel of a road trip. Change opens with a gentle acoustic ballad; Spud Infinity is a raucous hoedown with fiddle and jaw harp; Simulation Swarm glides on a mysterious guitar figure; Certainty is a warm, rolling country song shared between voices; Little Things is noisy and ecstatic. The styles range from Appalachian fiddle tunes to synthesiser experiments and electric rock, all held together by Adrianne Lenker's voice and the band's closeness.","Recorded in 2020 across four sessions, in upstate New York, Topanga Canyon in California, the Colorado Rockies and Tucson, Arizona, and released by 4AD in February 2022. The band worked through the pandemic with the producer and drummer James Krivchenia leading. The idea was to capture the feeling of each place and to let the songs change with the landscape, rather than making a unified studio album.","Many songs were recorded in the first or second take, with mistakes and laughter kept in; you can hear the band talking at points. Listen for the fiddle of Mat Davidson, a guest who adds an old-time country feeling, and for how Lenker's lyrics return to images of trees, animals and the body across the whole double album, tying the scattered sessions together.","Many publications ranked it among the best albums of 2022, and it received a Grammy nomination for Best Alternative Music Album. Some critics felt eighty minutes was too long and uneven; others argued the sprawl was the point, like a double album of the 1970s. It is now seen as the band's most ambitious and generous work.","Do not try to take it in all at once. Start with Change, Certainty and Simulation Swarm, then come back for Spud Infinity and Little Things. Notice how the sound of each recording location differs. Compare the Band's Music from Big Pink for a group recording together in a house, and the Carter Family for the old-time music under the surface."],"boygenius-the-record":["Twelve songs, each written and sung by one of Julien Baker, Phoebe Bridgers and Lucy Dacus with the other two harmonising. The album opens with Without You Without Them, a short a cappella hymn in three-part harmony. $20 is a fast, raucous rocker that ends in a shout; Not Strong Enough is a bright, driving guitar song; Cool About It and Leonard Cohen are quiet and wry. The record moves between intimate folk and full-band rock, united by the close interplay of three voices.","Released by Interscope in March 2023, five years after the trio's first EP. By then each member had become an established solo artist; Bridgers in particular was a star. They made the record in Los Angeles at Shangri-La studio with Catherine Marks. The album won three Grammys, including Best Alternative Music Album, and was followed by a sold-out tour and a short film by Kristen Stewart.","Cool About It borrows the melody of Simon and Garfunkel's The Boxer as its template, a knowing nod to a famous duo. The closing song, Letter to an Old Poet, reprises the melody of Me and My Dog from their 2018 EP, so the album ends by answering an old song. Listen for how the lead passes from voice to voice within songs, a structure that makes the band a real collective rather than three solo acts.","Critics praised it widely and it topped several year-end lists. Some felt it did not reach the heights of the three solo catalogues, and others pointed out how the band's fame drew attention to its friendship as much as its music. The Grammy haul confirmed their mainstream standing. It is now seen as a landmark of collaborative songwriting in 2020s rock.","Start with the a cappella opening, then $20 for its burst, then Not Strong Enough for its chorus. Listen for who is singing lead at each moment. Compare Leonard Cohen's Songs of Leonard Cohen, since one song here is named after him, and Taylor Swift's folklore for the same years' hushed, collaborative songwriting. Then return to each member's solo albums."],"car-seat-headrest-teens-of-denial":["Twelve songs in seventy minutes, many long and multi-part. Will Toledo's cracked, conversational baritone sings of depression, drugs, self-hatred and the comic absurdity of being young. Fill in the Blank opens with a surge of guitar and a shouted refrain; Vincent grows from a long, tense introduction into a sprawling jam; Drunk Drivers/Killer Whales builds to a huge, cathartic chant; The Ballad of the Costa Concordia runs eleven minutes and compares its narrator to the captain of the wrecked cruise ship. The sound is loose, big and joyful even when the words are not.","Toledo had released a dozen lo-fi albums on Bandcamp while still a teenager and in college in Virginia, before signing to Matador in 2015. Teens of Denial, released in May 2016, was his first album of all-new material recorded with a full band in a real studio in Seattle. It arrived at a time when indie rock was losing ground to pop and hip-hop, and its ambition surprised critics.","The first pressing of the album had to be recalled and destroyed: the song Not What I Needed originally included a reworking of the Cars' Just What I Needed, and Ric Ocasek declined permission. Toledo rewrote it. Listen for the self-quotation throughout: lines and melodies from earlier Bandcamp records return, disguised, as if the album were an argument with his own past.","Reviews were ecstatic and Teens of Denial placed high on many 2016 lists, often called the best guitar-rock album of the decade. Some found its length and wordiness exhausting. Toledo became a cult hero to younger listeners, and the album is regarded as a bridge between the lo-fi internet era and a classic indie-rock tradition that includes Pavement and the Strokes.","Start with Fill in the Blank, then Drunk Drivers/Killer Whales, and listen for the build to the chant. Then commit to The Ballad of the Costa Concordia. The words matter here: read along if you can. Compare Pavement's Crooked Rain, Crooked Rain and the Strokes' Is This It for the guitar lineage, and Neutral Milk Hotel's In the Aeroplane Over the Sea for raw personal epic."],"japanese-breakfast-jubilee":["Ten songs, deliberately bright, after two albums shaped by grief. Paprika opens with a marching band of horns and a swelling chorus; Be Sweet is a glossy 1980s-style synth-pop song; Kokomo, IN is a gentle, string-laden song from the perspective of a young man in a small Indiana town; Savage Good Boy satirises a billionaire planning to survive the apocalypse in a bunker. The closer, Posing for Cars, ends with a long, soaring guitar solo. The sound is sumptuous, orchestral and warm.","Michelle Zauner, who records as Japanese Breakfast, released Jubilee on Dead Oceans in June 2021, two months after her memoir Crying in H Mart, about her Korean mother's death, became a bestseller. The album was consciously about joy after years of writing about loss. It was co-produced with Craig Hendrix, and it earned Grammy nominations including Best New Artist and Best Alternative Music Album.","Posing for Cars was written with a guitar solo in mind from the start, and Zauner has spoken about deliberately taking a guitar hero's role. Listen for how the horn arrangements on Paprika echo marching bands and celebrations; the word jubilee itself signals festivity. Kokomo, IN borrows the viewpoint of a boy saying goodbye to a girl, a small, tender story told from outside the singer's own life.","Reviews were warm and it appeared on many 2021 lists. Critics praised the shift into brightness and the orchestral ambition, though some missed the rawness of the earlier records. Zauner's joint success as a memoirist and musician made her a rare crossover figure. It is regarded as her most confident record.","Start with Paprika and Be Sweet for the brightness, then Kokomo, IN for the tenderness, and end with Posing for Cars and its solo. Headphones reveal the orchestral details. Compare Kate Bush's Hounds of Love for ambitious art pop with a narrative bent, and Mitski's Be the Cowboy for a persona-driven record from a Japanese-American songwriter. Read Crying in H Mart alongside."],"wet-leg-wet-leg":["Twelve short songs of wiry guitar, walking bass and deadpan singing. Being in Love opens with a jittery riff; Chaise Longue, the breakthrough single, talks its way through double entendres over a bass line and a shouted chorus; Wet Dream and Angelica are tight, bouncy rockers; Ur Mum builds to a long, cathartic scream; Too Late Now slows into a glimmering, synth-backed ballad. The humour is dry and self-mocking, the music compact and catchy.","Rhian Teasdale and Hester Chambers, friends from the Isle of Wight, released Chaise Longue as their debut single on Domino in 2021; its homemade video went viral and the song became a hit. Their debut album, produced by Dan Carey in London, was released in April 2022 and went straight to number one in the UK. It later won the Grammys for Best Alternative Music Album and Best Alternative Music Performance.","Chaise Longue riffs on a line from the film Mean Girls, a nod to the millennial pop culture the band grew up with. Listen to Ur Mum, where Teasdale explains that she had been practising her longest, loudest scream, and then performs it; it is a joke about catharsis that turns into real catharsis. Dan Carey's production keeps the band's raw live sound, with little gloss.","The album was widely praised and the band's rise was astonishingly fast for an indie act. Some critics dismissed them as a novelty act, riding a single viral hit; others heard sharp songwriting and a winning attitude. The Grammy wins, over bigger names, surprised many. Their success pointed to a revival of post-punk-influenced guitar music in Britain.","Start with Chaise Longue and Being in Love, then Ur Mum for its scream, and finish with Too Late Now. Listen for how the bass carries the melody and the guitars scratch around it. Compare Wire's Pink Flag for the brisk post-punk ancestry, the Strokes' Is This It for the guitar sound, and Pavement for the slacker humour."],"alvvays-antisocialites":["Ten songs in half an hour, each built on jangling, reverb-soaked guitars, bright synthesisers and Molly Rankin's clear, sighing voice. In Undertow opens with a swirling guitar pattern and a song about letting go; Dreams Tonite is a dreamy, slow-dance ballad; Plimsoll Punks is a fast, sardonic rocker; Not My Baby floats in melancholy. The songs are very melodic and tightly written, with choruses that lodge in the head after one listen, and a mood of wistful, sunlit sadness.","Released in September 2017 by Polyvinyl in the United States and Royal Mountain in Canada, three years after Alvvays' debut had made them indie favourites. The band, based in Toronto but with roots in Nova Scotia and Prince Edward Island, produced it largely themselves, with guitarist Alec O'Hanley at the controls. It won the Juno Award for Alternative Album of the Year.","Rankin is the daughter of John Morris Rankin, fiddler of the Cape Breton family group the Rankin Family, and something of Celtic folk melody runs through her writing. Listen to Dreams Tonite, where the guitar and synthesiser play slightly off from each other, creating a woozy effect like an old film. Lollipop (Ode to Jim) is a playful nod to the Jesus and Mary Chain.","Reviews were very positive, praising its craft and the leap in production. Some critics felt the jangly dream-pop palette was familiar, drawing on the Smiths, Cocteau Twins and 1980s indie. Its reputation as a peak of 2010s guitar pop has grown, and Dreams Tonite in particular has become a signature song.","Start with In Undertow and Dreams Tonite, then Plimsoll Punks. Listen for how the guitar hooks are as melodic as the vocal lines. It is short enough to repeat immediately. Compare the Smiths' The Queen Is Dead for jangle and wit, and My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for the haze, then follow with Alvvays' own Blue Rev."],"alvvays-blue-rev":["Fourteen songs in under forty minutes, denser and louder than any Alvvays album before it. Pharmacist opens in a rush of distorted guitars; Easy On Your Own? and After the Earthquake race along; Belinda Says is a huge, soaring ballad that crashes into noise; Tom Verlaine nods to the guitarist of Television. Molly Rankin's voice rides above layers of shoegaze fuzz, and the songs often switch direction midway, like several ideas packed into one.","The album took five years, partly because the band's early demos were stolen in a break-in and a basement flood damaged their equipment. They recorded it with Shawn Everett in Los Angeles, playing songs straight through in long takes. It was released by Polyvinyl in October 2022, with a revised lineup including a new drummer and bassist, and it became their highest-charting record.","Belinda Says refers to the 1980s pop singer Belinda Carlisle's Heaven Is a Place on Earth, turning an old pop sentiment into something bittersweet. Listen for how the record plays almost without pause, as if it were one long take, which reflects how it was recorded. The guitar tones are thicker and more saturated than before, drawing on shoegaze bands of the early 1990s.","Blue Rev was widely hailed as the band's best album and placed high on many 2022 lists. Critics praised the leap in sonic ambition and songwriting, and Belinda Says was singled out as one of the songs of the year. A few found the density overwhelming on first listening. It is now regarded as a high point of recent guitar pop.","Play it loud, from start to finish, since the songs flow into each other. Start with Pharmacist, then Belinda Says, and listen for the moment the song opens up into its chorus. Compare My Bloody Valentine's Isn't Anything for the guitar wash, Television's Marquee Moon for the Tom Verlaine connection, and Beach House's Bloom for a gentler dream pop."],"deerhunter-microcastle":["A set of short, dreamy guitar songs split by a long, slow ambient section in the middle. Cover Me (Slowly) opens with a haze of guitar; Agoraphobia is a swaying, gentle pop song about wanting to be shut away; Never Stops and Nothing Ever Happened are propulsive and catchy, the latter building into a long, driving jam. In the middle, songs dissolve into drift and near silence. Bradford Cox's voice is reedy and far away, and the guitars ring and shimmer.","Deerhunter, from Atlanta, had been known for noisy, confrontational live shows. Microcastle, released by Kranky in October 2008, was a step toward melody and 1960s pop influences. It leaked online months before release, and the band responded by adding a second album, Weird Era Cont., to the physical release. Cox also recorded as Atlas Sound and wrote a busy, candid blog.","The ambient middle section is not dead space but a hinge: songs such as Calvary Scars and Green Jacket dissolve into drones before the album builds back up, so the record has the shape of a dream. Listen for how Lockett Pundt's Agoraphobia, his own song, has a gentler voice and melody than Cox's; the band's two writers are distinct and complementary.","Microcastle was widely acclaimed and appeared on many 2008 lists. Critics praised it as a leap from noise to pop, though some missed the raw edge of earlier records. It is now seen as a key album in the rise of hazy, melodic guitar music in the late 2000s, bridging shoegaze, garage rock and ambient.","Start with Agoraphobia and Nothing Ever Happened, then hear the whole album in order to feel the dip in the middle. Headphones help reveal the delay effects on the guitars. Compare My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for the haze, the Velvet Underground's third album for quiet melody, and Brian Eno's ambient records for the dream section."],"deerhunter-halcyon-digest":["Eleven songs about memory, nostalgia and loss, in a warm, hazy sound. Earthquake opens with a reversed, echoing guitar and a sleepy voice; Revival is a short, bright pop song; Desire Lines, written and sung by Lockett Pundt, drives into a long, shimmering guitar coda; Helicopter floats on a gentle, cascading riff; Coronado adds a honking saxophone; He Would Have Laughed closes the album in a long, pulsing meditation. The songs are more focused and more tender than before.","Released by 4AD in September 2010, recorded in Atlanta and produced with Ben H. Allen. Cox described the album as being about how memory rewrites the past, making it sweeter than it was. The closing song is dedicated to the garage-punk musician Jay Reatard, a friend of the band, who died in January 2010. It was the band's biggest commercial success.","The title suggests an imagined collection of happy memories, edited and rearranged like a magazine digest. Listen for how the guitars are processed so that they seem to decay and fade even as they play, a musical image of remembering. Desire Lines takes its name from the paths people wear across grass when they ignore official walkways, a quiet metaphor for choosing your own route.","It was among the best-reviewed albums of 2010 and placed high on many lists. Critics praised its warmth and focus and saw it as the band's most accessible record. Some fans missed the noise of the early work. Many now consider it Deerhunter's masterwork, and Desire Lines and Helicopter are among their most admired songs.","Start with Desire Lines and let its long coda run, then Helicopter, then He Would Have Laughed. Listen for the tape-like warmth of the sound. Compare the Velvet Underground's third album for gentle melody and the Beatles' Revolver for backward guitars and studio play. Then return to Microcastle to hear the band's growth."],"angel-olsen-burn-your-fire-for-no-witness":["Eleven songs, from hushed bedroom folk to fuzzy garage rock. Forgiven/Forgotten is a short, loud, distorted rock song; Hi-Five is a stomping, bluesy number with a twangy guitar; White Fire is long, quiet and haunting, with only voice and guitar; Windows closes the album with a gentle, hopeful ballad. Olsen's voice is the centre of everything: it moves from flat and deadpan to quavering vibrato to a full-throated howl, often within one song.","Released by Jagjaguwar in February 2014, produced by John Congleton, and Olsen's second album. She had moved from St. Louis to Chicago, sung backing vocals with Will Oldham's band, and self-released lo-fi folk records. This album added a full band and electric guitars, and marked her breakthrough to a wider audience. It arrived amid a new wave of women songwriters in indie rock.","The album's quieter songs, such as White Fire and Iota, were recorded with so much space and room noise that they feel almost like field recordings. Listen for Olsen's vibrato, which recalls singers of the 1950s and 1960s such as Roy Orbison and Patsy Cline. Hi-Five turns loneliness into a kind of joke, a wry reflection on meeting another lonely person.","It was widely praised and placed on many 2014 lists. Critics admired the range of styles and the power of the voice. Some felt it lacked coherence between the lo-fi and the rock songs. It is now seen as the beginning of Olsen's major period and one of the defining indie records of the mid-2010s.","Start with Forgiven/Forgotten and Hi-Five for the rock side, then White Fire for the quiet side, and listen for how her voice changes between them. Compare Leonard Cohen's Songs of Leonard Cohen for the hushed folk songs, and PJ Harvey's Rid of Me for the raw rock. Follow with My Woman for her next step."],"angel-olsen-my-woman":["Ten songs arranged in two halves. The first side is short, punchy and poppy: Intern opens with a haze of synthesisers; Never Be Mine is a bright, 1960s-style girl-group song; Shut Up Kiss Me is a fast, fuzzy rocker. The second side stretches out into long, slow songs: Sister runs over seven minutes, building from a gentle murmur to a long guitar solo; Woman is a smouldering, bluesy ballad that climaxes in a roar. The sound is richer and more varied than before.","Released by Jagjaguwar in September 2016, produced with Justin Raisen in Los Angeles. Olsen had toured extensively after her breakthrough and wanted to make a record that resisted the sad-songwriter label. The title suggests a statement of self-possession. It placed highly on many 2016 lists and consolidated her as one of the major figures of the era.","The split between a short-song first side and a long-song second side was deliberate, planned as two sides of a vinyl record. Listen to Sister, where the guitar solo that closes the song is not virtuosic but patient and circling, echoing the song's theme of change. The video for Shut Up Kiss Me, with Olsen in a silver wig, became a signature image.","It was widely acclaimed, and critics admired both the pop songs and the long epics. Some felt the two halves did not cohere, though others heard the contrast as the point. Sister and Woman became among her most admired songs. It is now often called her best album and a defining indie record of 2016.","Start with Shut Up Kiss Me for the rocker, then Sister for the long build, then Woman for the climax. Listen for how much the voice does. Compare Neil Young's Rust Never Sleeps for the split between acoustic and electric sides, and Fleetwood Mac's Rumours for the pop side. Then return to Burn Your Fire for No Witness."],"bathory-under-the-sign-of-the-black-mark":["Nine tracks of raw, trebly guitar played in fast tremolo strokes, drums that gallop and blur, and a voice pitched as a hoarse, distant shriek rather than a sung line. The production is thin and cavernous, as though the band were recorded at the far end of a stone hall. Enter the Eternal Fire slows the pace to a grim, mid-tempo march with a long chanted refrain, and that slow, ceremonial grandeur is what sets the record apart from its fast neighbours.","Bathory was essentially one young Stockholm musician, Quorthon, working with a shifting cast of helpers and a tiny budget. This third album came out in 1987, when Venom in England and Celtic Frost in Switzerland had already sketched a darker, cruder metal than the thrash then conquering America. Quorthon took their ideas further from the mainstream, and Swedish and Norwegian teenagers trading cassettes heard in this record a blueprint for a whole northern scene.","Listen closer to the guitar tone: almost no bass frequencies survive, so the riffs become a shimmering wash rather than a punch. That thinness, an accident of cheap recording, became a deliberate aesthetic for the Norwegian bands of the early 1990s. Notice too the keyboard drones and sound effects that open several tracks, an early hint of the atmospheric, almost orchestral ambitions Quorthon would pursue on later records.","Mainstream metal magazines largely ignored or mocked Bathory in the 1980s, finding the sound amateurish. Within a few years the record had become a sacred text for the second wave of black metal, cited by Darkthrone, Emperor and Mayhem. Historians now argue over whether Bathory or Venom deserves the title of founder, but most agree this album is where the genre's sound first fully cohered.","Start with Enter the Eternal Fire for its slow build, then go back to the faster tracks and listen to how the guitar becomes texture rather than riff. Compare it with Slayer's Reign in Blood, made the year before: similar speed, utterly different sound world. Then hear Darkthrone's A Blaze in the Northern Sky to see where it led."],"bathory-hammerheart":["Long, slow, majestic songs built on chugging mid-tempo riffs, layered choirs of overdubbed voices, and sound effects of wind, waves and crackling fire. Quorthon now sings in a cracked, earnest baritone instead of shrieking. Shores in Flames opens with ocean sounds and unfolds over eleven minutes; One Rode to Asa Bay closes the album with a stately, anthemic chorus. The overall effect is closer to a film soundtrack or a choral epic than to a metal record.","After pioneering black metal, Quorthon turned away from occult themes toward Scandinavia's pre-Christian past, and Hammerheart is the fullest statement of that turn. The songs are about Norse seafaring, ancestry and the coming of Christianity to the north. Recorded in Stockholm and released in 1990, it appeared just as the Norwegian scene Bathory had inspired was forming, and it pointed some of those musicians toward folk and heritage themes instead.","Listen closer to the massed vocals: they are Quorthon alone, stacked track upon track to imitate a chanting crowd. The harmonies move in slow parallel blocks like a hymn or a folk song rather than in the riff-driven patterns of metal. The drumming, too, often plays half time under the guitars, so the songs feel slower and heavier than their tempo suggests, the music striding rather than racing.","Some early fans felt betrayed by the shift from speed to grandeur, and the singing has always divided listeners. Over time Hammerheart has come to be seen as the founding document of Viking metal, the root of bands such as Enslaved and Moonsorrow and of the wider folk-metal boom, and its reputation has steadily climbed past that of Quorthon's earlier albums for many critics.","Begin with One Rode to Asa Bay and let the chorus carry you; then listen to Shores in Flames from the first wave sound to the end. Hear it beside Wagner's Ring, which it echoes in its saga ambition, and Sibelius's Tapiola, another northern landscape painted in slow, dark blocks."],"darkthrone-a-blaze-in-the-northern-sky":["Six long tracks of fuzzy, tremolo-picked guitar riffs over drums that switch between a punk-like skipping beat and slower, pounding sections. The vocals are a rasping whisper-shriek buried in the mix. Everything sounds as if heard through frost: no crisp low end, no polish, just a grey, hissing wall of sound. Kathaarian Life Code opens with a long eerie introduction before the band charges in, setting the record's mood of cold, hypnotic repetition.","Darkthrone, from Kolbotn outside Oslo, had released a technical death-metal album, Soulside Journey, in 1991. Within a year they rejected that style entirely, deliberately recording this follow-up with a raw, primitive sound in homage to Bathory, Celtic Frost and early Hellhammer. Their label, Peaceville, was reportedly uneasy about the change. The album became one of the first full statements of the Norwegian second wave of black metal.","Listen closer to the drums: Fenriz, the drummer, often plays a simple two-beat skank borrowed from hardcore punk rather than a metal blast. That punk pulse under the frozen riffs is a key to the genre. Several riffs and passages were written for the band's abandoned death-metal follow-up, so traces of that more complex style survive, reshaped, within the rawer songs.","At first many metal listeners heard only bad production. Within a few years the album was hailed as a cornerstone of black metal, and its low-fidelity sound became a doctrine for countless underground bands. Critics still debate how much the rawness was artistic choice and how much necessity, but the band has always insisted it was deliberate, a rejection of the polished death metal of its day.","Do not fight the hiss: turn it up and let the repetition work on you, as you might with minimalist music. Start with In the Shadow of the Horns. Compare its trance-like riff cycles with the drones of La Monte Young or Branca's guitar symphonies, then with the far more orchestral Emperor album two years later."],"darkthrone-transilvanian-hunger":["Eight songs that each rest on a handful of tremolo-picked guitar figures, repeated for minutes at a time over a steady, unchanging blast of drums. There are almost no solos, breakdowns or tempo changes. The sound is a hissing blizzard in which riff and cymbal blur together into a single shimmering texture. The title track sets the pattern: one melancholic phrase cycling until it becomes a kind of chant, with the rasped vocal floating on top.","By 1994 Darkthrone had shrunk to a duo. Fenriz played every instrument, recording at home on a four-track machine, and Nocturno Culto added the vocals. Several lyrics were contributed by an outside musician from the Norwegian scene. The album appeared amid a period of lurid press attention to Norwegian black metal, and the band deliberately turned away from publicity, refusing to tour and letting the recordings speak alone.","Listen closer to how little actually changes. Each riff is a slowly shifting minor-key pattern, and the drums hardly vary for whole songs. That stillness inside speed is close to the procedures of minimalism: the music works by saturation, and tiny changes of chord feel enormous after minutes of repetition. The guitar is barely separated from the cymbals in the mix, so harmony and noise genuinely merge.","Even admirers of the previous album found this one almost unlistenably bare on release, and a controversy over the original sleeve notes followed the band for years. Today it is regarded as the purest distillation of the second-wave black-metal style, endlessly imitated, and critics now often discuss it alongside drone and minimalist music rather than only as metal.","Treat it like a long drone piece: put it on loud, do not wait for a chorus, and listen for the moments a riff finally shifts. The title track and Over fjell og gjennom torner are good entry points. Then try Sunn O)))'s Monoliths and Dimensions to hear the same stillness slowed almost to a halt."],"death-scream-bloody-gore":["Ten short, furious songs built on chromatic, down-tuned riffs, sudden tempo changes and drums that lurch between galloping thrash beats and outright blur. Chuck Schuldiner's vocal is a ragged, guttural roar rather than singing, and his solos are fast and wild. The songs take their subjects from horror films. Zombie Ritual, with its slow, crushing middle section, is the track most listeners remember, and it shows the record's trademark lurch from frantic speed to grinding weight.","Schuldiner had founded the band as a teenager in Florida as Mantas, circulating demo tapes through the international tape-trading network. For this debut he moved briefly to California, recording with the drummer Chris Reifert and playing guitar and bass himself. Released by Combat in 1987, it arrived when thrash was peaking, and it pushed that style into something lower, uglier and more extreme that soon had its own name.","Listen closer to the tuning and the riffs: the guitar uses dense, close-packed note clusters that slide by half steps, a sound closer to horror-film scoring than to blues-rooted metal. The structures are also more episodic than thrash, with abrupt shifts between unrelated riffs. Those sudden cuts, crude here, became the seed of the elaborate, multi-section composition Schuldiner pursued on later albums.","Many consider it the first true death-metal album, though Possessed's Seven Churches of 1985 is a rival claim. Mainstream critics dismissed it, but it was enormously influential underground, and Tampa's studios soon filled with bands following its lead. Schuldiner himself later regarded its gory themes as juvenile, and the debate now is how much his later sophistication grew directly from this primitive start.","Start with Zombie Ritual and listen for the contrast between the racing verses and the slow, heavy break. Compare it with Slayer's Reign in Blood, its nearest ancestor, to hear how death metal lowered the voice and the tuning. Then jump to the band's Symbolic, made eight years later, to hear how far the same musician travelled."],"death-human":["Eight tightly packed songs in just over half an hour, full of angular riffs, odd-length phrases and sudden changes of metre. The guitars trade intricate harmonised lines, the fretless bass slides audibly underneath, and the drumming darts between blasts and jazz-like fills. Flattening of Emotions opens at full intensity; Cosmic Sea is an instrumental that drifts into almost spacey territory. The lyrics now address personal and social questions rather than horror themes.","Schuldiner rebuilt the band for this album, recruiting the guitarist Paul Masvidal and drummer Sean Reinert from the Miami group Cynic, and the bassist Steve DiGiorgio. It was recorded at Morrisound in Tampa, then the centre of the death-metal world, with producer Scott Burns. Released in 1991, it appeared as the genre was briefly courted by major labels and as many bands chased ever more brutal sounds.","Listen closer to the bass: DiGiorgio's fretless instrument slides between notes like a jazz player's, often playing its own melodic line rather than doubling the guitars. Reinert's drumming also brings jazz-fusion touches, cymbal accents and displaced beats that most metal drummers of the time avoided. Those details link the album to Mahavishnu Orchestra and Weather Report as much as to Slayer.","Human was hailed almost immediately as a leap forward, and it is regarded as one of the founding works of technical and progressive death metal. Some purists found it too clean and cerebral. Today it is often ranked the band's best record, and its lineup's jazz leanings are seen as the start of a line running through Cynic and Atheist to modern progressive metal.","Start with Lack of Comprehension, the album's best-known track, and listen for the moment the guitars split into harmony. On a second pass follow only the bass. Compare it with the rawness of Darkthrone, recorded a year later as a deliberate rejection of exactly this kind of precision."],"death-symbolic":["Nine songs that keep the aggression of death metal but open it out with clear melodies, long harmonised guitar themes and spacious mid-tempo sections. Schuldiner's voice has risen to a higher, rasping scream that carries words more clearly. The title track begins with a ringing, almost heroic riff; Crystal Mountain is built around an unforgettable melodic guitar figure; the closing Perennial Quest stretches past eight minutes with a long, reflective instrumental passage.","Recorded at Morrisound with producer Jim Morris and released by Roadrunner in 1995, Symbolic featured the drummer Gene Hoglan, known for his precise power, alongside guitarist Bobby Koelble and bassist Kelly Conlon. Death metal's commercial moment had passed and many Florida bands were struggling. Schuldiner was increasingly drawn toward melodic, traditional heavy metal, a direction he later pursued in his band Control Denied before his death from brain cancer in 2001.","Listen closer to how the riffs are developed: a melody introduced early in a song often returns transformed, slower or harmonised, near the end, giving the songs an almost classical sense of recapitulation. Hoglan's drumming, meanwhile, places double-kick patterns under slow guitar figures so the music feels simultaneously fast and stately. The production is unusually clear for the genre, every instrument audible.","Widely praised on release, Symbolic has only grown in stature and is often called the most accessible great death-metal album. Some fans of the band's brutal early work hear it as a softening; others argue it is the summit of Schuldiner's songwriting. It is a frequent entry point for listeners from progressive rock, and an acknowledged model for melodic and progressive extreme metal.","Start with Crystal Mountain, then hear the title track. Listen for the melodies that you can actually hum beneath the roar. Compare it with Iron Maiden's harmonised guitars and with Opeth's Blackwater Park, which inherited its blend of beauty and heaviness."],"morbid-angel-altars-of-madness":["Ten tracks of very fast, tightly controlled riffing, shifting tempos and blast-beat drumming, with David Vincent's growled vocals and bass at the centre. The guitar solos by Trey Azagthoth are unlike anything in thrash: whammy-bar dives, squeals and chaotic flurries that sound almost like free jazz or electronic noise. The songs draw on occult and mythological imagery. Immortal Rites opens the record with a slow, ominous introduction before the band explodes into speed.","Morbid Angel formed in Tampa in the mid-1980s and spent years refining their sound before this debut, recorded at Morrisound with Tom Morris and released by the British label Earache in 1989. Tampa was becoming the world capital of death metal, with Death, Obituary and Deicide nearby. Earache's distribution helped carry this sound to Europe, where it found a huge underground audience.","Listen closer to Azagthoth's solos: rather than scales and melodies, he uses pitch-bending, feedback and sheer gesture, closer to the textural writing of Penderecki or the free improvising of the 1960s than to guitar-hero convention. The rhythm section, meanwhile, tightens death metal enormously; Pete Sandoval's blast beats, borrowed from grindcore, are played with machine-like precision that set a new standard.","Altars of Madness was immediately recognised underground as a landmark and is often named the greatest death-metal debut. It established precision as a death-metal virtue alongside brutality. Critics have long discussed how its chaotic solos and tight riffs coexist, and it remains a touchstone cited by technical death-metal and black-metal bands alike.","Start with Chapel of Ghouls and listen for the difference between the disciplined riffs and the wild, unhinged solos. Then hear Penderecki's Threnody for its similar use of pure texture. Compare the drumming with Napalm Death's Scum, where the blast beat began in a much rawer form."],"morbid-angel-blessed-are-the-sick":["A heavier, slower and more atmospheric follow-up, in which crushing mid-tempo riffs replace much of the debut's speed. Short instrumental interludes for keyboards and acoustic guitar punctuate the album, giving it the feel of a dark suite. The title track lumbers at a funereal tempo, while Fall from Grace builds slowly into a churning, many-sectioned song. The occult themes continue, but the emphasis has moved toward weight and grandeur rather than velocity.","Recorded again at Morrisound in Tampa and released by Earache in 1991, the album came at the height of Florida death metal's international fame. The band was touring Europe heavily, and Trey Azagthoth was developing an interest in keyboards and in classical music, which he folded into the record. The cover uses a painting by the Belgian symbolist Jean Delville, an unusual choice for a metal sleeve.","Listen closer to the interludes: they are short keyboard and guitar pieces with a pseudo-classical or medieval flavour, written to sit between the heavy songs like chapel music between sermons. They change the pacing of the whole album, giving the heavy songs space to land. Notice too how the guitars are tuned lower and the tempos dropped, letting individual chords ring for their full weight.","Some fans missed the debut's speed, but the album was widely admired for its ambition and is regarded by many as the band's strongest. Its interludes and slower tempos are cited as an early sign of death metal reaching toward atmosphere and composition, a path later bands took much further. The Delville cover has itself become a famous example of fine art in metal.","Play it straight through, interludes included, as a single dark suite. Begin with Fall from Grace and listen for the moment it shifts gears. Compare the brooding pace with Candlemass's doom, and the keyboard pieces with the moody instrumental passages on Opeth's Blackwater Park."],"napalm-death-scum":["Twenty-eight tracks in about half an hour, most lasting under a minute and one, You Suffer, a famous second or so. Distorted bass and guitar churn through hardcore-punk riffs while the drummer plays the blast beat, an impossibly fast alternation of snare and kick that sounds like a jackhammer. Vocals are a mixture of hoarse shouting and deep growling. The songs address political subjects such as capitalism and animal rights. The overall sound is chaotic and overwhelming.","Napalm Death came from the Birmingham area and grew out of the anarcho-punk scene. The two sides of Scum were recorded by almost entirely different lineups in 1986, with only the drummer Mick Harris playing on both. Side one features Justin Broadrick, later of Godflesh; side two Lee Dorrian, later of Cathedral, and Bill Steer, later of Carcass. Released as Earache's first album, it was championed on BBC radio by John Peel.","Listen closer to the difference between the two sides: the first is murkier and more punk; the second is tighter, deeper and closer to metal. You are hearing the birth of grindcore and its splitting into heirs, since the departing members went on to found industrial metal, doom and goregrind bands. The blast beat itself, which Harris made famous here, reached death and black metal within a few years.","Press reaction ranged from bafflement to scorn, but John Peel's sessions brought the band a cult audience and a place in record-books for the shortest recorded song. Scum is now universally recognised as grindcore's founding album and a key influence on extreme metal. Critics continue to debate whether it is best heard as punk, metal, or a kind of noise music.","Do not try to follow every track; listen to the album as one torrent and notice how the blast beat feels more like texture than rhythm. Compare the first and second sides. Then hear Godflesh's Streetcleaner to hear what one founding member did next, at the opposite extreme of tempo."],"carcass-heartwork":["Ten songs that pair death metal's aggression with clean, memorable riffs and twin-guitar harmonies in the tradition of classic British heavy metal. The tempos are mostly mid-paced, the production bright and punchy, and Jeff Walker's rasped vocal is unusually intelligible. The title track rides on a soaring harmonised lead; No Love Lost is built around a rolling, catchy riff. Guitar solos by Bill Steer and Michael Amott are melodic and composed rather than chaotic.","Carcass, from Liverpool, began as a grindcore band whose early records were notorious for gruesome medical imagery. By 1993 they had moved toward melody and structure, and Heartwork, produced by Colin Richardson for Earache, completed the shift. The cover reproduces a sculpture by H. R. Giger. The album appeared just as Swedish bands in Gothenburg were exploring similar melodic territory, and it helped steer that movement.","Listen closer to how many riffs would sit happily on a Judas Priest or Iron Maiden record if played cleaner and faster: Carcass smuggled traditional metal melody into death metal. Listen too for the restraint of the drums, which mostly avoid blast beats, letting the riffs groove. That groove, rather than speed, is the record's real engine and the reason it remains so playable.","Heartwork divided old fans, who saw it as a sell-out of the band's grindcore roots, and won many new ones. It is now widely credited as a founding document of melodic death metal, influencing At the Gates, In Flames and later metalcore. The band split up soon after, which only increased the album's legend.","Start with the title track and listen for the guitar harmony line, then hear Judas Priest's Stained Class to recognise its ancestry. Compare it with Napalm Death's Scum, which Bill Steer also played on, to hear how far one guitarist moved in six years."],"candlemass-epicus-doomicus-metallicus":["Six long, slow songs built on huge, grinding riffs and a booming, operatic tenor voice. Tempos are funereal, often barely moving, so each chord seems to hang in the air. Solitude opens with a gentle acoustic introduction before the riff crashes in and the singer laments in a style closer to opera than to rock. Black Stone Wielder and A Sorcerer's Pledge stretch toward eight and nine minutes, the last with a ghostly female voice.","Candlemass came from Stockholm and was led by bassist and songwriter Leif Edling. For this debut, released in 1986, they used the session singer Johan Längqvist. It appeared at the height of the thrash era, when speed was everything, and it deliberately turned the other way, back to the slow, heavy model of early Black Sabbath. Its title, a mock-Latin phrase, gave the doom genre its name.","Listen closer to the melodic writing: Edling's riffs often move in slow, step-wise minor-key lines with a solemn, almost liturgical feel, and the vocals ride above them like a cantor. The guitars sometimes double the vocal melody in harmony, a classical-orchestral technique. Doom here takes Black Sabbath's dread and gives it the formal gravity of a requiem or a dirge.","Sales were small and the original label folded, but the album gradually acquired a towering reputation and is regarded as the founding text of epic doom metal. Längqvist's voice was later considered one of the genre's finest despite his brief stay. Critics often cite it as proof that metal could reach for operatic seriousness without speed.","Start with Solitude, and give it time: the slowness is the point. Listen for the way each riff is allowed to ring out. Compare it with Black Sabbath's debut, then with Sleep's Holy Mountain, which took the same slowness in a looser, more hypnotic direction."],"sleep-holy-mountain":["Nine songs of thick, fuzzy, slow riffs played by a trio that sounds far bigger. Matt Pike's guitar is massively distorted and warm; Al Cisneros sings in a nasal chant over his own rumbling bass; Chris Hakius's drums lope with a swing borrowed from 1970s rock. Dragonaut opens with one of the most famous riffs in stoner metal. The songs dwell on fantasy and science-fiction imagery, and the whole record moves in a heavy, hypnotic groove.","Sleep formed in San Jose, California, from the remains of an earlier hardcore band. Their second album, released by Earache in 1992, came out at the height of grunge, when the Seattle sound was conquering the charts. Sleep looked instead to Black Sabbath's early albums with a reverence few bands then shared. It helped define the stoner and doom scene alongside bands like Kyuss in the desert to the south.","Listen closer to how closely the album models Black Sabbath, in riff shapes, guitar tone, and the way Cisneros's vocal phrasing echoes Ozzy Osbourne. Critics at the time thought it almost a pastiche. But notice the difference: Sleep repeats riffs far longer than Sabbath ever did, beginning a move toward pure repetition that the band took to its limit on Dopesmoker.","Initially seen by some as a Sabbath tribute, the album has become a cornerstone of stoner metal and is frequently ranked among the genre's best. Its reputation grew in step with the band's legend after their split in the late 1990s. Many musicians cite Dragonaut as the riff that drew them into heavy music.","Start with Dragonaut and listen to the swing in the drums, which keeps all the weight from sinking. Then hear Black Sabbath's Master of Reality side by side to trace the lineage. Go on to Queens of the Stone Age's Songs for the Deaf for the desert branch of the same family."],"sleep-dopesmoker":["A single track of more than an hour, built on one colossal, slow riff and its variations, played at a crawling tempo with enormous amplified volume. Al Cisneros intones the vocals like a chant; the song is about a pilgrimage of desert-dwelling believers, in an allegory partly drawn from cannabis culture. Matt Pike's solos wander over the drone at long intervals. The effect is ritual and meditative, like a procession that never ends.","Sleep spent years on this one piece after signing to the major label London Records in the mid-1990s. The label balked at a single hour-long track, and the band broke up around 1998. A shortened version circulated as Jerusalem in 1999; the full mix, titled Dopesmoker, was finally released by Tee Pee in 2003. It became a legend of artistic stubbornness and the slowest end of the stoner-doom movement.","Listen closer to how the riff evolves: it is not one repeated figure but a set of near-identical variants that shift in tiny ways over many minutes, with drum fills marking new sections like bell strokes in a slow liturgy. The structure is closer to a drone composition or a raga's slow unfolding than to a song, and the volume makes overtones bloom in the room.","Once seen as an act of commercial self-destruction, Dopesmoker is now celebrated as a high point of doom and drone. Critics compare its patience to minimalism and to Indian classical music. Debates about which version, Jerusalem or Dopesmoker, best represents the band's intentions continue among fans, though Dopesmoker has generally prevailed.","Set aside an hour, play it loud, and do not wait for a chorus. Listen for small changes in the riff and for Pike's first solo arriving after a long wait. Compare its pace with La Monte Young's Trio for Strings and with Sunn O)))'s Monoliths and Dimensions."],"electric-wizard-dopethrone":["Eight long tracks of extremely slow, extremely distorted riffs, the guitar and bass so saturated with fuzz that they become a single murky roar. Jus Oborn's vocal floats, half-drowned, somewhere in the middle of the sound. Funeralopolis builds from a slow crawl to a pounding climax; the title track lasts over twenty minutes. The album's themes draw on horror films, occult imagery and drug culture, but it is the sheer density of sound that defines it.","Electric Wizard formed in Wimborne, Dorset, in the early 1990s. Dopethrone, their third album, was released in 2000 by Lee Dorrian's label Rise Above, which specialised in doom. The band had little money and a turbulent history, and the recording was made quickly. It arrived as interest in heavy, slow music was reviving across Europe and America, and it became a rallying point for a new generation of doom bands.","Listen closer to the mix: the bass and guitar are nearly impossible to tell apart, occupying the same murky low frequencies, and that blurring is the band's signature. Short film samples are woven between songs, and the drums are recorded with a dry, boxy thud that contrasts with the guitar's swamp. The muddiness is deliberate, building on the lo-fi aesthetics of black metal.","The album was praised by underground press on release and has since been called one of the heaviest albums ever made. It is often cited as the defining record of the band's career. Some critics find it monotonous; admirers argue that the monotony is precisely the point, a total immersion in sound.","Start with Funeralopolis and notice the moment it lurches from slow to faster. Play it loud enough to feel the bass. Compare it with Black Sabbath's Master of Reality, then with Sleep's Holy Mountain, and hear how much heavier and murkier this English take became."],"meshuggah-nothing":["Ten slow-to-mid-tempo tracks dominated by low, palm-muted guitar riffs that stutter in long, irregular cycles over a steady drum pulse. The tempos are slower than the band's earlier albums, making the grooves heavier and more hypnotic. Stengah and Rational Gaze present the style in its clearest form: a riff that seems to stumble, then lock back in. Long, liquid guitar solos float above, and closing passages drift toward ambient drone.","Released by Nuclear Blast in 2002, Nothing was the Swedish band's first album built around seven-string guitars tuned very low, later re-recorded with eight-string guitars for a 2006 reissue. It arrived as nu metal ruled the charts, and its slow, grinding grooves shared some low-tuned heaviness with that scene, but Meshuggah's rhythmic complexity set it apart. Touring with Tool, the band reached a wider audience.","Listen closer to the drums: the cymbals often keep a regular four-beat pulse while the kick drum doubles the guitar's irregular riff, so you are hearing two rhythmic layers at once. The riff cycles last odd numbers of beats and resolve only after many bars, a technique of rhythmic phasing. Guitars and kick are so precisely matched that they become one percussive instrument.","Some fans found the slower tempos a letdown after the band's faster earlier albums, but Nothing has come to be seen as the template for the 2000s djent movement and a key progressive-metal record. Its original guitar tuning and the re-recorded version have been debated, with many preferring the re-recording's clarity.","Start with Stengah, tapping your foot to the cymbals, then follow the kick drum and notice how differently it moves. Compare it with the band's earlier Destroy Erase Improve, and with Steve Reich's Clapping Music for the rhythmic phasing. Seek the 2006 re-recorded edition for the clearest guitar sound."],"meshuggah-bleed":["A seven-minute track built almost entirely on one relentless pattern: the kick drum plays fast triplets locked to a low, stuttering guitar riff while the cymbals keep a regular pulse. The riff shifts in subtle ways over the song, creating a churning, mechanical momentum. Jens Kidman's bark sits on top, and a brief, eerie solo appears near the end. The song is about a mind and body in breakdown, but its force lies in its rhythmic insistence.","Bleed appeared on Meshuggah's 2008 album obZen, released by Nuclear Blast. By then the band was a cult giant whose influence had spawned an entire subgenre, djent. The song became a notorious challenge for drummers, its pattern widely shared online in the new era of video-sharing, and it helped bring the band to its widest audience yet. The guitarists were now using eight-string instruments.","Listen closer to the kick drum: Tomas Haake plays almost continuous triplets with both feet for most of the song, a feat of endurance. But the real trick lies in how the guitar riff phrases those triplets in irregular groups, so accents shift against the cymbal pulse. What sounds like chaos is a strict, carefully calculated pattern.","Bleed quickly became Meshuggah's best-known song and is often called the definitive djent track. Drummers and musicologists have analysed its patterns in detail. Some listeners find it too repetitive; its admirers argue it shows how repetition with tiny shifts can be intensely engrossing, a link to minimalist composition.","Listen first to the cymbals, then the kick drum, then the guitar, and notice how each layer seems to tell a different rhythmic story. Compare it with Nancarrow's player-piano studies, and then with Reich's Drumming for another work built on unrelenting, shifting patterns."],"mastodon-leviathan":["Ten tracks of thick, churning riffs, busy, jazz-inflected drumming and shouted, harmonised vocals, organised as a loose retelling of Moby-Dick. Blood and Thunder opens with a galloping riff and a pounding chorus; Hearts Alive stretches past thirteen minutes, moving through quiet guitar passages to a heavy climax. The drumming of Brann Dailor is constantly inventive, filling every gap, and the guitars mix sludge-metal weight with progressive rock's intricate interplay.","Mastodon formed in Atlanta in 2000. Leviathan, their second album, was recorded with producer Matt Bayles and released by Relapse in 2004. It arrived when American heavy metal was in a creative revival after nu metal's decline, and it found an audience among both metal fans and critics who normally avoided the genre. The Moby-Dick concept became a talking point, pushing the band toward progressive ambition.","Listen closer to the drums: Dailor rarely plays a simple beat, instead filling the music with fills and cross-rhythms more often heard in jazz fusion. The guitars often play counter-lines rather than doubling each other. Notice too how the album's structure follows the novel's arc, from departure to the long, epic confrontation of Hearts Alive.","Leviathan was named album of the year by several metal magazines and won unusual praise in the mainstream press. It is now seen as a key work in the 2000s revival of progressive and sludge metal. Some critics initially found it overbusy; it is now regarded as one of the decade's defining heavy records.","Start with Blood and Thunder and listen to the drums. Then hear Hearts Alive in full, following its long arc. Compare it with King Crimson's Red, with which it shares an intensity, and with Neurosis's Through Silver in Blood, a key influence on its heavier passages."],"mastodon-crack-the-skye":["Seven long songs that move away from roaring vocals toward sung melody, with layered keyboards, swirling guitars and an expansive, almost psychedelic sound. The Czar is an eleven-minute suite in four parts; The Last Baron closes the album at thirteen minutes with intricate guitar interplay. The concept is a fantastical story involving astral travel, a paraplegic boy and Rasputin, partly a tribute to the drummer's late sister, Skye.","Recorded with producer Brendan O'Brien and released by the major label Reprise in 2009, this fourth album saw Mastodon embrace classic progressive rock influences such as Yes, Genesis and King Crimson more openly than ever. It arrived when progressive metal was flourishing, and it reached the top ten of the Billboard album chart, a rare feat for such ambitious heavy music.","Listen closer to the keyboards and vocal harmonies, which add colours borrowed from 1970s progressive rock, especially Pink Floyd and Yes. The drummer Brann Dailor sings lead on several passages for the first time. The structures borrow from progressive-rock suites, with recurring themes that tie the album together like the movements of a symphony.","Crack the Skye was greeted as the band's most mature work and is often ranked among the best metal albums of its decade. A few older fans missed the earlier ferocity. Its success is widely cited as evidence that progressive metal could reach the mainstream without compromise.","Start with The Czar and follow its four sections, then The Last Baron. Notice how melodies recur. Compare it with Genesis's Selling England by the Pound and Pink Floyd's Dark Side of the Moon to hear its progressive-rock lineage, and listen for the keyboards that tie the suites together."],"gojira-from-mars-to-sirius":["Twelve tracks of crushing, precisely played riffs and thunderous double-kick drumming, interspersed with spacious, atmospheric passages. Joe Duplantier's roared vocals address ecological themes in a loose story of a quest to restore life to a dying planet. Flying Whales opens with ghostly guitar sounds imitating whale song before a massive riff enters. Backbone and The Heaviest Matter of the Universe show the band's trademark: mechanical precision with a strange, organic weight.","Gojira formed in Bayonne, in the French Basque Country, and centred on the brothers Joe and Mario Duplantier. This third album, released by a small French label in 2005, brought them international attention and later a major-label deal. Environmental concern was not common in metal, and the band's sincerity on the subject stood out. Their sound drew on Morbid Angel, Meshuggah and Metallica.","Listen closer for the pick scrape: Joe Duplantier drags his pick along the strings to make a whooshing, sliding noise that became his signature. Listen too for the whale-like harmonics in Flying Whales, made by guitar tricks rather than samples. The drumming of Mario Duplantier often locks with the riffs in odd, staggered patterns reminiscent of Meshuggah, but with a distinct, earthy swing.","From Mars to Sirius is widely regarded as Gojira's breakthrough and one of the key metal albums of the 2000s. Critics have praised its blend of death-metal heaviness with progressive structure and serious themes. It paved the way for the band's later fame, culminating in their performance at the 2024 Paris Olympic opening ceremony.","Start with Flying Whales and listen for the whale-like guitar sounds before the riff crashes in. Then try Backbone, noting the pick scrapes. Compare it with Mastodon's Leviathan, a near-contemporary sea epic, and with Meshuggah's Nothing for its rhythmic precision."],"deftones-around-the-fur":["Ten songs that swing between quiet, brooding verses and crushing, down-tuned riffs, with Chino Moreno's voice moving from a whisper to a scream. My Own Summer (Shove It) opens with a lurching riff and a hazy, dreamlike verse; Be Quiet and Drive (Far Away) is a soaring, melancholy anthem that sounds more like shoegaze than metal. Stephen Carpenter's guitar is heavy but airy, and the drums of Abe Cunningham are subtle and swinging.","Deftones formed in Sacramento, California. Around the Fur, their second album, was produced by Terry Date and released by Maverick in 1997. It arrived as nu metal was taking shape around Korn and others, and Deftones were often grouped with that scene, but they drew equally on The Cure, the Smiths and My Bloody Valentine. The record's success established them as a major band.","Listen closer to Moreno's vocals: he often sings just behind the beat in a breathy murmur, a technique borrowed from 1980s gothic and dream-pop singers, then explodes into a scream. The guitar often holds a single chord as a wall of sound rather than playing riffs. These textural choices separate the band from the rhythm-first approach of other nu-metal acts.","Around the Fur was well received and has grown in stature, now seen as a key album of late-1990s heavy music. Critics increasingly distance Deftones from nu metal, praising their atmospheric ambitions. The album set up the bolder experiments of White Pony.","Start with Be Quiet and Drive (Far Away) and listen to how heavy guitars and dreamy vocals coexist. Then hear My Own Summer for the contrast between whisper and roar. Compare it with The Cure's Disintegration and My Bloody Valentine's Loveless."],"deftones-white-pony":["Eleven songs in which the band's heaviness is wrapped in layers of atmosphere: shimmering guitars, keyboards and electronic textures. Change (In the House of Flies) is a slow, haunting song built on a hypnotic guitar figure; Digital Bath drifts like a dream; Passenger features Tool's Maynard James Keenan in a vocal duet. Elite is the heaviest track, all distorted screams. The mood is moody, sensual and introspective, closer to trip-hop or shoegaze than to metal.","Released by Maverick in 2000 and again produced by Terry Date, White Pony came as nu metal reached its commercial peak, and Deftones consciously distanced themselves from that scene. Frank Delgado, now a full member, added keyboards and samples. The album was a commercial success and Elite won the 2001 Grammy for Best Metal Performance.","Listen closer to the keyboards and samples, which Delgado uses not as effects but as a full layer of sound, giving the album its atmosphere. Notice how Moreno's voice is often doubled or layered, creating a dreamy, ghostly effect. Several songs move in slow, repeated cycles like trip-hop tracks, borrowing that genre's moody use of space and texture.","White Pony was widely acclaimed and is now regarded as one of the best albums in heavy rock history. Critics often cite it as the record that transcended nu metal. It is a key influence on later bands that mix heaviness with atmosphere, from post-metal to shoegaze-revival groups.","Start with Change (In the House of Flies) and let its slow build work on you. Then hear Digital Bath and Passenger. Compare it with Massive Attack's Mezzanine for its moody textures, and with Tool's Lateralus for the meeting of minds on Passenger."],"system-of-a-down-toxicity":["Fourteen short, explosive songs that lurch between frenetic metal riffs, Armenian folk melodies, operatic vocal leaps and absurdist humour. Serj Tankian's voice moves from croon to shriek to rapid-fire chatter, often in a single line. Chop Suey! alternates frantic riffing with a soaring, mournful chorus; Aerials is slower and more hypnotic; the title track builds on a lilting, folk-like riff. The songs address political and social subjects with sharp, unpredictable shifts of mood.","System of a Down were four Armenian-Americans from Los Angeles, descendants of genocide survivors, and their political engagement shaped their music. Toxicity, produced by Rick Rubin, was released by American Recordings on 4 September 2001, a week before the September 11 attacks. It debuted at number one on the Billboard chart. Chop Suey! was briefly pulled from some radio playlists after the attacks.","Listen closer to the melodies, which often use the scales of Armenian and Middle Eastern folk music, with their distinctive intervals, rather than standard rock scales. Daron Malakian's guitar riffs frequently mimic folk dance rhythms. Notice too the operatic and theatrical quality of the vocals, closer to cabaret and Frank Zappa than to typical metal.","Toxicity was a huge commercial and critical success and is often ranked among the best metal albums of its decade. Critics praised its unpredictability and political engagement. Some found its absurdist moments jarring; most now hear the album as one of the most distinctive heavy records of its era.","Start with Chop Suey! and listen for the shifts between frantic and mournful. Then hear Aerials and the title track. Compare its theatrical absurdity with Frank Zappa's We're Only in It for the Money, and its folk melodies with Bartók's Romanian Folk Dances."],"korn-korn":["Twelve songs of extremely low-tuned, rhythmic riffs on seven-string guitars, a slapping, clanking bass and hip-hop-influenced drums. Jonathan Davis's voice moves from whispers and nursery-rhyme chants to anguished screams. Blind opens with a cymbal tap and a slowly building riff, then explodes. Shoots and Ladders features Davis playing bagpipes. The songs deal with personal pain and trauma, expressed with a raw directness unusual in metal at the time.","Korn formed in Bakersfield, California, and recorded this debut with producer Ross Robinson, released in 1994. Grunge was dominant, and metal seemed out of fashion. Korn's mix of down-tuned heaviness, hip-hop groove and confessional lyrics created a new style, nu metal, that would dominate rock radio within a few years. The band's look and sound influenced a generation of bands.","Listen closer to the bass: Fieldy tunes it very low and slaps the strings, producing a clanking, percussive sound more like a drum than a bass line. The guitars often play dissonant, spidery figures high on the neck between the heavy riffs. The drummer David Silveria uses hip-hop grooves rather than metal beats, a crucial ingredient in the nu-metal sound.","Korn sold slowly at first but became hugely influential, founding nu metal. Critics were divided, some praising its rawness, others dismissing it. Its reputation has risen as nu metal has been reappraised, and it is now seen as one of the most important heavy debuts of the 1990s.","Start with Blind and listen to how the riff builds from a whisper. Then follow the bass on a second listen. Compare it with Black Sabbath for the low-tuned heaviness and Public Enemy for its groove, then hear Deftones' Around the Fur for a moodier take on the same roots."],"ghost-meliora":["Ten songs of melodic, polished heavy rock with grand, churchly organ, harmonised vocals and catchy choruses. The singer's voice is clean and melodic rather than growled. Spirit opens with an eerie keyboard introduction before heavy guitars enter; Cirice builds from a slow introduction into a churning, anthemic chorus; He Is is an almost hymnlike ballad. The lyrics explore mock-satanic themes as theatrical camp, and the sound recalls 1970s and 1980s hard rock and pop.","Ghost, from Linköping in Sweden, was conceived by Tobias Forge, who performed in costume as a sinister cleric while the other musicians remained anonymous behind masks. Meliora, their third album, produced by Klas Åhlund and released in 2015, was a breakthrough. Cirice won the 2016 Grammy for Best Metal Performance. The band's theatrical style and pop melodies won fans far beyond metal.","Listen closer to the harmonies: Ghost borrows from 1970s pop and Blue Öyster Cult, ABBA and church music, not just metal. Organ and keyboard textures echo Pink Floyd and progressive rock. The production, with its bright, clear guitars and layered vocals, is deliberately polished, the opposite of black metal's raw sound, which makes the satanic theatre an affectionate parody.","Meliora was widely praised and is often called Ghost's best album, bringing the band mainstream success. Critics debated whether the theatrical approach was sincere or parody; most now accept it as both. Some metal purists found the sound too poppy, but its catchiness is now seen as its strength.","Start with Cirice and listen for the slow introduction giving way to a huge chorus. Then hear He Is, almost a hymn. Compare it with Black Sabbath's debut for the occult theatre and with the polished pop of ABBA for its melodic craft."],"sunn-o-monoliths-and-dimensions":["Four long pieces built on massively amplified, slowly sustained guitar chords, the sound felt as much as heard. On this album the drones are joined by brass, strings, a women's choir and harp. Aghartha opens with spoken vocals floating over shuddering low tones; Big Church sets the choir against the roar; Alice, the closing piece, turns gentle, with trombone and harp in a tribute to the jazz harpist Alice Coltrane.","Sunn O))) is the duo of Stephen O'Malley and Greg Anderson, who formed the group in Seattle in 1998 as a tribute to the drone band Earth. Monoliths & Dimensions, released by Southern Lord in 2009 and produced by Randall Dunn, marked their most orchestral work, with arrangements by the violist Eyvind Kang and the vocalist Attila Csihar. It drew praise far beyond the metal audience.","Listen closer to the overtones: the sustained guitar chords at extreme volume produce harmonics that seem to float above the fundamental, especially audible on good speakers. The orchestral parts are carefully written to blend with these overtones. The influence of La Monte Young, Giacinto Scelsi and Alice Coltrane is direct, and the final track's dedication makes the latter explicit.","Monoliths & Dimensions was greeted as Sunn O)))'s finest work and has been called one of the most ambitious metal records ever made. Critics praised its fusion of drone metal with contemporary classical and jazz. Some metal fans found it pretentious; others saw it as proof of the genre's limitless possibilities.","Play it loud, ideally on speakers rather than headphones, and let the drones fill the room. Start with Big Church for the choir. Then hear Alice. Compare it with La Monte Young's Well-Tuned Piano, Scelsi's Quattro pezzi and Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda."],"neurosis-through-silver-in-blood":["Nine long, slow tracks of crushing, repetitive riffs, tribal drumming and dense layers of samples and keyboards. The vocals are hoarse, anguished shouts. The title track opens with pounding drums and a slowly building riff that grinds on for over twelve minutes. Eye and Locust Star are darker, with sudden quiet passages and abrupt explosions. The album's sound is apocalyptic and ritual, the opposite of conventional metal song form.","Neurosis formed in Oakland as a hardcore-punk band in the mid-1980s, and gradually slowed and expanded their music. Through Silver in Blood, released by Relapse in 1996, was their most extreme statement of this new direction. It drew on industrial music, tribal percussion and Pink Floyd, and was widely regarded as defining a new style later called post-metal.","Listen closer to the drums, which often play pounding, tribal patterns on toms rather than standard rock beats, giving the music a ritual, ceremonial feel. The samples and keyboards fill the background with drones and noises, adding to the apocalyptic atmosphere. Song structures follow slow builds and sudden releases rather than verse and chorus, a model later post-rock bands also used.","Through Silver in Blood was considered difficult on release but has become one of the most influential heavy albums of the 1990s. It is credited with founding post-metal, the style later developed by Isis, Cult of Luna and Mastodon. Critics praise its intensity; some find it exhausting.","Start with the title track, giving it time to build. Listen to the drums and the layers of sound. Compare it with Swans' Children of God for its ritual heaviness, and Godspeed You! Black Emperor's F♯ A♯ ∞ for its slow builds."],"neurosis-times-of-grace":["Eleven tracks that alternate crushing, slow riffs with quieter, more atmospheric passages, including acoustic guitars, keyboard drones and samples. The vocals move from hoarse shouts to more melodic singing. The Last You'll Know opens the album with a pounding build; Under the Surface turns gentle; End of the Harvest closes with a massive, slow riff. The overall sound is more spacious and warmer than the band's earlier work.","Times of Grace was recorded with Steve Albini, known for his work with Nirvana and the Pixies, and released by Relapse in 1999. It was designed to be played simultaneously with Grace, an ambient album by the band's alter ego, Tribes of Neurot. The idea of two albums that combine into one recalled the experiments of avant-garde composers and of the Flaming Lips' Zaireeka.","Listen closer to the production: Albini's natural, uncompressed recording style gives the drums and guitars a raw, roomy sound, very different from the dense layering of the previous album. If you play the album alongside Grace by Tribes of Neurot, you hear ambient drones and samples filling in the spaces, creating a third, composite work.","Times of Grace was widely praised and is often considered the band's most accessible work. Critics admired its balance of heaviness and atmosphere. The simultaneous playback idea attracted attention, though few listeners have actually tried it, and it remains an intriguing curiosity of the late-1990s heavy underground.","Start with The Last You'll Know and End of the Harvest, then try playing the whole album alongside Grace by Tribes of Neurot. Compare it with the band's previous Through Silver in Blood, and with Swans' Soundtracks for the Blind for its mix of noise and atmosphere."],"converge-jane-doe":["Twelve tracks of frenzied, chaotic hardcore and metal: jagged guitar riffs, sudden tempo changes, pounding drums and Jacob Bannon's raw, screamed vocals. Concubine opens the album at breakneck speed and lasts barely a minute and a half. The songs lurch between frantic speed and slow, grinding passages. The title track closes the album at over eleven minutes, building slowly to a massive, emotional climax. The record is about the end of a relationship.","Converge formed in Salem, Massachusetts, in 1990, and blended hardcore punk with metal. Jane Doe, released by Equal Vision in 2001, was their breakthrough. Guitarist Kurt Ballou, who would become a leading producer, recorded much of it. Bannon designed the striking cover art. It arrived as metalcore was emerging as a commercial genre, but Converge's chaotic style was far more extreme and inventive.","Listen closer to the guitar: Kurt Ballou plays dissonant, angular riffs that switch abruptly between ideas, often with unusual chords. The drums of Ben Koller constantly change pattern, matching the guitar's restlessness. The title track's slow build, with layered guitars and repeated vocals, recalls post-rock and Neurosis, showing the band's range beyond hardcore speed.","Jane Doe was hailed as a landmark of extreme music and is frequently named one of the best metal or hardcore albums of all time. Critics praised its emotional intensity and inventiveness. It has influenced countless bands, from mathcore to post-hardcore, and established Converge as one of heavy music's most respected bands.","Start with Concubine for its frenzy, then the title track for its slow, emotional build. Compare it with Hüsker Dü's Zen Arcade for its hardcore roots, and with Neurosis's Through Silver in Blood for its slow, heavy climaxes, and notice how much space opens up in the final minutes."],"godflesh-streetcleaner":["Slow, grinding tracks built on a cold drum machine, a massively distorted bass and Justin Broadrick's screeching, abrasive guitar. Broadrick's voice is a hoarse roar, distant in the mix. Like Rats opens with a lurching, mechanical rhythm and a riff that grinds on relentlessly. The songs are short in structure but feel monumental, every element slowed and heavy. The sound is industrial, like machinery in an empty factory.","Godflesh formed in Birmingham, England, in 1988, after Broadrick left Napalm Death and played briefly in Head of David. With the bassist G. C. Green he created a new sound blending metal with industrial music. Streetcleaner, released by Earache in 1989, was their first full album. Birmingham was the birthplace of Black Sabbath and Napalm Death, and its industrial landscape shaped Godflesh's sound.","Listen closer to the drum machine: its cold, inhuman precision and slow tempos, against the band's raw guitars, create a tension at the heart of the sound. Broadrick's guitar often plays screeching high notes and feedback rather than riffs. The bass carries the heavy riffs, an inversion of normal metal roles, and shapes Godflesh's distinctive weight.","Streetcleaner was little noticed outside the underground at first but is now regarded as a founding document of industrial metal. It influenced bands from Neurosis to Korn and Fear Factory, and later Isis. Critics praise its bleak, uncompromising sound; some find it oppressive.","Start with Like Rats, listening to the drum machine and the bass. Compare it with Swans' Children of God for its slow heaviness, and with Napalm Death's Scum to hear the contrast between Broadrick's two bands. Then hear Neurosis's Through Silver in Blood, which drew on this."],"soda-stereo-signos":["Soda Stereo's third album is eight songs of cool, shadowed new wave: Gustavo Cerati's chorus-soaked guitar, Zeta Bosio's melodic bass lines, Charly Alberti's crisp, gated drums and banks of keyboards that hang behind the band like stage smoke. The pace is mostly mid-tempo and the mood nocturnal. 'Persiana americana' is the song everyone knows, built on a taut, clipped riff, while the title track and the closing 'Final caja negra' drift into longer, darker spaces.","Argentina had returned to democracy in December 1983, and the young Buenos Aires trio rode a wave of bands that wanted to sound like London rather than like the folk-rock of the dictatorship years. Signos came out on CBS in late 1986 and is often said to have been the first Argentine rock album issued on compact disc. The tour that followed carried the band across Latin America and made them the first Spanish-language rock act with a genuinely continental audience.","Listen to how little Cerati actually plays: the guitar often states a figure once and lets the delay unit finish the sentence. The production is built around space, with the bass carrying the melody in verses while the guitar comments. On 'Persiana americana' the hook is rhythmic rather than melodic, a stop-start pattern borrowed from post-punk dance records, and the keyboards double the bass so softly you notice them only when they drop out.","Some Argentine critics of the time heard Signos as an imported style, too close to The Cure and The Police to count as national rock. Within a few years that judgment had reversed: the album came to be seen as the record on which Soda Stereo found its own voice and on which Latin American rock stopped asking permission. It now regularly appears near the top of Argentine all-time lists.","Start with 'Persiana americana' at a decent volume and follow the bass rather than the voice. Then play the title track and notice how the band lets a single chord sit and shimmer. Compare it with The Police's Synchronicity to hear the borrowing, then with Canción Animal four years later to hear how far the band would move toward heavier guitar rock."],"soda-stereo-cancion-animal":["Ten songs that trade the keyboards and cold sheen of the 1980s for loud, warm, overdriven guitar. The opener '(En) el séptimo día' lurches in on a heavy riff; 'Un millón de años luz' and the title track swing between hazy psychedelia and hard rock; 'Té para tres' is a quiet acoustic interlude; and the album closes with 'De música ligera', whose descending riff became the most recognised guitar figure in Spanish-language rock. The sound is fuller, rawer and more physical than anything the trio had made before.","After the glossy Doble Vida, produced in New York by Carlos Alomar in 1988, the band deliberately turned back toward the guitar rock of the 1970s, both British and Argentine. Argentina was living through hyperinflation and the first years of Carlos Menem's presidency, and the record's mood of excess and abandon caught that disordered moment. The tour that followed culminated in a free concert on Buenos Aires's Avenida 9 de Julio in 1991, attended by a crowd usually estimated in the hundreds of thousands.","'Té para tres', barely two minutes long, is the emotional hinge of the album: it is usually understood as Cerati's response to his father's illness, and its near-silence makes the noise around it hit harder. Listen also for the layering of the guitars, often two or three parts panned apart, one clean and one distorted, so that the 'wall' is really an interlocking pattern rather than a single blur.","Canción Animal was an immediate commercial success and is now commonly ranked as the best Soda Stereo album and among the best in Latin American rock; Rolling Stone's Argentine edition placed it near the very top of its national list. Some fans of the band's sleek 1980s sound initially found it crude. Its reputation has only grown since Cerati's death in 2014, when its songs became communal mourning anthems.","Play it straight through: the sequencing, from the slam of the opener to the release of 'De música ligera', is part of the design. Notice how 'Té para tres' resets your ears before the final run. Then hear Signos to measure the change, and set it beside U2's Achtung Baby, from the following year, another band that roughened its surface at exactly the same moment."],"soda-stereo-de-musica-ligera":["Three and a half minutes built on one descending guitar riff, four chords stepping downward and circling back, played with a bright, slightly fuzzy tone that anyone in Latin America can identify from the first second. The song is about the afterglow of a passing romance and the lightness of letting it go. Over that riff the verses are brisk and the chorus opens wide; Alberti's drums push hard on the beat and the arrangement never adds more than it needs.","It closed Canción Animal in 1990 and was released as a single, quickly becoming the band's signature. When Soda Stereo split, it was the last song played at their farewell concert at River Plate stadium in Buenos Aires in September 1997, and Cerati's short spoken thanks at its end became a phrase repeated across the Spanish-speaking world. The band reunited for a stadium tour in 2007, and the song again closed the shows.","The riff is so dominant that the song's real cleverness goes unheard: the chorus melody moves against the riff rather than with it, rising while the guitar falls. The title is a joke at the band's own expense, since 'música ligera', light music, was the faintly dismissive Spanish term for pop. On the 1997 live version listen for the crowd singing the riff itself, wordlessly, as if it were a melody.","Few songs in Spanish-language rock have been so widely covered, quoted or chanted at football grounds. Critics who preferred Soda's more ambitious later records sometimes treat it as too simple, but its simplicity is the point, and polls of musicians and journalists in Argentina, Chile and Mexico regularly place it at or near the top of the best Latin rock songs ever recorded.","Listen first to the studio version and notice the way the riff and the vocal pull in opposite directions. Then find the 1997 farewell recording from River Plate and hear what tens of thousands of people do with that riff. Compare it with Nirvana's 'Smells Like Teen Spirit', a song from the following year that also hung everything on one unforgettable guitar figure."],"cafe-tacvba-re":["Twenty songs in an hour, each in a different Mexican or global style: norteño polka with accordion in 'La ingrata', son jarocho strumming, bolero, banda brass, ska, a thrash-metal outburst in 'El borrego', psychedelic pop, a sly trip-hop-ish groove. 'Las flores' is a bouncing folk tune; 'El aparato' a dreamy electric ballad. What holds it together is Rubén Albarrán's elastic voice, the band's sense of humour, and the feeling that a single imagination is flipping through the entire radio dial of Mexico City.","Café Tacvba formed in 1989 in Ciudad Satélite, a sprawling suburb north of Mexico City, taking their name from a historic downtown restaurant. Re, their second album, was produced by the Argentine Gustavo Santaolalla for Warner, at the moment the industry was building a 'rock en español' market across Latin America and the United States. Mexico in 1994 was a year of upheaval, with NAFTA, the Zapatista uprising and a currency crisis, and the album answers globalisation by embracing everything local at once.","The band had no regular drummer, and much of the rhythm is programmed or played on hand percussion, which is why the genre switches can be so abrupt: there is no kit sound to make the styles match. Albarrán is credited under one of the many pseudonyms he changes from album to album. Listen to how often a traditional instrument plays a part a rock guitar would normally take, and vice versa.","Re was received as a revelation in Mexico and has since been treated as the 'White Album' of Latin rock, a comparison critics make constantly, sometimes lazily but not unfairly. It routinely heads lists of the best Latin American rock albums. The band later stopped performing 'La ingrata' in concert because of its subject matter, an example of how the record is still being re-read.","Do not try to absorb it in one go. Start with 'Las flores' and 'El aparato' to hear the range, then go back and play the album in order, enjoying the collisions. Listen for the accordion, the jarana guitar and the drum machine sharing the same space. Afterwards try The Beatles' White Album and Os Mutantes' first record, the obvious ancestors."],"cafe-tacvba-eres":["A plain, tender love song: the singer tells someone they are everything to him. Musically it is the gentlest thing Café Tacvba ever released, a mid-tempo ballad carried by a warm keyboard figure, a softly pushing drum kit and a melody that rises in small steps and never strains. There is no irony and no genre game here, which is exactly what made it startling from a band known for collage. The chorus lands with an almost hymn-like simplicity.","'Eres' was written by keyboardist Emmanuel del Real for Cuatro Caminos, the 2003 album on which the band worked with outside producers including Dave Fridmann and, for the first time, recorded with real drummers rather than machines. The album won a Grammy for best Latin rock or alternative album, and 'Eres' became the band's most played song on radio, crossing from the alternative audience into weddings, karaoke bars and mainstream pop stations across Mexico and the US.","Listen to the drums: after a decade of programmed rhythm, the human kit is what gives the song its slight forward lean, a looseness the band had never had on record. The arrangement keeps adding small details in the second half, a counter-melody on keyboard, a background vocal, without ever raising the volume, so the song seems to grow warmer rather than louder.","Some longtime fans regarded 'Eres' as a sell-out, a band of tricksters going soft. That view did not last; the song is now recognised as one of the great Spanish-language love songs of its decade, and the fact that a band so devoted to irony could write something this direct is usually counted in its favour. It is among the most covered Mexican songs of the century.","Play it next to 'Las flores' or 'La ingrata' from Re to hear how far the band travelled in nine years. Listen for the moment the drums come fully in and the arrangement opens. It works best heard without distraction, and pairs naturally with Soda Stereo's quieter songs or with the Brazilian ballads of Legião Urbana."],"charly-garcia-clics-modernos":["Nine songs in which Argentina's most important songwriter swaps piano-led art rock for drum machines, slap bass, funky clipped guitar and synthesizers. 'Nos siguen pegando abajo' opens with a jittery dance beat; 'Dos cero uno (transas)' and 'No soy un extraño' are tight, sardonic pop; and at the close sits 'Los dinosaurios', a hushed piano ballad about people who vanish, which most listeners took to be about the disappeared of the military years. The contrast between dance floor and lament is the record's engine.","García recorded it in New York in 1983 at Electric Lady studios, working with engineer and co-producer Joe Blaney, and soaking up the sound of Prince and New York new wave. It was released in Argentina in November 1983, weeks before Raúl Alfonsín took office and the dictatorship formally ended. García had just left Serú Girán, the band that had defined Argentine rock under the junta, and the album announced both a new sound and a new era.","'Los dinosaurios' never names anyone or anything political; its force comes from the gap between the gentle melody and what everyone understood it to mean. Listen also to the backing vocals and handclaps across the record, many stacked by García himself, and to the way he uses the drum machine not as a substitute for a drummer but as a deliberately stiff, comic element.","Some fans of Serú Girán were bewildered by the synthesizers and dance rhythms, but the album was a large commercial success and is now regarded as one of the two or three greatest Argentine rock records; Rolling Stone's Argentine edition ranked it near the top of its national list. It is often credited with dragging Argentine rock into the 1980s in a single stroke.","Begin with 'Nos siguen pegando abajo' and let the beat carry you, then skip to 'Los dinosaurios' to feel the drop. After that, play it straight through. Compare it with Prince's early-1980s records and with Talking Heads to hear what García heard in New York, and with Sui Generis's Confesiones de invierno to hear how far he had come."],"charly-garcia-piano-bar":["Where Clics modernos was a studio construction, Piano Bar sounds like a band playing in a room: tight, fast, rocking songs led by García's piano and a lean guitar-bass-drums trio. 'Demoliendo hoteles' is a cheerful, chugging rocker about growing up under dictatorship; 'Promesas sobre el bidet' and 'Raros peinados nuevos' are witty, jumpy new wave; and 'Cerca de la revolución' builds from a slow, almost classical piano introduction into a big, swaying anthem.","Recorded in Buenos Aires in 1984 with the band that had toured Clics modernos, the trio of guitarist Pablo Guyot, bassist Alfredo Toth and drummer Willy Iturri, who would soon break off as GIT. It was García's first record made entirely in democratic Argentina, and the new freedom shows in its looseness and playfulness. García was by then the country's biggest rock star, and the album consolidated the commercial breakthrough of the previous year.","Listen for García's habit of slipping classical piano figures into rock songs; the opening of 'Cerca de la revolución' sounds like a nineteenth-century étude before the beat arrives. The album's speed is also a statement: many tracks were cut with minimal overdubbing, and small imperfections, a slightly rushed fill, a voice cracking upward, were left in deliberately.","Piano Bar was well received and is sometimes preferred to Clics modernos by fans who like García as a bandleader rather than a studio tinkerer. Critics generally rank it just below its predecessor, and together the two are considered the peak of his 1980s work. Several of its songs, 'Demoliendo hoteles' above all, are standards of the Argentine repertoire.","Start with 'Demoliendo hoteles' for the energy, then 'Cerca de la revolución' for the range, and listen for the piano introduction turning into a rock song. Play it after Clics modernos and hear the machines give way to people. For a wider view, set it beside The Police's Synchronicity, the tight trio sound that Argentine bands of the moment were all chasing."],"spinetta-almendra":["The first album by Almendra, the band led by the teenage Luis Alberto Spinetta, is gentle, lyrical and strange: acoustic and electric guitars, a soft organ, Spinetta's high, supple voice and, on some songs, orchestral arrangements. 'Muchacha (ojos de papel)' is a delicate love song that became a national standard; 'Plegaria para un niño dormido' and 'Laura va' have the hush of lullabies; and 'Color humano' stretches into a long electric jam. The melodies wander in unexpected directions, closer to tango and jazz harmony than to the blues.","Almendra was a Buenos Aires quartet: Spinetta, guitarist Edelmiro Molinari, bassist Emilio del Guercio and drummer Rodolfo García. They recorded for RCA in 1969, when General Onganía's military government viewed long-haired rock musicians with suspicion. Alongside Los Gatos and Manal, they founded what Argentines call 'rock nacional': rock sung in Spanish, with lyrics as ambitious as poetry. Arranger Rodolfo Alchourrón added strings and winds to several tracks.","Spinetta painted the album's sleeve himself, and his visual art and his songwriting share the same dreamlike logic. Listen to the chord changes in 'Muchacha (ojos de papel)': where a rock song of 1969 would move predictably, this one slides into chords borrowed from bossa nova and jazz, a habit that would define Spinetta's style for forty years.","Almendra was admired immediately by musicians and has only grown in stature: it is regularly placed among the two or three best Argentine rock albums, and Spinetta is often called the poet of the movement. Some listeners find the orchestral passages dated, but the songs themselves are counted among the foundations of Spanish-language rock songwriting.","Start with 'Muchacha (ojos de papel)', then 'Plegaria para un niño dormido', listening for how the melody avoids obvious resolutions. Save the long 'Color humano' for later. Compare it with the Beatles' Rubber Soul and the Brazilian songs of Milton Nascimento, kindred spirits from the same years."],"spinetta-artaud":["Nine songs that move between bare acoustic guitar and hard, ragged electric rock. 'Todas las hojas son del viento' opens with a gentle, rocking guitar pattern and one of Spinetta's loveliest melodies; 'Cementerio club' swings; 'Bajan' and 'La sed verdadera' are tender and searching; 'Superchería' and 'Las habladurías del mundo' are tough guitar workouts; 'Milonga de la anunciación' nods to Argentine folk dance. The sound is intimate and slightly rough, like a demo made by someone who knows exactly what he wants.","Released in 1973 under the name of Spinetta's band Pescado Rabioso, it is essentially a solo album, recorded with a few trusted collaborators. Spinetta conceived it as a response to the French writer Antonin Artaud, whose writings on madness and suffering he had been reading, and handed out a manifesto at concerts. Argentina in 1973 was a turbulent country awaiting Perón's return, and the record's private, inward voice stood apart from the political songs of the day.","The original sleeve was cut in an irregular, non-rectangular shape in green and yellow, which made it awkward for record shops to stock and file; it was Spinetta's insistence. Listen to how the songs are sequenced as a rising and falling arc of calm and agitation, and to the way Spinetta's guitar tunings make simple shapes ring with unexpected open strings.","Artaud sold modestly at first but its reputation grew steadily; in 2007 Rolling Stone's Argentine edition named it the greatest Argentine rock album, a verdict many critics share. Some argue it is so personal that it remains hard to love on first hearing, and its place at the top of the canon owes something to Spinetta's stature as the movement's poet-saint.","Start with 'Todas las hojas son del viento' and 'Bajan', the album's gentlest entrances, then play it in order to feel the swings between quiet and storm. Listen for the acoustic guitar's detail. Pair it with Nick Drake's Pink Moon and Neil Young's After the Gold Rush, records with the same mixture of fragility and toughness."],"sui-generis-confesiones-de-invierno":["The second album by the duo of Charly García and Nito Mestre is soft folk-rock built around García's piano and acoustic guitar and Mestre's clear tenor voice and flute, with bass, drums and occasional electric guitar. 'Rasguña las piedras' is a slow, aching piano song; 'Cuando ya me empiece a quedar solo' and the title track are wistful ballads; 'Bienvenidos al tren' and 'Lunes otra vez' are lighter and wryer. The harmonies are close, the arrangements uncluttered, the mood melancholy and young.","Sui Generis came out of the Buenos Aires school scene; García and Mestre had met as teenagers. Their 1972 debut Vida had made them popular with students, and Confesiones de invierno, produced by Jorge Álvarez for the independent Talent label in 1973, made them stars. The country was in political ferment, with the military government handing back power and Perón returning, and the record's tales of loneliness, family and school spoke to an audience of adolescents.","Listen for García's piano: at twenty-one he was already writing introductions and bridges with a classically trained hand, and several songs turn on unexpected key changes. 'Rasguña las piedras' grows from almost nothing to a full band, and is often read, perhaps over-read, as an image of being trapped. The flute is Mestre's, played with a folk player's breathiness.","It was a major commercial success and remains one of the best-loved records in Argentine rock. Critics sometimes found Sui Generis soft compared with Almendra or Pescado Rabioso, but the songs proved durable, and 'Rasguña las piedras' and 'Cuando ya me empiece a quedar solo' are staples learned by every Argentine guitarist. It is the record where García's songwriting first fully appears.","Begin with 'Rasguña las piedras' and listen for the slow build from piano to band. Then hear 'Bienvenidos al tren' for the lighter side. Compare the duo's harmonies with Simon and Garfunkel, an obvious model, and then play García's Clics modernos a decade later to hear the same writer reinventing himself completely."],"sui-generis-pequenas-anecdotas-sobre-las-instituciones":["Sui Generis's third album turns the folk duo into an electric band with synthesizers, a rhythm section and long, progressive-rock arrangements. It is loosely a concept record about society's institutions: family, church, army, police and the media. 'Tango en segunda' blends tango and rock; 'El show de los muertos' is a creeping, theatrical piece; 'Instituciones' is grand and swirling; 'Para quién canto yo entonces' turns the questioning on the singer himself. The sound is fuller and stranger than before.","Recorded in 1974, as Argentina slid toward violence after Perón's death and censorship tightened, it was the band's most ambitious and most politically pointed statement. Under pressure from the record company and the authorities, songs were withdrawn and some lyrics altered before release. Charly García was by now reading progressive rock and Pink Floyd as eagerly as folk, and the expanded line-up gave him an orchestra of keyboards to work with.","'Las increíbles aventuras del Señor Tijeras' is about a censor, widely understood to be the government's film censor of the day, which makes its own fate on the album, trimmed and softened, a joke the band could not have planned. Listen for the moments when a synthesizer line imitates a military bugle or church organ, the instruments themselves standing in for the institutions.","Fans and critics were divided: some missed the intimacy of the acoustic duo, others hailed the band's growth. Over time it has come to be seen as one of the bravest Argentine records of the period, and the songs cut from it, later recovered in live performance, became legends. The duo split the following year with a farewell concert at Luna Park.","Start with 'Instituciones' for the scale and 'Tango en segunda' for the national flavour. Then listen to the whole album as a suite. Compare it with Pink Floyd's Dark Side of the Moon, from the year before, and with Chico Buarque's coded protest songs in Brazil, written under a similar dictatorship."],"legiao-urbana-legiao-urbana":["Eleven songs of brisk, jangling post-punk: chiming electric guitar from Dado Villa-Lobos, a driving bass and Marcelo Bonfá's crisp drums, with Renato Russo's deep, declamatory baritone on top. 'Será' opens with a ringing riff and a chorus that asks restless questions; 'Geração Coca-Cola' is a fast punk shout about a generation raised on American imports; 'Ainda é cedo' is moodier and slower, with a bass line straight from Manchester; 'Por enquanto' closes on a quiet keyboard ballad.","The band came out of the punk scene of Brasília, the modernist capital, where the children of diplomats and civil servants traded imported records. Russo had led the punk band Aborto Elétrico before forming Legião Urbana. Their EMI debut appeared in early 1985, the same months as the first Rock in Rio festival and the end of two decades of military rule, and it became one of the voices of Brazilian youth in the return to democracy.","Listen to the bass and guitar on 'Ainda é cedo': the band openly modelled their sound on Joy Division, The Smiths and The Cure, but Russo sang in a register and with a seriousness closer to Brazilian song tradition, and that tension is the band's signature. 'Geração Coca-Cola' was written years earlier for Aborto Elétrico, which is why it sounds so much rawer than the songs around it.","The album sold steadily and established Legião Urbana alongside Os Paralamas do Sucesso and Titãs as the leaders of the 1980s Brazilian rock boom. Critics initially heard it as derivative of British post-punk; now it is regarded as one of the essential Brazilian rock albums, and Russo as the most important lyricist of his generation.","Start with 'Será' and listen for the way the guitar rings against the bass. Then 'Ainda é cedo', and then 'Geração Coca-Cola' for the punk side. Compare it with The Smiths' first album and Joy Division's Unknown Pleasures, and then with Dois, the following year, where the band turned acoustic and gentle."],"legiao-urbana-dois":["Legião Urbana's second album softens the punk edge into folk-rock and ballads: acoustic guitars, chiming electric arpeggios and Russo's voice now intimate rather than declamatory. 'Eduardo e Mônica' tells the story of an unlikely couple over a jaunty strum; 'Tempo perdido' builds from a gentle guitar figure to a huge, communal chorus; 'Índios' is a slow meditation on Brazil's history; 'Quase sem querer' is aching and tender. The pace is unhurried and the album flows like a single long mood.","Recorded in Rio de Janeiro for EMI in 1986, a year after the debut, it was probably first conceived as a double album, which the label declined. Brazil's new democracy was struggling with inflation and disillusion, and Russo's lyrics shifted from slogans to stories and private feeling. Dois became the band's commercial breakthrough, selling over a million copies, and made Legião Urbana one of the biggest bands in Latin America.","'Eduardo e Mônica' is a full short story in song, with characters, places and years, and listeners in Brasília can identify the real streets it mentions. Listen also for how the band record acoustic and electric guitars together, the electric often playing a quiet arpeggio inside the strummed acoustic, a texture borrowed from The Smiths' Johnny Marr and The Byrds.","Dois is widely considered Legião Urbana's finest album and one of the most important Brazilian rock records. Some early fans missed the punk anger, but Russo's growth as a lyricist won the argument. After Russo's death in 1996, songs such as 'Tempo perdido' became generational anthems, sung at gatherings and demonstrations across Brazil.","Begin with 'Tempo perdido', listening for the moment the chorus opens out, then 'Eduardo e Mônica' with a translation to follow the story. Play the album in order on a quiet evening. Compare it with R.E.M.'s Murmur and The Smiths' The Queen Is Dead, and with Brazilian MPB such as Milton Nascimento for the melodic roots."],"los-prisioneros-la-voz-de-los-80":["Ten fast, scrappy songs by a trio from a working-class suburb of Santiago: punk and new wave with a ska bounce, chopped guitar, thudding drums and Jorge González's sharp, sardonic voice. The title track is an upbeat manifesto announcing a new generation; 'Brigada de negro' and 'Paramar' are taut and danceable; and 'Latinoamérica es un pueblo al sur de Estados Unidos' mocks Latin American dependence on the north. The recording is thin and cheap, which only adds to the urgency.","Los Prisioneros were Jorge González, Claudio Narea and Miguel Tapia, schoolfriends from San Miguel in Santiago. Chile was under Augusto Pinochet's dictatorship, and the album appeared in 1984 on a tiny independent label, distributed largely on cassette. The band would soon be shut out of radio and television, yet the songs spread by tape and word of mouth until they became the most popular band in the country.","The band were deliberately unsentimental about Chilean folk-protest traditions and about rock's imported glamour alike; listen for how González's lyrics mock both. The guitar parts are almost all short choppy strums on the offbeat, a direct lift from The Clash and The Specials, and the cheapness of the recording turns that ska rhythm into something rough and homemade.","Initially ignored by the establishment, the album came to be regarded as the founding record of Chilean rock in the 1980s, and Rolling Stone's Latin editions have placed it high among the best Latin American albums. González's songwriting is now taught and studied in Chile, and the title song is used to mark the era itself.","Start with the title track and 'Latinoamérica es un pueblo al sur de Estados Unidos', with translations to hand, then play the rest. Listen for the offbeat guitar and the clipped, conversational singing. Compare it with The Clash's first album and with Charly García's Clics modernos, its Argentine contemporary."],"los-prisioneros-el-baile-de-los-que-sobran":["A slow, steady, almost marching song about young people who leave school with no prospects and nowhere to go. Over a plain drum pattern and a repeating keyboard figure, González sings in a level, unshowy voice; the guitar strums quietly underneath, and the chorus, an invitation to dance, arrives without any lift in volume, which makes it sound more bitter than joyful. It runs about five minutes and never changes its pace.","It appeared on Pateando piedras in 1986, the album on which Los Prisioneros signed to EMI and turned toward synthesizers. Chile was still under Pinochet, and the band were effectively barred from mainstream radio and television, yet the song spread across the country. It was written about the gap between the children of the rich and the children of the poor in Chile's school system, a subject that would never leave public debate.","The title's dance is a bleak irony: the song imagines the excluded dancing because there is nothing else for them to do. Listen to how the melody barely moves, hovering around a few notes, which gives the song its weary, endless feeling. Decades later, crowds in Santiago turned its chorus into a protest chant without needing to change a word.","At first a hit with young listeners rather than critics, it has become one of the most important songs in Chilean history. It was sung in mass protests during the 2011 student movement and again during the 2019 uprising, when it became an unofficial anthem. Polls regularly name it the greatest Chilean song, and González's writing is now widely studied.","Listen once for the sound, the plain beat and repeating keyboard, and once with a translation to follow the story. Then find footage of crowds singing it in Santiago in 2019. Compare it with The Clash's political songs, and with Víctor Jara's songs from the previous generation, whose anger it shares but whose folk idiom it rejects."],"kino-gruppa-krovi":["Kino's sixth album is the definitive sound of Soviet post-punk: Yuri Kasparyan's chiming, chorus-heavy guitar lines, a steady, stoic beat from Georgy Guryanov's drums and drum machine, and Viktor Tsoi's deep, flat baritone, singing like a man reporting rather than pleading. The title song is a slow, resolute march about a soldier's resolve, named for the blood group sewn on a uniform sleeve; 'Zakroy za mnoy dver', ya ukhozhu' is brisk and restless; 'Spokoynaya noch'' a hushed lullaby for a sleeping city.","Kino came out of the Leningrad Rock Club, the semi-official venue where Soviet rock was tolerated from 1981. Their earlier albums circulated as home-recorded tapes; by 1988, under glasnost and perestroika, they were filling stadiums, and Tsoi starred in the film Igla. The Soviet war in Afghanistan was ending, which gave the title song an immediate weight. Gruppa krovi spread first on cassette and was then released officially.","Listen to the guitar: Kasparyan's lines are often a melody in their own right, running against Tsoi's vocal, and they owe as much to The Cure and early U2 as to anything Russian. The drum machine is mixed with live drums, so the beat has a slightly mechanical steadiness. Tsoi's singing is deliberately monotone, almost spoken, so small rises in pitch feel enormous.","It was received as a masterwork in the USSR and became the band's biggest-selling album. After Tsoi's death in a car accident in 1990, the record took on a near-sacred status across the former Soviet Union, and the title song has been covered by countless bands. It has found new Western listeners in recent years, and is now often named the greatest Russian rock album.","Start with the title track and listen for the guitar's ringing melody against Tsoi's flat voice. Then 'Spokoynaya noch'' for the quiet side. Play it after Joy Division's Closer and The Cure's Disintegration to hear the kinship, and with Shostakovich's Eighth Quartet for another Leningrad artist writing about endurance under pressure."],"kino-peremen":["A song about the hunger for change. It begins quietly, with a ticking drum and a dry guitar figure, and builds steadily without ever really breaking into a gallop: the bass repeats, the guitar thickens, and Tsoi's voice rises from a low, steady narration to a shout on the title phrase. The whole song is a slow crescendo, a single long tightening that never quite releases, which is what makes it so effective sung by a crowd.","Written in 1986, the song reached a mass audience through the final scene of Sergei Solovyov's film Assa in 1987, where Tsoi performs it to a hall of fans holding lit lighters. The studio version appeared in 1989. It arrived as Gorbachev's reforms were opening Soviet public life, and was immediately heard as the anthem of perestroika, though Tsoi reportedly insisted it was not a political song.","Listen to how little the harmony changes: the song works almost entirely through repetition and gradual addition, more like Ravel's Boléro than a conventional rock song. Tsoi's statement that the song was personal, about changing oneself, has never stopped crowds hearing it as a political demand, and its power lies partly in that ambiguity.","It is one of the most famous Russian songs of the late Soviet era. In Belarus in 2020 two DJs played it at an official event in Minsk and were arrested, and it has been sung at protests in Russia, Ukraine, Kazakhstan and elsewhere. Some critics argue its meaning has been flattened by its use as a slogan; others see that use as its true life.","Hear it first as a crescendo: notice how each verse adds weight without speeding up. Then watch the final scene of Assa to see how it was first received. Compare it with Los Prisioneros' 'El baile de los que sobran', another song that crowds adopted, and Ravel's Boléro for the same slow-build principle."],"rammstein-sehnsucht":["Rammstein's second album fuses chugging, heavily distorted guitars, locked to a mechanical drum beat, with cold synthesizers and Till Lindemann's rolling, theatrical bass voice. 'Engel' has a whistled hook and a clean female vocal; 'Du hast' builds a vast, pounding riff around a German pun; 'Tier' and 'Spiel mit mir' are slower and heavier. The riffs are short and repetitive, the beat relentless, the keyboard sounds camp and cinematic. It feels less like a metal record than a giant machine.","Formed in Berlin in 1994 by musicians from East Germany's punk scene, Rammstein recorded Sehnsucht in Malta with producer Jacob Hellner. David Lynch had used two of their songs in Lost Highway (1997), introducing them to American audiences, and Sehnsucht became a major international hit despite being sung entirely in German. Its six alternative covers showed each member photographed by the artist Gottfried Helnwein, faces bound in metal instruments.","'Du hast' turns on a pun: the German for 'you have' and 'you hate' sound the same, and the song plays with the shape of a wedding vow. Listen to the guitars, often playing only on precise, machine-like beats with silence between; the influence of electronic dance music and of Kraftwerk is as strong as that of metal, and the riffs are quantised almost like samples.","Sehnsucht was a commercial breakthrough, selling strongly in the United States as well as across Europe. Critics were split between those who heard brilliant industrial theatre and those who found the imagery and martial rhythms disturbing, a debate about the band's ironies that has never settled. It is now regarded as the founding record of the 'Neue Deutsche Härte' style.","Start with 'Engel' and 'Du hast', listening for the way guitars and synths lock to the beat. Turn it up: the record is mixed for weight. Compare it with Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express for the machine-precision, with Metallica's Black Album for the heaviness, and with Wagner for the scale of the theatrical ambition."],"rammstein-mutter":["Rammstein's third album is grander and more melodic: orchestral strings, choir-like backing vocals and enormous, slowly pounding riffs. 'Mein Herz brennt' opens with a hushed, sinister spoken introduction before the guitars explode; 'Sonne' is built on a huge stamping beat and a countdown; 'Links 2-3-4' is a march; 'Ich will' a call-and-response stadium chant; the title track a slow, aching ballad. The production is clean and massive, with every instrument placed precisely.","Recorded in southern France in 2000 with Jacob Hellner, Mutter came after the band had become a global stadium act. They had faced accusations of flirting with fascist imagery, especially after using footage from Leni Riefenstahl's films in a video. 'Sonne' was originally written as entrance music for the boxer Vitali Klitschko. The album sold millions across Europe and established Rammstein as the most successful German-language band in the world.","'Links 2-3-4' is the band's answer to their critics: its title means 'left, two, three, four', a march count in which they declare their heart beats on the left, setting a political statement to a parade-ground rhythm. Listen for the strings in 'Mutter' and 'Mein Herz brennt', which echo film score and late Romantic orchestration more than metal. 'Mein Herz brennt' probably parodies the introduction of a children's bedtime television programme.","Mutter was widely acclaimed and is often called Rammstein's best album. Critics who had dismissed them as crude shock-rockers began to take their musicianship and irony seriously. The debate about the band's provocations continues, but the album's place as one of the great European rock records of its decade is rarely contested.","Start with 'Mein Herz brennt' for the drama and 'Sonne' for the power, then the title track for the softer side. Listen for the strings. Compare it with Wagner's Götterdämmerung for scale, with Mahler for the use of march rhythms, and with Sehnsucht to hear the band moving from machine toward orchestra."],"mano-negra-putas-fever":["A whirlwind of short, fast songs in French, Spanish, English and Arabic, mixing punk, rockabilly, ska, flamenco, salsa, rai and street-band brass. Songs crash into each other, often lasting barely two minutes; there are shouted chants, radio samples, sirens, a horn section, hand claps and flamenco guitar. 'King Kong Five' is a rapid punk-rap, 'Sidi h'bibi' a frenetic version of an Algerian song, 'Pas assez de toi' a breakneck rock song. The energy is relentless.","Mano Negra formed in Paris in 1987 around Manu Chao, his brother Antoine and cousin Santiago Casariego, children of Spanish exiles. Puta's Fever, their second album, came out on Virgin in 1989, as the French alternative-rock scene flourished and Paris's immigrant communities were creating a new multilingual popular culture. The band toured relentlessly, later sailing a cargo ship around Latin America in 1992 and touring Colombia by train.","Listen for the cut-up radio broadcasts and found sounds between and inside the songs; Manu Chao would turn this technique into the defining trait of his solo work. The album's languages are not decoration: each song chooses the language and style of a different Paris community, so the record is a map of the city's streets.","Puta's Fever was a hit in France and Spain and made Mano Negra stars across Europe and Latin America, though they never broke in the English-speaking world. Critics praised the energy and eclecticism, and the band is now credited as a founder of the 'mestizo' style. Some found it chaotic; that was the point.","Do not expect a polished record: listen for the energy and the collisions. Start with 'King Kong Five' and 'Sidi h'bibi', then play it through. Compare it with The Clash's Sandinista!, an obvious model, with Café Tacvba's Re, and with Manu Chao's Clandestino to hear where the radio-collage approach went next."],"manu-chao-clandestino":["Sixteen short, gentle songs in Spanish, French, English and Portuguese, built on loping acoustic guitar, soft reggae and Latin rhythms, hand percussion, and snippets of radio broadcasts, street noise and voices. The title track, about migrants without papers, sets a quiet, lulling melody over a strummed guitar; 'Desaparecido' and 'Bongo Bong' are bouncy and playful; 'Je ne t'aime plus' is tender. The songs flow into one another as if the album were a single radio broadcast picked up while travelling.","After Mano Negra broke up in the mid-1990s, Manu Chao travelled across Latin America and Africa, recording on a portable studio as he went. Clandestino, released by Virgin in 1998, was assembled from these recordings. It sold slowly at first, then by word of mouth, eventually reaching several million copies, and it became the soundtrack of the anti-globalisation movement that gathered in the late 1990s.","Several songs share the same backing track: 'Bongo Bong' and 'Je ne t'aime plus' are built on identical music with different words, and the album repeatedly recycles riffs and samples, so it feels like a single piece of music turning in circles. Listen for the radio voices from different countries, which act as the record's connective tissue.","Clandestino was initially overlooked but became one of the most successful French albums of all time, and one of the defining world-music records of its era. Some critics have found its gentle repetition monotonous, but its influence on global pop is undeniable, and it is now regarded as a classic of multilingual popular music.","Play it from start to finish, ideally on a long journey, and notice how the songs blend into one another. Listen for the recurring riffs and the radio voices. Compare it with Mano Negra's Puta's Fever to hear the shift from noise to quiet, and with Bob Marley's Exodus and Lee Perry's dub records for the musical roots."],"noir-desir-tostaky":["Noir Désir's fourth album is loud, abrasive and urgent: Serge Teyssot-Gay's jagged, noisy guitar, a heavy, driving rhythm section and Bertrand Cantat's hoarse, theatrical voice, singing in French and English. 'Tostaky (le continent)' is a vast, churning piece with a Latin American flavour; 'Ici Paris' and 'Oublié' are tense and propulsive; 'Sober Song' lurches. The songs are long, often building from tense quiet into storms of feedback.","Formed in Bordeaux in 1980, Noir Désir had become one of the leading bands of French rock by 1992. Tostaky, recorded with American producer Ted Niceley, marked a shift toward a harder, noisier sound influenced by American alternative bands such as Fugazi and the Pixies. It came out as French rock was claiming new confidence, and it established Noir Désir as the most important French rock band of the 1990s.","Listen to Teyssot-Gay's guitar, which rarely plays chords in the conventional way; instead it scrapes, sustains and squeals, often more like a noise generator than a rhythm instrument. The band's commitment to French as a rock language is also unusual: Cantat sings in a deliberately literary, poetic register that sits against the noise like a voice from a different century.","Tostaky was a commercial and critical success, widely regarded as one of the best French rock albums. Noir Désir's story ended in 2003 when Cantat was convicted over the death of the actress Marie Trintignant, and the band formally split in 2010. The album's reputation has been complicated by those events, and many listeners now approach it with ambivalence.","Start with 'Tostaky (le continent)' and 'Ici Paris', listening for the guitar noise. Then play the whole album at volume. Compare it with Fugazi, the Pixies' Surfer Rosa and Sonic Youth's Daydream Nation for the American roots, and with Jacques Brel for the French tradition of intense, literary song."],"indochine-l-aventurier":["A bouncy, irresistible new-wave single: a crisp, mechanical beat, a staccato guitar riff, saxophone stabs and Nicola Sirkis's high, androgynous voice. The song tells a breathless adventure story in comic-strip style, and the melody bounces in short, punchy phrases. The chorus is pure pop and the arrangement lean, the synthesizer adding only colour. It runs under four minutes and never slows.","Indochine formed in Paris in 1981, inspired by British new wave bands such as The Cure, Duran Duran and Joy Division. 'L'Aventurier' was the title song of their first record, released in 1982, and became a huge hit in France in 1983, establishing Indochine as the leading French new wave band. The French music industry of the time was dominated by variety and chanson, and the band's success opened a door for French-language rock.","The lyrics are a collage of characters and situations from the Bob Morane adventure novels by the Belgian writer Henri Vernes, a series beloved by French and Belgian teenagers; the song is essentially fan fiction set to a beat. Listen for the saxophone, played by Dimitri Bodiansky, which gives the song its slightly retro, almost ska-like character.","Critics initially dismissed Indochine as teen pop, but 'L'Aventurier' became one of the best-known French songs of the 1980s, and the band have enjoyed a remarkable longevity, filling stadiums in the 2000s. The song is now regarded as a classic of French pop, and its influence on later French rock is widely acknowledged.","Listen for the bounce of the guitar and saxophone against the mechanical beat, and enjoy the pop rush. Compare it with The Cure's 'The Lovecats' and Duran Duran for the British models, and with Serge Gainsbourg's 'Initials B.B.' for a very different French take on pop story-telling."],"x-japan-blue-blood":["X's major-label debut lurches between breakneck speed metal and sweeping piano ballads. The title track and 'X' are relentless: double-bass drums at a gallop, twin guitars in harmony, Toshi's soaring, high voice. 'Endless Rain' is a slow, string-laden ballad led by Yoshiki's piano; 'Kurenai' begins quietly before exploding into speed; and 'Rose of Pain' is a twelve-minute epic with an orchestral introduction. The album is long, extravagant and dramatic, in sound as in presentation.","X formed in Chiba in 1982 around drummer and pianist Yoshiki and singer Toshi, joined by guitarists hide and Pata and bassist Taiji. They had built a huge following through independent releases and theatrical shows, with towering hair, make-up and outrageous costumes. Blue Blood, released by CBS/Sony in 1989, made them stars and helped found the 'visual kei' movement. They later became X Japan to avoid confusion with the American band X.","Listen for Yoshiki's classical training: the piano introductions and string arrangements draw on Romantic piano music, and 'Rose of Pain' opens with a passage that sounds like a film score. The contrast between the delicate ballads and the speed metal is not a compromise but a design, the band's motto promising visual and sonic shock in equal measure.","Blue Blood was a commercial breakthrough, selling hundreds of thousands of copies, and is regarded as a landmark of Japanese rock. Western metal critics were slow to notice, but the band's influence on Japanese music and fashion is enormous. Some critics find the album uneven, with weak production, but its ambition and energy are widely celebrated.","Start with 'Endless Rain' for the melodic side and the title track for the speed, then 'Rose of Pain' for the scale. Listen for the classical piano in the introductions. Compare it with Iron Maiden for the galloping metal and with Rachmaninoff or Chopin for Yoshiki's Romantic roots."],"x-japan-art-of-life":["A single twenty-nine-minute piece for rock band and orchestra, released on its own as an album. It moves through sections: a slow, orchestral opening, a melodic rock song, speed metal passages, long guitar solos, and at its heart a long, dissonant solo piano passage by Yoshiki that slides from Romantic harmony into chaos before the band and orchestra return for a vast finale. It is Yoshiki's attempt to tell the story of his inner life.","Written by Yoshiki over several years and released by Atlantic in 1993, after the band had signed a deal aimed at an international market, Art of Life was recorded with a full orchestra alongside the band. X Japan were by then the biggest rock band in Japan, filling the Tokyo Dome. Yoshiki has said that the piece reflects his struggles with despair, including his father's death when he was a child.","The long piano section is the part most listeners find puzzling: Yoshiki deliberately breaks down the Romantic piano writing into clusters and noise, a musical picture of mental collapse, before the orchestra rebuilds order. Listen to how the main melody returns at the end in a new key and with full orchestra, a classical device of thematic transformation.","Art of Life was a commercial success in Japan but divided critics: some praised its ambition, others found it overblown and the piano section indulgent. It remains one of the most ambitious works in Japanese rock and is a favourite of X Japan's devoted fans, who see it as the band's most personal statement.","Set aside half an hour and listen without interruption. Notice the main melody early on and listen for its return at the end. Brace yourself for the piano section. Compare it with Queen's 'Bohemian Rhapsody' for the rock-opera ambition, Deep Purple's Concerto for Group and Orchestra, and Rachmaninoff's Second Piano Concerto for the Romantic roots."],"cornelius-oyamada-fantasma":["A dazzling collage of pop, rock, noise, bossa nova, easy listening and electronic music, assembled from samples and played parts. 'Mic Check' bounces a voice around your head; 'Star Fruits Surf Rider' is a lush, slow electronic love song; 'Count Five or Six' bursts into heavy guitar; 'Chapter 8 – Seashore and Horizon' layers Beach Boys-like harmonies. Songs switch moods suddenly, with sounds leaping from speaker to speaker. The whole record feels like a playful, intensely detailed toy.","Keigo Oyamada, who records as Cornelius, had been one half of Flipper's Guitar, a founding band of the Tokyo 'Shibuya-kei' scene that mixed Western pop, lounge and indie. Fantasma, released in Japan in 1997 and by Matador in the US in 1998, was his breakthrough, praised by Western critics and musicians. It appeared as digital sampling and home studios were transforming music-making, and as Tokyo's record shops held one of the world's great stocks of vinyl.","Fantasma is designed for headphones; the stereo placement is extreme, with sounds moving across and around the head, and the Japanese edition was probably sold with earphones. The single 'Star Fruits Surf Rider' was issued on two CDs meant to be played simultaneously on two players. Listen for samples of sport, nature and children's records hidden in the mix.","Fantasma was widely acclaimed in the West, with critics comparing Cornelius to Beck and Brian Wilson. Some found it too clever, more a collection of tricks than songs, but it is now regarded as one of the great albums of the 1990s and the defining record of Shibuya-kei. Oyamada's later career was marked by controversy over old magazine interviews, which led to his resignation from the Tokyo Olympics ceremony team in 2021.","Use headphones. Start with 'Mic Check' and 'Star Fruits Surf Rider', and listen for the movement of sounds across the stereo field. Then play it through. Compare it with the Beach Boys' Pet Sounds, Stereolab's Emperor Tomato Ketchup and Yellow Magic Orchestra's Solid State Survivor."],"cornelius-oyamada-point":["A minimalist, airy record built from tiny sounds placed precisely in space: a guitar note, a drop of water, a bird call, a single drum hit, a breath. 'Drop' uses the sounds of water; 'Bird Watching at Inner Forest' sets bird song against guitar; 'Smoke' and 'Point of View Point' are spare, rhythmic and playful; and 'Brazil' is a gentle, dreamy cover of the famous Ary Barroso song. The songs are short and the textures clean.","Released in 2001, four years after Fantasma, Point was a deliberate move away from the maximalism of that record. Cornelius had by then become an international figure, touring with his own band and visual shows. He later worked with members of Yellow Magic Orchestra and with Yoko Ono's Plastic Ono Band. The album appeared as minimal electronic music and digital production were reshaping the possibilities of the studio.","Each sound on Point has a precise place in the stereo field, and many songs are built on a principle of call and response between left and right speakers. Listen for how silence is used as an instrument; whole bars are empty except for one sound. The live shows synchronised video to every note, so that watching and listening became a single experience.","Point was widely praised and is now regarded by many critics as Cornelius's finest album, more original and influential than Fantasma. Some listeners missed the exuberance of the earlier record. Its influence on electronic pop and sound design is considerable.","Use headphones and a quiet room. Start with 'Drop' and 'Bird Watching at Inner Forest', listening for how individual sounds are placed. Compare it with Steve Reich's Music for Pieces of Wood and Brian Eno's ambient records for the minimalism, and with Fantasma for the contrast."],"fishmans-long-season":["A single thirty-five-minute piece in several sections, built around a gently looping piano figure and a slow, dubby rhythm. Shinji Sato's high, fragile voice floats above; long instrumental passages drift through echo, noise, field recordings and strings; a hypnotic drum solo forms the centre; and the opening melody returns at the end like a memory. The music is warm, hazy and unhurried, more like a dream of a summer afternoon than a rock song.","Fishmans formed in Tokyo in 1987, starting as a reggae-influenced pop band before moving toward dub, ambient and psychedelic rock. By the mid-1990s they had their own private studio in Tokyo, which gave them time to experiment. Long Season, released by Polydor in 1996, grew out of a shorter single, 'Season'. It sold modestly but is now regarded as their masterwork.","Listen to the drumming of Kin-ichi Motegi, which sustains the piece through subtle variations of a single pattern; the drum solo at the centre is not a display but a meditation. The use of dub techniques, long echoes and the sudden removal of instruments, comes from Jamaican reggae, and the band's engineer treated the mixing desk as an instrument.","Long Season was a cult record in Japan for years, but since the 2010s it has found a huge international audience online, and is now often ranked among the greatest Japanese albums. Critics praise its originality, though some find it too long and unfocused. Sato's death in 1999 at the age of thirty-three has added a poignant aura.","Set aside thirty-five minutes and listen without distraction, ideally on headphones. Notice the piano figure at the start and wait for its return. Compare it with King Tubby's dub records, Can's Future Days and Talk Talk's late albums for the long-form, drifting approach."],"fishmans-98-12-28-otokotachi-no-wakare":["A two-disc live recording of a long concert, capturing the band at their most expansive. The songs are stretched, slowed and deepened: dub echoes, extended guitar and keyboard passages, and Sato's fragile voice. A long performance of 'Long Season' forms the centre, growing even longer and more hypnotic than the studio version, and 'Night Cruising' floats in a warm haze. The playing is relaxed, intense and intimate.","The concert was recorded at Akasaka Blitz in Tokyo on 28 December 1998, the last show with bassist Yuzuru Kashiwabara, who was leaving the band; the title, roughly 'The Farewell of Men', refers to his departure. Shinji Sato died suddenly in March 1999, and the recording, released later that year, became the band's unintended farewell.","Listen for how the band play with the audience's silence; the crowd is hushed for long stretches, and the space becomes part of the sound. The long version of 'Long Season' differs from the studio record in structure, with new sections and solos, so the two versions reward comparison.","The album was received as a moving document and is regarded by many fans as the greatest Japanese live album. Its reputation has grown with the band's international rediscovery. Some critics find its length demanding, but most consider it essential.","Listen to the long 'Long Season' first, then compare it with the studio version. Let the concert play from beginning to end on a quiet evening. Compare it with Talking Heads' Stop Making Sense or Bob Dylan's Live 1966 for other great live documents."],"shonen-knife-pretty-little-baka-guy":["Short, simple, joyful songs played on guitar, bass and drums by three women from Osaka. The guitar is buzzy and basic, the drums thump, the bass bounces, and the vocals are cheerful, sung in Japanese and English. 'Riding on the Rocket' and 'Ice Cream City' are sugar-rush pop songs with Ramones-like drive. The subjects are food, animals, toys and daydreams; the sound is naive in the best sense, like a garage band playing for its own delight.","Shonen Knife formed in Osaka in 1981: sisters Naoko and Atsuko Yamano and their friend Michie Nakatani. Their records, released on small Japanese labels, found their way to American college radio and indie bands. Pretty Little Baka Guy appeared in 1986. In 1989 a tribute album featuring Sonic Youth and many others was released, and in 1991 they opened for Nirvana on a UK tour.","The band's apparent simplicity hides a sharp sense of humour and a real gift for melody; listen for how the songs, though short and basic, are built on well-crafted hooks. Their love of the Ramones and the Beatles is obvious, but the lyrical world, a child's view of everyday pleasures, is entirely their own.","Shonen Knife were championed by American indie musicians, Kurt Cobain among them, who described his delight on seeing them live. Critics have sometimes treated them as a novelty, but their influence on indie pop and punk is widely acknowledged. They continue to tour and record.","Play it loud and do not look for depth; the pleasure is in the bounce. Start with 'Riding on the Rocket' and listen for how quickly the hook arrives. Compare it with the Ramones' Road to Ruin and the Beatles' early singles, then with the Nirvana records made by the band who admired them most."],"luther-vandross-never-too-much":["Seven songs, split between bright mid-tempo dance tracks and long, slow ballads. The title song bounces on a popping, melodic electric bass and crisp hand-claps, with Vandross gliding over the top as if the tempo cost him no effort. The album closes with a seven-minute reworking of A House Is Not a Home that slows a familiar song almost to a standstill, stretching single words across whole bars while the band breathes with him.","By 1981 Vandross had spent a decade as one of New York's most sought-after session and jingle singers, arranging backing vocals for David Bowie's Young Americans and singing lead for the dance group Change. Epic finally let him make a record on his own terms, producing himself. It arrived as disco's crash left labels nervous about dance music, and he answered with something sleeker and more grown-up.","That buoyant bass line on the title track belongs to the young Marcus Miller, later Miles Davis's bassist and producer, and it is effectively a second lead voice. On A House Is Not a Home, written by Burt Bacharach and Hal David for Dionne Warwick in 1964, listen for how Vandross delays the melody, entering late on almost every phrase so the song seems to be remembered rather than sung.","The album topped the R&B chart and the title song became his first number-one R&B single, though pop radio largely ignored him for years. Critics heard at once a singer of rare control and taste. Its reputation has only grown: the record is now treated as a founding text of polished 1980s R&B, and his version of A House Is Not a Home is widely studied by singers.","Start with the title track and follow the bass guitar rather than the voice; then go back and hear how lightly Vandross rides it. Then give A House Is Not a Home your full attention, ideally on headphones, and compare it with Dionne Warwick's brisk original. Afterwards try Anita Baker's Rapture to hear the same patience applied to new songs."],"luther-vandross-dance-with-my-father":["A song about a grown man remembering his late father. It opens on soft piano and gathers strings, bass and brushed percussion so gradually that the arrangement never competes with the voice. Vandross sings almost entirely in his gentle middle range, saving the few rising phrases for the chorus, and the track ends quietly rather than with a big final note, which makes its restraint all the more affecting.","Vandross wrote it with Richard Marx about his own father, who died when Luther was a small boy. In April 2003, just before the album of the same name came out, Vandross suffered a severe stroke and never fully recovered. The record became his first number-one album on the main American chart, and the song won the Grammy for Song of the Year, which he accepted by video message.","Listen for how little vibrato he uses: after twenty years of famously ornamented singing, Vandross sings this nearly plain, as if decoration would be dishonest. The melody is also kept deliberately narrow, sitting within a small span of notes, so that any listener could sing it at a family gathering, which is exactly where it has lived since.","Some reviewers at the time found it sentimental, and its success was inseparable from public sympathy over his illness. But it has outlasted that moment and is now one of the most requested songs at memorials and weddings. Many critics hear it as a dignified late statement, a singer known for luxury choosing simplicity at the end.","Hear it straight after A House Is Not a Home from 1981 and notice how much he has stripped away in twenty years. Pay attention to the piano at the start and to the moment the strings first come in under the second verse. It sits well beside Stevie Wonder's gentler ballads and Bill Withers's plain-spoken songs."],"anita-baker-rapture":["Eight songs, almost all slow or mid-tempo, played by a small band with real piano, fretless-sounding bass, brushed drums and the occasional saxophone. The sound is closer to a late-night jazz club than to the drum-machine pop of 1986. Baker's deep, smoky contralto carries everything; she lingers on low notes, scoops up into phrases and improvises around the melody in the closing minutes of songs like Sweet Love and Been So Long.","Baker, from Detroit, had sung with the band Chapter 8 and made one solo album for a small label before a contract dispute freed her to sign with Elektra. She insisted on running the sessions herself as executive producer, working with her old bandmate Michael J. Powell. At a moment when R&B was racing toward machines, she bet on acoustic instruments and an adult audience, and won.","Mystery, one of the album's quietest songs, was written by Rod Temperton, the English songwriter behind Michael Jackson's Thriller, and first recorded by the Manhattan Transfer; Baker's version is the one that stuck. Listen too for how often the band simply stops playing behind her, leaving the voice suspended for a beat before the groove returns.","It sold millions, won two Grammys and made Baker a star in her thirties, an unusual path at the time. Some critics then filed it as tasteful background music for dinner parties. Today it is generally seen as the high point of the quiet storm style and as proof that jazz-inflected singing could thrive in 1980s pop.","Begin with Sweet Love, a love song, and listen to how she holds back through the verses before opening up near the end. Then play the title track and notice the jazz chords on the piano. Compare her phrasing with Sarah Vaughan's, then with Sade's Diamond Life, a cooler English cousin from two years earlier."],"anita-baker-giving-you-the-best-that-i-got":["A song about devotion inside a long relationship. It moves at an easy walking pace over a warm bed of electric piano and a round, supple bass line, with soft backing voices answering Baker in the chorus. Her voice starts low and conversational, almost spoken, then climbs in the final minute into ad-libbed runs that bend and stretch the melody without ever losing the steady groove underneath.","It was the title track of the follow-up to Rapture, written by Baker with Skip Scarborough and Randy Holland, and released while she was the most successful jazz-leaning singer in pop. The record went to number one on the album chart and the song gave her the biggest pop hit of her career, along with more Grammys, confirming that Rapture had not been a fluke.","Notice that the chorus never resolves where the ear expects it to: the harmony keeps sliding sideways, a habit borrowed from jazz ballads, so the song feels unfinished until the very end. Her ad-libs in the fade are not ornaments added later; they follow the bass line closely, almost duetting with it.","Reviewers welcomed it as a worthy successor and praised its grown-up subject at a time when R&B often chased teenage audiences. Some felt the album played safe after Rapture. The song itself has kept its reputation as one of the most graceful adult R&B singles of the late 1980s, and singers still cover it.","Listen first to the bass and electric piano in the opening bars, which set the mood before she sings a word. Then follow her voice from the low first verse to the free final minute. Play it beside Luther Vandross's ballads to hear two singers who share a love of slow, patient build-ups."],"teddy-pendergrass-close-the-door":["A slow romantic song, an invitation to intimacy. Over the lush Philadelphia sound of sweeping strings, soft horns, electric piano and a steady, unhurried rhythm section, Pendergrass sings in a voice that moves between a husky murmur and a gravelly, gospel-trained roar. The arrangement swells and recedes beneath him like a tide, leaving space for the moments when he drops almost to a whisper.","Pendergrass had been the lead voice of Harold Melvin and the Blue Notes before going solo with Kenny Gamble and Leon Huff's Philadelphia International label, which wrote and produced this song for his second solo album, Life Is a Song Worth Singing. It topped the R&B chart. His concerts of the period were famously billed for women only, a marketing idea that made him one of the biggest live draws in Black music.","The strings and horns come from the same pool of Philadelphia session players, known on record as MFSB, who had helped create the disco sound a few years earlier; here they slow everything right down. Listen for the trademark Pendergrass rasp, which he uses sparingly, almost always on the last word of a phrase, so that each grainy note lands as an event.","It was a major hit and fixed his image as a romantic baritone sex symbol, which some critics found limiting given his range. In 1982 a car crash left him paralysed, and his story has since lent his late-1970s records a poignant weight. They are now regarded as the bridge between classic Philadelphia soul and the quiet storm of the 1980s.","Notice the contrast between the plush orchestra and his rough-edged voice; that tension is the whole effect. Listen for where he drops his volume rather than raising it. Then go back to the Blue Notes' If You Don't Know Me by Now to hear the younger singer, and forward to Luther Vandross for a smoother successor."],"teddy-pendergrass-love-tko":["A song about being knocked down by a failing love affair, with a boxing image in the title. The groove is cool and mid-tempo, built on a rubbery bass line, chiming electric piano and a gently insistent drum pattern. Pendergrass sings with weary restraint, never shouting, and the backing singers echo him softly. It is a far leaner, more modern sound than the orchestral ballads that made him famous.","It appeared on his 1980 album TP, still on Philadelphia International but written outside the Gamble and Huff team, by the songwriter Cecil Womack and collaborators. It became one of his signature songs. Released less than two years before the car crash that ended his first career, it captures him at the height of his fame, already moving toward the sleeker sound of the coming decade.","Listen to how the song never really climaxes: the band holds the same easy groove from start to finish while the emotion is carried entirely by small changes in his tone. The understated arrangement anticipates the spare, synthesizer-tinged quiet storm records of the mid-1980s more than it looks back to 1970s orchestral soul.","It was a solid R&B hit at the time rather than a crossover smash, but its reputation has risen steadily, helped by covers and by DJs who prize its groove. Many listeners now rank it among his finest performances precisely because it asks him to hold back, revealing the subtlety under the famous power.","Put it on after Close the Door and hear how much has been taken away: no big strings, no grand climax. Follow the bass line throughout, then listen to how he colours the same melody differently each time it returns. Pair it with Marvin Gaye's Sexual Healing for another mature, minimal soul record."],"isley-brothers-fight-the-power":["A song about pushing back against those in charge. The groove is tight and chugging: a choppy clavinet, a fat, repeating bass figure and a drum pattern that never lets up, with Ernie Isley's fuzzed, wah-wah guitar snarling through the gaps. Ronald Isley's high, sweet tenor sounds almost amused as he complains, and the chorus is shouted by the whole group like a chant at a rally.","The Isleys had been recording since the late 1950s and by 1975 were a six-man band on their own T-Neck label, the three elder brothers singing and the younger brothers and their brother-in-law Chris Jasper playing. The song opened their album The Heat Is On, which reached number one on the main American album chart. It caught the mood of mid-1970s Black America, disillusioned after the civil-rights era and Watergate.","Radio versions masked a mild curse word in the lyric, and the uncensored original made the song quietly notorious. Listen to the guitar: Ernie Isley, who had watched Jimi Hendrix up close when Hendrix played in the Isleys' band in the mid-1960s, plays fills that owe a clear debt to him. Public Enemy took the title for their own protest anthem in 1989.","It was a number-one R&B single and a top-five pop hit, and critics saw it as the Isleys' boldest statement. It has rarely been out of favour since, though it is often overshadowed by Public Enemy's later song of the same name. Historians of funk now place it alongside Sly Stone and James Brown as a key political funk record.","Lock on to the clavinet and bass first; together they make a single interlocking machine. Then listen for Ernie Isley's guitar commenting in the spaces. Afterwards play Public Enemy's Fight the Power to hear how the same idea sounds fourteen years later, and James Brown's Funky President for its cousin."],"isley-brothers-between-the-sheets":["A slow love song in the late-night radio tradition. It floats on a soft drum machine pattern, warm synthesizer chords and a gliding, melodic synth line, with a gentle bass underneath. Ronald Isley's light, airy tenor sits close to the microphone, sounding intimate rather than showy, and Ernie Isley adds a few glowing guitar phrases. The whole track moves very slowly, like a ballad with a faint dance pulse.","It was the title track of the last album made by the classic six-man Isley lineup before the younger members left to form their own trio. By 1983 the group had moved from guitar funk toward synthesizers, and this record shows them adopting the new machines without losing their warmth. It became a major R&B hit and a fixture of the evening quiet storm radio format.","The slow, rolling groove became one of hip-hop's favourite raw materials: the Notorious B.I.G. built Big Poppa on it in 1994, introducing it to a new generation. Listen to the synthesizer melody that answers the vocal; it is the hook that producers have borrowed again and again,, instantly recognisable within two notes.","Reviewers at the time treated it as a smooth late-career success for a veteran group. Its reputation grew enormously through sampling, and it is now heard as one of the purest examples of the quiet storm sound and a key link between 1970s soul and 1990s rap and R&B.","Listen on good speakers or headphones to the soft drum machine and how little it does; the space is the point. Then follow Ronald Isley's voice, which never strains. Afterwards play the Notorious B.I.G.'s Big Poppa and listen for how the producers lifted the synth melody, then compare with Marvin Gaye's Sexual Healing from a year earlier."],"chaka-khan-aint-nobody":["A love song, sung with joyous force. It begins with a long, shimmering synthesizer introduction before a bubbling, sequenced synth bass line and crisp electronic drums set up a mid-tempo groove. Chaka Khan's voice enters with enormous power and agility, leaping into her upper range and riffing freely over the band. The arrangement is sparse, leaving the voice and that restless bass line to drive everything.","It was credited to Rufus and Chaka Khan, the band with which she had found fame in the 1970s, and was one of four new studio songs added to their live album Stompin' at the Savoy. It was written by Rufus keyboardist David Wolinski. By then Khan already had a solo career, and this was effectively the group's farewell, released as synthesizers and drum machines were taking over Black pop.","Listen to the bass part: it is not a bass guitar but a synthesizer, playing a running pattern that never stops, and that machine-like motion against Khan's very human voice is what makes the record feel both futuristic and warm. The long instrumental opening, often cut on radio, sets up the whole mood and is worth hearing in full.","It topped the R&B chart, won a Grammy and became one of Khan's signature songs. Critics have consistently ranked it among the finest R&B singles of the 1980s, and later remixes and covers have kept it in clubs ever since. It is often cited as a model of how to marry electronic production to a big soul voice.","Hear the full-length version so you get the introduction. Follow the bubbling bass line for a minute, then switch to Khan's voice and notice how she plays with the beat, landing slightly early or late. Compare with Whitney Houston's I Wanna Dance with Somebody to hear a later big voice over machines."],"chaka-khan-i-feel-for-you":["A love song turned into a high-speed electronic collage. It opens with a rapper stuttering Khan's name over clattering drum machines, then her voice bursts in over a bright, pumping synthesizer groove. Stevie Wonder's harmonica darts in and out, and the production is full of cut-up effects, stabs and echoes. The result is a pop single that sounds like hip-hop, electro and soul colliding.","The song was written by Prince for his 1979 second album. Producer Arif Mardin, who had worked with Aretha Franklin, rebuilt it for Khan's solo album of the same name. He brought in Melle Mel of Grandmaster Flash and the Furious Five, one of the first times a rapper appeared on a mainstream pop single, and Stevie Wonder, whose harmonica tied the track to soul tradition.","The stutter of her name at the start, which sounds like a DJ cutting a record back and forth, became the most recognisable sound on the record. Listen also to the harmonica: Wonder's playing recalls the bright, bluesy style of his own early hit Fingertips, a nod from one generation of Motown-trained musicians to another.","It was a huge hit on both sides of the Atlantic and won Prince a Grammy as songwriter. Some critics found the production gimmicky at the time. It is now recognised as a pioneering fusion of rap and R&B, years before new jack swing made that blend standard, and as a turning point in Khan's solo career.","Start by listening to Prince's original, a modest, bouncy demo-like song, and then put Khan's version on to hear how much a producer can change. Notice the stutter, the rap, the harmonica and how her voice cuts through all of it. Afterwards try Run-DMC's early records and Bobby Brown's My Prerogative."],"rick-james-come-get-it":["Rick James's debut album, mixing long, driving funk workouts with slower soul songs. The opening You and I runs for about eight minutes on a pounding beat, slap-heavy bass, blaring horns and a chorus of voices, building and breaking down like a live show. Mary Jane is slower and smokier, with a loping groove. Throughout, the sound is raw and crowded, louder and rougher than most Motown records of the time.","James, from Buffalo, had knocked around the music business for a decade, including a stint in a Toronto band with Neil Young, before Motown signed him and released this on its Gordy label. It arrived at disco's peak, but he deliberately offered something grittier. You and I topped the R&B chart, and he assembled his Stone City Band to play the music on the road.","Listen to how the long tracks are structured like concert jams, with breakdowns where everything drops out except bass and percussion, then rebuilds. These breakdowns were exactly what club DJs and, later, hip-hop producers looked for. Notice too how the horns are used like percussion, short shouted blasts rather than melodies, a trick learned from James Brown.","It was an immediate success and made James a star. Critics heard it as a rougher-edged answer to disco and to George Clinton's P-Funk. It is now seen as the opening shot of his punk-funk style, which reached its peak three years later with Street Songs, and as a record that helped keep funk alive at Motown.","Start with You and I and listen to how it builds, drops and rebuilds over eight minutes; it is designed for the dance floor. Then play Mary Jane for the slower side. Compare with Parliament's Mothership Connection for the funk he was answering, and with Street Songs to hear where he went next."],"zapp-more-bounce-to-the-ounce":["A party song about dancing, stretched to about nine minutes. A slow, heavy, rolling beat with hand-claps and a fat, rubbery synth bass line never changes, while Roger Troutman sings and comments through a talk box, a tube that shapes his guitar or synthesizer into robotic, wah-wah vowels. Over this repeating groove, small melodic ideas come and go, making it hypnotic rather than climactic.","Zapp were the Troutman brothers from Dayton, Ohio, a city with a deep funk scene. Bootsy Collins of Parliament-Funkadelic helped them get signed to Warner Bros. and co-produced their debut album. This was its first single and it topped the R&B chart. Its sound arrived as disco faded and as cheaper synthesizers and drum machines began to reshape funk.","The talk box was not new, but Roger made it a voice of its own, singing whole lines through it. Listen to how slow and spacious the beat is; that unhurried bounce became a building block of West Coast rap. EPMD sampled it in 1988, and in 1995 Roger himself played talk box on 2Pac and Dr. Dre's California Love, completing the circle.","It was a big R&B hit but barely touched pop radio, and critics then saw Zapp as a novelty. Hip-hop changed that verdict: the track is now regarded as one of the most important funk records of its decade, effectively the ancestor of the G-funk sound that dominated 1990s rap.","Hear the full version and let the repetition work on you; do not wait for a big chorus. Listen for the talk-box phrases and for how the bass line slides between notes. Then play Dr. Dre's The Chronic and 2Pac's California Love to hear where this groove ended up."],"zapp-computer-love":["A love song about romance found through a computer. It moves at a slow, swaying tempo over soft drum machine, glowing synthesizer chords and a gentle bass line. Roger Troutman sings the hook through his talk box, while guest singers take the human verses, so the song becomes a duet between machine voice and natural voice. It is far mellower than Zapp's party funk, closer to a slow dance.","Credited to Zapp and Roger, it came from their 1985 album The New Zapp IV U and was released as a single in 1986. Roger Troutman wrote and produced it with his brothers, and brought in Charlie Wilson of the Gap Band and the singer Shirley Murdock for the vocals. Home computers were spreading fast, and the idea of meeting someone through one still sounded futuristic.","The title nods toward the electronic world of Kraftwerk, whose own song Computer Love appeared in 1981, but the two songs share nothing else. Listen for how the talk box carries the emotional hook while Wilson and Murdock supply the warmth; Roger uses the machine for longing, not novelty.","It was a solid R&B hit rather than a major one, but its reputation has grown greatly. Rappers and R&B singers have sampled and covered it repeatedly, and it is now regarded as one of the defining slow jams of the 1980s and a direct ancestor of the talk-box and Auto-Tune love songs of later decades.","Notice first how the talk box sounds tender rather than robotic here. Follow the swap between Roger's machine voice and the human singers. Then play Kraftwerk's Computer World to hear the European electronic music that shares its title idea, and Kanye West's 808s and Heartbreak to hear the machine-voiced love song decades later."],"gap-band-burn-rubber-on-me":["A song about being let down by a lover who has driven off. It moves at a brisk dance tempo on a slapping, popping bass line, chicken-scratch guitar and punchy horns. Charlie Wilson's voice is bright and gritty, full of gospel inflections, and the backing singers answer him in a shouted call and response. The groove is relentless and celebratory despite the complaint.","The Gap Band were three Wilson brothers from Tulsa, Oklahoma, sons of a Pentecostal preacher, who named themselves after streets in the city's historic Greenwood district. Working with producer Lonnie Simmons and his Total Experience studio in Los Angeles, they released this on the album The Gap Band III. It topped the R&B chart and helped define the post-disco dance sound now called boogie.","Listen to how much comic theatre is packed in: spoken asides and exaggerated vocal reactions, all held together by an extremely tight rhythm section. Charlie Wilson's ad-libs come straight from the church, where he learned to sing; strip away the subject and this is gospel call-and-response over funk.","It was a big R&B hit but, like much Black dance music of the time, was largely ignored by pop radio and rock critics. Later DJs and historians reassessed the Gap Band as one of the great early-1980s funk bands, and Charlie Wilson's later comeback as an elder statesman of R&B brought new attention to these records.","Start with the bass line, which is busy and percussive, and the guitar scratching against it. Then listen to Wilson's shouts and the backing singers' replies. Play it beside Earth, Wind and Fire's September and Chic's Good Times to hear three different ways of keeping a dance floor full around 1980."],"gap-band-outstanding":["A love song of praise and gratitude. Unlike the band's earlier, frantic funk, it settles into a slow-to-mid-tempo groove with a warm, rounded synthesizer bass line, gentle keyboard chords and a relaxed drum pattern. Charlie Wilson sings smoothly, saving his gospel rasp for the ad-libs near the end, and the backing voices float softly behind him. The feel is easy, sunny and spacious.","It came from the album Gap Band IV, the group's commercial peak, and was written by Raymond Calhoun, a musician in their circle, rather than the Wilson brothers. Released on Lonnie Simmons's Total Experience label, it reached number one on the R&B chart. By 1982 synthesizers had begun to replace horns and live bass in Black pop, and this track embraces the new sound.","That easy-going synth bass line and the hand-clap pattern have made Outstanding one of the most sampled records in hip-hop and R&B, used by dozens of artists across the 1980s, 1990s and beyond. Listen to how much space there is in the arrangement: that openness is exactly what made it easy to borrow.","It was a major R&B hit, though mostly overlooked by mainstream rock critics at the time. Its standing has risen steadily through sampling and through its constant use at family gatherings and on soul radio. It is now regarded as a defining example of early-1980s boogie, a bridge between funk and the R&B ballads that followed.","Listen first to the bass synthesizer, then to how relaxed the drums are compared with the Gap Band's earlier records. Notice how Wilson builds toward the ad-libs at the end. Play it after Burn Rubber on Me to hear the band slowing down, and then try Zapp's Computer Love, where Wilson sings again."],"cameo-word-up":["A call to the dance floor, built from very few parts. A hard, crisp drum machine beat and a thick, bouncing bass line hold everything up, while short whistling synthesizer phrases and stabs punctuate the groove. Larry Blackmon's nasal, half-spoken, almost cartoonish voice delivers the verses, and the group answers in clipped chants.","Cameo began in New York in the 1970s as a large funk band, the New York City Players. By the mid-1980s leader Larry Blackmon had pared it down to a trio, moved its base to Atlanta and set up his own label. Word Up! was the title track of their best-selling album, a top-ten pop hit in the United States and a big success in Britain, helped by a widely shown video.","Notice how much the song relies on silence: the beat drops out repeatedly so that Blackmon's voice and the bass line stand alone. Rock bands later covered it, including the Scottish group Gun and the American metal band Korn, revealing how sturdy the riff is and how close this lean funk sits to hard rock.","It was Cameo's biggest hit and won them a new pop audience, though some funk fans felt the stripped-down sound sacrificed the band's earlier richness. Today it is regarded as one of the defining funk records of the mid-1980s, and its minimal construction is often cited as an influence on dance-pop and hip-hop production.","Count how few sounds are playing at any moment; then notice what each one contributes. Listen to the drum machine and the bass first, then to the whistling synth and the clipped group chants. Play it after Parliament's Flash Light to hear funk slimmed down from a crowded band to a lean trio."],"bobby-brown-dont-be-cruel":["Ten tracks of hard-edged dance music and a few slow songs. The up-tempo numbers hit with booming drum machines, swung hip-hop beats, chopped samples and bright synthesizer stabs; Brown sings in a tough, slightly hoarse voice and half-raps some lines. My Prerogative, a song about doing things one's own way, stomps on a heavy swinging beat, while Every Little Step bounces and Roni slows into a ballad.","Brown had left New Edition in his late teens and his first solo album sold modestly. For this second album MCA paired him with the hottest producers of the moment: L.A. Reid and Babyface on the title track, Every Little Step and Roni, and Teddy Riley with Gene Griffin on My Prerogative. It became one of the best-selling albums of 1989 and made him the biggest young star in R&B.","Listen to the difference between the two production teams. Riley's My Prerogative is all swinging hip-hop drums and gritty bass, while Reid and Babyface's songs are sleeker, with pop melodies tucked into the hard beats. The album balances these two sounds, which together defined new jack swing for a mass audience.","Critics praised its energy and the production more than Brown's voice, and some found its swagger crude. It won him a Grammy for Every Little Step. Its standing has held up as a key new jack swing album, though his later troubled public life has often overshadowed the music.","Start with My Prerogative and listen to the swung, slightly lopsided drum pattern, the signature of new jack swing. Then play Every Little Step to hear the smoother Reid and Babyface approach. Afterwards try Guy's debut album for Riley's purest version of the style, and New Edition's Candy Girl to hear Brown as a boy."],"new-edition-candy-girl":["A teenage love song sung by boys barely in their teens. Over a bright, bubbling synthesizer riff and a crisp electro drum machine beat, the group trades verses and chorus, with Ralph Tresvant's high, light lead at the centre. In the middle, the boys take turns speaking short rhyming lines about the girl, a playful nod to rap. The whole record is cheerful, quick and full of youthful bounce.","New Edition were five friends from the Roxbury neighbourhood of Boston. Producer Maurice Starr, working with Michael Jonzun, shaped them on the model of the Jackson 5 and released this on the small Streetwise label. It reached number one on the American R&B chart and topped the British pop chart, launching the careers of Bobby Brown, Ralph Tresvant and three future members of Bell Biv DeVoe.","Listen to the melody and arrangement and you can hear Starr deliberately echoing the Jackson 5's ABC, but updated with the electro drum sounds of early-1980s New York. The rap-like spoken section was unusual for a pop group then and hints at the hip-hop-flavoured R&B the members would later help create.","It was a big hit but was often dismissed as a bubblegum imitation of the Jacksons. The group's later importance has changed that view: Candy Girl is now seen as the start of the modern boy band and as an early sign of how hip-hop and R&B would combine.","Notice the bubbly synth riff and the clipped electronic drums first, then listen for the boys' different voices in the spoken section. Play the Jackson 5's ABC to hear the model, then Bobby Brown's Don't Be Cruel and Bell Biv DeVoe's Poison to hear what these boys became five and seven years later."],"new-edition-heart-break":["A grown-up R&B album from a group that had begun as children. The up-tempo songs, such as If It Isn't Love, snap and lurch on hard, swinging drum machines and stabbing synthesizers, while ballads like Can You Stand the Rain glide on soft keyboards and close harmony. The group's voices now range from Johnny Gill's deep, powerful baritone to Ralph Tresvant's high, airy tenor, and they trade leads constantly.","Bobby Brown had left, and Johnny Gill, a gospel-trained singer, joined in his place. The group went to Minneapolis to work with Jimmy Jam and Terry Lewis, the former members of Prince's circle who had just made Janet Jackson's Control. The result was their most mature record, released in the same year that new jack swing broke into the pop mainstream.","Listen to how the drum programming on the fast songs clanks and hits like metal, the same hard, industrial edge Jam and Lewis gave Janet Jackson, softened here by five voices of harmony. Notice also the contrast between Gill's church-trained power and Tresvant's lighter voice on the same track: the producers deliberately set them against each other.","It was a major success and critics praised its polish and its harmony singing. It is often overshadowed by members' solo hits of the same period, but historians increasingly treat it as one of the essential albums of late-1980s R&B and a key example of the Jam and Lewis sound outside Janet Jackson's records.","Start with If It Isn't Love and listen to the clanking, swinging rhythm, then switch to Can You Stand the Rain for the harmonies. Pay attention to how leads pass between singers. Compare with Janet Jackson's Control for the same producers' signature sound, and with Boyz II Men's first album for the group New Edition helped launch."],"bell-biv-devoe-poison":["A song warning against trusting a seductive heartbreaker. It opens with a lurching, swinging drum machine beat, deep bass and scraps of sampled sound, then the three members alternate between sung harmony and rapped verses. The chorus is chanted rather than crooned, and the production is busy with stutters, echoes and sudden stops. It sounds much closer to a hip-hop record than to traditional R&B.","Ricky Bell, Michael Bivins and Ronnie DeVoe had been the less prominent members of New Edition. While the group was on hiatus they formed this trio, encouraged by producers who thought their mix of singing and rapping suited the moment. Poison, written and produced by Dr. Freeze, was the title track of their debut album and became a top-three pop hit in America.","Listen to how the beat seems to stumble forward: it is a swung rhythm, with drums slightly delayed, which gives new jack swing its name. The trio deliberately avoided the smooth falsetto approach of their old group; the rougher, chanted delivery was meant to show they had grown up and to court the hip-hop audience.","It was a huge hit and surprised critics who had underestimated the three as backing members. Some reviewers found it slight, but it is now considered one of the defining new jack swing singles, often played at retro parties, and a clear model for the hip-hop-and-R&B groups of the early 1990s.","Focus first on the drum pattern and how it swings rather than marching straight. Then listen to the trade between sung and rapped parts. Play New Edition's Candy Girl to hear where these singers started, and then Blackstreet's No Diggity to hear the same blend of rap and R&B six years later."],"guy-guy":["The debut album of the trio that defined new jack swing. The faster songs, such as Groove Me and I Like, combine hard, swinging hip-hop drum machines with gospel-tinged harmonies and Aaron Hall's powerful, churchy lead voice. Synthesizer stabs, sampled hits and bright keyboards fill the space. There is even an instrumental, Teddy's Jam, that shows off Riley's production on its own. The ballads slow down without losing the heavy beat.","The group was formed in Harlem by Teddy Riley, a young producer from the city's projects, with singers Aaron Hall and Timmy Gatling. They signed to Andre Harrell's new Uptown Records, which aimed to fuse hip-hop attitude with R&B singing. Released in 1988, the album went platinum and established Riley as the most important producer of a new style.","Listen to the drums on Groove Me: they swing, with the second beat in each pair arriving slightly late, a feel taken from jazz and go-go and programmed into the drum machine. That swing is the whole idea behind the name new jack swing, coined by the journalist Barry Michael Cooper.","Critics noticed it, but it was R&B radio and dance floors that made it a success. Its historical importance has grown greatly: it is now considered one of the founding albums of new jack swing, and Riley's production on it shaped Bobby Brown, Michael Jackson's Dangerous and countless others.","Start with Groove Me and nod your head to the swung beat; it is impossible to march to. Then hear Teddy's Jam with no vocals at all, to focus on the production. Afterwards play Michael Jackson's Dangerous to hear Riley's sound on a much larger stage, and Blackstreet's No Diggity for his later band."],"keith-sweat-i-want-her":["A song of romantic longing. Over a hard, swinging drum machine beat, punchy synthesizer bass and stabbing keyboard chords, Keith Sweat sings in a thin, pleading, slightly nasal voice that sounds almost like begging. The groove is tough and danceable while the vocal stays vulnerable, and that contrast is the record's signature. Breaks and drop-outs keep the long track lively.","Sweat, from Harlem, had been singing in local bands for years. He worked with Teddy Riley, then barely twenty, on his debut album Make It Last Forever, and this single became a number-one R&B hit and a pop crossover. It is widely regarded, along with Riley's other early productions, as one of the first true new jack swing records.","Listen to the drum programming: like Guy's records, it swings slightly, so that the beat feels like it is leaning forward. Notice too that Sweat's vocal style, pleading and full of small cries, became so influential that many R&B singers of the early 1990s copied it closely.","It was a major hit, though some critics mocked Sweat's whiny voice. Today it is regarded as a cornerstone of new jack swing and a model for the hip-hop-flavoured R&B ballad and dance track. Make It Last Forever is often ranked among the essential R&B albums of the late 1980s.","Start by listening to the beat on its own for the first minute, noticing the swing. Then follow Sweat's voice and the way it contrasts with the hard production. Afterwards play Guy's debut album, where Riley refined this sound, and Jodeci's Forever My Lady for singers who took Sweat's pleading style further."],"blackstreet-no-diggity":["A song admiring a confident woman. It is built around a short, rolling piano loop and a low humming vocal sample, under a slow, heavy, head-nodding beat. Dr. Dre opens with a rapped verse, Blackstreet's singers take the hook in tight, gospel-trained harmony, and the rapper Queen Pen adds a verse later. The sound is spare and laid back, far from the busy production of new jack swing.","Blackstreet was Teddy Riley's group after Guy split. He produced this for their second album, Another Level, with collaborators including William Stewart, and enlisted Dr. Dre to open the record. It topped the American pop chart for four weeks in late 1996 and won a Grammy, at a moment when R&B and hip-hop had almost fully merged.","The piano loop and hum come from Bill Withers's 1971 song Grandma's Hands, slowed and repeated until a gentle folk-soul tune becomes a swaggering groove. Notice how Riley, the inventor of busy new jack swing, here strips everything back to almost nothing; the restraint was a sign of how much the style had changed.","It was a huge hit and received strong reviews for its fusion of rap and harmony singing. Its reputation has grown with time; it is now regarded as one of the defining R&B singles of the 1990s and a model of how sampling can transform a source.","Listen first to the piano loop, then find Bill Withers's Grandma's Hands to hear its origin. Then focus on the shift from Dre's rap to the group's harmonies. Compare it with Guy's Groove Me to hear how Riley's sound moved from busy and swinging to slow and minimal over eight years."],"jodeci-forever-my-lady":["Jodeci's debut album, mixing up-tempo new jack swing with long, slow ballads. The ballads, such as the title track, Stay and Come and Talk to Me, are the core: K-Ci Hailey's raw, gospel-scorched lead soars and cracks over soft keyboards and slow, heavy beats, while the others add dense harmony. The faster songs use hard, swinging drum machines and synthesizers in the manner of Teddy Riley.","Jodeci were two pairs of brothers from North Carolina, K-Ci and JoJo Hailey and DeVante Swing and Mr. Dalvin DeGrate, all raised in church. They signed to Uptown Records, where Sean Combs was shaping their image. DeVante Swing wrote and produced most of the album. They presented themselves as streetwise rebels, a deliberate contrast with the clean-cut Boyz II Men.","Listen to the way the ballads borrow directly from gospel: the call-and-response between lead and harmony, the long climbing ad-libs and the moments where the beat drops away for pure voices. Under the streetwise image, these are church singers, and the tension between the sacred style and secular subject is what gives the record its heat.","It sold millions and established Jodeci as the leading R&B group of the early 1990s, though some critics found their image aggressive. Its reputation has grown; it is now regarded as one of the essential albums of 1990s R&B and a major influence on later male vocal groups and on hip-hop soul.","Start with Come and Talk to Me and notice K-Ci's ragged, gospel delivery. Then compare with Boyz II Men's Cooleyhighharmony to hear the two contrasting faces of early-1990s R&B groups. Afterwards try Mary J. Blige's What's the 411?, made on the same label with some of the same people."],"boyz-ii-men-cooleyhighharmony":["A debut album split between bright new jack swing and lush harmony ballads. Motownphilly bounces on a swinging beat, with the quartet trading lines and a short rap. Uhh Ahh and Please Don't Go are slow and warm. The album ends with It's So Hard to Say Goodbye to Yesterday, sung almost entirely without instruments, where the four voices, from Wanya Morris's high tenor to Michael McCary's deep bass, blend like a doo-wop group.","The four met at Philadelphia's High School for Creative and Performing Arts and were discovered by Michael Bivins of New Edition, who helped bring them to Motown. Motown, looking for new stars, promoted them heavily. The album won a Grammy and sold millions, restoring Motown's commercial standing and making the group stars in their late teens.","It's So Hard to Say Goodbye to Yesterday is a cover of a song from the 1975 film Cooley High, which also inspired the album title, so the record quietly honours an earlier Black coming-of-age story. Listen to McCary's bass in the a cappella sections, providing the floor that instruments would usually supply.","Critics praised its harmonies and freshness, though some found it too clean next to the hip-hop-influenced R&B of the moment. Today it is regarded as the start of one of the most successful vocal-group careers in history, and as a bridge between the doo-wop and Motown tradition and 1990s R&B.","Start with Motownphilly for the bouncy side, then go straight to It's So Hard to Say Goodbye to Yesterday and listen to the four parts. Notice which voice carries the melody and which supports. Compare with Jodeci's Forever My Lady, released the same year, for a rougher style, and with the Temptations' My Girl for the tradition."],"boyz-ii-men-end-of-the-road":["A song pleading with a lover not to end a relationship. It sways in a slow triple-time lilt, like an old doo-wop ballad, over soft keyboards and a light, steady beat. The four voices pass the lead between them and swell into dense harmony on the chorus. Midway, Michael McCary delivers a spoken passage in his deep bass, before the others return with climbing, gospel-tinged ad-libs.","It was written and produced by Babyface, L.A. Reid and Daryl Simmons for the soundtrack of the Eddie Murphy comedy Boomerang. Released in 1992, it spent thirteen weeks at number one on the American pop chart, breaking a record set by Elvis Presley in the 1950s, and won Grammys. It made Boyz II Men the biggest vocal group of the decade.","Notice the triple-time rhythm, unusual in 1990s pop: it links the song directly to 1950s and early 1960s vocal-group ballads. The spoken bass section is a deliberate echo of the doo-wop tradition, where the bass singer often had a spoken part. Babyface wrote the melody to give each member a moment.","It was hugely popular and won a Grammy, although some critics found it overly sentimental. Its record-breaking run made it a symbol of early-1990s R&B ballads. It is now remembered as one of the most successful singles of the decade and a model for the slow vocal-group ballad.","Count along to feel the triple time. Listen for when each singer takes the lead and the moment McCary speaks. Then play Babyface's own Tender Lover album to hear the songwriter's voice, and Toni Braxton's Un-Break My Heart for another huge 1990s ballad from the same circle."],"mary-j-blige-whats-the-411":["Mary J. Blige's debut, setting a raw soul voice over hard hip-hop beats. Real Love drives on a sampled drum loop and a big, bouncing bass line; Reminisce and Love No Limit slow down into jazzy, late-night grooves. Her voice is husky and urgent, more church and street corner than polished studio, and she phrases like a rapper as often as a singer. Guest rapper Grand Puba appears on the title track.","Blige grew up in a housing project in Yonkers, New York. A karaoke recording of an Anita Baker song reached Uptown Records, which signed her. Sean Combs, then a young executive, guided the album. Uptown wanted to fuse hip-hop and R&B more completely than new jack swing had, and this record became the template for what was soon called hip-hop soul.","Real Love is built around a sample of Audio Two's 1987 rap track Top Billin', grounding a love song in a hip-hop classic. Listen to how Blige sings slightly behind the beat, the way rappers often rap, rather than floating over it in the manner of earlier R&B divas.","It sold millions and critics hailed her as a fresh voice, though some found the production rough. Her reputation has grown into that of the defining R&B singer of the 1990s; the album is now regarded as a landmark that made the fusion of hip-hop production and soul singing the norm.","Start with Real Love and listen to the drum loop and bass. Then hear Reminisce for the slower side. Compare with Anita Baker's Rapture to hear the quiet storm she grew up on, and with Lauryn Hill's Miseducation to hear where hip-hop soul went later in the decade."],"mary-j-blige-my-life":["A long, dark, intensely personal album about depression, a painful relationship and the struggle to find happiness. The sound is slow and warm, built from samples of 1970s soul records, soft keyboards and heavy, laid-back beats. Blige's voice is rough, wounded and searching, often cracking at the top. Songs flow into each other with interludes, making it feel like one continuous confession.","Blige made it with Sean Combs and producer Chucky Thompson while dealing with personal struggles and a troubled relationship. Released in late 1994, a high point of hip-hop soul, it reached the top of the R&B chart and went multi-platinum. It moved her from a promising newcomer to the voice of a generation of listeners who heard their own lives in it.","The title track is built on Roy Ayers's 1976 Everybody Loves the Sunshine, a sunny jazz-funk song turned into something weary. Listen across the album for how many grooves come from 1970s soul records, layered under her voice so that each song carries an echo of an earlier era, a kind of musical memory.","Some critics initially found it too sombre, but it was quickly acclaimed as her finest work. It is now regularly ranked among the best R&B albums ever made and is credited with opening R&B to raw confessional writing that later singers from Beyoncé to SZA drew on.","Listen to it from start to finish, in order, preferably at night. Start by noticing the warm, sampled grooves, then follow how her voice shifts from tired to hopeful across the album. Afterwards play Roy Ayers's Everybody Loves the Sunshine, then SZA's Ctrl for a later confession album in the same line."],"toni-braxton-un-break-my-heart":["A song of heartbreak, begging a departed lover to undo the pain. It opens with soft piano and Braxton's very low, smoky voice, then builds through strings, a steady beat and a short Spanish-flavoured guitar solo into a huge final chorus. Her contralto, unusually deep for a pop star, carries the opening in its lowest range and then climbs, giving the song a slow, aching arc.","Written by Diane Warren and produced by David Foster, it was the second single from Braxton's second album, Secrets, released on L.A. Reid and Babyface's LaFace label. It spent eleven weeks at number one on the American pop chart and won a Grammy. A dance remix was also a club hit, and the song became one of the best-selling singles of the decade.","Listen to the nylon-string guitar solo: a touch of flamenco colour unusual in R&B ballads. Notice too how low Braxton sings in the verses; many singers transpose the song up when covering it, because her range there sits in what is usually a male register.","It was a huge hit, though some critics found it overblown next to her more soulful debut. It remains one of the most popular ballads of the 1990s and the song she is best known for, a model of the big power ballad that the decade's R&B singers and talent show contestants have imitated since.","Notice how low her voice is in the first verse, then follow the build to the final chorus. Listen for the guitar solo in the middle. Compare with Whitney Houston's I Will Always Love You, the decade's other giant ballad, and with Anita Baker's quieter, jazzier approach to heartbreak."],"babyface-tender-lover":["Babyface's second solo album, half sleek new jack swing dance tracks and half soft ballads. It's No Crime and the title track bounce on crisp drum machines and synthesizer stabs; Whip Appeal and Soon As I Get Home are slow, glowing love songs on soft keyboards. His voice is light, sweet and unforced, more a songwriter's voice than a powerhouse's, and the melodies are clean and memorable.","Kenneth Edmonds, from Indianapolis, had written and produced a string of hits with L.A. Reid for others, including Bobby Brown's Don't Be Cruel. Tender Lover was produced by the pair on the Solar label, and the same year they founded LaFace Records in Atlanta. The album made Babyface a star singer in his own right while he became the most in-demand songwriter in R&B.","Listen to how carefully the songs are built: every chorus is simple and sung over a steady pulse, and the harmonies are stacked so neatly they could almost be sung by a choir. The same craftsmanship he applies here runs through the hits he wrote for Whitney Houston, Boyz II Men and Toni Braxton.","It sold well and critics praised its songcraft, though some found his singing slight. Its reputation rests largely on its role in a larger career; it is now seen as a key document of the Reid and Babyface sound, which shaped mainstream R&B for a decade.","Start with It's No Crime for the dance side and then Whip Appeal for the ballad side. Notice how easy the melodies are to sing back. Then play Boyz II Men's End of the Road and Toni Braxton's Breathe Again to hear his songs in other voices, and Bobby Brown's Don't Be Cruel for his production."],"three-6-mafia-mystic-stylez":["Three 6 Mafia's debut album is a long, murky, deliberately frightening record built from a Roland 808, cheap synthesizer strings, horror-film organ and tape hiss. DJ Paul and Juicy J produced it; Lord Infamous, Koopsta Knicca, Crunchy Black and Gangsta Boo trade verses in clipped triplets and half-whispered chants. Tracks such as 'Da Summa' and 'Tear da Club Up' set group hooks over dragging, booming drums, and the whole thing feels recorded in a basement after midnight.","Memphis rap grew in the early nineties from hand-dubbed cassettes sold out of car boots and corner shops, with DJ Paul and Juicy J among its busiest tape makers. Mystic Stylez came out on their own Prophet label in 1995, before any major company was interested in the city. It drew on local club culture, on the occult imagery of horror videos, and on the slow, heavy funk of nearby Southern soul rather than on New York's sample-collage style.","Listen to the hi-hats: they are programmed in rapid, rattling bursts that would become the signature of trap a decade later. The triplet flow, three syllables to each beat, was common on Memphis tapes long before it was credited to anyone in Atlanta. Many of the eerie vocal hooks are chanted in unison rather than sung, more like a congregation than a chorus.","Outside the South it was barely reviewed in 1995, and its lo-fi sound was dismissed as amateurish. Over time it has become a cult text: producers in trap, cloud rap and the internet genre called phonk sample and imitate its textures, and critics now hear it as one of the hidden roots of twenty-first-century rap. Its long running time and uneven mixing still divide listeners.","Start with 'Da Summa' and notice how slow the tempo is and how much space the bass is given. Then listen for the rattling hi-hats and the chanted hooks, and compare them with any trap record from 2015. Play it loud, at night, and treat the hiss as part of the atmosphere rather than a flaw."],"three-6-mafia-stay-fly":["A lead single from Most Known Unknown, 'Stay Fly' is a song about style and swagger built on a sped-up soul sample: a falsetto voice and strings from Willie Hutch, looped into a bright, almost sunny hook. Under it DJ Paul and Juicy J lay heavy 808 kicks and snapping claps. Juicy J, DJ Paul and Crunchy Black are joined by Young Buck and by 8Ball & MJG, so the track becomes a relay of Memphis voices across two generations.","By 2005 the group had shrunk to its core and signed with Columbia through its own Hypnotize Minds label. Southern rap now dominated American radio, and 'Stay Fly' gave the Memphis pioneers their biggest mainstream hit. The following year Three 6 Mafia won the Academy Award for Best Original Song for a song from the film Hustle & Flow, and performed it at the ceremony, an unthinkable turn for a group that began on dubbed tapes.","The sample is from Willie Hutch, the Motown writer and singer best known for his blaxploitation soundtrack work, and the producers keep his voice high and slightly warped rather than disguising it. Notice that the beat drops the sample out under some verses, leaving only drums and bass, so its return feels like sunlight. The guest list is a small history lesson in Memphis rap.","Critics who had ignored the group's tape era greeted the song as a crossover triumph, and it remains one of the most played Southern singles of the decade. Some longtime fans argued that the polished, soul-sampling sound traded away the menace of the early records; others heard the same producers simply proving they could work in any register.","Listen first to the sample alone in the opening seconds, then to the moment the 808 kick lands under it. Follow the change of voice with each verse and notice how each rapper rides the same beat differently. Then go back to Mystic Stylez to hear how far the same producers travelled in ten years."],"scarface-the-diary":["Scarface's third solo album is short, dark and unusually inward: a record about mortality, paranoia and grief in Houston's Southside. The production, shared by Scarface with N.O. Joe and Mike Dean, uses live bass, wah-wah guitar and slow, heavy drums, so the songs feel like late-night soul rather than loop collages. Scarface raps in a low, conversational baritone, often sounding as if he is thinking aloud. 'No Tears' and a remake of the Geto Boys' 'Mind Playing Tricks on Me' frame it.","Brad Jordan had become famous with the Geto Boys on James Prince's Houston label Rap-A-Lot, and by 1994 he was the label's most respected solo voice. The Diary appeared in the same year as Nas's Illmatic and Biggie's Ready to Die, when New York critics still treated Southern rap as a novelty. Houston had its own infrastructure of studios, independent distribution and car culture, and this album was made almost entirely inside it.","Mike Dean, later Kanye West's and Travis Scott's go-to engineer and synthesizer player, is already here, and his bluesy guitar and Moog-like synth lines are a large part of the album's warmth. Notice how often the songs fade on long instrumental tails, as if the band kept playing after the rapping stopped. Scarface's habit of ending a verse on a question, not a boast, is his signature.","It received strong reviews on release and sold well, yet for years it stood in the shadow of the New York albums of 1994. Southern artists never forgot it: Scarface is routinely named by rappers from Lil Wayne to Kendrick Lamar as one of the greatest storytellers in the form, and The Diary is the record most often cited as proof.","Start with 'No Tears', listening to how the bass and guitar breathe behind the voice. Then hear the 1994 version of 'Mind Playing Tricks on Me' beside the 1991 Geto Boys original. Compare the album with Nas's Illmatic from the same year: two great narrators, two utterly different senses of tempo and space."],"scarface-the-fix":["The Fix is Scarface's most polished and soulful album, a record about memory, loyalty and getting older. Its beats blend warm soul samples with live keyboards and guitar; a young Kanye West contributed production, including 'Guess Who's Back' with Jay-Z and Beanie Sigel, alongside work by Mike Dean and others. 'On My Block' is a tender, nostalgic portrait of his neighbourhood, and 'Someday', with Faith Evans, closes on gospel-tinged hope. It runs under an hour and never hurries.","By 2002 Scarface was president of Def Jam South, the Southern arm of the New York label, where he had signed Ludacris. He made The Fix while running the company, and it brought his Houston sound into conversation with East Coast producers and guests. Kanye West was still known only as a producer for Roc-A-Fella; his beats here came a year and a half before his own debut album.","Listen to Kanye West's beats here and you hear the sped-up soul style of The College Dropout fully formed, months before most people knew his name. 'On My Block' uses a gentle, chiming loop and conversational verses rather than a big hook, and its structure is closer to a memoir than a single. Scarface's voice is more relaxed than in the nineties, leaning behind the beat.","Reviewers were nearly unanimous: many called it the best album of his career, and it is still often ranked among the finest rap albums of the early 2000s. Some fans of the darker Rap-A-Lot records found it too smooth. Its reputation has grown as Kanye West's early production has become a subject of study in itself.","Begin with 'On My Block' and let the loop settle before the verses arrive. Then play 'Guess Who's Back' and listen for the soul sample sped up into a bright, insistent hook. Compare it with The Diary to hear the same voice eight years older, calmer and more reflective, then move on to The College Dropout."],"8ball-and-mjg-comin-out-hard":["The debut album by the Memphis duo 8Ball and MJG is slow, smooth and heavy: deep bass, mellow electric piano, wah guitar and an unhurried drum-machine pulse. Its songs are street narratives and boasts told with a pimp's cool. 8Ball's voice is round and booming, MJG's higher and quicker, and the contrast gives every song a conversational shape. The title track sets the template, a laid-back groove over which the two pass verses back and forth like a long phone call.","Premro Smith and Marlon Goodwin grew up in Orange Mound, one of Memphis's oldest Black neighbourhoods, and moved to Houston to record for Tony Draper's independent Suave House label. Comin' Out Hard was sold largely through regional distribution and word of mouth in 1993. It linked the Memphis tape scene to Houston's more established industry, and it showed that Southern rap could be built around live-feeling soul and funk rather than New York-style breakbeats.","Listen to how much of the arrangement sounds played rather than sampled: keyboards and bass lines move like a session band, a sound that would define Suave House. The duo often finish each other's verses mid-line, a trick that makes them sound like one storyteller with two voices. Their slowness is a choice, the same Southern drag that later fed Houston's chopped and screwed tapes.","National magazines largely ignored it at first, but it sold steadily across the South and made the duo stars there. In retrospect it is credited, alongside UGK and the Geto Boys, as one of the albums that established a distinct Southern sound. Later critics also note its influence on the smoother, soul-based side of Atlanta and Houston rap.","Start with the title track and let the groove carry you; notice the electric piano and the bass more than the drums. Follow how the two voices hand off to each other. Then compare with UGK's Super Tight and Three 6 Mafia's Mystic Stylez to hear two very different Memphis-rooted moods from the same years."],"8ball-and-mjg-on-top-of-the-world":["8Ball and MJG's third album is their most expansive: lush keyboards, slow funk bass lines, talk-box touches and long, cruising grooves designed for car stereos. It is a record about success, loyalty and the street life they came from. The duo trade verses in their familiar contrast, 8Ball's rolling baritone against MJG's nimble, higher delivery, and the songs stretch out with instrumental passages that owe more to seventies soul and Parliament than to any rap model.","By 1995 Suave House had built a real regional business, and this album gave the duo their biggest national exposure yet. The South was moving from the margins of rap toward its centre: OutKast's debut had appeared the year before and UGK were rising in Texas. On Top of the World was a statement that Southern artists could sell on their own terms, without New York producers or radio-friendly compromise.","Notice the space-age synthesizer textures, pitched and sliding, borrowed from P-Funk and early electro; the duo cast themselves as cosmic players. The basslines often carry the melody while the keyboards only colour the harmony, a reversal of the usual arrangement. The album's sequencing moves from bright, confident opening tracks to darker, reflective late songs.","Reviews were warm and it reached a far wider audience than the earlier albums, cementing the duo as elder statesmen of Southern rap. Later writers place it beside Ridin' Dirty and ATLiens as part of the mid-nineties Southern renaissance, though it has received less critical attention than either. Fans often cite it as the duo's peak.","Put it on in a car, if you can, and listen to the bass first. Notice the long instrumental stretches where the groove is the point. Then compare it with Parliament's Mothership Connection to hear where the cosmic funk came from, and with OutKast's ATLiens, which took the same space imagery somewhere stranger."],"ludacris-back-for-the-first-time":["Ludacris's major-label debut is loud, funny and rhythmically restless, a record about partying, Atlanta and his own outsized personality. The beats are bright and bouncy: snapping 808s, plinking synths and call-and-response hooks. 'Southern Hospitality' rides a sparse, playful track by The Neptunes, and Timbaland produced 'Phat Rabbit'. Ludacris raps with elastic timing and cartoonish voices, speeding up, slowing down and punching single words, so the comedy is in the rhythm as much as the words.","Chris Bridges was a radio DJ in Atlanta before he was a star rapper. He released much of this material independently as Incognegro, and the album was reissued by the newly formed Def Jam South, run by Scarface, in October 2000. Atlanta was becoming the capital of Southern rap, with OutKast already famous and a local strip-club and car culture shaping what DJs played.","Listen to how often Ludacris changes the pace of his delivery inside a single verse, bunching syllables then stretching them, a technique closer to a drummer's fills than a singer's phrasing. The Neptunes' beat for 'Southern Hospitality' is almost empty, just a few synth stabs and claps, and the gaps are where the energy comes from.","It was a commercial hit and made Ludacris one of the most recognisable voices in rap. Some critics dismissed him at first as a novelty act; over the following years he came to be respected as one of the most technically skilled rappers of his era, and the album is now seen as a key bridge between Atlanta's bass-music past and its pop future.","Start with 'Southern Hospitality' and notice how little is in the beat. Then follow Ludacris's voice as an instrument, listening for each change of speed. Compare with OutKast's Stankonia from the same year to hear two Atlanta answers to the question of how strange a hit can be."],"ludacris-stand-up":["The lead single from Chicken-n-Beer is a club song built on almost nothing: a stuttering kick drum, a whistling synth figure, handclaps and a shouted chant. Kanye West co-produced it with Shondrae, and the beat's spareness leaves all the room to Ludacris, who barks, drawls and sing-songs his verses in rapid changes of character. Its hook is a crowd instruction, designed to work in a packed room rather than on headphones, and it lasts under four minutes.","By 2003 crunk, the loud, chant-driven club music of Atlanta and Memphis, was sweeping American radio, and Ludacris was one of its most visible faces. 'Stand Up' gave him his first number-one single in the United States. Kanye West was still primarily a producer, and the track shows him working well outside the soul-sample style he was known for, in a stripped-down Southern club idiom.","Listen to the drum programming: the kick lands off the beat in places, so the groove seems to hiccup, and that hiccup is what makes the dance. There is almost no bass line in the usual sense; the low end comes from the kick itself. The whistle-like synth is closer to a referee's signal than a melody, and the whole arrangement is built around cues.","It was a huge hit and a staple of clubs and stadiums, and remains one of the defining singles of the crunk era. Critics enjoyed its humour and energy; some found it slight beside Ludacris's more elaborate verses. It is now often cited as a reminder of Kanye West's range as a producer before his own debut.","Listen to the drums alone for the first thirty seconds and try clapping along; the off-beat kicks will catch you out. Then follow how Ludacris's voice changes character from line to line. Compare with Missy Elliott's 'Get Ur Freak On' for another sparse, strange club beat from the same years."],"ti-trap-muzik":["T.I.'s second album is a record about street life in Atlanta and the urge to escape it, and it gave a name to a genre. DJ Toomp's '24's' sets the tone: triumphant synth horns, rolling 808 bass and hi-hats ticking in fast subdivisions. David Banner produced the swaggering 'Rubber Band Man', and 'Be Better Than Me' is a reflective song addressed to younger listeners. T.I. raps with a clear, confident drawl, unhurried yet precise, equally at home boasting or confessing.","Clifford Harris had been dropped by Arista after a weak-selling debut and built his own Grand Hustle company with Atlantic. He promoted himself through mixtapes and Atlanta clubs, and Trap Muzik arrived in August 2003 as the city's rap industry exploded. The word 'trap', Atlanta slang for a place where drugs were sold, became the name for the whole sound partly through this album's title.","DJ Toomp's productions here use orchestral brass and string patches from cheap synthesizers in a grand, almost ceremonial way; this fanfare-like sound is one of the most imitated textures in later trap. Notice how the 808 bass is tuned to follow the melody, so the drum machine plays a bass line. T.I.'s reflective songs sit deliberately beside the brash ones, giving the album an argument rather than a mood.","Reviewers praised his charisma and the album's balance of swagger and conscience, and it made him a star. Over time its importance has grown: critics and historians often trace trap as a named genre to Trap Muzik, alongside early Jeezy and Gucci Mane, though T.I. himself has claimed the title more emphatically than some writers grant.","Start with '24's' and listen for the synth horns and the ticking hi-hats. Then play 'Be Better Than Me' to hear the album's other face. Compare it with Gucci Mane's Trap House and Jeezy's Let's Get It, and then with any Future record, to hear the sound grow from these first sketches."],"ti-king":["King is T.I. at the height of his confidence, a long, glossy album about power, wealth and the cost of both. Its centrepiece, 'What You Know', produced by DJ Toomp, rides a slow, regal synthesizer progression and booming 808s that sound like a coronation march. 'Why You Wanna' borrows from Jody Watley's 'Looking for a New Love', and other tracks range from bouncing club songs to dark, reflective closing numbers. T.I.'s flow is relaxed and authoritative throughout.","T.I. had proclaimed himself 'King of the South' in his early mixtapes, and by 2006 the claim had become plausible. King was released alongside the film ATL, in which he starred, and the album debuted at number one in the United States. Atlanta now dominated rap and R&B radio, and the album's polished production shows how far trap's street sound had moved into the mainstream.","The chords of 'What You Know' move very slowly, changing only every few bars, and the synth patch swells and fades like an organ; it is closer to a processional than a pop hook. Listen for how the hi-hats are kept relatively sparse compared with later trap: the busy rolls had not yet become standard. Several tracks quote T.I.'s own earlier songs.","It was his best-selling album to that point and 'What You Know' won a Grammy. Critics praised the production and his poise, though some found the album too long. It is now seen as the moment trap became pop without losing its sound, and 'What You Know' is regularly ranked among the best rap singles of its decade.","Start with 'What You Know' and listen to the slow synth chords as if they were a church organ. Notice how T.I.'s voice sits calmly over a huge beat. Then compare with Trap Muzik, three years earlier, to hear the same sound grown rich and confident, and with Jeezy's records for the grittier route."],"jeezy-lets-get-it-thug-motivation-101":["Jeezy's major-label debut is an album framed as a hustler's motivational speech, and its sound is one of the purest early statements of trap. The beats, many by Shawty Redd, are huge and dark: minor-key synth strings, ominous brass stabs, thundering 808 kicks and long, booming bass notes. Jeezy raps slowly and hoarsely, almost growling, punctuating lines with his famous ad-libs. 'Soul Survivor', with Akon, and 'And Then What' are the best-known tracks.","Jay Jenkins had built a following in Atlanta through mixtapes and his group Boyz n da Hood before Def Jam released this album in July 2005. It arrived at the same moment as T.I.'s rise and Gucci Mane's first album, and the three are often named as trap's founders. The record's motivational framing, its talk of hard work and loyalty, gave it an audience far beyond the street.","Listen to the synth strings: Shawty Redd voices them low and thick, like a film score for a thriller, and the 808 kick is tuned so deep it is felt more than heard. Jeezy's ad-libs are placed between lines like percussion hits, and they became so recognisable that fans could identify a song from a single grunt. The album is paced like a long mixtape.","It sold strongly and made Jeezy a star, though critics were divided about its subject matter and his limited technique as a rapper. Over time its sonic influence has become clear: the cinematic, menacing trap beat that dominated the next decade was largely set here, and Jeezy's presence and delivery are now considered central to trap's identity.","Start with 'Soul Survivor' and notice how slowly Jeezy raps against the big, dark beat. Then listen for the synth strings and the deep 808s, as you would listen to the brass in a film score. Compare with T.I.'s Trap Muzik and Gucci Mane's Trap House to hear three founders with three tempers."],"jeezy-soul-survivor":["'Soul Survivor' is a song about endurance and staying loyal under pressure, and it is the most melodic track on Jeezy's debut. Akon sings the hook in his plaintive, slightly nasal tenor over a slow beat of mournful synth strings, deep 808 bass and crisp snares. Jeezy's verses are hoarse and unhurried, almost spoken, so the contrast between the sung chorus and the gruff rapping becomes the song's whole drama. It runs about four minutes.","Released as a single in 2005, it became Jeezy's breakthrough hit and carried trap onto national pop radio. Akon was then one of the most in-demand hook singers in American music, and pairing a gruff Atlanta street rapper with a melodic singer was a commercial formula of the moment. The song helped persuade the industry that the hard-edged Atlanta sound could chart.","The synth strings carry a simple descending line that repeats throughout, more like a lament than a pop riff, and it is this sadness that sets the song apart from Jeezy's more aggressive tracks. Listen to how Jeezy leaves pauses at the ends of lines, letting the bass fill the space. Akon's voice is double-tracked in places, giving it a choir-like edge.","It was a major hit and remains Jeezy's best-known song. Critics noted its emotional pull, unusual for trap at the time, and it is often cited as an early sign of the melodic turn that rap would take later in the decade, from Lil Wayne's sung hooks to Future's Auto-Tune laments.","Listen first to the string line and notice that it never resolves. Then follow the alternation between Akon's sung hook and Jeezy's half-spoken verses. Compare it with Future's Pluto, where the singer and the rapper became the same person, to hear where this melodic thread led."],"gucci-mane-trap-house":["Gucci Mane's debut album is rough, funny and strangely catchy, a record about the hustle in East Atlanta told in a lazy, slurring drawl. The beats are cheap and bright: plinking keyboards, toy-like synth melodies, booming 808s and rattling hi-hats. Several are by Zaytoven, a church-trained keyboardist whose piano and organ lines give the music an unexpected bounce. 'Icy', with Jeezy, became a regional hit and is the record's best-known song.","Radric Davis grew up between Alabama and East Atlanta and released Trap House on a small independent label in May 2005, the same year as Jeezy's and only a little after T.I.'s defining album. 'Icy' made both rappers locally famous and then led to a long, public feud between them. Gucci built his career on an enormous flow of mixtapes rather than on major-label promotion.","Listen to Zaytoven's keyboard parts: they use gospel voicings, the full rolling chords of a church pianist, transplanted into a drum-machine track. That combination of sanctified harmony and booming 808s became a whole school of trap production. Gucci's delivery often lands slightly behind the beat, so his rhymes seem to stumble into place, which is part of the charm.","It was barely noticed outside Atlanta at first, and Gucci was often dismissed as a sloppy rapper. Opinion turned sharply in the following decade, when a generation of artists including Migos, Young Thug and Future named him as a mentor. Trap House is now considered a founding document of trap, rawer and more eccentric than its rivals.","Start with 'Icy' and listen for the keyboard line before the voices. Then notice how relaxed, almost sleepy, Gucci's rapping is against the busy drums. Compare with Jeezy's debut from the same year, which is grand where this is homemade, and then jump to Migos's 'Versace' to hear the next generation."],"gucci-mane-lemonade":["'Lemonade', from The State vs. Radric Davis, is a playful song about flashy wealth in which the hook simply lists things that are yellow. The beat, by Bangladesh, sets a sing-song, childlike melody and a sampled choir over booming 808 kicks and skittering hi-hats. Gucci Mane raps in his relaxed, slurring drawl, half-amused by his own boasts, and the song's appeal lies in that contrast between nursery-rhyme brightness and heavy Atlanta drums.","By 2009 Gucci Mane had become the most prolific figure in Atlanta rap, releasing mixtape after mixtape and finally landing a major-label deal with Warner Bros. The State vs. Radric Davis was his big commercial push, released in December 2009. Bangladesh was then one of Atlanta's hottest producers, having made Lil Wayne's 'A Milli' the year before, and the two shared a taste for strange, minimal hooks.","The choral sample gives the beat a strangely innocent, almost churchy glow, which makes the boasting sound comic rather than menacing. Listen to how few elements there are: a melody, a voice sample, drums and bass, with drops and returns doing all the structural work. The hook's colour-word game is closer to a playground chant than a pop chorus.","It became one of Gucci's signature songs and a cult favourite, praised by critics for its absurd humour and inventive production. It is often cited as the moment wider audiences realised how eccentric and witty Gucci could be, and as an example of trap's playful side, which later artists such as Young Thug pushed much further.","Listen to the first few seconds for the childlike melody and choir before the drums arrive. Then notice how Gucci almost chuckles through his verses. Compare with Lil Wayne's 'A Milli', another Bangladesh beat built from a single repeated vocal fragment, and with Young Thug's Jeffery for the playfulness taken further."],"lil-wayne-tha-carter-ii":["Tha Carter II is the album on which Lil Wayne turned from a teenage New Orleans star into a serious, hungry rapper. Its sound is lush and soulful: live horns, swirling strings, gospel organ and warm samples rather than the bounce beats of his Cash Money youth. 'Fireman' is a bright, booming single; 'Hustler Musik' is slow and reflective; 'Best Rapper Alive' is a long, rock-tinged boast. Wayne raps with dense wordplay and a raspy, increasingly eccentric voice.","Dwayne Carter had joined Cash Money as a child and grown up in the Hot Boys alongside Juvenile. By 2005 the label's in-house producer Mannie Fresh had left, and Wayne made this album with a range of outside producers. It was released in December 2005, only months after Hurricane Katrina had devastated New Orleans, and its pride in the city took on new weight.","Listen to how often the beats use live horn and string arrangements instead of loops; the album sounds more like a soul record than its predecessors. Wayne's voice shifts register within songs, from a nasal growl to a near-croon, a habit that would become his signature. Several tracks run long, giving him extended verses without a hook, a sign of his growing ambition.","Critics greeted it as a breakthrough, and many date Wayne's claim to be among the best rappers of his time from this record. It sold strongly and set up the extraordinary run of mixtapes and Tha Carter III that followed. Some fans still prefer its warmth to the sprawl of the later albums.","Start with 'Hustler Musik' for its slow, soulful arrangement, then 'Fireman' for the energy. Listen to Wayne's voice as an instrument, noticing its changes of tone. Then compare with Juvenile's bounce era and with Tha Carter III three years later, to hear a rapper becoming a star of a different order."],"lil-wayne-tha-carter-iii":["Tha Carter III is sprawling and unpredictable: Auto-Tuned pop, minimal club tracks, soul samples, rock guitar and a closing monologue. 'A Milli' is almost nothing but a vocal loop and drums; 'Mr. Carter', with Jay-Z, is a passing of crowns; 'Tie My Hands', with Robin Thicke, is a soft, mournful song about New Orleans after Katrina; 'Let the Beat Build', co-produced by Kanye West, constructs its beat in real time. Wayne's raspy voice and free-associative wordplay hold it together.","Between 2006 and 2008 Lil Wayne released an enormous stream of free mixtapes, and by the time this album arrived in June 2008 he was arguably the most popular rapper in America. It sold around a million copies in its first week, a feat almost unheard of in the download era, and won the Grammy for Best Rap Album. Its success confirmed that the South now led the genre commercially and creatively.","The closing track, 'Dontgetit', ends with a long spoken monologue over a sample of Nina Simone's 'Don't Let Me Be Misunderstood', in which Wayne talks about race, media and justice, a turn few listeners expect. Listen also to how 'Let the Beat Build' adds one element at a time, a lesson in hip-hop arrangement hidden inside a song.","Critics hailed it as a peak of his career and one of the defining albums of its decade, though some found it uneven and overlong. Its commercial triumph changed the industry's view of the mixtape as a promotional tool. Its influence is everywhere in the generation that followed, from Drake and Nicki Minaj, whom Wayne signed, to Young Thug.","Start with 'A Milli' to hear how little a beat needs, then 'Let the Beat Build' to hear one assembled. Then play 'Dontgetit' to the end. Compare with Kanye West's 808s & Heartbreak, released the same year, which took Auto-Tune somewhere colder and sadder."],"lil-wayne-a-milli":["'A Milli' is a song about wealth and self-belief, and it is one of the starkest hits in rap. Bangladesh's beat is a single pitched-down vocal fragment, sampled from an earlier hip-hop record and repeated relentlessly, with booming 808 kicks, snapping snares and almost nothing else. There is no chorus in the usual sense. Lil Wayne raps continuously for nearly four minutes, darting through wordplay and voices, so the track becomes an endurance test and a display of free association.","Released in spring 2008 as a single from Tha Carter III, it reached the upper reaches of the American charts despite its minimalism and lack of a sung hook. Wayne was at the peak of his mixtape fame, and dozens of rappers released their own freestyles over the beat within weeks, a sign of how central the instrumental had become to rap culture.","Listen to how the vocal sample is chopped so that a single syllable stutters and loops; the whole song is a variation on that one fragment. Bangladesh drops the drums out for a moment here and there, and each return hits harder. Wayne's verses are largely written in long chains of punning images rather than a narrative.","Critics praised its audacity, and it won the Grammy for Best Rap Solo Performance. It is often cited as proof that Wayne's charisma could carry a song without conventional structure, and as one of the purest examples of the minimalism in Southern rap production. Some listeners find it monotonous, which is partly the point.","Listen once only to the beat, ignoring the words, and count how few elements there are. Then follow Wayne's voice as it slides around that grid. Compare with Steve Reich's early tape loops for another piece built from a single repeated vocal fragment, and with Gucci Mane's 'Lemonade', also by Bangladesh."],"future-pluto":["Future's debut album is spacey, melancholy and melodic, a record about ambition, love and loneliness sung as much as rapped through heavy Auto-Tune. The beats, by producers including Mike WiLL Made-It and Sonny Digital, pair shimmering synth pads with trap's rattling hi-hats and deep 808s. 'Turn On the Lights' is a tender, searching love song; 'Magic' is a bright club track. Future uses Auto-Tune not to correct his pitch but to crack, slide and wail, like a guitarist using distortion.","Nayvadius Wilburn came from Atlanta and was a cousin of Rico Wade of Organized Noize, the production team behind OutKast. He built his reputation through mixtapes before Epic released Pluto in April 2012. Auto-Tune had been popularised by T-Pain and taken to darker places by Kanye West's 808s & Heartbreak, and Future fused it with the trap beat to create a new kind of sung rap.","Listen to how Future's voice breaks into the Auto-Tune's pitch steps when he strains for a high note: the warble is deliberate, a way of showing emotion through the machine. The synth pads are often in a slightly different mood from the drums, wistful where the drums are aggressive, and that tension is the album's signature. The space theme recalls OutKast and Parliament.","Reviews were mixed to positive, with some critics unsure about the Auto-Tune. Within a few years, however, Future had become one of the most influential artists in rap, and Pluto is now seen as the start of the melodic trap style that dominated the 2010s. His later mixtapes are often ranked higher, but the template is here.","Start with 'Turn On the Lights' and listen to how Future's voice cracks through the Auto-Tune. Then notice the contrast between soft synths and hard drums. Compare with Kanye West's 808s & Heartbreak to hear the same technology used in a colder way, and with The Weeknd's House of Balloons for a parallel melancholy."],"future-mask-off":["'Mask Off', from Future's self-titled 2017 album, is a song about success and its excesses, built on one of the most memorable loops in modern rap: a breathy, minor-key flute melody sampled from Tommy Butler's 'Prison Song', from the musical Selah, with a ghostly choir behind it. Metro Boomin's production adds sparse 808 bass and crisp, ticking hi-hats. Future raps in a low, hypnotic monotone, letting the flute carry the melody. It lasts just over three minutes.","By 2017 Future was one of the biggest stars in rap, and he released two albums in successive weeks that February. Metro Boomin, still in his early twenties, had become the most sought-after producer in Atlanta. 'Mask Off' grew into a sleeper hit over the following months, helped by a viral flute-playing internet craze in which people posted their own covers of the melody.","The flute loop comes from a 1970s gospel-tinged stage musical, and Metro Boomin keeps the original's breathy, slightly out-of-tune quality rather than cleaning it up. Listen for the choir voices underneath, which give the beat a sorrowful, almost liturgical depth. The 808 enters only after the flute has been heard alone, so the sample is introduced like a theme.","It became one of Future's biggest hits and a fixture of 2017. Critics singled out the beat as one of Metro Boomin's finest and noted the strange poignancy of the flute against Future's detached delivery. The flute covers turned it into a broader cultural moment, and it is now frequently cited as a classic of the sample-based side of trap.","Listen first only to the flute: its line is short, minor and circling. Then notice how sparse the drums are beneath it. Compare with Pluto, five years earlier, to hear how Future's style moved from wailing to cool restraint, and with Isaac Hayes's flute-heavy soundtracks for a soul ancestor of the texture."],"migos-versace":["'Versace' is a song about luxury fashion whose hook simply repeats the brand's name, and its fame rests almost entirely on rhythm. Zaytoven's beat is light and bright: a bell-like synth melody, booming 808s and fast hi-hats. Quavo, Offset and Takeoff rap in the rapid triplet pattern that became known as the Migos flow, three syllables to each beat, stacking ad-libs and echoes so the three voices interlock like a percussion section.","Migos came from Lawrenceville, in Atlanta's northern suburbs, and released 'Versace' on a mixtape in 2013. Drake then recorded a remix that carried the song onto national radio, and the triplet flow spread through rap at astonishing speed. The group was managed by Quality Control, a small Atlanta label that would become one of the industry's most powerful.","The triplet flow was not invented by Migos: it appears on Memphis tapes of the nineties, including Three 6 Mafia's. What Migos added was density and interplay, with one member delivering the main line and the others echoing its last word a fraction later. Listen to how the ad-libs fill every gap, so the vocal track becomes almost continuous rhythm.","Many critics initially treated the song as a novelty, mocking the repetitive hook. Within a couple of years the Migos flow was everywhere, from mainstream pop to rap from Chicago to London, and critics began to credit the group with one of the most influential rhythmic innovations of the decade. 'Versace' is now seen as the starting gun.","Listen once only to the rhythm of the voices, ignoring the words, and tap out the three-to-a-beat pattern. Then follow how the ad-libs fill the spaces. Compare with Three 6 Mafia's Mystic Stylez to hear the triplets in their Memphis origins, and then with Culture to hear them polished for the charts."],"migos-culture":["Culture is Migos's commercial and artistic peak, a polished, cohesive album about success, loyalty and the group's own rise. Its centrepiece, 'Bad and Boujee', with Lil Uzi Vert, rides Metro Boomin's eerie, sparse beat of tinkling synths and deep bass. 'T-Shirt' is cinematic and wintry; 'Slippery', with Gucci Mane, glides. Quavo, Offset and Takeoff trade verses in tightly interlocking triplets, with Quavo usually supplying the melodic hooks. It runs just under an hour.","Released in January 2017 on Quality Control and 300 Entertainment, Culture arrived when 'Bad and Boujee' was already a number-one single. Atlanta trap had become the dominant sound of American pop, and Migos were its most visible group. Takeoff, the youngest member, died in 2022, and the album now stands as the clearest record of the trio at their height.","Listen to Metro Boomin's beat for 'Bad and Boujee': it is almost entirely made of quiet, high synth tones and a sub-bass, leaving a large empty middle register for the voices. Notice too how the three rappers' timbres are distinct, Quavo bright and melodic, Offset agile, Takeoff low and steady, which is what makes the triplets legible as three parts.","It was a major critical and commercial success, topping the American charts and earning Grammy nominations. Critics praised its focus after the group's sprawling mixtapes. Some argued that the Migos style had become formulaic; others hear the album as the purest statement of trap as pop. Its influence on the sound of late-2010s rap is hard to overstate.","Start with 'T-Shirt' for its cold, cinematic beat, then 'Bad and Boujee' for its eerie spareness. Follow one rapper at a time through a song to hear each voice's role. Compare with 'Versace' four years earlier, and with Young Thug's Jeffery for a stranger, looser take on the same Atlanta moment."],"travis-scott-rodeo":["Travis Scott's debut album is dark, maximal and psychedelic, a record about fame and restlessness drenched in reverb, distorted synths and Auto-Tuned vocals. Songs shift shape midway, beats switch, and guests such as Future, 2 Chainz, The Weeknd and Kanye West appear as voices in a murky dream. 'Antidote' is the hit; '3500' stretches across nearly eight minutes. T.I. narrates passages between songs, framing the album as a Western-style legend.","Jacques Webster grew up in Houston and was mentored by both Kanye West and T.I., signing with T.I.'s Grand Hustle and Kanye's production circle. Rodeo came out in September 2015, when trap was the dominant sound of American rap. Travis approached it as a producer and arranger as much as a rapper, working closely with figures such as Mike Dean and Metro Boomin.","Listen to the transitions: Travis treats the album like a continuous mix, with songs bleeding into each other and beats changing mid-track, techniques borrowed from Kanye West's later albums and from psychedelic rock. The Auto-Tune is often pushed into heavy distortion, making the voice part of the synth texture. Mike Dean's sustained synth solos recall seventies progressive rock.","Reviews were mixed, with critics praising the sound design but finding Travis's own rapping and writing thin. Its reputation has grown with his fame: Rodeo is now seen as the start of a psychedelic, producer-driven strand of trap, and its atmospheric approach influenced a generation of artists who treat mood as much as rhymes.","Start with 'Antidote', then '3500', and listen for the moments when the beat changes under you. Notice the reverb, as you would in a psychedelic rock record. Compare it with Kanye West's Yeezus and with Pink Floyd's sound-collage albums, then move on to Astroworld for the bigger, brighter sequel."],"travis-scott-astroworld":["Astroworld is a psychedelic theme-park ride of an album, named after the Houston amusement park Travis Scott visited as a child. Its sound is wide and colourful: swirling synths, live drums, guitar, harmonica and Auto-Tuned voices. 'Sicko Mode', with Drake, switches beats three times in five minutes; 'Stargazing' opens in dreamy psychedelia and crashes into heavy trap; 'Stop Trying to Be God' features Stevie Wonder on harmonica. Frank Ocean, Kevin Parker of Tame Impala and James Blake appear too.","Six Flags AstroWorld closed in 2005 and was demolished, and Travis framed the album as a lament for lost childhood spaces and for Houston itself. Released in August 2018, it arrived when he was one of the most popular live performers in the world. He worked with a long list of producers and musicians, treating the album as a collaborative studio project in the manner of Kanye West's records.","Listen to the beat switches: in 'Sicko Mode' each section is in a different tempo and mood, so the song behaves more like a medley or a progressive-rock suite than a single. The guest list hides many uncredited voices and players. Stevie Wonder's harmonica is a direct link to seventies soul, and Kevin Parker's psychedelic layering is audible on 'Skeletons'.","It was a major critical and commercial success and his most acclaimed album, with 'Sicko Mode' becoming a huge hit and earning Grammy nominations. Critics praised its sound design and ambition, though some still found Travis more a curator than an artist. It is widely seen as one of the defining rap albums of the late 2010s.","Start with 'Stargazing' and wait for the switch from dream to bass. Then follow the three sections of 'Sicko Mode' as you would the movements of a suite. Compare with Tame Impala's Currents and Frank Ocean's Blonde, both kindred in their hazy textures, and with Rodeo for the darker original."],"21-savage-savage-mode":["Savage Mode is a short, chilling collaborative EP between 21 Savage and the producer Metro Boomin, a street narrative told with an almost blank calm. Metro's beats are made of horror-film textures: tinkling music-box bells, eerie synth choirs and minor-key piano, with deep 808s and skittering hi-hats. 21 Savage raps in a flat, quiet monotone, never raising his voice. Nine tracks, including 'No Heart' and 'X' with Future, run about half an hour.","Shéyaa Abraham-Joseph was born in London and grew up in Atlanta, where he became known through mixtapes. Savage Mode was released in July 2016, during Metro Boomin's rise to the top of the producer world, and it made 21 Savage a star. Its cold, horror-influenced sound reached back to the Memphis tapes of the nineties and forward to the darker, more minimal trap of the late 2010s.","Listen to how Metro Boomin uses bells and toy-like piano: these are the sounds of children's music, and their innocence makes the beats far more unsettling. Notice too how 21 Savage's monotone works almost as another instrument, flattening every line to the same level. The EP's brevity is part of its effect; nothing is wasted.","Critics praised its focus and atmosphere, and it is often ranked as one of the best rap projects of 2016. Some found its subject matter grim and its delivery monotonous; others argued that the deadpan was the point. It established the 21 Savage and Metro Boomin partnership, which produced further collaborations, and its sound has been widely imitated.","Listen with headphones and focus on the beats: the bells, choirs and empty spaces. Then notice how quiet 21 Savage's voice is. Compare with Three 6 Mafia's Mystic Stylez to hear the horror lineage, and with Bernard Herrmann's Psycho score for a classical cousin of its icy tension."],"young-thug-barter-6":["Barter 6 is Young Thug's breakthrough mixtape-album, a record about loyalty, wealth and his own rise, delivered in one of the strangest voices in rap. The beats, largely by Wheezy and London on da Track, are bright, airy and melodic, with chiming synths and bouncing 808s. Thug yelps, croons, squeaks and slurs, bending syllables until they become pure sound. 'Check' and 'With That' are the standouts, and Birdman and Lil Wayne's Cash Money circle hover over its story.","Jeffery Williams grew up in Atlanta's Jonesboro South projects and was mentored early by Gucci Mane. Originally announced as Carter 6, the title deliberately teased Lil Wayne, Thug's idol, during a period of public tension between Wayne and Birdman. It was released in April 2015, when Thug was already the most imitated new voice in Atlanta.","Listen to how often Thug abandons words altogether, turning a line into a melodic squeal or a mumble placed exactly on the beat; his voice works like a lead synthesizer. The beats leave a lot of open space in the middle frequencies so that his register leaps stand out. The title itself is a joke on a dispute, not a plan.","Critics were divided at first: some found his style incomprehensible, others heard a radical new approach to the voice. By the late 2010s Thug was recognised as one of the most influential rappers of his generation, and Barter 6 is often ranked as his most cohesive early project. Its experiments with vocal texture echo through a generation of melodic rappers.","Start with 'With That' and listen to the voice as an instrument, not as words. Notice the bright, airy beats underneath. Compare with Lil Wayne's Tha Carter III, the record Thug was answering, and then with Jeffery to hear him push the experiment much further."],"young-thug-jeffery":["Jeffery is a short, colourful mixtape in which every track is named after one of Young Thug's heroes: 'Wyclef Jean', 'Harambe', 'Guwop' for Gucci Mane, 'RiRi' for Rihanna. Its music ranges from dancehall bounce to acoustic-tinged pop to warped trap, and Thug's voice ranges further: falsetto, growl, yodel, near-sob. The beats, by producers including Wheezy, London on da Track and TM88, are spacious and melodic, letting his vocal acrobatics run free.","Released in August 2016 under his real first name, Jeffery came after a run of prolific mixtapes. Its cover, in which Thug wears a flowing ruffled gown by the designer Alessandro Trincone, challenged rap's codes of masculinity and caused a stir. Thug was then at the height of his influence on Atlanta's younger artists, who were adopting his melodic, elastic delivery.","Listen to 'Wyclef Jean', which nods to the Fugees member's Caribbean-tinged pop with a lilting, off-kilter rhythm; Thug's melody seems to wander deliberately across the beat. The titles are tributes, but the songs rarely mention their namesakes, so the naming works as a kind of dedication page. Many vocal takes sound improvised, left raw with cracks and breaths.","Critics greeted it as one of his best projects, praising its adventurousness and its cover's statement. Some found it slight, but it is widely regarded as a key album of 2010s rap, a record that widened what a rapper's voice and image could be. Its influence on genre-fluid artists of the following years is often noted.","Start with 'Wyclef Jean' and let the off-kilter rhythm settle. Then follow Thug's voice through its changes of register. Compare with the Fugees' The Score to hear the namesake's world, and with Frank Ocean's Blonde, released the same month, for another record that rewrote expectations of a male voice in hip-hop and R&B."],"lil-baby-my-turn":["My Turn is a long, consistent album about struggle, success and the burden of fame, built on melancholy trap beats of piano, minor-key synths and rolling hi-hats. Lil Baby raps in rapid, conversational bursts that spill across bar lines, with a slightly hoarse, urgent edge. 'Emotionally Scarred' is reflective and melodic; 'Woah' became a dance craze; 'Sum 2 Prove' is a confession of insecurity. The album runs about an hour and keeps a single sombre mood.","Dominique Jones grew up in Atlanta and began rapping only in his twenties, encouraged by the Quality Control label, home of Migos. His rise was unusually fast. My Turn was released in February 2020, weeks before the pandemic closed American cities, and became one of the most streamed albums of the year in the United States.","Listen to Lil Baby's flow: his phrases often begin in the middle of a bar and end in the middle of the next, so his rapping seems to run on ahead of the beat. The piano loops are simple and sad, closer to a ballad than a club record. His verses are dense with plain-spoken self-scrutiny rather than metaphor.","Critics praised its consistency and his technical skill, though some found the album too long and uniform. Its streaming success made him one of the defining commercial figures of rap in 2020, and many observers credit him with bringing a more introspective tone to the mainstream trap sound.","Start with 'Emotionally Scarred' and notice the piano loop and the sung hook. Then listen to how his verses run across the bar lines. Compare with Migos's Culture, from the same label, to hear how trap's mood grew darker and more personal in only three years."],"lil-baby-the-bigger-picture":["'The Bigger Picture' is a song about police brutality and the protests of 2020, delivered in Lil Baby's urgent, run-on flow. The beat is sombre and restrained: a mournful piano figure, soft synth pads and muted trap drums that never overwhelm the voice. It opens with audio from news coverage and protests, then moves through verses that mix personal testimony with calls for change. There is a short, sung hook, and the track runs about four minutes.","Lil Baby released the song in June 2020, less than a month after the killing of George Floyd in Minneapolis set off protests across the United States. Its proceeds were directed to social-justice organisations. Coming from a rapper not previously known for political songs, it marked a moment when mainstream trap artists engaged directly with the national debate on race and policing.","Listen to how the production stays out of the way: there is no dramatic drop, and the drums are mixed low so that the voice carries the urgency. The news samples frame the song like a documentary. Notice that Lil Baby avoids slogans for most of the song, speaking from his own experience and doubts, which gives it a measured, uncertain tone.","It was widely praised as one of the defining protest songs of 2020 and earned Grammy nominations. Critics noted its balance of anger and nuance, though some wished it were more musically adventurous. It is often discussed alongside Kendrick Lamar's 'Alright' as a key hip-hop response to the Black Lives Matter movement.","Listen to the opening audio, then focus on the piano and the restraint of the beat. Follow the shift between personal and political lines. Compare with Kendrick Lamar's 'Alright' and Public Enemy's 'Fight the Power' to hear how protest rap has changed its sound over three decades."],"megan-thee-stallion-fever":["Fever is Megan Thee Stallion's first full-length mixtape, a record about confidence and independence delivered with relentless energy. The beats are heavy Houston trap, with booming 808s, slow, menacing synths and sampled hooks that nod to the city's past. Juicy J of Three 6 Mafia produced several tracks and appears on 'Simon Says'. Megan raps in a commanding, deep-voiced drawl with crisp diction and tight rhythmic control. 'Realer' and 'Simon Says' are standouts.","Megan Pete grew up in Houston, the daughter of a rapper mother, and built her following through freestyle videos online before signing with 1501 Certified and 300 Entertainment. Fever was released in May 2019 and launched her national career. Its cover and promotional imagery drew on seventies blaxploitation films, and the 'Hot Girl Summer' phrase associated with her became a cultural catchphrase that year.","Listen to how Megan's flows recall the Houston and Memphis rappers she grew up on, including Pimp C of UGK and the Three 6 Mafia crew; her clipped triplets and deep register are a homage. Juicy J's beats bring the Memphis horror textures back into a 2019 setting. Notice how short the album is, eighteen-odd tracks of brief, punchy songs.","Critics praised her technical skill and charisma, and it was widely named among the best rap releases of 2019. It made her one of the leading voices in a wave of women in rap, and later writers credit her with reviving the classic Houston sound for a new generation while making it unmistakably her own.","Start with 'Realer' and listen to how precise her rhythm is against the slow beat. Then play 'Simon Says' and notice Juicy J's Memphis production. Compare with UGK's Ridin' Dirty and Three 6 Mafia's Mystic Stylez to hear the lineage she draws on."],"megan-thee-stallion-savage":["'Savage', from the EP Suga, is a short, swaggering song about self-confidence, built on a bouncing, minimal beat by J. White Did It: snapping claps, rolling 808 bass and a quirky synth riff. Megan raps in a playful, punchy cadence, shifting between staccato bursts and drawled syllables, and the chorus is designed as a dance chant. The original lasts under three minutes; the remix featuring Beyoncé, recorded later that spring, adds new verses and a Houston-proud duet.","Released in March 2020, just as the pandemic closed much of the world, 'Savage' went viral on TikTok through a dance routine that spread across the app. Beyoncé, also from Houston, joined the remix in April, and it became Megan's first number-one single in the United States. The proceeds from the remix were directed to Houston charities.","Listen to the beat's structure: it uses only a few elements, and the synth riff is slightly off-centre rhythmically, which is why it suits a dance routine so well. In the remix, Beyoncé raps rather than only singing, and her verses nod to Houston slang and to the city's rap heritage, a meeting of two eras of Houston pop.","The remix won two Grammy Awards, Best Rap Song and Best Rap Performance, and was widely praised. Critics noted how the song exemplified the new influence of social media on hits. Some argued that its fame rested more on the dance than the music; others praised Megan's sharp delivery and the remix as a summit of Houston pride.","Listen to the original first and focus on the claps and the synth riff. Then play the Beyoncé remix to hear the song expanded. Compare with Beyoncé's Renaissance for her own dance-floor turn, and with 21 Savage's Savage Mode for a very different use of the same word."],"the-roots-do-you-want-more":["The first major-label album by the Philadelphia band plays hip-hop the way a jazz combo plays a standard: Questlove's dry, behind-the-beat drums, Leonard Hubbard's upright and electric bass, Scott Storch's keyboards and Black Thought's calm, dense rapping, all recorded without samples. Tracks run long, grooves stretch out, and interludes of human beatboxing stand where a DJ's scratches would be. The sound you remember is the snare: close-miked, soft, and never quite on the grid.","The Roots began as a busking act on South Street in Philadelphia, drums and voice on the pavement, and spent time gigging in Europe before Geffen's DGC imprint signed them. In 1995 the idea of a hip-hop group that played its own instruments struck the industry as a novelty, and the label marketed the album partly to jazz and alternative-rock audiences. The band made a point of stressing that no samples or programmed loops had been used.","The no-samples rule is a manifesto, but listen to how the band imitates a sampler anyway: Questlove repeats the same two-bar drum figure with machine-like stubbornness, and the bass loops a phrase as if it had been cut from an old record. The beatboxing tracks show off Rahzel, then an associate of the crew, whose ability to sing and beatbox at once later became his calling card.","Reviews were warm but puzzled, and sales were modest; some hip-hop critics heard the live sound as polite, a coffee-house version of a street music. Its reputation has risen steadily since, partly because the band's later career made it look prophetic. It is now heard as the first draft of the organic, musician-led hip-hop that shaped the neo-soul movement and the Soulquarians collective.","Start with Proceed, where the band locks into a simple groove and Black Thought rides it with conversational ease, then try Distortion to Static. Listen to the drums as a lead instrument: the ghost notes on the snare, the hi-hat that breathes. Compare it with A Tribe Called Quest's sample-built jazz grooves to hear the same idea built by hand."],"the-roots-illadelph-halflife":["The Roots' second major-label album is darker and harder than its predecessor. The band still plays most of what you hear, but the drums are fatter and grimier, sometimes treated to sound like samples, and the mood is late-night and wary. Black Thought and Malik B. trade verses over minor-key keyboard figures and clipped bass lines, and short interludes knit the record into a continuous flow. What They Do, a lazy, gorgeous groove, is the track everyone knows.","Made in 1996 as East Coast hip-hop hardened around Mobb Deep, the Wu-Tang Clan and Nas, Illadelph Halflife was partly a response to criticism that the band's live sound was too soft for the street. The Roots began to accept some programming and sampling, and brought in outside collaborators such as the singer and producer Raphael Saadiq. The title folds Philadelphia's nickname into a phrase about decay, matching the record's portrait of a city under pressure.","The video for What They Do was a deadpan parody of rap-video clichés, with captions undermining every luxury shot, and it reportedly irritated the Notorious B.I.G., who felt targeted. Listen also to the album's close, where Ursula Rucker's spoken-word piece The Unlocking steps outside rap altogether; she would close several later Roots albums the same way, a recurring signature.","Critics praised the album as a big step forward in focus and grit, and What They Do gave the band its first real radio presence. Fans still argue whether this or Things Fall Apart is the band's peak: Illadelph is the moodier, more cohesive set, while its successor has the bigger songs. It is now widely ranked among the best East Coast albums of 1996.","Begin with What They Do and notice how little happens in the groove: a rubbery bass line, a soft keyboard chord, and drums that sit back. Then play the album straight through at night, listening for the interludes that tie it together. Pair it with Mobb Deep's The Infamous, from the same year, to hear two answers to the same city mood."],"the-roots-things-fall-apart":["The Roots' breakthrough is a long, warm, intricately played album in which every groove feels hand-made. Questlove's drums lean back into the pocket, Kamal Gray's keyboards drift in jazz harmonies, and Black Thought's rapping is at its most precise. Guests include Erykah Badu, Mos Def and Common. The shape alternates hard, boom-bap tracks with softer, soul-leaning ones, and it closes, as Roots albums often did, with a spoken-word piece by Ursula Rucker.","Recorded largely at Electric Lady Studios in New York, the album grew out of the loose collective later called the Soulquarians, in which Questlove, D'Angelo, J Dilla, James Poyser, Common and Erykah Badu shared studio time and ideas; D'Angelo's Voodoo and Common's Like Water for Chocolate came out of the same rooms. The title comes from Chinua Achebe's 1958 novel, itself borrowed from Yeats, and the album was issued with several alternative covers of documentary photographs.","The album opens with a dialogue sample from Spike Lee's film Mo' Better Blues, in which two jazz musicians argue about why Black audiences have stopped coming to hear jazz, a statement of purpose about tradition and audience. J Dilla produced Dynamite!, and his unquantised, slightly drunken drum feel can be heard rubbing off on Questlove's playing across the record.","Things Fall Apart went gold quickly, later platinum, and You Got Me, with Erykah Badu, won a Grammy for best rap performance by a duo or group. Critics hailed it as the moment the band's ideas and songs finally met. It routinely appears on lists of the best hip-hop albums, and it fixed the image of the Roots as the genre's house band, later literal on late-night television.","Start with You Got Me, then The Next Movement, where a sparse groove and wordless vocal hook show how much space the band leaves. Listen for Questlove's ghost notes and how the bass player follows the kick drum. Play D'Angelo's Voodoo next: same studio, same circle, and the same lazy, human sense of time."],"black-star-mos-def-and-talib-kweli-are-black-star":["A single album by a Brooklyn duo, built mostly on beats by the Cincinnati producer Hi-Tek: dusty soul and jazz loops, crisp drums, and plenty of air around two contrasting voices. Talib Kweli raps in rapid, tumbling lines; Mos Def is looser, funnier and more melodic, sliding between rapping and singing. The record moves from playful party tracks to Respiration, a slow nocturne about city life with Common, to songs about self-respect and Black history.","Black Star was released in 1998 on Rawkus, the New York independent label that became the centre of the late-1990s underground. The duo took its name from Marcus Garvey's Black Star Line shipping company, a symbol of Black self-determination. Coming two years after the killings of Tupac Shakur and the Notorious B.I.G., the album was widely heard as a deliberate turn away from rivalry and luxury toward community and thought.","Two tracks talk back to the past. Definition borrows its hook from Boogie Down Productions' Stop the Violence, a 1980s plea for peace reapplied to the late-1990s climate, and Children's Story is a remake of Slick Rick's 1988 cautionary tale, updated to describe the music business. Thieves in the Night draws its central idea from a passage in Toni Morrison's novel The Bluest Eye.","The album sold modestly but was received as a landmark of conscious rap, and the label of conscious rapper followed both men for years, sometimes to their irritation. Critics now rank it with the defining independent hip-hop records. A long-delayed second Black Star album, No Fear of Time, produced by Madlib, appeared in 2022 to a respectful but quieter reception.","Begin with Respiration, listening for how the beat's slow piano loop and distant city sounds let three different voices describe the same night. Then play Definition for the duo's back-and-forth energy. Compare Children's Story with Slick Rick's original, and follow it with Mos Def's Black on Both Sides and Kweli's work with Hi-Tek as Reflection Eternal."],"mos-def-black-on-both-sides":["Mos Def's solo debut is a sprawling, generous record that moves through boom bap, soul, jazz, and even hard rock. Producers include DJ Premier, Diamond D, Ayatollah and Mos himself, who also plays instruments on several tracks. The sound shifts from Premier's chopped, clattering beat on Mathematics to the slow, sung Umi Says, built on electric piano and a long gospel-tinged vamp. His voice, warm and elastic, holds the variety together.","Released on Rawkus in October 1999, a year after Black Star, the album caught the independent label at its commercial peak. Mos Def was already acting and was becoming the public face of conscious hip-hop. The record answers late-1990s mainstream rap with topics drawn from Brooklyn street life, faith, money, and the environment: New World Water is a song about water scarcity and pollution, unusual subject matter for rap at the time.","Rock n Roll is an argument in sound: Mos describes how Black musicians were written out of rock's history, and then the track turns into a full-tilt hard-rock band, as if reclaiming the genre by playing it. On Mathematics, DJ Premier builds his hook entirely from scratched vocal snippets of other records, a collage that enacts the song's barrage of statistics.","Reviews were rapturous and the album went gold, a strong result for an independent label. Some critics found it overlong and uneven in its genre experiments, but its range is now seen as its point. Mos Def, later known as Yasiin Bey, never quite followed it up with the same consistency, which has only increased the album's standing as his central statement.","Start with Umi Says to hear Mos as a singer, then Mathematics for his density as a rapper. Notice how often the beats leave room for live bass and guitar. Play Rock n Roll with the history in mind, then turn to Black Star's album for the duo context, and to Bad Brains or Living Colour for the rock he is claiming."],"common-resurrection":["Common's second album, released when he still went by Common Sense, is a compact Chicago record built almost entirely by the producer No I.D.: jazz and soul samples, warm basslines, and drums with a soft, rounded thump. Common raps in long, conversational verses full of wordplay and Chicago detail. The best-known track, I Used to Love H.E.R., rides a mournful guitar loop for four minutes while telling a single extended story. The mood is reflective rather than aggressive.","In 1994 hip-hop's centres of gravity were New York and Los Angeles, and a Chicago rapper on the independent Relativity label had little industry leverage. No I.D., then a teenage producer from the South Side, would later mentor Kanye West. Shortly after this album, a California band called Common Sense sued over the name, and the rapper shortened his to Common.","I Used to Love H.E.R. tells the story of a relationship with a woman who grows up, changes and drifts away; only at the end does the listener learn that she is hip-hop itself, a lament about the genre's commercial turn. The beat is a loop from George Benson's 1970s recording The Changing World, its title a quiet comment on the theme.","The album sold little at first but the song became one of the most discussed in hip-hop, and its criticism of West Coast gangsta trends prompted a pointed response from Ice Cube and a brief feud, later settled. Critics now regard Resurrection as one of the cornerstones of Chicago hip-hop and of the jazz-inflected mid-1990s style.","Begin with I Used to Love H.E.R. and listen through once without guessing the twist. Then play the title track and notice No I.D.'s drums, soft-edged and unhurried. Compare it with A Tribe Called Quest's Midnight Marauders, from the year before, and follow the producer line to Kanye West's The College Dropout."],"common-like-water-for-chocolate":["A long, warm, loose album produced mostly by J Dilla, with Questlove and James Poyser shaping the live playing. The beats sway: Dilla's drums lag and push in unpredictable ways, basslines thicken, and live keys and horns blur into the samples. Common's voice is relaxed and romantic one moment, sharply political the next. The Light, a gentle love song on a soft-rock sample, became his first real hit; Time Travelin' brings in Afrobeat horns.","This was Common's first album for MCA and his fullest immersion in the Soulquarians circle around Electric Lady Studios, where the Roots' Things Fall Apart and D'Angelo's Voodoo were also made. The cover is a 1956 Gordon Parks photograph of a Black woman drinking from a segregated water fountain in Alabama. The title comes from Laura Esquivel's novel, and the album leans into Black diasporic history.","Time Travelin' (A Tribute to Fela) features Fela Kuti's son Femi, whose horns and long rhythmic build follow the Afrobeat model. A Song for Assata tells the story of Assata Shakur's arrest and escape, with Cee-Lo Green singing. The Light is built on Bobby Caldwell's Open Your Eyes, and Dilla's way of letting the sample drift slightly against his drums gives it its swoon.","The album went gold, The Light earned a Grammy nomination, and critics greeted it as Common's reinvention. Some found it overlong at nearly eighty minutes; others hear the length as part of its jam-session character. It is now considered one of the essential records of the Soulquarians era and a key document of Dilla's influence on drumming and production.","Start with The Light, then Time Travelin' to hear the Afrobeat connection unfold slowly. Listen for the drums arriving fractionally late, the feel that later shaped neo-soul and beat music. Compare it with Things Fall Apart and Voodoo, then go back to Fela's Zombie to hear the source of those horns."],"common-be":["Common's sixth album is short and tightly edited: eleven tracks, around forty minutes, produced mostly by Kanye West with two beats by J Dilla. West's style here is warm sped-up and slowed-down soul, live strings and gospel piano, and the mood is focused and hopeful. Common raps with new economy about Chicago, love and faith. The Corner, with the Last Poets, sets a pounding drum loop beneath reflections on street-corner life; Testify tells a crime story with a twist.","After the experimental Electric Circus of 2002 sold poorly, Common joined Kanye West's GOOD Music label, and Be was West's attempt to give his fellow Chicagoan a mainstream hit without losing his identity. West was at the height of his early soul-sample style after The College Dropout and Late Registration. J Dilla, already ill, contributed Love Is... and It's Your World.","The Last Poets, the Harlem spoken-word group of the late 1960s who helped invent rap's ancestry, deliver the chorus of The Corner, a deliberate line of descent. Testify plays as a courtroom drama in which a woman defends her partner, until the final moments reveal her own role in the crime; the soulful vocal sample under it changes meaning once you know.","Be was Common's best-selling album to that point and received several Grammy nominations. Critics praised its focus and saw it as proof that West could serve another artist's voice. Some felt it smoothed off the rough edges of his earlier work. It remains his most accessible album and a standard example of mid-2000s soul-sample hip-hop.","Start with The Corner and notice how the drum loop never changes while voices pile up. Then play Testify for its storytelling, and Love Is... to hear Dilla's lighter touch beside West's. Compare it with Kanye West's Late Registration, from the same year, and go back to Resurrection to hear how far Common had travelled."],"the-pharcyde-bizarre-ride-ii-the-pharcyde":["A loose, comic, musically rich debut from four Los Angeles rappers, produced mostly by J-Swift. The beats are jazzy and bouncing, full of upright bass, horn stabs and piano, and the four voices tumble over each other with skits, harmonised hooks and goofy accents. The tone swings between pranks and confessions; the standout, Passin' Me By, is a wistful song about unrequited crushes, built on a hazy, dreamlike loop.","Released in 1992, the year Dr. Dre's The Chronic defined Los Angeles hip-hop as G-funk, the Pharcyde offered a completely different picture of the city: dancers and class clowns from South Central who had met through a dance and performance scene. The small Delicious Vinyl label let them make a sprawling, joke-heavy album. They were aligned in spirit with New York's Native Tongues groups rather than with the gangsta mainstream.","Passin' Me By takes its dreamy base from Quincy Jones's arrangement of Summer in the City and layers in a snatch of Jimi Hendrix guitar, reversed, so the track floats rather than marches. Listen to the hooks too: the group often sings in loose, untrained harmony, a vocal idea that sets them apart from almost every rap crew of the period.","The album became a slow-burn success, carried by Passin' Me By, and has grown into a cult classic of West Coast alternative rap. Some of its playground humour has dated, and critics now acknowledge that. But its vocal inventiveness and musicality are widely credited with showing that Los Angeles hip-hop could be whimsical, jazzy and introspective.","Start with Passin' Me By, listening for how the loop seems to hover and how each member's verse has its own voice and rhythm. Then try Officer and Oh Shit for the group's comic energy. Contrast it with Dr. Dre's The Chronic, from the same city and year, and pair it with De La Soul's 3 Feet High and Rising."],"the-pharcyde-labcabincalifornia":["The Pharcyde's second album trades the giddy comedy of their debut for a mellow, melancholy sound: soft drums, muted jazz and bossa nova samples, floating keyboard chords. The group produced much of it themselves, and a then little-known Detroit producer, Jay Dee, later J Dilla, contributed key beats. Runnin' is the centrepiece, a song about fear and avoidance set over a gently looping Brazilian guitar phrase.","By 1995 the group's members were older and more reflective, and the label hoped for a bigger follow-up. Instead the album came out quietly and sold below expectations, partly because the market wanted hard-edged rap. J Dilla's beats for Runnin' and Drop came through A Tribe Called Quest's Q-Tip connection and were among the first to bring him wider notice outside Detroit, alongside his work for A Tribe Called Quest.","Runnin' is built on a fragment from Stan Getz and Luiz Bonfá's recording of Saudade Vem Correndo, slowed into a lazy sway. The video for Drop, directed by Spike Jonze, was filmed with the group performing backwards and then reversed, so their lips sync while everything around them moves strangely; the members learned the lyrics phonetically in reverse.","Reviews were mixed at first, some critics missing the debut's energy. The album's reputation has grown as J Dilla's legacy has grown, and Runnin' is now regarded as one of the defining beats of mid-1990s hip-hop. Fans often prefer it to the debut for its emotional honesty and coherent sound, though it remains the less famous of the two.","Start with Runnin' and listen to the drums: slightly behind, slightly uneven, the early J Dilla fingerprint. Then watch the Drop video. Play the album at low volume, late. Follow it with Dilla's Donuts and A Tribe Called Quest's Beats, Rhymes and Life era to hear the same producer at work in other hands."],"digable-planets-reachin":["A cool, airy debut from a trio of rappers who called themselves Butterfly, Ladybug Mecca and Doodlebug. The beats, produced mainly by Butterfly, loop upright bass lines, muted trumpets and brushed drums from 1960s and 1970s jazz records, with plenty of empty space. The three voices are calm and conversational, trading lines rather than competing. Rebirth of Slick (Cool Like Dat) is the hit, but the album works as one continuous, finger-snapping mood.","Released in 1993, at the height of the jazz-rap moment that included A Tribe Called Quest and Gang Starr's jazz collaborations, the album presented a bohemian Brooklyn of coffee shops, poetry and leftist politics. Butterfly, Ishmael Butler, had grown up in Seattle and drew on his father's jazz records. The insect names reflected an interest in collective societies, and the subtitle borrows from a Jorge Luis Borges essay.","The group sampled jazz with obvious love, crediting players and namechecking musicians in their verses. Listen to how Butterfly loops short phrases so that a bass line or horn figure becomes a mantra rather than a sample. The song Femme Fetal addresses abortion rights from a pro-choice position, a rare overtly political statement on a pop-crossover hip-hop album of the time.","The album went gold and won a Grammy, though some hip-hop purists dismissed it as soft or as jazz rap for a college audience. Over time, critics have come to treat it as one of the purest expressions of the jazz-rap idea. Butler's later work as Shabazz Palaces has cast it in a new light, as the first chapter of a long experimental career.","Play it straight through and listen to the bass: almost every track rests on an upright bass loop. Start with Rebirth of Slick, then Where I'm From and Pacifics. Compare it with A Tribe Called Quest's The Low End Theory, then play Miles Davis's Kind of Blue to hear the coolness these tracks are reaching for."],"digable-planets-rebirth-of-slick":["A four-minute single built on a looping upright bass line, a muted horn phrase and a relaxed drum groove, over which three rappers describe their own cool in calm, jazzy cadences. There is no big hook in the pop sense: the chorus is a quiet chant of the title phrase. The track's appeal is its poise, a groove that never hurries and voices that sound as though they are talking in a club rather than performing.","Released as a single in late 1992 ahead of the album, it became an unlikely crossover hit in early 1993, reaching the upper levels of the American pop chart. Its success made Digable Planets the commercial face of jazz rap and placed a coffeehouse aesthetic in heavy rotation on MTV at a moment dominated by G-funk and harder East Coast sounds.","The bass line comes from Art Blakey and the Jazz Messengers' recording Stretching, and the horn phrases are drawn from jazz sources too, slowed and looped so the original players become a backing band. The three verses are deliberately unshowy, almost spoken, an aesthetic choice that gives the track its sense of cool detachment.","The single won the Grammy for best rap performance by a duo or group in 1994. Purists grumbled about novelty, but it outlasted the jazz-rap fad and still turns up on film soundtracks and in advertising as shorthand for the era. Critics now treat it as one of the defining hip-hop singles of the early 1990s.","Listen first only to the bass: a few notes repeated, yet it carries the whole track. Then notice how each rapper speaks rather than shouts. Follow it with Art Blakey's hard bop to hear the source world, and with A Tribe Called Quest's Can I Kick It? for another loop-built crossover."],"digable-planets-blowout-comb":["Digable Planets' second and last album of the 1990s is darker, funkier and more political than their debut. Live musicians thicken the sound: electric bass, Rhodes piano, saxophone and hand percussion woven into loops, giving long tracks a jam-band looseness. The voices are lower and more insistent, and the mood moves from the jazz club to the Brooklyn street and the community meeting. It sounds less like a cool pose and more like a neighbourhood.","After the crossover success of Rebirth of Slick, the group retreated from the pop audience. Blowout Comb, released late in 1994, drew on Black Panther imagery and Brooklyn activism; its packaging imitated a radical newspaper. The record sold poorly, the label lost interest, and the group split in 1995, though its members later reunited for live shows and Butler went on to found Shabazz Palaces.","Listen for the way the live players are mixed so that you cannot easily tell sample from performance; a played bass line will loop with the stiffness of a sampler, and a sampled horn will be answered by a live one. The title refers to the wide-toothed comb used for Afro hair, a deliberately Black-political symbol on the cover and in the lyrics.","Commercially a failure and critically underrated on release, Blowout Comb has been steadily reappraised and is now often preferred to the debut by serious fans, prized for its depth and groove. Its 2000s reissues and the vinyl revival brought it a new audience. The argument now is less whether it is good than why it was ignored.","Start with Dial 7 (Axioms of Creamy Spies) or 9th Wonder (Blackitolism) and listen for live instruments emerging from loops. Play the album as a whole on a slow afternoon. Compare it with the debut to hear jazz cool turning into funk and politics, and then go forward to Shabazz Palaces for Butler's later direction."],"company-flow-funcrusher-plus":["A dense, abrasive record from the New York trio of El-P, Bigg Jus and DJ Mr. Len. El-P's production is gritty and claustrophobic: distorted drum breaks, ominous synths and film-score fragments, warped samples that feel like broken machinery. The rapping is wordy and rhythmically complex, full of science fiction imagery and dystopian anxiety. It is long, sometimes punishing, and unlike most hip-hop of 1997 it makes no concessions to radio.","Funcrusher Plus expanded a 1996 EP and was one of the first major releases on Rawkus, which turned independent New York rap into a scene. Company Flow made a slogan of independence from major labels, and El-P went on to found Definitive Jux, the key underground label of the early 2000s, before forming Run the Jewels with Killer Mike in 2013.","Listen to how El-P treats drums as texture: breaks are pitched down, crushed and layered so that the rhythm sounds almost industrial, an approach he would scale up in Run the Jewels. The record's interludes and samples draw on science-fiction and film sources, framing the album as a kind of dystopian broadcast. Mr. Len's cuts often act as a second voice.","At the time it was a cult record, admired in the underground and ignored elsewhere. It is now treated as foundational for the independent rap of the late 1990s and 2000s, from Definitive Jux artists such as Aesop Rock to the noisier experiments of the 2010s. Some listeners still find it impenetrable; that difficulty is part of its identity.","Start with 8 Steps to Perfection and Vital Nerve, listening for the grain of the drums. Then try the instrumental-heavy passages for El-P's world-building. Compare it with Run the Jewels' first album to hear the same producer twenty years on, and with Public Enemy's It Takes a Nation of Millions for its noise lineage."],"aesop-rock-labor-days":["Aesop Rock's breakthrough album is a concept record about work: the nine-to-five, the wage, the daily routine, and the artist's struggle to make something under that pressure. Most beats are by Blockhead, whose production is melancholy and cinematic, with mournful strings, soft guitar and clanging drums, and Aesop produced several himself. His voice is gruff and his writing famously dense, packed with surreal images. Daylight, with its gentle loop and quietly defiant tone, became an underground anthem.","Labor Days came out in September 2001 on El-P's Definitive Jux, the New York label that defined independent rap in the early 2000s. Aesop, a Long Island native who had studied painting, had been working ordinary jobs while self-releasing records. The label's audience was largely young, college-educated and suspicious of mainstream rap, and the album's vocabulary matched it.","Listen to how the album is organised around its theme: song after song returns to the image of clocks, employers and lunch breaks, so the record works almost like a song cycle. Blockhead's beats often loop a short orchestral or guitar phrase until it becomes hypnotic, then drop it out so Aesop's voice sits nearly unaccompanied.","The album was hailed as an underground classic and gave Definitive Jux a commercial footing. Critics praised its ambition but argued about its density, some finding the writing more impressive than moving. A later data analysis of hip-hop vocabulary placed Aesop at the top for the number of unique words used, which gave his reputation for verbal excess a statistic.","Start with Daylight and No Regrets, a story-song about a girl who keeps drawing as life passes. Listen for Blockhead's string loops. Do not try to catch every word on first listen; let the rhythm carry you. Compare it with Company Flow's Funcrusher Plus and with MF DOOM's Operation: Doomsday for two other strands of verbal surrealism."],"aesop-rock-the-impossible-kid":["Aesop Rock's seventh album is his most direct and autobiographical, entirely self-produced. The beats are bright and punchy, with synthesizers, chunky drums and rock-guitar fragments, more playful than his earlier gloomy textures. He tells stories about therapy, his brothers, giving up painting, ageing, and adopting a cat, with fewer cryptic images and more plain anecdote, though the rhyme density remains high. Rings and Lazy Eye are the best-known tracks.","Aesop wrote much of the album after a period of personal upheaval, including the death of a close friend and a move away from the city into rural isolation, where he worked in a converted barn. By 2016 he was a fixture of the Minneapolis label Rhymesayers, having outlasted Definitive Jux, which went on hiatus in 2010. The album found a broad audience among listeners who had grown up with his earlier work.","Rings is about abandoning painting, his first art form, and the regret of letting a skill fade; knowing that Aesop studied fine art turns it into self-portrait. Kirby, about adopting a cat during depression, is one of the most openly tender songs in his catalogue. Listen for how his own production reuses short, cartoonish synth riffs, a deliberate lightness against heavy subjects.","The album was warmly reviewed and charted higher than any of his previous records. Critics praised its emotional clarity and called it his most accessible work; some longtime fans missed the impressionistic writing of the earlier albums. It is often recommended as the best entry point to his catalogue.","Start with Rings and Lazy Eye. Listen for the stories first, then go back for the rhymes; the album rewards a lyric sheet. Notice how bright the beats are compared with Labor Days. Pair it with Atmosphere's God Loves Ugly for another Rhymesayers confessional, and with Earl Sweatshirt's Some Rap Songs for a contrasting approach to grief."],"atmosphere-god-loves-ugly":["The Minneapolis duo of rapper Slug and producer Ant made their name with this raw, confessional album. Ant's beats are warm, dusty boom bap: soul and rock samples, heavy drums, mournful guitar loops. Slug raps about relationships, self-loathing, ambition and life in the Twin Cities, swinging from comic self-mockery to open anger. The title track and Modern Man's Hustle, a story about a couple scraping by, are among the best-known songs.","Atmosphere were the founding act of Rhymesayers Entertainment, an independent Minneapolis label built through relentless touring and direct sales to fans. In 2002, the label released God Loves Ugly through its own channels and it became one of the best-selling independent rap albums of its day. Its audience overlapped heavily with punk and emo listeners, a new crowd for hip-hop.","Listen for how Ant's samples often come from unexpected places, including rock and folk records, which helps explain the album's crossover to rock audiences. Slug's songs are frequently addressed to a specific woman or to himself in second person, making the record feel like overheard conversation. Its confessional mode anticipated the emotional rap of the 2010s.","The album earned Atmosphere a national following and established the Midwest as a centre of independent hip-hop. Some critics were wary of its emotional rawness, and the label emo rap was applied with a sneer. Later critics treat it as an important bridge between underground boom bap and the more vulnerable rap that followed.","Start with the title track, then Modern Man's Hustle, listening for the guitar loops and the plain storytelling. Notice how often Slug is the butt of his own jokes. Compare it with Aesop Rock's Labor Days, and contrast it with mainstream 2002 rap to hear how differently it imagines who a rapper is."],"jurassic-5-concrete-schoolyard":["A breezy single built on a bright, jazzy loop and crisp drums, over which four rappers pass the microphone in quick handoffs and occasionally rap in unison. The tone is celebratory and nostalgic, recalling playgrounds and block parties. The group's signature, heard clearly here, is the choreographed vocal routine: lines split between voices, call-and-response, unison phrases, all executed with the precision of a doo-wop group.","Jurassic 5 formed in Los Angeles in the early 1990s from two crews meeting at the Good Life Café, an open-mic venue that nurtured alternative West Coast rap. Concrete Schoolyard first appeared on their 1997 independent EP and became an underground hit, then a calling card. At a time of high-gloss rap videos, the group presented itself as a throwback to the routines of late-1970s and early-1980s hip-hop.","Listen to the vocal arrangement: Chali 2na's deep baritone acts like a bass voice beneath the others, and the unison passages are rehearsed with the care of a vocal group. The two DJs, Cut Chemist and Nu-Mark, treat turntables as instruments, cutting short fragments into rhythmic fills rather than simply playing the loop.","The track made Jurassic 5 a festival favourite and won them a reputation as one of the best live groups in hip-hop. Critics praised the craft while some found the retro positivity too neat. It remains the group's most-loved song and a touchstone for the late-1990s backpacker aesthetic.","Listen to the hand-offs between rappers as if they were a vocal quartet, noticing who takes the low part. Then listen for the DJs' cuts in the gaps. Compare it with Grandmaster Flash and the Furious Five's routines, which it consciously echoes, and with the Pharcyde's harmonised hooks."],"jurassic-5-quality-control":["Jurassic 5's first full-length album polishes the group's formula: bright, funky, scratch-heavy beats by Cut Chemist and DJ Nu-Mark, and four voices weaving tight routines. Chali 2na's deep voice anchors; Akil, Zaakir and Marc 7 provide quicker, lighter parts. The tracks mix old-school party energy with gentle commentary on the state of hip-hop, and the title track is a declaration of craft.","Released on a major label in 2000, after the group's independent EP had made its name, Quality Control positioned Jurassic 5 as part of a loose wave of underground acts including Black Star, the Roots and Dilated Peoples. The group toured widely, often with rock and alternative bills, and found an audience well beyond hip-hop's core.","Cut Chemist and Nu-Mark build hooks by scratching snippets of other records, so the choruses are often a collage of voices from older rap and funk. Listen for the way verses are split mid-line between rappers, a technique more common in 1970s and 1980s group rap than in the solo-star era of 2000.","The album was well reviewed and sold respectably, making the group one of the most visible faces of the alternative scene. Critics praised its craft and positivity but sometimes found it too careful, more homage than innovation. It is now valued as a high-water mark of the turn-of-the-millennium backpacker sound.","Start with the title track and Great Expectations. Listen for the DJ work in the gaps between verses. Compare the group's routines with those of Grandmaster Flash and the Furious Five, and with the Roots' Things Fall Apart from the year before for a contrasting idea of tradition."],"little-brother-the-listening":["A warm, everyday debut from a trio out of Durham, North Carolina: rappers Phonte and Big Pooh and producer 9th Wonder. The beats are soul loops, often sped up, with fat, slightly stiff drums; the subject matter is ordinary life, work, girlfriends, money worries and the frustration of making music in obscurity. Phonte's wit and conversational ease lead. The album has the charm of a demo that grew into a statement.","Little Brother came together at North Carolina Central University in Durham, far from hip-hop's coastal centres. 9th Wonder made his beats on the inexpensive software FruityLoops, which became a story in itself and encouraged a generation of bedroom producers. The album appeared on a small independent label and spread through the internet and word of mouth, an early example of the online underground.","9th Wonder's sound owes an obvious debt to Pete Rock and to Kanye West's sped-up soul, but listen to his drums: programmed in software, they have a heavy, slightly stiff thump that became his signature. The album is framed with skits imagining a radio station, a device the group would expand on the next record.","The Listening became a cult classic and launched 9th Wonder's career; he soon produced for Jay-Z and Destiny's Child. Critics praised its everyday humour and craft, while some found its boom-bap nostalgia conservative. It is remembered as a key record of the early-2000s Southern underground and a blueprint for the self-made rap career.","Start with Speed and Whatever You Say, listening for 9th Wonder's soul loops. Notice how Phonte's verses read like stand-up and diary entries. Compare it with Kanye West's The College Dropout, released the next year, and with A Tribe Called Quest's records for the model they were following."],"little-brother-the-minstrel-show":["Little Brother's major-label album is a concept record framed as a broadcast on a fictional television network, complete with sketches, commercials and theme songs. 9th Wonder's beats are richer and more polished than before, still soul-based but with more live elements and more elaborate hooks. Phonte and Big Pooh rap about family, marriage, money and the absurdity of the industry, with Phonte frequently singing.","Signed to Atlantic after their underground success, the group used the major-label platform to critique the entertainment industry's packaging of Black performers, taking its title from the nineteenth-century minstrel tradition. The concept was a pointed one: that modern rap often sold caricature. Released in 2005, it arrived in an industry moving toward Southern club music and ringtone hits.","The fictional network skits are not filler; they build a satire of how Black entertainment is programmed and sold. The video for Lovin' It was reportedly declined by BET with the explanation that it was too intelligent for its audience, an anecdote that perfectly confirmed the album's thesis.","Critics greeted the album warmly, and the BET story made it a cause. Commercially it underperformed, and 9th Wonder left the group shortly afterwards. Its reputation has grown as later artists, from J. Cole to Kendrick Lamar, have made similar critiques. Some fans prefer its ambition, others the rawness of the debut.","Play it in order, letting the skits frame the songs. Start with Lovin' It and Not Enough. Listen for Phonte's singing, which points toward his later R&B work. Compare it with De La Soul's Stakes Is High, an earlier critique of the industry, and with Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly."],"vince-staples-summertime-06":["A double album about growing up in North Long Beach, California, produced largely by No I.D. and Clams Casino, with DJ Dahi among others. The beats are stark and cold: thudding drums, wide bass, metallic synths and long silences, a West Coast sound stripped of G-funk's warmth. Vince Staples raps in a flat, precise, unsentimental voice. Norf Norf, with its bare, punishing beat, is the track most people know.","Released on Def Jam in 2015, the album takes its title from the summer of 2006, when Staples was a young teenager and his neighbourhood was in turmoil. He had emerged from the orbit of Odd Future and released acclaimed mixtapes. The album arrived in the same year as Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly and shares its Southern California setting but not its optimism or jazz.","No I.D., who had produced Common's Resurrection two decades earlier, here makes a sound nearly opposite to that warm jazz: hard, minimal, full of empty space. Listen for the use of silence and sudden drop-outs, and for the album's division into two halves, each opening and closing with ambient interludes that feel like scene changes.","The album was acclaimed as one of the year's best hip-hop records and praised for its refusal to glamorise or apologise. Some found it unrelentingly bleak; Staples himself has resisted being cast as a conscious rapper, insisting on description over message. It is now seen as a key West Coast album of the 2010s.","Start with Norf Norf and Lift Me Up, listening for how much space surrounds the voice. Then play the album through in one sitting. Compare it with Kendrick Lamar's good kid, m.A.A.d city, another Southern California coming-of-age record, and with Common's Resurrection to hear the producer's range."],"vince-staples-big-fish-theory":["Vince Staples' second album is a short, startling turn toward electronic dance music: UK garage shuffles, Detroit techno pulses, hyperpop textures and glitchy bass. Producers include SOPHIE, Flume, Zack Sekoff and Jimmy Edgar, and guests include Kendrick Lamar, Damon Albarn and Kilo Kish. Staples raps over beats designed for club systems, but his subjects remain fame, anxiety and violence around him. The album moves fast and ends abruptly.","Released in 2017, Big Fish Theory surprised listeners expecting a sequel to Summertime '06. Staples described it as Afrofuturist and pointed to Detroit techno, itself a Black American invention, as a source. The album sat within a wider moment of rap absorbing electronic music, but it went further than most toward club music.","Listen to Yeah Right, produced by SOPHIE and Flume: the bass is bent and distorted until it sounds like metal flexing, a signature of SOPHIE's work. Many tracks borrow structures from dance music, with long build-ups and drops, rather than from verse-chorus rap; the sequencing works like a DJ set.","Critics praised its daring and called it one of the year's most forward-looking rap albums, though some listeners missed the narrative depth of the debut. Its standing has grown with the posthumous reputation of SOPHIE, and it is now regularly cited in discussions of rap's crossover with electronic and experimental pop.","Play it loud on good speakers. Start with Yeah Right and Big Fish, listening to the bass more than the words. Compare it with Burial's Untrue for UK garage rhythms, and with Detroit techno pioneers such as Kevin Saunderson to hear the lineage Staples claimed."],"earl-sweatshirt-doris":["Earl Sweatshirt's major-label debut is murky and introverted, built on slow, lurching beats with smeared samples, detuned keyboards and muffled drums. Earl produced much of it himself under the alias randomblackdude, with contributions from Tyler, the Creator, the Neptunes, RZA and BadBadNotGood. His voice is low, his rhymes dense and internal. Frank Ocean sings on Sunday; Vince Staples appears on Hive. The mood is foggy, intimate and unsettled.","Earl had become a cult figure as a teenage member of Odd Future, then disappeared for months, sent by his family to a therapeutic school in Samoa, while fans clamoured for his return. Doris, released in 2013 after he came back to Los Angeles, was his response to that attention. His father was the South African poet Keorapetse Kgositsile, an absence that shadows the album.","Listen to how Earl buries his own voice in the mix rather than putting it on top, as if he were half-hiding. The beats often have loops that never quite resolve, drifting out of time. Chum, the most autobiographical song, addresses his father's absence and his own reputation, with a melancholy piano loop beneath.","Doris was praised for its technical rapping and mood, and debuted high on the charts. Some critics found it shapeless and joyless; others heard it as the arrival of a major talent. In hindsight it is a transitional record, pointing toward the stripped, personal sound of his later work.","Start with Chum and Hive. Listen at night with headphones, attending to the layering of muffled samples. Compare it with MF DOOM's dense, murky rhyme style and with Madvillainy, which Earl has often cited. Then jump to Some Rap Songs to hear where he went next."]});